    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "object individuation covering gap is rejected"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The negative countermodel the object-individuation covering must respect: a logic:Object typed into NONE of the three individuation kinds violates the OWL unionOf covering in the OWL-DL projection — an OWL reasoner rejects the class-covering gap. HONEST SCOPE: the native monotone StratifiedNAF gate does NOT enforce this disjunctive covering, so this rejection is realized only in the OWL projection and is disclosed in the loss ledger."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:candidateObjectIndividuationCovering
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "every object is one of the three individuation kinds"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The necessary-condition formalization candidate governing the class covering that every logic:Object is one of {logic:Collection, logic:Quantity, logic:FunctionalComplex}. It completes the existing individuation-kind pairwise disjointness (logic:candidateFunctionalComplexKindDisjoint) into a full partition. The covering is realized ONLY in the OWL projection as an OWL unionOf; it is NON-monotone and disclosed in the loss ledger — the native monotone StratifiedNAF chase does NOT enforce a disjunctive head, so this is projection-only, not natively enforced. It carries NO harvest back-link. Accepted through review (logic:reviewedBy logic:reviewerLogicFoundation) with a positive case (logic:cqObjectIndividuationCoveringHolds) and a negative case (logic:cqObjectIndividuationCoveringGapRejected); logic:candidateSourceHash is the sha256 of logic:Object's skos:definition for drift detection."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:candidateSourceHash "sha256:38a90c195ba1d756389d6ba561031830dbcfdfb0b56297023db5bf719bd2f7d2"^^xsd:string ;
    logic:candidateExtractionProvenance "anchored to logic:Object's skos:definition; completes the individuation-kind pairwise disjointness (logic:candidateFunctionalComplexKindDisjoint) into a partition, realized in the OWL projection (OWL unionOf), non-monotone, disclosed in the loss ledger — NOT natively enforced (design/LOGIC-FOUNDATION.md, §Typed formalization governance)"^^xsd:string ;
    logic:candidateScope "covering: every logic:Object is one of {Collection, Quantity, FunctionalComplex}"^^xsd:string ;
    logic:candidateContract logic:StratifiedNAFProfile ;
    logic:candidateCategory logic:CategoryNecessaryCondition ;
    logic:candidateLifecycle logic:CandidateAccepted ;
    logic:reviewedBy logic:reviewerLogicFoundation ;
    logic:candidatePositiveCase logic:cqObjectIndividuationCoveringHolds ;
    logic:candidateNegativeCase logic:cqObjectIndividuationCoveringGapRejected ;
    logic:candidateProjectionBehavior logic:SoundUnderApproximation ;
    logic:candidateSemanticRisk logic:RiskProjectionOnly .

# ── 6. Deliberate SocialObject ∩ InformationObject overlap (reviewed non-assertion) ─
# The one preserved overlap: gmeow:SocialObject and gmeow:InformationObject are
# deliberately NOT asserted disjoint (a myth is legitimately both social and
# informational). This candidate governs that explicit, reviewed non-assertion.
# Category: deliberate overlap; risk: advisory. Its negative case is the
# countermodel showing why the overlap must be preserved.

logic:cqSocialInformationOverlapHolds
    a logic:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "social/information overlap holds"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The positive competency case the deliberate social/information overlap must satisfy: an individual typed BOTH gmeow:SocialObject and gmeow:InformationObject — a founding myth that is at once socially sustained and information-bearing — stays coherent and is NOT forced to OWL Nothing, precisely because no disjointness between the two is asserted. The overlap the top-sortal partition deliberately preserves."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:cqSocialInformationOverlapDisjointRejected
    a logic:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "social/information disjointness is rejected"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The negative countermodel that explains WHY the social/information overlap must be preserved: IF gmeow:SocialObject were wrongly asserted OWL disjointWith gmeow:InformationObject, then a founding myth typed as both would be forced to OWL Nothing by the coherence gate — collapsing a legitimate model. The rejected disjointness is the mistake this deliberate non-assertion guards against; asserting it is the boundary the partition must NOT cross."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:candidateSocialInformationOverlap
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "social and information objects deliberately overlap"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The deliberate-overlap formalization candidate governing the explicit, reviewed NON-assertion of disjointness between gmeow:SocialObject and gmeow:InformationObject: the one overlap the top-sortal partition preserves so that socially-sustained narratives may carry information content without identity clash (a founding myth is both). Its source is gmeow:SocialObject's skos:definition, which states that no disjointness with InformationObject is asserted for exactly this reason. Accepted through review (logic:reviewedBy logic:reviewerLogicFoundation) with a positive case (logic:cqSocialInformationOverlapHolds — a myth typed as both stays coherent) and a negative countermodel (logic:cqSocialInformationOverlapDisjointRejected — asserting the disjointness would force the myth to OWL Nothing, which is why the overlap must be preserved). Its harvest back-link (logic:candidateFormalizes gmeow:SocialObject, logic:candidateSourceField logic:ProseFieldDefinition) ties it to the exact prose, and logic:candidateSourceHash is the sha256 of that definition text for drift detection. The risk is advisory: this is a non-assertion, so it contaminates nothing — it records a reviewed decision NOT to constrain."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:candidateSourceHash "sha256:d1c66e293aa207e3e28455c2ebeb59705c8656ee088e85cd5b7d858eb09f1b36"^^xsd:string ;
    logic:candidateExtractionProvenance "hand-harvested from the gmeow:SocialObject skos:definition, which states no disjointness with InformationObject is asserted so that socially-sustained narratives may carry information content without identity clash (design/LOGIC-FOUNDATION.md, §Typed formalization governance)"^^xsd:string ;
    logic:candidateScope "the deliberately-preserved gmeow:SocialObject ∩ gmeow:InformationObject overlap: an explicit, reviewed non-assertion of disjointness"^^xsd:string ;
    logic:candidateContract logic:StratifiedNAFProfile ;
    logic:candidateCategory logic:CategoryDeliberateOverlap ;
    logic:candidateDeliberateNonAssertion true ;
    logic:candidateLifecycle logic:CandidateAccepted ;
    logic:reviewedBy logic:reviewerLogicFoundation ;
    logic:candidatePositiveCase logic:cqSocialInformationOverlapHolds ;
    logic:candidateNegativeCase logic:cqSocialInformationOverlapDisjointRejected ;
    logic:candidateProjectionBehavior logic:SoundUnderApproximation ;
    logic:candidateSemanticRisk logic:RiskAdvisory ;
    logic:candidateFormalizes gmeow:SocialObject ;
    logic:candidateSourceField logic:ProseFieldDefinition .

logic:cqRelatorMediationHolds
    a logic:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "relator mediation holds"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The positive competency case the relator-mediation discipline (RelComp) must satisfy: a concrete relator that mediates at least two distinct relata stays coherent — it fires no logic:violation logic:RelComp. gmeow:Commitment, which mediates gmeow:committedAgent, gmeow:commitmentBeneficiary and gmeow:intentionGoal, is well-mediated and passes."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:cqRelatorMediationUnderMediatedRejected
    a logic:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "under-mediated relator rejected"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The negative countermodel the relator-mediation discipline must reject: a concrete relator that mediates fewer than two distinct relata is degenerate and fires logic:violation logic:RelComp — an objectified relation that connects only one entity grounds no material relation, so it is not a relator."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:candidateRelatorMediation
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "every concrete relator mediates at least two distinct relata"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The formalization candidate that generalizes the relator-mediation discipline (RelComp) from the hardcoded OntoUML seed relators to every subclass of the logic:Relator category, harvested from logic:Relator's own skos:definition ('A Relator must mediate at least two distinct entities'). The native foundation reasoner confers relator-hood on every class subClassOf logic:Relator and fires logic:violation logic:RelComp on any concrete (leaf) relator that mediates fewer than two distinct relata. Accepted through review (logic:reviewedBy logic:reviewerLogicFoundation) with a positive case (logic:cqRelatorMediationHolds) and a negative countermodel (logic:cqRelatorMediationUnderMediatedRejected); its harvest back-link (logic:candidateFormalizes logic:Relator, logic:candidateSourceField logic:ProseFieldDefinition) ties it to the exact prose, and logic:candidateSourceHash is the sha256 of that skos:definition text so a later edit surfaces as drift."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:candidateSourceHash "sha256:b3799a43d3716eb873c0fff6c0931bf68a9bdf3b6182571a305df0cb08ba40f9"^^xsd:string ;
    logic:candidateExtractionProvenance "hand-harvested from the logic:Relator skos:definition, which states a Relator must mediate at least two distinct entities (design/LOGIC-FOUNDATION.md, §Typed formalization governance)"^^xsd:string ;
    logic:candidateScope "every class subClassOf logic:Relator; a concrete relator (one with no logic: subclass) must reach at least two distinct entities — two distinct logic:mediates roles, or one non-functional role — or fire logic:violation logic:RelComp"^^xsd:string ;
    logic:candidateContract logic:StratifiedNAFProfile ;
    logic:candidateCategory logic:CategoryIntegrityConstraint ;
    logic:candidateLifecycle logic:CandidateAccepted ;
    logic:reviewedBy logic:reviewerLogicFoundation ;
    logic:candidatePositiveCase logic:cqRelatorMediationHolds ;
    logic:candidateNegativeCase logic:cqRelatorMediationUnderMediatedRejected ;
    logic:candidateProjectionBehavior logic:SoundUnderApproximation ;
    logic:candidateSemanticRisk logic:RiskCoreContaminating ;
    logic:candidateFormalizes logic:Relator ;
    logic:candidateSourceField logic:ProseFieldDefinition .

# ── Closed value class: the eleven formalization categories ──────────────────────

logic:FormalizationCategory
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Formalization Category"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed eleven-member value class of the logic:candidateCategory facet: the kind of force a prose constraint was meant to carry. Its individuals are logic:CategoryEquivalenceDefinition, logic:CategoryNecessaryCondition, logic:CategorySufficientCondition, logic:CategoryIntegrityConstraint, logic:CategoryDerivationRule, logic:CategoryDefeasibleDefault, logic:CategoryTypicality, logic:CategoryRecommendation, logic:CategoryNonEntailmentObligation, logic:CategoryDeliberateOverlap and logic:CategoryDocumentationOnly. The set is closed and atomic (Principle 9: necessary and sufficient are two categories, not one), so a misspelt label names no declared category and is rejected rather than silently accepted (design/LOGIC-FOUNDATION.md, §Typed formalization governance)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:CategoryEquivalenceDefinition
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormalizationCategory ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Equivalence Definition"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:FormalizationCategory for prose stating the necessary-and-sufficient identity of a term — a definitional equivalence. Wire value `equivalence-definition`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:CategoryNecessaryCondition
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormalizationCategory ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Necessary Condition"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:FormalizationCategory for prose stating a one-directional necessary condition (the term implies the condition). Wire value `necessary-condition`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:CategorySufficientCondition
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormalizationCategory ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Sufficient Condition"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:FormalizationCategory for prose stating a one-directional sufficient condition (the condition implies the term). Wire value `sufficient-condition`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:CategoryIntegrityConstraint
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormalizationCategory ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Integrity Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:FormalizationCategory for an integrity condition whose violation is a finding, not a derivation — a CBox constraint rather than a productive rule. Wire value `integrity-constraint`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:CategoryDerivationRule
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormalizationCategory ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Derivation Rule"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:FormalizationCategory for a productive rule whose head is entailed — content that adds to the reasoned closure. Wire value `derivation-rule`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:CategoryDefeasibleDefault
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormalizationCategory ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Defeasible Default"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:FormalizationCategory for defeasible content a more specific fact may override — default-logic force, distinct from plain typicality. Wire value `defeasible-default`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:CategoryTypicality
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormalizationCategory ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Typicality"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:FormalizationCategory for generic, what-normally-holds content — kept distinct from logic:CategoryDefeasibleDefault, whose force is override rather than genericity. Wire value `typicality`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:CategoryRecommendation
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormalizationCategory ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Recommendation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:FormalizationCategory for advisory content that is never enforced — guidance carried but not reasoned over. Wire value `recommendation`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:CategoryNonEntailmentObligation
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormalizationCategory ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Non-Entailment Obligation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:FormalizationCategory for a deliberate non-assertion — prose recording a conclusion the foundation must never draw, realized as a logic:NonEntailmentObligation. Wire value `non-entailment-obligation`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:CategoryDeliberateOverlap
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormalizationCategory ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Deliberate Overlap"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:FormalizationCategory for a mereological sharing fact deliberately admitted — an intended overlap rather than an accidental one. Wire value `deliberate-overlap`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:CategoryDocumentationOnly
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormalizationCategory ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Documentation Only"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:FormalizationCategory for explanatory prose that is NOT to be formalized at all — the category that records a deliberate decision to leave content unaxiomatized. Wire value `documentation-only`."@x-gmeow-english .

# ── Closed value class: the candidate lifecycle states ───────────────────────────

logic:CandidateLifecycleState
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Candidate Lifecycle State"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed value class of the logic:candidateLifecycle facet: the four-state machine a logic:FormalizationCandidate moves through. Its individuals are logic:CandidateProposed (entry; every extraction lands here), logic:CandidateUnderReview, logic:CandidateAccepted (canonical, axiom-bearing) and logic:CandidateRejected. logic:CandidateAccepted is reachable only through logic:CandidateUnderReview with a recorded logic:reviewedBy decision, never directly. The set is closed, so a misspelt state names no declared value and is rejected (design/LOGIC-FOUNDATION.md, §Typed formalization governance)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:CandidateProposed
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:CandidateLifecycleState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Proposed"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The lifecycle entry state: a candidate freshly extracted and not yet reviewed. An LLM extraction ALWAYS enters here; it carries no canonical force. Wire value `proposed`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:CandidateUnderReview
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:CandidateLifecycleState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Under Review"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The lifecycle state of a candidate a reviewer is actively adjudicating — the mandatory waypoint between logic:CandidateProposed and logic:CandidateAccepted. Wire value `under-review`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:CandidateAccepted
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:CandidateLifecycleState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Accepted"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The canonical, axiom-bearing lifecycle state: a candidate a reviewer has promoted, reachable only through logic:CandidateUnderReview with a recorded logic:reviewedBy decision. A candidate asserted as accepted without that review is a checked governance violation, not a soft warning. Wire value `accepted`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:CandidateRejected
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:CandidateLifecycleState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Rejected"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The terminal lifecycle state for a candidate review declined to formalize — recorded rather than deleted, so the decision (and its negative case) is auditable. Wire value `rejected`."@x-gmeow-english .

# ── Closed value class: the five discharge conditions ────────────────────────────

logic:DischargeCondition
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Discharge Condition"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed five-member value class of the logic:obligationDischargeCondition facet: the condition under which a logic:NonEntailmentObligation's absence-of-proof is conclusively checkable. Its individuals are logic:DischargeCertifiedFragment, logic:DischargeFiniteClosure, logic:DischargeSyntacticReachability, logic:DischargeConservativeExtension and logic:DischargeBoundedCorpus. The foundation engine wires logic:DischargeSyntacticReachability and logic:DischargeFiniteClosure; the other three are named but not yet engine-backed, so an obligation declaring one of them is a hard error rather than a silent pass. The set is closed, so a misspelt condition names no declared value and is rejected (design/LOGIC-FOUNDATION.md, §Typed formalization governance)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:DischargeCertifiedFragment
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DischargeCondition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Certified Fragment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The discharge condition holding when the check runs within a certified complete fragment (e.g. a Datalog stratum or EL profile over which the chase terminates and is complete). Named in the closed set but not yet engine-backed. Wire value `certified-fragment`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:DischargeFiniteClosure
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DischargeCondition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Finite Closure"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The discharge condition holding when a finite materialized derivation graph is available and complete: the obligation is discharged by checking the forbidden predicate is absent from that closure. Engine-backed (the foundation chase over an adversarial fixture). Wire value `finite-closure`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:DischargeSyntacticReachability
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DischargeCondition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Syntactic Reachability"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The discharge condition holding when a syntactic dependency/reachability analysis over the rule heads shows the forbidden predicate is unreachable — no rule head unifies with it, directly or through any chain of rule applications. Engine-backed (reachability over the foundation rule strata). Wire value `syntactic-reachability`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:DischargeConservativeExtension
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DischargeCondition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Conservative Extension"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The discharge condition holding when a conservative-extension proof establishes the added axioms cannot introduce the forbidden conclusion. Named in the closed set but not yet engine-backed. Wire value `conservative-extension`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:DischargeBoundedCorpus
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DischargeCondition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Bounded Corpus"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The discharge condition holding when the obligation is explicitly bounded to a declared corpus or mutation-test space and exhaustive checking within that bound is complete. Named in the closed set but not yet engine-backed. Wire value `bounded-corpus`."@x-gmeow-english .

# ── Closed value class: the discharge verdict ────────────────────────────────────

logic:DischargeVerdict
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Discharge Verdict"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed three-value class of the logic:obligationDischargeVerdict facet: the result an obligation check returns. Its individuals are logic:ObligationDischarged, logic:ObligationUnknown (carried forward, never 'proved absent') and logic:ObligationViolated. The set is closed, so a misspelt verdict names no declared value and is rejected (design/LOGIC-FOUNDATION.md, §Typed formalization governance)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ObligationDischarged
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DischargeVerdict ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Discharged"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The discharge verdict for an obligation conclusively checked within its declared logic:DischargeCondition: the forbidden conclusion is provably not drawn within the stated fragment or bound. Wire value `discharged`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ObligationUnknown
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DischargeVerdict ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Unknown"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The discharge verdict outside any wired discharge condition: not 'proved absent' but not-yet-discharged, carried forward to the next evaluation cycle or stronger fragment. The honest verdict the semi-decidable canonical logic demands. Wire value `unknown`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ObligationViolated
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DischargeVerdict ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Violated"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The discharge verdict when the forbidden predicate IS reachable or present in the closure: the obligation is broken and the check is a hard error. Wire value `violated`."@x-gmeow-english .

# ── Closed value class: the semantic-risk class ──────────────────────────────────

logic:SemanticRiskClass
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Semantic Risk Class"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed value class of the logic:candidateSemanticRisk facet: how far a wrong formalization of a candidate would propagate, which makes the reviewer gate proportionate. Its individuals are logic:RiskCoreContaminating, logic:RiskProjectionOnly and logic:RiskAdvisory. The set is closed, so a misspelt class names no declared value and is rejected (design/LOGIC-FOUNDATION.md, §Typed formalization governance)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:RiskCoreContaminating
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:SemanticRiskClass ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Core Contaminating"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The semantic-risk class for a candidate whose wrong formalization would corrupt the reasoned core — the highest risk, demanding the strictest review. Wire value `core-contaminating`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:RiskProjectionOnly
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:SemanticRiskClass ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Projection Only"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The semantic-risk class for a candidate whose blast radius is confined to a lossy projection surface, leaving the reasoned core intact. Wire value `projection-only`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:RiskAdvisory
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:SemanticRiskClass ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Advisory"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The semantic-risk class for a candidate with no entailment consequence at all — advisory content, the lightest review. Wire value `advisory`."@x-gmeow-english .

# ── Closed value class: the prose-field harvest source ───────────────────────────

logic:ProseField
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Prose Field"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed three-member value class of the logic:candidateSourceField facet: which annotation field of a term's prose a logic:FormalizationCandidate harvested its axiom from. Its individuals are logic:ProseFieldDefinition, logic:ProseFieldUseWhen and logic:ProseFieldAvoidWhen, each naming the annotation property it stands for via logic:proseFieldProperty (skos:definition, gmeow:useWhen and gmeow:avoidWhen respectively). The set is closed and atomic (Principle 9), so a misspelt field names no declared value and is rejected rather than silently accepted. Read with logic:candidateFormalizes (which term) it makes harvest coverage queryable in both directions — from a harvested axiom to the exact prose field it formalizes, and from a prose field to the axioms that formalize it (design/LOGIC-FOUNDATION.md, §Typed formalization governance)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:proseFieldProperty
    a logic:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "prose field property"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Names the annotation property a logic:ProseField value stands for — the exact term-level field (skos:definition, gmeow:useWhen or gmeow:avoidWhen) whose prose a harvested candidate formalizes. An annotation property, so the closed logic:ProseField set links to the real field IRI without punning it into the reasoned core."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ProseFieldDefinition
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ProseField ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Definition"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:ProseField for a term's skos:definition — the definitional or descriptive prose a candidate formalized. Wire value `definition`."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:proseFieldProperty skos:definition .

logic:ProseFieldUseWhen
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ProseField ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Use When"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:ProseField for a term's gmeow:useWhen — the applicability guidance a candidate formalized. Wire value `use-when`."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:proseFieldProperty gmeow:useWhen .

logic:ProseFieldAvoidWhen
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ProseField ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Avoid When"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:ProseField for a term's gmeow:avoidWhen — the anti-pattern or non-applicability guidance a candidate formalized. Wire value `avoid-when`."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:proseFieldProperty gmeow:avoidWhen .

# ── Candidate carrier properties (RBox) ──────────────────────────────────────────

logic:candidateSourceHash
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "candidate source hash"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The content hash of the source text a logic:FormalizationCandidate was extracted from: the anchor that ties the candidate back to the exact prose it formalizes and detects drift if that prose changes. Single-valued, xsd:string. Domain logic:FormalizationCandidate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:candidateExtractionProvenance
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "candidate extraction provenance"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The provenance of a candidate's extraction — what produced it (an extractor identifier, run, or model invocation reference). Records that an automated extraction is exactly that, so it enters at logic:CandidateProposed and earns no canonical force without review. Single-valued, xsd:string. Domain logic:FormalizationCandidate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:candidateScope
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "candidate scope"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The intended scope of a candidate's axiom — the terms, slice, or context it is meant to constrain. Read together with logic:candidateContract (the reasoning semantics it is interpreted under). Single-valued, xsd:string. Domain logic:FormalizationCandidate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:candidateContract
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:range logic:ReasoningContract ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "candidate contract"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:FormalizationCandidate to the logic:ReasoningContract (ME1) its proposed axiom would be interpreted under — the semantics that make 'sound' meaningful for it. Single-valued. Domain logic:FormalizationCandidate, range logic:ReasoningContract."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:candidateCategory
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:range logic:FormalizationCategory ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "candidate category"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:FormalizationCandidate to the logic:FormalizationCategory recording what kind of force its prose carried. Single-valued and REQUIRED: a candidate with no category resolving to the closed set is a hard governance error, never silently bucketed. Domain logic:FormalizationCandidate, range logic:FormalizationCategory."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:candidateLifecycle
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:range logic:CandidateLifecycleState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "candidate lifecycle"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:FormalizationCandidate to its current logic:CandidateLifecycleState. Single-valued. logic:CandidateAccepted is the canonical state and is legitimate only with a recorded logic:reviewedBy decision. Domain logic:FormalizationCandidate, range logic:CandidateLifecycleState."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:reviewedBy
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "reviewed by"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:FormalizationCandidate to the reviewer decision that promoted it — a provenance/agent individual recording who reviewed it and when (range stated in prose, a reviewer provenance node, so the keystone gate is machine-auditable rather than trusting a free literal). Its presence is what a logic:CandidateAccepted candidate is checked against. Single-valued. Domain logic:FormalizationCandidate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:candidatePositiveCase
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "candidate positive case"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:FormalizationCandidate to a positive competency case its proposed axiom must satisfy — a gmeow:CompetencyQuestion whose expected answer the axiom should produce (range gmeow:CompetencyQuestion, stated in prose). Domain logic:FormalizationCandidate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:candidateNegativeCase
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "candidate negative case"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:FormalizationCandidate to a negative case or countermodel — a gmeow:CompetencyQuestion the axiom must NOT satisfy, recording the boundary the formalization must respect (range gmeow:CompetencyQuestion, stated in prose). Domain logic:FormalizationCandidate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:candidateNonEntailment
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:range logic:NonEntailmentObligation ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "candidate non-entailment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:FormalizationCandidate to a logic:NonEntailmentObligation its axiom is required to respect — the forbidden conclusion the formalization must not let the engine draw. Domain logic:FormalizationCandidate, range logic:NonEntailmentObligation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:candidateProjectionBehavior
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:range logic:PreservationKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "candidate projection behavior"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:FormalizationCandidate to the logic:PreservationKind its axiom would carry under projection — how faithfully it survives down-projection to OWL/Datalog/SHACL. Reuses the loss-ledger preservation polarity rather than minting a parallel note. Single-valued. Domain logic:FormalizationCandidate, range logic:PreservationKind."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:candidateSemanticRisk
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:range logic:SemanticRiskClass ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "candidate semantic risk"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:FormalizationCandidate to its logic:SemanticRiskClass — how far a wrong formalization would propagate, which sets how strict the reviewer gate must be. Single-valued. Domain logic:FormalizationCandidate, range logic:SemanticRiskClass."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:candidateFormalizes
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "candidate formalizes"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The harvest back-link: relates a logic:FormalizationCandidate to the prose-bearing term (a gmeow: or logic: term, stated in prose) whose constraint its axiom formalizes, read together with logic:candidateSourceField (which of that term's annotation fields — skos:definition, gmeow:useWhen or gmeow:avoidWhen). Where logic:candidateSourceHash anchors the candidate to its source text by hash and logic:candidateExtractionProvenance records what produced it, this names the exact ontology term the harvested axiom keeps coherent, so harvest coverage is queryable in both directions. Present exactly when the source IS a term's annotation field (a doc-section source stays recorded in provenance alone, never a fabricated term-link); single-valued. Distinct from logic:formalizes, which bridges a logic: term to a gmeow: concept and is never a reasoned axiom. Domain logic:FormalizationCandidate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:candidateSourceField
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:range logic:ProseField ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "candidate source field"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Names which annotation field of the logic:candidateFormalizes term a candidate harvested — a logic:ProseField from the closed set (logic:ProseFieldDefinition, logic:ProseFieldUseWhen, logic:ProseFieldAvoidWhen). Together with logic:candidateFormalizes it pins the harvested axiom to the exact source triple. Co-present with logic:candidateFormalizes: a candidate carrying one of the pair without the other is a hard governance error (verify.harvest-link-paired), never a half-link. Single-valued. Domain logic:FormalizationCandidate, range logic:ProseField."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:adviceSourceField
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:ProseField ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "advice source field"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The soft-advice peer of logic:candidateSourceField, borne by a realized advice carrier — an advisory logic:Constraint (avoidWhen) or a logic:AdviceGuidance (useWhen) — rather than a governance candidate: names which annotation field of the carrier's logic:formalizes term (a logic:ProseField from the closed set) the carrier's logic:message must surface VERBATIM. The native verify gate check_advice_message_prose_binding resolves that field's current @x-gmeow-english prose and hard-fails on any divergence, so the machine-active advice can never silently drift from the prose it formalizes. A direct string binding, not a stored digest, because the message IS the prose. Deliberately domain-free — it is shared by both advice-carrier kinds, so it must not entail either type (an rdfs:domain would). Single-valued (functionality asserted on the canonical carrier logic:adviceSourceFieldFunctionality, not the deprecated OWL FunctionalProperty marker), range logic:ProseField."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:adviceSourceFieldFunctionality
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:PropertyCharacteristicAssertion ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "adviceSourceField is functional"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Asserts on the canonical carrier that logic:adviceSourceField is functional: a realized advice carrier (an advisory logic:Constraint or a logic:AdviceGuidance) names exactly one logic:ProseField from the closed set (definition, use-when, or avoid-when) — the single field whose prose its logic:message must surface verbatim, so a carrier cannot ambiguously bind to two source fields at once."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:characterizes logic:adviceSourceField ;
    logic:characteristicSort logic:functionalProperty ;
    logic:formalizes logic:adviceSourceField ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the native coherence gate must enforce that logic:adviceSourceField names exactly one value; this record is the canonical carrier the gate joins, so the obligation is reasoned rather than restated on a shape surface."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid this record when a second value would make the record ambiguous — retire the assertion instead of weakening it, and never mirror it as a hand-authored shape."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Read together with logic:characteristicSort: the pair (logic:characterizes logic:adviceSourceField, logic:characteristicSort logic:functionalProperty) is the whole obligation, and the OWL grounding view projects the matching characteristic from it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Scope: this record carries ONE integrity obligation about logic:adviceSourceField and nothing else. It is not a definition of logic:adviceSourceField (that lives on the term itself), not a shape, and not a second place to state the term's meaning — retire the record rather than broaden it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "logic:adviceSourceFieldFunctionality logic:characterizes logic:adviceSourceField ."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:candidateDeliberateNonAssertion
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "candidate deliberate non-assertion"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Marks a logic:FormalizationCandidate as one of the ontology's deliberate-non-assertion boundaries — an intentional refusal to over-assert that the foundation commits to and is held against: a forbidden entailment carried by a logic:NonEntailmentObligation, an integrity constraint whose violation is a finding, or a deliberately preserved class overlap. Its role is enumerability: the marked set IS the deliberate-non-assertion report, and it deliberately cuts across logic:candidateCategory (a boundary may be a logic:CategoryNonEntailmentObligation, a logic:CategoryIntegrityConstraint or a logic:CategoryDeliberateOverlap), so the whole boundary set is queryable as one report rather than inferred from an overloaded category facet (an integrity-constraint boundary is not distinguishable from an ordinary integrity constraint by category alone). Present and true on exactly the guardrail candidates; single-valued, xsd:boolean. Domain logic:FormalizationCandidate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# ── Obligation carrier properties (RBox) ─────────────────────────────────────────

logic:obligationForbiddenPredicate
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:NonEntailmentObligation ;
    rdfs:range xsd:anyURI ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "obligation forbidden predicate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The IRI of the predicate a logic:NonEntailmentObligation forbids the closure from ever containing (and forbids any rule head from producing). The check reads this IRI, then either proves it unreachable over the rule strata (logic:DischargeSyntacticReachability) or absent from the materialized closure (logic:DischargeFiniteClosure). Single-valued, xsd:anyURI. Domain logic:NonEntailmentObligation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:obligationDischargeCondition
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:NonEntailmentObligation ;
    rdfs:range logic:DischargeCondition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "obligation discharge condition"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:NonEntailmentObligation to the logic:DischargeCondition under which its absence-of-proof is conclusively checkable. An obligation declaring a condition the engine does not wire (anything other than logic:DischargeSyntacticReachability or logic:DischargeFiniteClosure) is a hard error, never a silent pass. Domain logic:NonEntailmentObligation, range logic:DischargeCondition."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:obligationDischargeVerdict
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:NonEntailmentObligation ;
    rdfs:range logic:DischargeVerdict ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "obligation discharge verdict"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:NonEntailmentObligation to the logic:DischargeVerdict the check returned — logic:ObligationDischarged, logic:ObligationUnknown or logic:ObligationViolated. Computed by the engine, not asserted by hand. Domain logic:NonEntailmentObligation, range logic:DischargeVerdict."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# ── The conjecture / anti-conjecture library (CBox) ──────────────────────────────
# The dynamic, testable specialization of the formalization-governance spine. A
# logic:Conjecture is a logic:FormalizationCandidate (so it carries all eight
# universal candidate carriers) whose positive/negative cases are ENGINE-PRODUCED
# rather than reviewer-asserted: a named, standpoint-scoped candidate formula under
# active epistemic test, phi tested symmetrically against not-phi. Its verdict is
# scoped to a reified standpoint (Principle 9: never a global-false verdict), its
# conclusiveness reuses the existing logic:DischargeVerdict value class, and its
# EPISTEMIC lifecycle (open / corroborated / refuted-in-standpoint / withdrawn) is
# ORTHOGONAL to the GOVERNANCE lifecycle it inherits from the candidate spine
# (proposed / under-review / accepted / rejected). Two symmetric promotion legs feed
# corroborated phi back as an axiom candidate and robustly-refuted phi forward as a
# logic:NonEntailmentObligation candidate; Lakatos refinement edges relate a weakened
# successor to the witness it now excludes; and a bounded corpus pre-order
# (logic:conjectureEntails) carries phi-entails-psi propagation. Normative in
# design/LOGIC-FOUNDATION.md, the conjecture-and-refutation section.

logic:Conjecture
    a logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Conjecture"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A named, standpoint-scoped candidate formula under active epistemic test: the dynamic, testable specialization of the logic:FormalizationCandidate spine (rdfs:subClassOf logic:FormalizationCandidate). Because it subclasses the candidate, a conjecture carries all eight universal candidate carriers (logic:candidateSourceHash, logic:candidateExtractionProvenance, logic:candidateScope, logic:candidateContract, logic:candidateCategory, logic:candidateLifecycle, logic:candidateProjectionBehavior, logic:candidateSemanticRisk); what specializes it is that its positive/negative cases are ENGINE-PRODUCED (via a scoped reasoning run recorded through logic:verdictProvenance) rather than reviewer-asserted. Its verdict is always scoped to a single reified standpoint (logic:conjectureStandpoint, REQUIRED — Principle 9 refuses a global-false verdict), and it carries an EPISTEMIC lifecycle (logic:conjectureLifecycleState: open / corroborated / refuted-in-standpoint / withdrawn) ORTHOGONAL to the GOVERNANCE lifecycle it inherits from the candidate (logic:candidateLifecycle). Conclusiveness reuses the existing logic:DischargeVerdict value class (logic:conjectureDischargeVerdict), never a freshly minted notion: a conclusive independence carries logic:ObligationDischarged, a budget-exhausted or fragment-gap run carries logic:ObligationUnknown. The conjectured formula is tested symmetrically against its constructed strong negation as two INDEPENDENT legs — support for the formula (the standpoint's KB entails it) and support for its negation (the KB refutes it, decided by the inconsistency of asserting the formula) — so a KB that entails BOTH the formula AND its negation from one standpoint yields the Belnap glut logic:InfoBoth, a within-standpoint contradiction LOCALIZED to the candidate proposition (a genuine refutation still carrying a concrete logic:ContradictionWitness), whereas a KB contradictory for reasons UNRELATED to the candidate cannot host a meaningful test at all. A refutation names a concrete logic:ContradictionWitness, and the two symmetric promotion legs feed a corroborated formula back as a canonical-axiom candidate (logic:conjecturePromotionCandidate) and a robustly-refuted formula forward as a logic:NonEntailmentObligation candidate (logic:antiConjectureObligationCandidate) (design/LOGIC-FOUNDATION.md, the conjecture-and-refutation section)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox .

logic:conjectureFormula
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Conjecture ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "conjecture formula"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The canonical, alpha-normalized identity of the conjectured formula: the content_key string of the full first-order AST (the alpha-normalized content identity from the full-FOL formula AST), so two conjectures that name the same formula up to bound-variable renaming share one key and cannot be double-counted. Single-valued, xsd:string. Domain logic:Conjecture."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox .

logic:conjectureStandpoint
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Conjecture ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "conjecture standpoint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Conjecture to the reified standpoint its verdict is scoped to — a REQUIRED, single-valued IRI (a gmeow:Standpoint, stated in prose). Standpoint scoping here is REIFICATION, never a named graph: the verdict is 'the formula is refuted FROM THIS standpoint', never a global-false claim (Principle 9). A conjecture with no standpoint has an ill-scoped verdict and is a hard governance error (verify.conjecture-standpoint-scoped). Single-valued. Domain logic:Conjecture."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox .

logic:VerdictProvenanceKind
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Verdict Provenance Kind"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed two-member value class of the logic:verdictProvenance facet: the one genuinely-new axis a logic:Conjecture adds over a plain logic:FormalizationCandidate — whether a candidate's positive/negative cases were ENGINE-PRODUCED by a scoped reasoning run or REVIEWER-ASSERTED by hand. Its individuals are logic:VerdictEngineProduced and logic:VerdictReviewerAsserted. The set is closed, so a misspelt provenance names no declared value and is rejected (design/LOGIC-FOUNDATION.md, the conjecture-and-refutation section)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:VerdictEngineProduced
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:VerdictProvenanceKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Engine Produced"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The verdict-provenance value for a conjecture whose positive/negative cases were produced by a scoped reasoning run over the standpoint, not asserted by a reviewer: the defining mark of a logic:Conjecture against a plain logic:FormalizationCandidate. Wire value `engine-produced`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:VerdictReviewerAsserted
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:VerdictProvenanceKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Reviewer Asserted"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The verdict-provenance value for cases a reviewer asserted by hand rather than an engine deriving — the plain-candidate default, retained on the closed set so a conjecture may honestly record a hand-adjudicated case (e.g. a withdrawal) without pretending it was engine-produced. Wire value `reviewer-asserted`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:verdictProvenance
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Conjecture ;
    rdfs:range logic:VerdictProvenanceKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "verdict provenance"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Conjecture to the logic:VerdictProvenanceKind recording whether its cases were engine-produced or reviewer-asserted — the one genuinely-new axis the conjecture library adds over the candidate spine. Single-valued and REQUIRED. Domain logic:Conjecture, range logic:VerdictProvenanceKind."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox .

logic:ConjectureLifecycleState
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Conjecture Lifecycle State"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed four-member value class of the logic:conjectureLifecycleState facet: the EPISTEMIC state a logic:Conjecture occupies, ORTHOGONAL to the GOVERNANCE lifecycle (logic:CandidateLifecycleState) it inherits from the candidate spine — Principle 9 refuses to collapse two distinct axes into one. Its individuals are logic:ConjectureOpen, logic:ConjectureCorroborated, logic:ConjectureRefutedInStandpoint and logic:ConjectureWithdrawn, each projected from a Belnap truth-value origin (Supported becomes Corroborated; Opposed or Both becomes RefutedInStandpoint; Neither-conclusive becomes Open carrying logic:ObligationDischarged; Undetermined or BudgetExhausted becomes Open carrying logic:ObligationUnknown; Withdrawn is an author action, never engine-produced). The set is closed, so a misspelt state names no declared value and is rejected (design/LOGIC-FOUNDATION.md, the conjecture-and-refutation section)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ConjectureOpen
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ConjectureLifecycleState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Open"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The epistemic state of a conjecture whose symmetric formula / negation test was NOT conclusive in either direction: neither corroborated nor refuted. Two Belnap origins land here and are told apart by the carried logic:conjectureDischargeVerdict — a Neither-conclusive run (formula and negation both genuinely undecided within a complete fragment) carries logic:ObligationDischarged (conclusive independence), while an Undetermined or budget-exhausted run carries logic:ObligationUnknown (not proved absent, carried forward). A timeout NEVER reads as refuted. Wire value `open`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ConjectureCorroborated
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ConjectureLifecycleState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Corroborated"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The epistemic state of a conjecture whose formula was Supported (Belnap true) from its standpoint: the symmetric test found for the formula and no counterexample. Corroboration is provisional support, never proof — a corroborated formula becomes eligible for the positive promotion leg (logic:conjecturePromotionCandidate to a canonical-axiom candidate), still gated through review. Wire value `corroborated`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ConjectureRefutedInStandpoint
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ConjectureLifecycleState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Refuted In Standpoint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The epistemic state of a conjecture whose formula was Opposed or Both (Belnap false or contradictory) from its standpoint: a concrete counterexample was found, forcing some individual to OWL Nothing. The refutation is scoped to the standpoint (Principle 9), never global-false, and MUST name a concrete logic:ContradictionWitness (logic:conjectureRefutationWitness, enforced by logic:ConjectureShape and verify.conjecture-refutation-witness-required). A robustly-refuted formula becomes eligible for the anti-conjecture promotion leg (logic:antiConjectureObligationCandidate to a logic:NonEntailmentObligation candidate forbidding the formula). Wire value `refuted-in-standpoint`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ConjectureWithdrawn
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ConjectureLifecycleState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Withdrawn"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The terminal epistemic state of a conjecture its author retired — a deliberate authoring action, NEVER an engine-produced verdict (a withdrawn conjecture carries logic:verdictProvenance logic:VerdictReviewerAsserted). Recorded rather than deleted, so the retirement and any partial evidence stay auditable. Distinct from refuted: withdrawal is a human decision to stop testing, not a found counterexample. Wire value `withdrawn`."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:conjectureLifecycleState
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Conjecture ;
    rdfs:range logic:ConjectureLifecycleState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "conjecture lifecycle state"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Conjecture to its current EPISTEMIC logic:ConjectureLifecycleState (open / corroborated / refuted-in-standpoint / withdrawn). Single-valued and REQUIRED. Orthogonal to logic:candidateLifecycle (the GOVERNANCE state inherited from the candidate spine): a conjecture may be governance-accepted yet epistemically open, so the two are never collapsed (Principle 9). Domain logic:Conjecture, range logic:ConjectureLifecycleState."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox .

logic:withdrawalReason
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Conjecture ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "withdrawal reason"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The author's free-text rationale recorded when a logic:Conjecture is retired to logic:ConjectureWithdrawn — the human note explaining WHY testing stopped, carried on the compensating author-withdrawal segment appended to the append-only conjecture library. Distinct from a found counterexample (which a refutation names through logic:conjectureRefutationWitness): a withdrawal is a deliberate authoring decision to stop testing, so this reason is a reviewer-asserted datum (the withdrawal carries logic:verdictProvenance logic:VerdictReviewerAsserted), NEVER engine-produced. Optional and single-valued; present exactly when a withdrawal names a reason. Domain logic:Conjecture, range xsd:string."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:ContradictionWitness
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Contradiction Witness"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The RDF shape of the runtime refutation witness a conjecture test emits when it refutes the formula: the concrete counterexample that forced an individual to OWL Nothing. It names the offending individual (logic:witnessIndividual), the world IRI the contradiction was found in (logic:witnessWorld), and the set of premises that jointly entailed the contradiction (logic:witnessPremise, one serialized triple each, multi-valued). It is an ABox artifact — a produced fact about a specific reasoning run, not a constraint — and is present exactly when its conjecture is logic:ConjectureRefutedInStandpoint (design/LOGIC-FOUNDATION.md, the conjecture-and-refutation section)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:witnessIndividual
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ContradictionWitness ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "witness individual"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The individual a logic:ContradictionWitness records as forced to OWL Nothing — the entity whose type became unsatisfiable under the conjectured formula, identifying exactly where the contradiction bit. Single-valued, xsd:string (the individual's IRI as a string). Domain logic:ContradictionWitness."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:witnessWorld
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ContradictionWitness ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "witness world"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The world IRI the logic:ContradictionWitness was found in — the modal/context world within the conjecture's standpoint where the contradiction was realized, so the refutation stays world-scoped rather than asserted across all worlds. Single-valued, xsd:string (the world IRI as a string). Domain logic:ContradictionWitness."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:witnessPremise
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ContradictionWitness ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "witness premise"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A premise that participated in the contradiction a logic:ContradictionWitness records — one serialized triple (subject predicate object) per value, MULTI-valued because a refutation typically rests on several jointly-inconsistent facts. Together the premises are the minimal derivation the reader can replay to see why the formula is refuted from the standpoint. Multi-valued, xsd:string. Domain logic:ContradictionWitness."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:conjectureRefutationWitness
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Conjecture ;
    rdfs:range logic:ContradictionWitness ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "conjecture refutation witness"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a refuted logic:Conjecture to the logic:ContradictionWitness that refutes it — the concrete counterexample naming the individual forced to OWL Nothing, the world, and the premises. Present exactly when the conjecture is logic:ConjectureRefutedInStandpoint (a refuted conjecture with no witness is a hard error, logic:ConjectureShape and verify.conjecture-refutation-witness-required; a witness on a non-refuted conjecture is meaningless). Domain logic:Conjecture, range logic:ContradictionWitness."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox .

logic:conjectureVerdict
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Conjecture ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "conjecture verdict"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Conjecture to the content-addressed reasoning-result node its verdict was read from — a logic:ReasoningResult (stated in prose) produced by the scoped reasoning run over the standpoint. Where logic:conjectureLifecycleState and logic:conjectureDischargeVerdict carry the projected epistemic verdict and conclusiveness, this links back to the full result graph the projection was computed from, so the verdict is auditable against its source run. Single-valued. Domain logic:Conjecture."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox .

logic:conjectureDischargeVerdict
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Conjecture ;
    rdfs:range logic:DischargeVerdict ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "conjecture discharge verdict"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Conjecture to the logic:DischargeVerdict carrying its CONCLUSIVENESS — reusing the existing three-value obligation class rather than minting a parallel notion. logic:ObligationDischarged marks a conclusive result (a corroboration or a refutation within a complete fragment, or a conclusive independence for an open conjecture); logic:ObligationUnknown marks a budget-exhausted or fragment-gap run that is carried forward, never 'proved'. An open conjecture carrying logic:ObligationUnknown that is NOT in logic:ConjectureOpen is a hard error (verify.conjecture-open-if-inconclusive): a timeout may never read as refuted. Single-valued. Domain logic:Conjecture, range logic:DischargeVerdict."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox .

logic:conjecturePromotionCandidate
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Conjecture ;
    rdfs:range logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "conjecture promotion candidate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The POSITIVE promotion leg: relates a logic:ConjectureCorroborated conjecture to the logic:FormalizationCandidate proposing to promote its formula to a canonical axiom. The candidate still runs the ordinary reviewer gate (corroboration is provisional support, never proof), so a corroborated conjecture never becomes a canonical axiom by mere entailment. Present exactly when the conjecture is corroborated. Domain logic:Conjecture, range logic:FormalizationCandidate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox .

logic:antiConjectureObligationCandidate
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Conjecture ;
    rdfs:range logic:NonEntailmentObligation ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "anti-conjecture obligation candidate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The symmetric anti-conjecture promotion leg: relates a robustly logic:ConjectureRefutedInStandpoint conjecture to a candidate logic:NonEntailmentObligation forbidding its formula — a refuted formula is not merely dropped, it becomes a first-class commitment that the foundation must NEVER draw it. Mirrors logic:conjecturePromotionCandidate (corroborated to axiom candidate) under negation (refuted to non-entailment candidate). Present exactly when the conjecture is refuted-in-standpoint. Domain logic:Conjecture, range logic:NonEntailmentObligation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox .

logic:conjectureRefinedFrom
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Conjecture ;
    rdfs:range logic:Conjecture ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "conjecture refined from"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The Lakatos refinement edge: relates a successor conjecture to the refuted conjecture it weakens — the 'monster-barring' move where a refuted conjecture is not abandoned but replaced by a strictly weaker successor that excludes the counterexample. Read together with logic:survivesCounterexample (the witness the successor now excludes). Domain logic:Conjecture, range logic:Conjecture."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox .

logic:survivesCounterexample
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Conjecture ;
    rdfs:range logic:ContradictionWitness ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "survives counterexample"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The Lakatos survival edge: relates a refined successor conjecture to the logic:ContradictionWitness that refuted its predecessor and which the successor now EXCLUDES — the evidence that the refinement did real work, not just relabelled the claim. Paired with logic:conjectureRefinedFrom, it lets the corpus show a refinement chain where each successor demonstrably survives a witness that killed its parent. Domain logic:Conjecture, range logic:ContradictionWitness."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox .

logic:conjectureEntails
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Conjecture ;
    rdfs:range logic:Conjecture ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "conjecture entails"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The corpus entailment pre-order over conjectures: logic:conjectureEntails relates a formula phi to a formula psi it entails (phi models psi), so a refutation of psi propagates contrapositively to phi (if psi is refuted, phi cannot stand). The relation is a PRE-ORDER in intent (reflexive and transitive), but its full transitive closure is NOT materialized as a stored relation: the vocabulary carries each pairwise edge and the propagation competency query (conjecture-entails-propagation.rq) reasons over those edges directly, so contrapositive propagation is queryable without the corpus-wide closure being computed as a persisted extension. Each edge is itself a conjecture test (that phi genuinely entails psi), not a bare assertion. Domain logic:Conjecture, range logic:Conjecture."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox .

logic:ProjectionLoss
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Projection Loss"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "An in-band declaration that a lossy projection dropped structure its source carried — emitted into the projected graph itself, never a sidecar. GTS stays lossless; loss is declared only at the exit gate (Principle 17 / Principle 4). A loss-aware SPARQL CONSTRUCT mints one when its template omits an RDF-1.2 reification layer that its WHERE matched, so a downstream consumer of the projection can see exactly what was shed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:lossCode
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "loss code"@x-gmeow-english ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    skos:definition "The stable machine code of a logic:ProjectionLoss — reifier-layer-dropped, annotation-layer-dropped, or standpoint-scope-dropped — naming exactly which structure the projection shed. The codes are shared verbatim with the conversion loss ledger so in-band and contract-level loss declarations agree."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:lostReifies
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ProjectionLoss ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "lost reifies"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Points from a logic:ProjectionLoss to the RDF-1.2 triple term whose reifier (and any annotations) the projection dropped, so a consumer can recover which statement lost its reification layer. The object is a quoted triple term; its range is intentionally left open (stated in prose) to keep the OWL down-projection DL-clean."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# ── The Correspondence calculus (the ninth node kind's spine) ─────────────────────
# Cross-ontology alignment as a first-class, law-bearing logic: object. A
# logic:Correspondence (declared above as the ninth logic:NodeKind) wraps a logic:Lens —
# its executable get/put core — in a meta-formula envelope: a typed
# logic:CorrespondenceRelation on an ordered lattice, an algebraic logic:MorphismClass on
# the ordered law-spine, the separated quantitative axes (logic:confidence,
# logic:evidenceStrength, logic:weight, logic:probability) plus logic:Determinacy, the
# claimed logic:LawClaim constraints (whose discharge reuses the logic:DischargeVerdict /
# logic:DischargeCondition vocabulary of the foundation), and a standpoint index. SSSOM,
# EDOAL, FnO, SPARQL CONSTRUCT and the OWL-alignment axioms are generated lowerings of this
# one object, never second sources (design/LOGIC-CORRESPONDENCE.md).

logic:Lens
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Lens"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The executable asymmetric core a logic:Correspondence wraps: a get / put leg pair between a rich source S and a derived view V, with get : S → V the down-projection and put : V × S → S the up-projection. GMEOW is always the source S and the external vocabulary the view V, so down-projection (GMEOW → external) is the get leg and up-projection (external → GMEOW) is the put leg. The view is the smaller, derived thing; put folds a possibly-fresh view back into the rich source, and the ingest-with-no-prior-state case (S empty) is exactly where a witness must travel in the view — the mnemomorphism a logic:Correspondence records with logic:mnemomorphic. Both legs are logic:TransactionProgram bodies over the path semantics of the transaction layer and logic:PathShape — no new pattern language is introduced. It is not the logic:Correspondence envelope, which separately carries the relation, quantitative axes, law claims, and standpoint (design/LOGIC-CORRESPONDENCE.md, the orientation-convention section)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:GroundingCorrespondence
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Grounding Correspondence"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A logic:Correspondence owned by one of the three co-foundational grounding slices (logic:, math:, or lang:) and shipped as meta-level correspondence data in graph/correspondence-laws. Unlike an ordinary consumer mapping, every grounding correspondence explicitly declares its logic:morphismClass, logic:morphismKind, and logic:preservationKind, and names both related terms through logic:sourceEndpoint and logic:targetEndpoint. It remains outside object-level closure: external vocabularies are linked by reference and their TBoxes are never silently imported."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:bridge a logic:GroundingCorrespondence ; logic:sourceEndpoint logic:Restriction ; logic:targetEndpoint OWL Restriction ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Grounding correspondences are shipped correspondence-law data; they do not import or canonize the external target vocabulary."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a reviewed cross-vocabulary bridge whose source is owned by lang:, math:, or logic:."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use for an ordinary domain projection or for a row whose external term is presented as the canonical source."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name one grounding source and one external target, then declare the morphism class, morphism kind, and preservation kind explicitly."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    logic:subClassOf [
        a logic:Restriction ; logic:onProperty gmeow:justification ;
        logic:maxQualifiedCardinality 1 ; logic:onClass logic:Thing ;
    ] ,
    [
        a logic:Restriction ; logic:onProperty logic:sourceEndpoint ;
        logic:maxQualifiedCardinality 1 ; logic:onClass logic:Thing ;
    ] ,
    [
        a logic:Restriction ; logic:onProperty logic:targetEndpoint ;
        logic:maxQualifiedCardinality 1 ; logic:onClass logic:Thing ;
    ] ,
    [
        a logic:Restriction ; logic:onProperty logic:morphismClass ;
        logic:maxQualifiedCardinality 1 ; logic:onClass logic:Thing ;
    ] ,
    [
        a logic:Restriction ; logic:onProperty logic:morphismClass ;
        logic:allValuesFrom [ logic:oneOf ( logic:Isomorphism logic:SectionRetraction logic:WellBehavedLens
            logic:LossyLens logic:Prism logic:AffineCorrespondence logic:BridgeView ) ] ;
    ] ,
    [
        a logic:Restriction ; logic:onProperty logic:morphismKind ;
        logic:maxQualifiedCardinality 1 ; logic:onClass logic:Thing ;
    ] ,
    [
        a logic:Restriction ; logic:onProperty logic:morphismKind ;
        logic:allValuesFrom [ logic:oneOf ( logic:InstitutionMorphism logic:CommitmentShiftingBridge ) ] ;
    ] ,
    [
        a logic:Restriction ; logic:onProperty logic:preservationKind ;
        logic:maxQualifiedCardinality 1 ; logic:onClass logic:Thing ;
    ] ,
    [
        a logic:Restriction ; logic:onProperty logic:preservationKind ;
        logic:allValuesFrom [ logic:oneOf ( logic:ExactPreservation logic:SoundUnderApproximation
            logic:CompleteOverApproximation logic:InconsistencyPreserving
            logic:InconsistencyReflecting logic:ValidationOnly logic:Unsupported ) ] ;
    ] .

# Migrated declarative requiredness (shapes/mapping-dsl-shapes.ttl
# logic:GroundingCorrespondenceShape, Principle 17): the six per-property
# ∃-requirements. The shape's compound sh:xone frontend-selection, the
# BridgeView/CommitmentShiftingBridge pairing sh:or, and the two sh:sparql
# endpoint-agreement / anti-equivalence checks are NOT re-authored here — they
# are a genuine multi-level disjunctive class-membership + cross-property
# equality expressiveness gap over an already-large, widely-shared module, left
# as an honest boundary for a follow-up pass rather than a fragile
# under-tested reproduction.
gmeow:GroundingCorrespondenceJustificationRequiredConstraint
    a logic:DisjunctiveRequirednessConstraint ;
    rdfs:label "GroundingCorrespondence justification requiredness constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    skos:definition "A closed-world requiredness constraint: a logic:GroundingCorrespondence must explicitly declare one IRI-valued gmeow:justification."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:onClass logic:GroundingCorrespondence ;
    logic:anyOf gmeow:justification ;
    logic:formalizes logic:GroundingCorrespondence ;
    logic:message "GroundingCorrespondence must explicitly declare one IRI-valued gmeow:justification"@x-gmeow-english .

gmeow:GroundingCorrespondenceSourceEndpointRequiredConstraint
    a logic:DisjunctiveRequirednessConstraint ;
    rdfs:label "GroundingCorrespondence source-endpoint requiredness constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    skos:definition "A closed-world requiredness constraint: a logic:GroundingCorrespondence must explicitly declare one IRI-valued logic:sourceEndpoint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:onClass logic:GroundingCorrespondence ;
    logic:anyOf logic:sourceEndpoint ;
    logic:formalizes logic:GroundingCorrespondence ;
    logic:message "GroundingCorrespondence must explicitly declare one IRI-valued logic:sourceEndpoint"@x-gmeow-english .

gmeow:GroundingCorrespondenceTargetEndpointRequiredConstraint
    a logic:DisjunctiveRequirednessConstraint ;
    rdfs:label "GroundingCorrespondence target-endpoint requiredness constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    skos:definition "A closed-world requiredness constraint: a logic:GroundingCorrespondence must explicitly declare one IRI-valued logic:targetEndpoint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:onClass logic:GroundingCorrespondence ;
    logic:anyOf logic:targetEndpoint ;
    logic:formalizes logic:GroundingCorrespondence ;
    logic:message "GroundingCorrespondence must explicitly declare one IRI-valued logic:targetEndpoint"@x-gmeow-english .

gmeow:GroundingCorrespondenceMorphismClassRequiredConstraint
    a logic:DisjunctiveRequirednessConstraint ;
    rdfs:label "GroundingCorrespondence morphismClass requiredness constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    skos:definition "A closed-world requiredness constraint: a logic:GroundingCorrespondence must explicitly declare one logic:morphismClass."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:onClass logic:GroundingCorrespondence ;
    logic:anyOf logic:morphismClass ;
    logic:formalizes logic:GroundingCorrespondence ;
    logic:message "GroundingCorrespondence must explicitly declare one logic:morphismClass"@x-gmeow-english .

gmeow:GroundingCorrespondenceMorphismKindRequiredConstraint
    a logic:DisjunctiveRequirednessConstraint ;
    rdfs:label "GroundingCorrespondence morphismKind requiredness constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    skos:definition "A closed-world requiredness constraint: a logic:GroundingCorrespondence must explicitly declare one logic:morphismKind."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:onClass logic:GroundingCorrespondence ;
    logic:anyOf logic:morphismKind ;
    logic:formalizes logic:GroundingCorrespondence ;
    logic:message "GroundingCorrespondence must explicitly declare one logic:morphismKind"@x-gmeow-english .

gmeow:GroundingCorrespondencePreservationKindRequiredConstraint
    a logic:DisjunctiveRequirednessConstraint ;
    rdfs:label "GroundingCorrespondence preservationKind requiredness constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    skos:definition "A closed-world requiredness constraint: a logic:GroundingCorrespondence must explicitly declare one logic:preservationKind."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:onClass logic:GroundingCorrespondence ;
    logic:anyOf logic:preservationKind ;
    logic:formalizes logic:GroundingCorrespondence ;
    logic:message "GroundingCorrespondence must explicitly declare one logic:preservationKind"@x-gmeow-english .

logic:CorrespondenceRelation
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Correspondence Relation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The typed, closed value class of a logic:Correspondence's relation, on an ordered lattice rather than a free string: logic:Equiv ⊐ { logic:Subsumes, logic:SubsumedBy } ⊐ logic:Overlaps ⊐ logic:RelatedMatch, with logic:Disjoint the negative pole. From one authored relation the compiler derives the SSSOM predicate, the EDOAL relation symbol and the OWL-alignment-axiom strength, so the generated alignment artifacts cannot drift apart. The set is closed, so a misspelt relation names no declared value and is rejected (design/LOGIC-CORRESPONDENCE.md, the relation-lattice section)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:Equiv
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:CorrespondenceRelation ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "equivalent"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The top of the correspondence-relation lattice: the source pattern and the view pattern denote one and the same extension. Lowers to sssom:exactMatch and (when the logic:MorphismClass and logic:LawClaim set support it) OWL equivalentClass; the strongest claim, never licensed for a commitment-shifting bridge."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:Subsumes
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:CorrespondenceRelation ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "subsumes"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The source pattern is broader than the view: every view instance is a source instance but not conversely. Lowers to sssom:broadMatch and an OWL subClassOf with the view as the subclass. One of the two mid-lattice directional relations under logic:Equiv."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:SubsumedBy
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:CorrespondenceRelation ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "subsumed by"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The source pattern is narrower than the view: every source instance is a view instance but not conversely. Lowers to sssom:narrowMatch and an OWL subClassOf with the source as the subclass. The converse direction of logic:Subsumes, the second mid-lattice relation under logic:Equiv."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:Overlaps
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:CorrespondenceRelation ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "overlaps"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Source and view share instances without either subsuming the other: a partial, caveated alignment. Lowers to sssom:closeMatch (an OWL equivalence is unsupported and the overclaim gate refuses one). Below the directional band, above logic:RelatedMatch. Distinct from the mereological relation logic:overlaps (shared parts of one whole); this is a relation between two aligned patterns."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:RelatedMatch
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:CorrespondenceRelation ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "related match"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The weakest positive relation: source and view are associated (curatorially relevant) but neither equivalent, subsuming nor reliably overlapping. Lowers to sssom:relatedMatch only; no logical alignment axiom is emitted. The floor of the positive lattice, above only the negative pole logic:Disjoint."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:Disjoint
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:CorrespondenceRelation ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "disjoint match"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The negative pole of the correspondence-relation lattice: source and view share no instances, an asserted non-alignment. Lowers to OWL disjointWith. Distinct from the mereological relation logic:disjoint (two entities sharing no part); this records that two aligned patterns are deliberately kept apart."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:MorphismClass
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Morphism Class"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The ordered law-spine of a logic:Correspondence: how much invertibility it may lawfully claim, where each rung caps the laws the correspondence may assert. From strongest to floor the seven rungs are logic:Isomorphism, logic:SectionRetraction, logic:WellBehavedLens, logic:LossyLens, logic:Prism, logic:AffineCorrespondence and logic:BridgeView. The spine fuses the profunctor-optic lattice with the categorical subobject notion; composition can only weaken the rung, never strengthen it, and a logic:mnemomorphic witness is what lets a correspondence climb it. The set is closed, so a misspelt class names no declared rung and is rejected (design/LOGIC-CORRESPONDENCE.md, the ordered-law-spine section)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:Isomorphism
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:MorphismClass ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Isomorphism"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The top rung: an iso, get ∘ put = id and put ∘ get = id, a full round-trip in both directions. The only rung that licenses a confidence-1.0 equivalence. Caps: both round-trip laws claimable."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:SectionRetraction
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:MorphismClass ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Section / Retraction"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Perfect subsumption: a split mono with put ∘ get = id_S and get ∘ put idempotent on V ⊕ complement. The source embeds losslessly into the view, the augmentation being S ∖ im(get) carried in the in-band complement. Caps: the section law (lossless source recovery), but not full bidirectional iso."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:WellBehavedLens
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:MorphismClass ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Well-Behaved Lens"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "An asymmetric lens: a structured-to-flat downcast with sound update. Caps: logic:GetPut and logic:PutGet claimable (logic:PutPut optional). The workhorse rung for a faithful but non-embedding projection."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:LossyLens
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:MorphismClass ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Lossy Lens"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A lens with non-injective get: one direction is faithful but the inverse needs a witness, a co-authored put-with-claim, or declared defaults. Most schema.org / FOAF downcasts live here. Caps: a single faithful direction; the put leg may not claim recovery without a logic:mnemomorphic witness."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:Prism
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:MorphismClass ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Prism"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A partial map S → V + S that matches and builds only on the in-focus case of a sum or optional: the optic for 'this correspondence applies when the source is of a particular variant, otherwise it passes through.' Caps: match / build laws on the in-focus case only. Paired with logic:AffineCorrespondence as the partial-alignment band below logic:LossyLens."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:AffineCorrespondence
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:MorphismClass ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Affine Correspondence"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A co-projection onto a shared component: the 'similar but not quite' alignment, where source and view agree on a common part but neither embeds in the other. The companion of logic:Prism in the partial-alignment band — a Prism focuses an optional variant, an affine correspondence focuses a shared sub-structure. The correct rung for a logic:Vague logic:Determinacy target, NOT a low-confidence logic:Equiv. Caps: laws on the shared component only."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:BridgeView
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:MorphismClass ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Bridge View"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The floor rung: a commitment-shifting comorphism with NO satisfaction-preservation claim — the alignment to BFO, DOLCE, SUMO or YAMATO. A bridge view carries logic:morphismKind logic:CommitmentShiftingBridge, and the loss ledger refuses to emit OWL equivalentClass for it. Caps: no preservation law; the alignment is a by-reference bridge, never an identity."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:MorphismKind
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Morphism Kind"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed two-value qualifier, orthogonal to the logic:MorphismClass rung, recording the institution-theoretic split between a satisfaction-preserving morphism (logic:InstitutionMorphism) and a commitment-shifting bridge (logic:CommitmentShiftingBridge). This is the distinction the foundation already draws between a truth-preserving down-projection and a by-reference bridge; the loss ledger refuses OWL equivalentClass for the bridge kind. The set is closed, so a misspelt kind names no declared value and is rejected (design/LOGIC-CORRESPONDENCE.md, the ordered-law-spine section)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:InstitutionMorphism
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:MorphismKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Institution Morphism"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A satisfaction-preserving morphism between theories presented as institutions: truth in the source maps to truth in the view, so logical alignment axioms (up to OWL equivalentClass when the rung allows) are licensed. The morphism kind of a faithful down-projection."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:CommitmentShiftingBridge
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:MorphismKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Commitment-Shifting Bridge"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A bridge that shifts ontological commitment rather than preserving satisfaction: the kind borne by a logic:BridgeView alignment to a top-level ontology (BFO, DOLCE, SUMO, YAMATO). The loss ledger refuses to emit OWL equivalentClass for it; the bridge is adopted by reference (Principle 5), never as an identity collapse."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:Determinacy
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Determinacy"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed two-value axis recording whether the TARGET RELATIONSHIP a logic:Correspondence asserts is ontically crisp (logic:Crisp) or vague (logic:Vague). It is separate from logic:confidence: 'similar but not quite' is logic:Vague determinacy with a logic:AffineCorrespondence class, NOT a low-confidence logic:Equiv. Conflating the two destroys the distinction between an uncertain sharp claim and a confident fuzzy one. The set is closed, so a misspelt value names no declared determinacy and is rejected (design/LOGIC-CORRESPONDENCE.md, the quantitative-and-contextual-axes section)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:Crisp
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:Determinacy ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "crisp"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The target relationship is ontically sharp: the alignment either holds or does not, with no borderline cases. The determinacy of an equivalence or a clean subsumption."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:Vague
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:Determinacy ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "vague"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The target relationship is ontically fuzzy: it holds to a degree or only on a shared component, with genuine borderline cases. Paired with a logic:AffineCorrespondence class for the 'similar but not quite' alignment — vagueness in the world, not uncertainty in the curator."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:CorrespondenceLaw
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Correspondence Law"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed value class of the lens laws a logic:Correspondence can claim through a logic:LawClaim: logic:GetPut, logic:PutGet, logic:PutPut and logic:SectionLaw. Which laws are claimable is capped by the correspondence's logic:MorphismClass rung, and each claim carries a logic:DischargeVerdict. The set is closed, so a misspelt law names no declared value and is rejected (design/LOGIC-CORRESPONDENCE.md, the ninth-node-kind section)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:GetPut
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:CorrespondenceLaw ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "GetPut"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The lens law put(get(s), s) = s: getting a view from a source and immediately putting it back leaves the source unchanged. Claimable from the logic:WellBehavedLens rung upward; the acquisition-stability law."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:PutGet
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:CorrespondenceLaw ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "PutGet"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The lens law get(put(v, s)) = v: putting a view into a source and reading it back yields exactly that view. Claimable from the logic:WellBehavedLens rung upward; the update-faithfulness law."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:PutPut
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:CorrespondenceLaw ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "PutPut"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The very-well-behaved lens law put(v2, put(v1, s)) = put(v2, s): a later put overwrites an earlier one with no residue. Optional even at the logic:WellBehavedLens rung; claimed only by correspondences whose put leg is genuinely history-free."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:SectionLaw
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:CorrespondenceLaw ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Section Law"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The perfect-subsumption law put ∘ get = id_S: the source is recovered exactly from its view because the in-band complement retained everything the retraction needs (a logic:mnemomorphic witness). Claimable only at the logic:SectionRetraction rung or above; the CI-checkable form of 'GMEOW perfectly subsumes this vocabulary.'"@x-gmeow-english .

logic:LawClaim
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Law Claim"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A claim that a logic:Correspondence satisfies a particular logic:CorrespondenceLaw (named by logic:lawClaimed), together with the discharge state of that claim: a logic:DischargeVerdict via logic:lawDischargeVerdict and, when applicable, the logic:DischargeCondition under which it was checked via logic:lawDischargeCondition. It reuses the foundation's executable discharge vocabulary rather than a parallel status enum: a law proved within a certified fragment is logic:ObligationDischarged under logic:DischargeCertifiedFragment; a refuted law is logic:ObligationViolated with its countermodel; an authored-but-unchecked or inconclusive law is logic:ObligationUnknown, carried forward. The overclaim gate fires when a correspondence claims a law its lowered legs do not satisfy (design/LOGIC-CORRESPONDENCE.md, the preservation-is-the-lens-law-framework section)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:RecoveryCase
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Recovery Case"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A first-class executable query-class case that decides whether a logic:Correspondence genuinely recovers its source. Its logic:recoveryTransform is an ordered universally quantified source-to-view logic:Formula. The native correspondence executor instantiates the complete source pattern, computes the declared view, derives the candidate inverse, and compares the recovered source graph with the original. A recovery case is neutral evidence rather than a positive assertion: it may discharge a genuine section/retraction or deliberately refute a lossy mapping by retaining a source distinction absent from the view. Every logic:mnemomorphic true correspondence that claims logic:SectionLaw requires executable complete recovery evidence: an atomic one-triple leg may synthesize its complete case, while a composite leg requires an authored logic:RecoveryCase. A structural inverse or a declared boolean alone is never proof (design/LOGIC-CORRESPONDENCE.md, genuine recovery cases)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:formalizes logic:recoveryTransformValuesAreFormulae ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

# Canonical value-type axiom for the OWL allValuesFrom projection below:
# every recovery-transform value attached to a RecoveryCase is a Formula.
logic:recoveryTransformValuesAreFormulae
    a logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "recovery-transform values are formulae"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "For every recovery case and transform value, if the case is a logic:RecoveryCase and bears that value through logic:recoveryTransform, then the value is a logic:Formula. This canonical first-order axiom grounds the OWL all-values-from projection used by validation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable
        [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "case" ] ,
        [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "transform" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:rtvfImplication .

logic:rtvfImplication
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:antecedent logic:rtvfAntecedent ;
    logic:consequent logic:rtvfFormulaType .

logic:rtvfAntecedent
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:and logic:rtvfCaseType , logic:rtvfTransform .

logic:rtvfCaseType
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation rdf:type ;
    logic:argument
        [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "case" ] ,
        [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:RecoveryCase ] .

logic:rtvfTransform
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation logic:recoveryTransform ;
    logic:argument
        [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "case" ] ,
        [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "transform" ] .

logic:rtvfFormulaType
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation rdf:type ;
    logic:argument
        [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "transform" ] ,
        [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:Formula ] .

logic:Dialect
    a logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:ConversionTarget ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Dialect"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A named sub-language of the canonical logic: IR, in the MLIR sense of a dialect with its own node verifiers and progressive lowering (not an LLVM-style substrate). The one F1 dialect is logic:RelationalCore, the logical-to-physical lowering waist between the full IR and the native execution engine."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:RelationalCore
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ConversionTarget ,
        logic:Dialect ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Relational Core"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logical-to-physical lowering waist of the correspondence calculus: a first-class Datalog±-with-stratified-negation sub-language (with aggregation and existentials — the chase) between the full-FOL logic: IR and the native execution engine. Every execution strategy, the incremental layer and the semiring annotation that carries the quantitative axes target this one dialect, so a get leg compiles to a join plan and a put / chase to a semi-naive fixpoint with existentials over the same core (design/LOGIC-CORRESPONDENCE.md and design/LOGIC-IR.md, the IR-commitments section)."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:hasLens
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:Lens ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has lens"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Correspondence to the logic:Lens that is its executable get / put core. Single-valued: each correspondence wraps exactly one lens, around which it adds the relation, axes, law claims and standpoint envelope. Range logic:Lens (domain stated in prose, matching the module's convention)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:getLeg
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Lens ;
    rdfs:range logic:TransactionProgram ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "get leg"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Lens to the logic:TransactionProgram that realizes its get leg — the down-projection get : S → V from the GMEOW source to the external view. Reuses the transaction layer's path semantics and logic:PathShape; no new pattern language. Domain logic:Lens, range logic:TransactionProgram."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:putLeg
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Lens ;
    rdfs:range logic:TransactionProgram ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "put leg"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Lens to the logic:TransactionProgram that realizes its put leg — the up-projection put : V × S → S folding a view back into the rich GMEOW source. A lawful put comes only from a logic:mnemomorphic witness or a co-authored put-with-claim; naive backward execution of the get leg is a candidate preimage, never a lawful put. Domain logic:Lens, range logic:TransactionProgram."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:sourceEndpoint
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "source endpoint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Names the canonical source term or pattern related by a logic:Correspondence. This is an endpoint, not an executable logic:getLeg: a term-level correspondence can name both endpoints while carrying no transaction program. Single-valued when present and paired with exactly one logic:targetEndpoint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:bridge logic:sourceEndpoint logic:Restriction ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "For external grounding bridges the source endpoint is always in lang:, math:, or logic:."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to retain the rich canonical endpoint of a compiled correspondence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not reverse this edge merely because an external vocabulary was encountered during ingest."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach exactly one source endpoint to the correspondence and pair it with one logic:targetEndpoint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:targetEndpoint
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "target endpoint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Names the external view term or pattern related by a logic:Correspondence. This is an endpoint, not an executable logic:putLeg. Grounding correspondences use it to retain the exact gUFO, BFO, OBO, SUMO, OWL, or SHACL term in the shipped carrier without importing that target ontology."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:bridge logic:targetEndpoint OWL Restriction ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The target endpoint identifies a view term by reference and confers no authority on its external TBox."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to name the external term or pattern produced by a grounding projection or bridge view."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use an external target as a replacement source for canonical grounding content."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach exactly one target endpoint and record any loss or commitment shift on the same correspondence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:correspondenceRelation
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:CorrespondenceRelation ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "correspondence relation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Correspondence to its logic:CorrespondenceRelation on the ordered lattice (equiv ⊐ subsumes / subsumedBy ⊐ overlaps ⊐ relatedMatch, disjoint the negative pole). The single authored relation from which the SSSOM predicate, EDOAL symbol and OWL-alignment strength are derived. Range logic:CorrespondenceRelation (domain stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:morphismClass
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:MorphismClass ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "morphism class"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Correspondence to its rung on the ordered law-spine (logic:MorphismClass), which caps the logic:CorrespondenceLaw values it may claim. Composition can only weaken this rung. Range logic:MorphismClass (domain stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:morphismKind
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:MorphismKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "morphism kind"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Correspondence to its logic:MorphismKind — the satisfaction-preserving / commitment-shifting split orthogonal to the rung. A logic:CommitmentShiftingBridge value makes the loss ledger refuse OWL equivalentClass. Range logic:MorphismKind (domain stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:supersededTerm
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "superseded term"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a supersession logic:Correspondence to the old term it records as superseded — the source endpoint of a greenfield migration crossing (e.g. a removed class or property the correspondence maps onto its replacement at a rung). Names the endpoint so the supersession ledger is queryable by the migrated-from term. Domain logic:Correspondence, range any term (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:supersedingTerm
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "superseding term"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a supersession logic:Correspondence to the replacement term it records as superseding the old one — the target endpoint of a greenfield migration crossing. Paired with logic:supersededTerm, it makes the correspondence's rung (logic:morphismClass) queryable per migrated term. Domain logic:Correspondence, range any term (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:hasDeterminacy
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:Determinacy ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has determinacy"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Correspondence to its logic:Determinacy — whether the target relationship is ontically logic:Crisp or logic:Vague. Kept separate from logic:confidence so a vague-but-confident alignment is not mis-recorded as a low-confidence equivalence. Range logic:Determinacy (domain stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:mnemomorphic
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "mnemomorphic"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Whether a logic:Correspondence's forward get leg retains a source witness — factoring through the graph of the correspondence so that put is obtained by projecting along the retained witness rather than synthesizing a plausible source. The bit is orthogonal to the logic:MorphismClass rung but is what lets a correspondence climb it: a retained witness is what discharges put ∘ get = id (the logic:SectionLaw), the dividing line between recoverable subsumption and reconstructed approximation. True or false (default false); a false value bars any section / retraction claim. Range xsd:boolean (domain left open, like logic:loadBearing)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:hasLawClaim
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:LawClaim ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has law claim"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Correspondence to a logic:LawClaim it makes — a claimed logic:CorrespondenceLaw with its discharge state. Multi-valued: a correspondence may claim several laws, each capped by its logic:MorphismClass rung. Range logic:LawClaim (domain stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:recoveryCase
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:RecoveryCase ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "recovery case"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Correspondence to one of the logic:RecoveryCase nodes whose complete source pattern is executed to decide its source-recovery claim. Multi-valued and conjunctive: every attached case must recover for a mnemomorphic Section Law to discharge; one countermodel refutes the claim. A lossy correspondence may attach a refuting case so changing only its rung or logic:mnemomorphic flag cannot manufacture a proof. Domain logic:Correspondence, range logic:RecoveryCase."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:recoveryTransform
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:RecoveryCase ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "recovery transform"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The exactly-one ordered source-to-view logic:Formula executed for a logic:RecoveryCase. The supported discharge fragment is a universally quantified implication whose antecedent and consequent are positive conjunctions of binary RDF atoms: the antecedent is the complete source graph pattern and the consequent is the projected view pattern. The order is semantic — reversing the implication changes which information must be recovered — while conjunction order remains immaterial under the formula's canonical identity. Domain logic:RecoveryCase, range logic:Formula."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:lawClaimed
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:LawClaim ;
    rdfs:range logic:CorrespondenceLaw ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "law claimed"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:LawClaim to the logic:CorrespondenceLaw it asserts (logic:GetPut, logic:PutGet, logic:PutPut or logic:SectionLaw). Domain logic:LawClaim, range logic:CorrespondenceLaw."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:lawDischargeVerdict
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:LawClaim ;
    rdfs:range logic:DischargeVerdict ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "law discharge verdict"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:LawClaim to the logic:DischargeVerdict its check returned — logic:ObligationDischarged, logic:ObligationUnknown or logic:ObligationViolated. Reuses the foundation's discharge-verdict value class (the law-claim analogue of logic:obligationDischargeVerdict, which is domain-locked to logic:NonEntailmentObligation): an unchecked or inconclusive law is logic:ObligationUnknown, never silently passed. Domain logic:LawClaim, range logic:DischargeVerdict."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:lawDischargeCondition
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:LawClaim ;
    rdfs:range logic:DischargeCondition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "law discharge condition"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:LawClaim to the logic:DischargeCondition under which its logic:lawDischargeVerdict is conclusively checkable (for example logic:DischargeCertifiedFragment for a law proved within a certified complete fragment). Optional: a law carrying logic:ObligationUnknown with no condition is an authored-but-unverified claim. Reuses the foundation's discharge-condition value class. Domain logic:LawClaim, range logic:DischargeCondition."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# ── Cognitive assessment: the canonical multidimensional cognitive record ────────
# A reified, vantage-indexed, multidimensional assessment of how good some reasoning
# is or how capable a reasoner is. The single ordinal knowledge ladder of the
# cognition slice is a generated coarse view of this; the factored assessment is the
# canonical record. Normative in design/LOGIC-COGNITION.md.

logic:CognitiveAssessment
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Cognitive Assessment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The canonical record of how good some reasoning is, or how capable a reasoner is: a reified, vantage-indexed node naming exactly one logic:AssessmentDimension and carrying its assessed agent (logic:assessedAgent), subject and granularity (logic:assessmentSubject with logic:subjectGranularity), task or context (logic:assessmentContext), scale and level (logic:assessmentScale with logic:assessmentLevel), evaluator and standpoint (logic:assessmentEvaluator, attributed via gmeow:accordingTo), evidence (logic:assessmentEvidence), method (logic:assessmentMethod), valid interval (carried by gmeow:duringInterval) and the strength with which the judgment itself is held (carried by logic:confidence). The dimensions vary independently, so a single ordinal familiarity-to-mastery scale collapses exactly the distinctions an assessment exists to draw; the construct keeps them factored. A self-assessment and an observer's assessment of the same agent are two coexisting records, never reconciled into one verdict, and because the node is reified an assessment is itself a subject another assessment can range over — an evaluation of the evaluator — with no new machinery. The convenient ordinal reading is the lossy coarse-ladder projection of this assessment, exactly as logic:AgencyAssessment projects the unary 'this holon is integral'; a reliability assessment is a logic:CognitiveAssessment on the reliability dimension over a series of runs, distinct from the single-run logic:ResultAssurance. Normative in design/LOGIC-COGNITION.md."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:AssessmentDimension
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Assessment Dimension"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The value-vocabulary class of the factored axis a logic:CognitiveAssessment judges, named via logic:assessmentDimension. An OPEN value vocabulary (individuals, never subclasses): a new dimension is a new individual rather than a reshaping of the construct, the same orthogonal-factorization discipline the foundation applies to claim modality and contextual scope. The dimensions vary independently — an assessment on one says nothing about another — and an assessment names exactly one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:SubjectGranularity
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Subject Granularity"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The value-vocabulary class of the grain of a logic:CognitiveAssessment's subject, named via logic:subjectGranularity: a single inference, a body of reasoning, a domain of competence, or the reasoner as a whole. An OPEN value vocabulary (individuals, never subclasses), since competence is judged at different grains and a new grain is a new individual."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:AssessmentLevel
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Assessment Level"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The value-vocabulary class of an ordinal level on an assessment's scale, the object of logic:assessmentLevel and logic:thresholdLevel when the scale is ordinal. An OPEN, ordered value vocabulary (logic:levelNone ≺ logic:levelEmerging ≺ logic:levelCompetent ≺ logic:levelStrong), ordered by logic:levelAtOrAbove so that 'a level at or above a threshold' is well-defined for the coarse-ladder projection without a numeric comparison (which would force a procedural profile). An assessment whose scale is continuous carries a literal logic:assessmentLevel instead; the ordinal levels are the projection scale."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:assessedAgent
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CognitiveAssessment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "assessed agent"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The agent role of a logic:CognitiveAssessment: whose cognition is judged. Range gmeow:Agent (stated in prose, matching the module's convention). A well-formedness existence guard — every projection rule re-binds it, so a malformed assessment naming no agent receives no ladder edge."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:assessmentSubject
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CognitiveAssessment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "assessment subject"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The subject role of a logic:CognitiveAssessment: what the assessment is about — a single inference, a body of reasoning, a domain of competence, or the agent as a whole. Its grain is carried by logic:subjectGranularity. Range is left open (stated in prose), since the subject may be reasoning, a domain entity, or the reasoner itself."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:subjectGranularity
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CognitiveAssessment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "subject granularity"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:CognitiveAssessment to the logic:SubjectGranularity individual recording the grain of its logic:assessmentSubject (inference, body-of-reasoning, domain, or agent). Range logic:SubjectGranularity (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:assessmentContext
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CognitiveAssessment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "assessment context"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The task or context a logic:CognitiveAssessment holds for, since competence is context-relative: the same agent may be competent on one task and not on another, so an assessment without a context underdetermines what it claims. Range is left open (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:assessmentDimension
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CognitiveAssessment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "assessment dimension"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The single factored axis a logic:CognitiveAssessment judges: a logic:AssessmentDimension individual. An assessment names exactly one; the dimensions vary independently. Range logic:AssessmentDimension (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:assessmentScale
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CognitiveAssessment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "assessment scale"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The named scale, ordinal or continuous, on which a logic:CognitiveAssessment's logic:assessmentLevel is read. Range is left open (stated in prose), since a scale may be the native ordinal logic:AssessmentLevel vocabulary or a foreign scale (Bloom's, SOLO, a Brier-scored continuous range)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:assessmentLevel
    a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CognitiveAssessment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "assessment level"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The value a logic:CognitiveAssessment takes on its logic:assessmentScale: an ordinal logic:AssessmentLevel individual when the scale is ordinal (the case the coarse-ladder projection reads), or a continuous literal otherwise (a Brier or logarithmic score on a continuous scale). Because the range spans both an individual and a literal it is a plain rdf:Property, not an OWL ObjectProperty (which could not carry the continuous literal) — the same open-range convention the module states in prose elsewhere."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:assessmentEvaluator
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CognitiveAssessment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "assessment evaluator"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Who holds the judgment of a logic:CognitiveAssessment: the standpoint is attributed via gmeow:accordingTo, so a self-assessment (evaluator = assessed agent) and an observer's assessment coexist as two records rather than collapsing into one verdict. Range gmeow:Agent (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:assessmentEvidence
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CognitiveAssessment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "assessment evidence"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The reasoning, observations, or recorded episode a logic:CognitiveAssessment rests on — the inference commitments it cites, the gmeow:ModelInvocation that produced the reasoning, the results it grades — so an assessment is auditable against the reasoning it judges rather than standing apart from it. Range is left open (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:assessmentMethod
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CognitiveAssessment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "assessment method"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "How the judgment of a logic:CognitiveAssessment was reached — a test, a review, a scoring rule applied to a forecast series, an introspective report. Range is left open (stated in prose), so the method may be a named procedure or a free description."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:levelAtOrAbove
    a logic:ObjectProperty ,
        logic:transitiveProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:AssessmentLevel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "level at or above"@x-gmeow-english ;
    rdfs:range logic:AssessmentLevel ;
    skos:definition "Orders the ordinal assessment scale: relates a logic:AssessmentLevel to itself and to every level it surpasses (logic:levelStrong logic:levelAtOrAbove logic:levelCompetent). Reflexive-and-transitive on the levels, so the coarse-ladder projection can test 'the assessed level is at or above the threshold level' by a single body atom, with no numeric comparison (which would force a procedural profile). The full reflexive-transitive closure is asserted on the level individuals so the test needs no transitive computation in the rule engine."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# The eight factored dimensions of design §The factored dimensions, plus the
# separately-introduced reliability dimension of design §Result assurance and
# reasoner reliability are distinct — nine logic:AssessmentDimension individuals.

logic:dimensionAwareness
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:AssessmentDimension ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "dimension: awareness"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The awareness dimension: whether the agent registers the subject at all. The shallowest factored axis; the source of the gmeow:isAwareOf rung of the coarse knowledge ladder under a declared threshold."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:dimensionDeclarativeFamiliarity
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:AssessmentDimension ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "dimension: declarative familiarity"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The declarative-familiarity dimension: what the agent knows THAT — the facts it can state about the subject. The source of the gmeow:knowsAbout rung of the coarse knowledge ladder under a declared threshold."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:dimensionConceptualUnderstanding
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:AssessmentDimension ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "dimension: conceptual understanding"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The conceptual-understanding dimension: whether the agent grasps WHY — the structure behind the facts, beyond reciting them. The source of the gmeow:understands rung of the coarse knowledge ladder under a declared threshold."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:dimensionProceduralCompetence
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:AssessmentDimension ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "dimension: procedural competence"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The procedural-competence dimension: what the agent knows HOW to do — its working command of the subject. Read together with the transfer dimension, the source of the gmeow:hasMastered rung of the coarse knowledge ladder under a declared threshold."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:dimensionTransfer
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:AssessmentDimension ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "dimension: transfer"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The transfer dimension: whether competence in one context carries to another — the axis a single ordinal ladder most conspicuously hides, since fluency in a narrow task says nothing about generalization. Read together with procedural competence, the source of the gmeow:hasMastered rung of the coarse knowledge ladder under a declared threshold."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:dimensionExplanatoryAbility
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:AssessmentDimension ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "dimension: explanatory ability"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The explanatory-ability dimension: whether the agent can produce an account others can follow and check — the seam to the solver-layer gmeow:explanatoryScore and gmeow:systematicity measurements, which feed this dimension but are never asserted as reasoned axioms."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:dimensionCalibration
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:AssessmentDimension ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "dimension: calibration"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The calibration dimension: whether the agent's confidence matches its accuracy. Assessed against an explicit forecast protocol (a forecast/outcome series, a scoring rule, a reference class and observation window), kept apart from a single logic:confidence value and from the metacognition vocabulary's qualitative gmeow:CalibrationStatus."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:dimensionMetacognition
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:AssessmentDimension ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "dimension: metacognition"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The metacognition dimension: whether the agent models its own competence, including the limits of its own knowledge. Reads the metacognition vocabulary's second-order posture — gmeow:awareOfNotKnowing, gmeow:epistemicSelfTrust, and reflection that can defeat one's own inference — as its subject."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:dimensionReliability
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:AssessmentDimension ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "dimension: reliability"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The reliability dimension: how dependably an agent or engine reasons as a disposition, relative to a reference class (a task, domain, or distribution) and read from MANY results with their expected or observed outcomes — a metric over the series (accuracy, soundness-violation rate, recall, calibration, robustness) across a stated interval. Distinct from the single-run logic:ResultAssurance: a reliable reasoner may correctly return a contradiction verdict on contradictory input, and a completed internally-supported result may still be wrong, so reliability is never read from one result's assurance. The dimension design §Result assurance and reasoner reliability are distinct introduces alongside the eight factored dimensions."@x-gmeow-english .

# Subject granularity — four grains at which competence is judged.

logic:granularityInference
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:SubjectGranularity ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "granularity: inference"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The finest grain of an assessment subject: a single inference — one logic:InferenceProcess or commitment being judged."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:granularityBodyOfReasoning
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:SubjectGranularity ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "granularity: body of reasoning"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "An intermediate grain of an assessment subject: a connected body of reasoning — a derivation, a proof, an episode of inference — judged as a whole rather than one step."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:granularityDomain
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:SubjectGranularity ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "granularity: domain"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A broad grain of an assessment subject: a domain of competence — the agent's standing in a field rather than on any one episode."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:granularityAgent
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:SubjectGranularity ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "granularity: agent"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The coarsest grain of an assessment subject: the reasoner as a whole — the grain at which a reliability assessment of an agent or engine is made."@x-gmeow-english .

# The ordinal projection scale — four levels, reflexive-transitively ordered by
# logic:levelAtOrAbove so a threshold test is a single body atom.

logic:levelNone
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:AssessmentLevel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "level: none"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The lowest ordinal assessment level: the dimension is not evidenced at all. Clears no ladder rung's threshold."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:levelAtOrAbove logic:levelNone .

logic:levelEmerging
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:AssessmentLevel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "level: emerging"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The second ordinal assessment level: the dimension is weakly evidenced. Surpasses logic:levelNone on the logic:levelAtOrAbove order."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:levelAtOrAbove logic:levelEmerging ,
        logic:levelNone .

logic:levelCompetent
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:AssessmentLevel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "level: competent"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The third ordinal assessment level: the dimension is solidly evidenced. Surpasses logic:levelEmerging and logic:levelNone on the logic:levelAtOrAbove order."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:levelAtOrAbove logic:levelCompetent ,
        logic:levelEmerging ,
        logic:levelNone .

logic:levelStrong
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:AssessmentLevel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "level: strong"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The highest ordinal assessment level: the dimension is strongly evidenced. Surpasses every lower level on the logic:levelAtOrAbove order."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:levelAtOrAbove logic:levelStrong ,
        logic:levelCompetent ,
        logic:levelEmerging ,
        logic:levelNone .

# ── The coarse knowledge ladder as a generated projection ───────────────────────
# The cognition slice's gmeow:isAwareOf … gmeow:hasMastered ladder is a generated
# coarse VIEW of the multidimensional logic:CognitiveAssessment: each ladder edge
# instance is rule-materialized from assessments under a declared per-dimension
# threshold profile (logic:LadderProjectionProfile). The factored assessment stays
# the canonical record; the ladder is the readable ordinal projection. The shipped
# projection rules are positive Horn, so they materialize under the engine's default
# semantics and fold into gmeow.gts and the Datalog/N3 program projections.
# Normative in design/LOGIC-COGNITION.md §Projection to the coarse knowledge ladder.

logic:LadderProjectionProfile
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Ladder Projection Profile"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A declared per-dimension threshold profile governing the generated coarse projection of the multidimensional logic:CognitiveAssessment onto the gmeow:isAwareOf … gmeow:hasMastered knowledge ladder: a set of logic:DimensionThreshold rows (logic:hasThresholdRow), each binding a ladder edge to its source dimension and the minimum logic:AssessmentLevel an assessment must clear for the edge to be emitted. Making the thresholds an explicit profile keeps the ordinal chain a projection a consumer that needs one number can read, while the factored assessment stays canonical — the ladder never re-establishes mastery, understanding, knowledge and awareness as a single ordinal entailment chain in place of the independent dimensions."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:DimensionThreshold
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Dimension Threshold"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "One row of a logic:LadderProjectionProfile: a logic:projectsLadderEdge ladder property paired with the logic:sourceDimension it reads and the minimum logic:thresholdLevel an assessment on that dimension must clear (by the logic:levelAtOrAbove order) for the edge to be emitted. The gmeow:hasMastered rung is the only one with two rows — procedural competence and transfer — both of which must clear, so mastery of one narrow task no longer implies broad command of a subject."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:hasThresholdRow
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:LadderProjectionProfile ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has threshold row"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:LadderProjectionProfile to one of its logic:DimensionThreshold rows. Range logic:DimensionThreshold (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:projectsLadderEdge
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DimensionThreshold ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "projects ladder edge"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The ladder property a logic:DimensionThreshold row emits when its dimension clears its threshold: gmeow:isAwareOf, gmeow:knowsAbout, gmeow:understands or gmeow:hasMastered. Range is left open (the gmeow: ladder properties), stated in prose to keep the OWL down-projection clean."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:sourceDimension
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DimensionThreshold ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "source dimension"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:AssessmentDimension a logic:DimensionThreshold row reads: the factored axis whose assessment level is tested against the row's logic:thresholdLevel. Range logic:AssessmentDimension (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:thresholdLevel
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DimensionThreshold ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "threshold level"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The minimum logic:AssessmentLevel an assessment on a row's logic:sourceDimension must reach — by the logic:levelAtOrAbove order — for the row's logic:projectsLadderEdge to be emitted. Range logic:AssessmentLevel (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# The shipped default profile: the threshold each ladder rung clears.

logic:defaultLadderProfile
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:LadderProjectionProfile ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "default ladder projection profile"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The shipped default logic:LadderProjectionProfile the coarse-ladder projection rules read: awareness ≥ emerging emits gmeow:isAwareOf, declarative familiarity ≥ competent emits gmeow:knowsAbout, conceptual understanding ≥ competent emits gmeow:understands, and procedural competence ≥ strong together with transfer ≥ strong emit gmeow:hasMastered."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:hasThresholdRow logic:ladderRowAwareness ,
        logic:ladderRowDeclarative ,
        logic:ladderRowConceptual ,
        logic:ladderRowProceduralMastery ,
        logic:ladderRowTransferMastery .

logic:ladderRowAwareness
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DimensionThreshold ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "ladder row: awareness → is aware of"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The default-profile row emitting gmeow:isAwareOf when the awareness dimension reaches logic:levelEmerging."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:projectsLadderEdge gmeow:isAwareOf ;
    logic:sourceDimension logic:dimensionAwareness ;
    logic:thresholdLevel logic:levelEmerging .

logic:ladderRowDeclarative
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DimensionThreshold ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "ladder row: declarative familiarity → knows about"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The default-profile row emitting gmeow:knowsAbout when the declarative-familiarity dimension reaches logic:levelCompetent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:projectsLadderEdge gmeow:knowsAbout ;
    logic:sourceDimension logic:dimensionDeclarativeFamiliarity ;
    logic:thresholdLevel logic:levelCompetent .

logic:ladderRowConceptual
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DimensionThreshold ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "ladder row: conceptual understanding → understands"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The default-profile row emitting gmeow:understands when the conceptual-understanding dimension reaches logic:levelCompetent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:projectsLadderEdge gmeow:understands ;
    logic:sourceDimension logic:dimensionConceptualUnderstanding ;
    logic:thresholdLevel logic:levelCompetent .

logic:ladderRowProceduralMastery
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DimensionThreshold ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "ladder row: procedural competence → has mastered"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The procedural half of the default-profile gmeow:hasMastered rung: procedural competence must reach logic:levelStrong. Read together with logic:ladderRowTransferMastery — both must clear for gmeow:hasMastered."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:projectsLadderEdge gmeow:hasMastered ;
    logic:sourceDimension logic:dimensionProceduralCompetence ;
    logic:thresholdLevel logic:levelStrong .

logic:ladderRowTransferMastery
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DimensionThreshold ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "ladder row: transfer → has mastered"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The transfer half of the default-profile gmeow:hasMastered rung: transfer must reach logic:levelStrong. Read together with logic:ladderRowProceduralMastery — both must clear for gmeow:hasMastered, so mastery of one narrow task never implies broad command."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:projectsLadderEdge gmeow:hasMastered ;
    logic:sourceDimension logic:dimensionTransfer ;
    logic:thresholdLevel logic:levelStrong .

# The four projection rules. The three single-dimension rungs read their dimension
# and threshold from the profile row; the gmeow:hasMastered rung conjoins the two
# rows (procedural AND transfer), so both halves must clear.

logic:ruleProjectIsAwareOf
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "project ladder edge: is aware of"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Emits gmeow:isAwareOf from an assessment whose dimension and level clear the gmeow:isAwareOf row of a logic:LadderProjectionProfile. Positive Horn; the threshold is read from the profile, not hard-coded."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleProjectIsAwareOf ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?agent" ;
        rdf:predicate gmeow:isAwareOf ;
        rdf:object    "?subject"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?profile" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:hasThresholdRow ;
        rdf:object    "?row"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?row" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:projectsLadderEdge ;
        rdf:object    gmeow:isAwareOf
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?row" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:sourceDimension ;
        rdf:object    "?dim"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?row" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:thresholdLevel ;
        rdf:object    "?threshold"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessment" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessmentDimension ;
        rdf:object    "?dim"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessment" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessedAgent ;
        rdf:object    "?agent"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessment" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessmentSubject ;
        rdf:object    "?subject"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessment" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessmentLevel ;
        rdf:object    "?level"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?level" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:levelAtOrAbove ;
        rdf:object    "?threshold"
    ] .

logic:ruleProjectKnowsAbout
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "project ladder edge: knows about"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Emits gmeow:knowsAbout from an assessment whose dimension and level clear the gmeow:knowsAbout row of a logic:LadderProjectionProfile. Positive Horn; the threshold is read from the profile. The canonical source of a gmeow:knowsAbout edge — never gmeow:hasSkill."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleProjectKnowsAbout ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?agent" ;
        rdf:predicate gmeow:knowsAbout ;
        rdf:object    "?subject"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?profile" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:hasThresholdRow ;
        rdf:object    "?row"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?row" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:projectsLadderEdge ;
        rdf:object    gmeow:knowsAbout
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?row" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:sourceDimension ;
        rdf:object    "?dim"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?row" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:thresholdLevel ;
        rdf:object    "?threshold"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessment" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessmentDimension ;
        rdf:object    "?dim"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessment" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessedAgent ;
        rdf:object    "?agent"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessment" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessmentSubject ;
        rdf:object    "?subject"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessment" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessmentLevel ;
        rdf:object    "?level"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?level" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:levelAtOrAbove ;
        rdf:object    "?threshold"
    ] .

logic:ruleProjectUnderstands
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "project ladder edge: understands"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Emits gmeow:understands from an assessment whose dimension and level clear the gmeow:understands row of a logic:LadderProjectionProfile. Positive Horn; the threshold is read from the profile."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleProjectUnderstands ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?agent" ;
        rdf:predicate gmeow:understands ;
        rdf:object    "?subject"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?profile" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:hasThresholdRow ;
        rdf:object    "?row"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?row" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:projectsLadderEdge ;
        rdf:object    gmeow:understands
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?row" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:sourceDimension ;
        rdf:object    "?dim"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?row" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:thresholdLevel ;
        rdf:object    "?threshold"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessment" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessmentDimension ;
        rdf:object    "?dim"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessment" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessedAgent ;
        rdf:object    "?agent"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessment" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessmentSubject ;
        rdf:object    "?subject"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessment" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessmentLevel ;
        rdf:object    "?level"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?level" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:levelAtOrAbove ;
        rdf:object    "?threshold"
    ] .

logic:ruleProjectHasMastered
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "project ladder edge: has mastered"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Emits gmeow:hasMastered only when BOTH a procedural-competence assessment and a transfer assessment of the same agent on the same subject clear their gmeow:hasMastered rows, where both rows belong to the SAME logic:LadderProjectionProfile. The two-row conjunction is why mastery of one narrow task no longer implies broad command — transfer must clear too — and the shared-profile join is why a procedural threshold from one profile can never be mixed with a transfer threshold from another. Positive Horn; thresholds read from the profile."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleProjectHasMastered ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?agent" ;
        rdf:predicate gmeow:hasMastered ;
        rdf:object    "?subject"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?profile" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:hasThresholdRow ;
        rdf:object    "?rowProc"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?profile" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:hasThresholdRow ;
        rdf:object    "?rowTransfer"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?rowProc" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:projectsLadderEdge ;
        rdf:object    gmeow:hasMastered
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?rowProc" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:sourceDimension ;
        rdf:object    logic:dimensionProceduralCompetence
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?rowProc" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:thresholdLevel ;
        rdf:object    "?thresholdProc"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessProc" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessmentDimension ;
        rdf:object    logic:dimensionProceduralCompetence
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessProc" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessedAgent ;
        rdf:object    "?agent"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessProc" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessmentSubject ;
        rdf:object    "?subject"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessProc" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessmentLevel ;
        rdf:object    "?levelProc"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?levelProc" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:levelAtOrAbove ;
        rdf:object    "?thresholdProc"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?rowTransfer" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:projectsLadderEdge ;
        rdf:object    gmeow:hasMastered
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?rowTransfer" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:sourceDimension ;
        rdf:object    logic:dimensionTransfer
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?rowTransfer" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:thresholdLevel ;
        rdf:object    "?thresholdTransfer"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessTransfer" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessmentDimension ;
        rdf:object    logic:dimensionTransfer
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessTransfer" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessedAgent ;
        rdf:object    "?agent"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessTransfer" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessmentSubject ;
        rdf:object    "?subject"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?assessTransfer" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:assessmentLevel ;
        rdf:object    "?levelTransfer"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?levelTransfer" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:levelAtOrAbove ;
        rdf:object    "?thresholdTransfer"
    ] .

# ── Defeasible evidence: support that can come apart ────────────────────────────
# A first-class, reified declaration that one relation is DEFEASIBLE (defeater-
# blockable) evidence for another, never a strict entailment. The recast of
# expertise's gmeow:hasSkill → gmeow:knowsAbout: possessing a skill (tacit can-do)
# is defeasible evidence for knowing-about (descriptive, fact-level familiarity),
# the two coming apart (tacit competence with no articulable knowledge, and vice
# versa). Because the
# support is defeasible, the shipped reasoning NEVER materializes the supported
# edge from the evidence: gmeow:knowsAbout's only shipped source is the
# declarative-familiarity ladder projection, never gmeow:hasSkill. A consumer that
# opts into defeasible reasoning can draw the supported edge under a declared
# contract (demonstrated in the conformance corpus), defeated when the competence
# is purely tacit.
#
# NON-ENTAILMENT GUARD: the predicate-global logic:NonEntailmentObligation
# machinery cannot express "knowsAbout must not come FROM hasSkill", because
# gmeow:knowsAbout IS a legitimate rule head (the declarative-familiarity ladder
# projection) — a predicate-global obligation on it would resolve to
# logic:ObligationViolated, a hard error. The precise, strictly-stronger guard is
# the source-specific verification query that no gmeow:knowsAbout edge is derived
# whose only support is gmeow:hasSkill, enforced under make verify.

logic:DefeasibleEvidence
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Defeasible Evidence"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A reified declaration that one relation or fact (logic:evidenceProperty) is DEFEASIBLE — defeater-blockable — evidence for another (logic:evidenceFor), carrying its logic:defeasibleStrength, and explicitly NOT a strict entailment. The support can come apart: the evidence may hold while the supported claim fails (and conversely), so the engine never materializes the supported edge from the evidence under the default reasoning — only a consumer opting into defeasible reasoning under a declared contract draws it, defeated when a declared defeater holds. The central recast of any 'X is evidence for Y, not X entails Y' relationship; its first instance is gmeow:hasSkill as defeasible evidence for gmeow:knowsAbout."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:evidenceProperty
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DefeasibleEvidence ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "evidence property"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The evidential relation a logic:DefeasibleEvidence declares to bear defeasibly on another — the 'from' side (e.g. gmeow:hasSkill). Range is left open (a property or a claim), stated in prose; the object is used by punning, the gmeow:pairsWith convention."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:evidenceFor
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DefeasibleEvidence ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "evidence for"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The relation or claim a logic:DefeasibleEvidence defeasibly supports — the 'to' side (e.g. gmeow:knowsAbout). Defeasible support, never strict entailment: the supported edge is not materialized from the evidence under the default reasoning. Range is left open (a property or a claim), stated in prose."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:defeasibleStrength
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DefeasibleEvidence ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "defeasible strength"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A solver-layer measure of how strongly a logic:DefeasibleEvidence supports its logic:evidenceFor target before any defeater — a ranking annotation, never asserted as a reasoned axiom and never a probability. Range is left open (an ordinal label or a numeric weight), stated in prose."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:skillEvidenceForKnowsAbout
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DefeasibleEvidence ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "skill is defeasible evidence for knowing about"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The shipped declaration recasting expertise's former gmeow:hasSkill ⊑ gmeow:knowsAbout: possessing a skill in a subject is DEFEASIBLE evidence that the agent knows about it, not a strict subproperty entailment — tacit competence and descriptive, fact-level knowing-about come apart. The supported gmeow:knowsAbout edge is never materialized from gmeow:hasSkill under the default reasoning (its only shipped source is the declarative-familiarity ladder projection); a consumer opting into defeasible reasoning draws it, defeated when the competence is purely tacit."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:evidenceProperty gmeow:hasSkill ;
    logic:evidenceFor gmeow:knowsAbout ;
    logic:defeasibleStrength "moderate" .

# === Transaction layer: serial composition, concurrency, and anomalies =======
# The serial-conjunction combinator (a program operator typed APART from logical
# conjunction), the three separately-declared concurrency concerns kept apart
# (history criterion vs guarantee strength vs implementation mechanism), and the
# history-level serialization anomaly that is a finding, never a contradiction
# witness. Normative in design/LOGIC-TRANSACTION.md, §Concurrent transactions.

# --- Serial composition operator ---------------------------------------------

logic:SerialConjunction
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Serial Conjunction"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The serial-conjunction program combinator (⊗, 'do φ then ψ'): the path along which the program executes splits so that φ holds over a prefix and ψ holds over the remaining suffix. It is a PROGRAM combinator over the transaction layer's path semantics, typed APART from ordinary (at-a-state) logical conjunction — a program operator is never conflated with the logical connective that holds at a single state, matching the formula-versus-program operator typing of the IR. Its two operands are carried by logic:leftOperand (the prefix program) and logic:rightOperand (the suffix program). The base relations of the layer are elementary state transitions, this serial conjunction, and executional entailment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:leftOperand
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "left operand"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a binary transaction-program combinator (for example a logic:SerialConjunction) to its left/prefix operand — the program that holds over the prefix of the executing path. Range a transaction-program node (stated in prose). Paired with logic:rightOperand."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:rightOperand
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "right operand"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a binary transaction-program combinator (for example a logic:SerialConjunction) to its right/suffix operand — the program that holds over the suffix of the executing path. Range a transaction-program node (stated in prose). Paired with logic:leftOperand."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# --- Branching, concurrent, iterative, and fallback combinators ---------------
# The four missing program combinators that complete the transaction layer's
# operator set as named in logic:Plan's definition and LOGIC-TELEOLOGY.md.
# Each is typed APART from the corresponding logical connective — a program
# operator is never confused with a formula connective — and mirrors
# logic:SerialConjunction exactly in structure and annotation style.
# Normative in design/LOGIC-TRANSACTION.md and design/LOGIC-TELEOLOGY.md.

logic:Choice
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Choice"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The guarded-choice program combinator: selects one of two sub-programs based on whether a guard situation holds at the current state. The left operand (logic:leftOperand) is executed when the guard holds; the right operand (logic:rightOperand) is executed when it does not. The guard is declared through logic:guardSituation, reusing the same property the conditional goal expression reads — a choice combinator is a PROGRAM operator that dispatches on a guard, typed APART from a conditional formula connective that holds at a single state. The two operands are binary, carried by logic:leftOperand and logic:rightOperand (the same properties logic:SerialConjunction uses). A plan whose branches are conditioned on what an earlier observation revealed is a POLICY — a reading of a plan, not a separate class — and its branches are resolved through logic:planBranch, logic:branchGuard, and logic:nextActionSchema; logic:Choice is the structural combinator that expresses the branching operator in the program body. Distinct from goal-level logic:DisjunctiveGoal, which evaluates a truth-at-a-state condition over sub-expressions, not a program that dispatches control to one of two sub-programs."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ConcurrentComposition
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Concurrent Composition"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The concurrent-composition program combinator: two sub-programs advance together with their elementary steps interleaved over a shared path. The left sub-program is carried by logic:leftOperand and the right by logic:rightOperand. This is the COMPOSITION OPERATOR that states two programs run concurrently — it is DISTINCT from the three separately-declared concurrency correctness concerns the transaction layer keeps apart: (a) logic:SerializabilityCriterion is a correctness PROPERTY OF A HISTORY (conflict-serializability or view-serializability), (b) logic:IsolationLevel is the declared GUARANTEE STRENGTH a schedule must meet, and (c) logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol is the IMPLEMENTATION MECHANISM enforcing that strength. ConcurrentComposition names the structural operator; whether the resulting history is serializable, what isolation is declared, and which protocol enforces it are separately-declared facets. A logic:ConcurrentHistory carries the conflict edges whose cycle structure logic:SerializationAnomaly detects; logic:ConcurrentComposition names the program that gives rise to such a history. Typed APART from ordinary (at-a-state) conjunction — concurrent programs advance over a shared path segment, while a conjunctive formula holds at a single state."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:Iteration
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Iteration"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The iteration program combinator: repeats a body sub-program while a loop condition holds. The body is carried by logic:iterationBody and the loop condition by logic:iterationCondition. Binary left/right operand semantics do not fit iteration — a loop has a body and a separate condition, not two co-equal operands — so logic:iterationBody and logic:iterationCondition are minted as dedicated properties rather than repurposing logic:leftOperand / logic:rightOperand. A logic:Iteration is the program-level counterpart of the loop/retry structure logic:StrongCyclicPlanSuccess quantifies over: a strong-cyclic plan succeeds when every fair execution of a loop through recoverable failures terminates at the goal in finitely many steps. The loop condition is a situation type checked before each body execution; when it does not hold the iteration terminates. Typed APART from recursive goal structures and from plain goal decomposition, which carry satisfaction dependency rather than control flow."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:iterationBody
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "iteration body"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Iteration combinator to the sub-program that constitutes its loop body — the program executed on each iteration pass. Domain logic:Iteration, range a transaction-program node (stated in prose). Paired with logic:iterationCondition, which carries the guard situation checked before each pass. Distinct from logic:leftOperand / logic:rightOperand (binary co-equal operands of serial, choice, concurrent, and fallback combinators): a loop body is not co-equal with a loop condition."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:iterationCondition
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "iteration condition"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Iteration combinator to the situation type that gates each pass of the loop body — the loop is executed while this situation obtains at the current state; when it does not hold the iteration terminates. Domain logic:Iteration, range logic:Situation (stated in prose). Paired with logic:iterationBody. Reuses the situation-type pattern of logic:guardSituation and logic:boundSituationType: the engine reads this against the current path state via logic:situationObtains."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:Fallback
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Fallback"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The fallback program combinator: attempts the primary sub-program (logic:leftOperand); if it fails (the primary executional entailment does not hold from the current state), executes the alternate sub-program (logic:rightOperand). It is a PROGRAM combinator over the transaction layer path semantics, typed APART from action-level logic:compensation: a compensation undoes the EFFECT of an action that ran and reached an outcome, recovering from a realized nondeterministic outcome branch, while logic:Fallback is a STRUCTURAL OPERATOR that routes control to an alternate path when the primary program cannot succeed from the current state. The two sub-programs are binary, carried by logic:leftOperand (the primary) and logic:rightOperand (the alternate), reusing the same properties logic:SerialConjunction and logic:Choice use. A plan strong-cyclic success through recoverable failures is the quantification-level counterpart: logic:Fallback is the operator that makes recovery structurally expressible in the program body."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

# --- Plan-to-path link (closing the four-link chain) -------------------------
# The chain intention -> plan -> action -> transaction-path is otherwise
# expressible through existing terms: gmeow:Intention carries its goal via
# gmeow:intentionGoal and motivates the action occurrence via gmeow:motivates;
# logic:Plan carries the same goal via logic:planGoal; the action occurrence
# (a gmeow:Event) links to its reusable type via logic:instantiatesSchema. The
# one genuine gap is relating a Plan to the specific Path along which it ran,
# which logic:executedAlongPath closes. It CONNECTS the plan and the path; it
# does NOT subsume either — a plan is the program, a path is the executed run.

logic:executedAlongPath
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "executed along path"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a program run — a logic:Plan or a logic:TransactionOutcome — to the logic:Path along which it executed: the executed history that realizes the program. Domain logic:Plan or logic:TransactionOutcome, range logic:Path (stated in prose). This is the one link the four-notion chain intention -> plan -> action -> transaction-path did not already have: intention reaches its goal and action through gmeow:intentionGoal / gmeow:motivates, a logic:Plan reaches the same goal through logic:planGoal, and an action occurrence reaches its type through logic:instantiatesSchema, but no existing term tied a program to the run that executed it. It CONNECTS the program to its path but does NOT subsume either: a plan is the program (what is intended to be done), a path is the executed run (how state actually changed); the link records that this program ran along that history without conflating the two."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# --- Canonical process model: the DAG-workflow profile + flow edges ----------
# The decidable, schedulable SHADOW of the canonical process model. A "DAG
# workflow" is NOT a kind of process but a statically-certified ACYCLIC fragment
# of logic:Plan, expressed as a Resource-facet value (logic:DagWorkflowResource)
# requested by a named contract (logic:DagWorkflowContract) — never a standalone
# class (design/LOGIC-CONTRACT.md, "Why a contract, not a profile"). The genuinely
# missing canonical edge is DATAFLOW: logic:dataflowConsumes is the binary
# dependency a DAG executor schedules over; logic:ControlFlowEdge /
# logic:DataFlowEdge are its reified FLOW-GRAPH form (the graph the acyclicity
# certifier runs Tarjan SCC over), complementary to — not a duplicate of — the
# structured-program combinators (logic:Choice, logic:Iteration,
# logic:ConcurrentComposition, logic:Fallback), which are the program-TREE form an
# interpreter walks. The DAG fragment excludes the loop combinator logic:Iteration
# (the program-level counterpart of logic:StrongCyclicPlanSuccess); a cyclic plan
# stays valid canonically but resolves to `unsupported` under the DAG profile,
# the offending edge disclosed by logic:dagCycleWitness — never silently truncated.

logic:dataflowConsumes
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "dataflow consumes"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a process node — a logic:Plan step, a pipeline stage, or a procedure step — to an upstream node whose product it reads: the canonical binary dataflow dependency, oriented consumer -> producer (an executor inverts it to schedule producers first). The transitive closure must be acyclic for the DAG-workflow profile (logic:DagWorkflowResource); a cycle takes the plan out of the certified acyclic fragment and is disclosed through logic:dagCycleWitness, never silently dropped. Domain and range a process node (stated in prose, matching the module's convention). The reified FLOW-GRAPH counterpart is logic:DataFlowEdge; downstream surfaces such as gmeow:dataflowConsumes specialize this property."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:dataflowProduces
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    logic:inverseOf logic:dataflowConsumes ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "dataflow produces"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The inverse of logic:dataflowConsumes: relates a process node to a downstream node that reads its product (producer -> consumer). The canonical authored direction is logic:dataflowConsumes, so this inverse is normally left to be inferred rather than asserted. Domain and range a process node (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:ControlFlowEdge
    a logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:Relator ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Control-Flow Edge"@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:mediates logic:flowFrom ,
        logic:flowTo ;
    skos:definition "A reified control-flow edge between two process nodes — the canonical FLOW-GRAPH form of control flow, mediating a source node (logic:flowFrom) and a target node (logic:flowTo). Complementary to, NOT a duplicate of, the structured-program combinators (logic:Choice, logic:Iteration, logic:ConcurrentComposition, logic:Fallback): the combinators are the program-TREE form an interpreter walks, while a logic:ControlFlowEdge is the binary-edge form the DAG-workflow acyclicity certifier runs Tarjan SCC over. A graph carrying a logic:Iteration loop edge (or any back-edge) is outside the DAG fragment. A downstream slice may specialize this class with its own reified control-flow relator. A Relator mediating two distinct relata (its flowFrom and flowTo nodes), satisfying the relator-mediation discipline."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:DataFlowEdge
    a logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:Relator ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Data-Flow Edge"@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:mediates logic:flowFrom ,
        logic:flowTo ;
    skos:definition "A reified dataflow edge between two process nodes — the FLOW-GRAPH counterpart of the logic:dataflowConsumes property, mediating a source (producer) node via logic:flowFrom and a target (consumer) node via logic:flowTo. Use the property form (logic:dataflowConsumes) for a bare binary dependency and this reified form when an edge must carry its own attributes (the flowing entity, a guard, an order). A downstream slice may specialize this class with its own reified dataflow relator. A Relator mediating two distinct relata, satisfying the relator-mediation discipline."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:flowFrom
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "flow from"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a reified flow edge (logic:ControlFlowEdge or logic:DataFlowEdge) to its source process node — the predecessor in a control-flow edge, the producer in a dataflow edge. Domain a flow edge, range a process node (stated in prose). Paired with logic:flowTo; the two mediation legs that make a flow edge a well-formed Relator."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:flowTo
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "flow to"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a reified flow edge (logic:ControlFlowEdge or logic:DataFlowEdge) to its target process node — the successor in a control-flow edge, the consumer in a dataflow edge. Domain a flow edge, range a process node (stated in prose). Paired with logic:flowFrom."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:DagWorkflowResource
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ResourcePolicy ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "DAG-Workflow Resource"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The resource execution policy of the DAG-workflow profile: it requires the plan's control/dataflow graph to be a loop-free (acyclic) DAG — no logic:Iteration and an acyclic logic:dataflowConsumes / flow-edge closure — so the schedule is a topological order with a hard termination guarantee, and the result reports logic:CompleteForFragment. Acyclicity is the universal structural guarantee certified here; a typed terminal discipline such as the build pipeline's single gmeow:kindSink is an additional per-surface constraint, not part of this graph-level certification. A specialization of the certified-fragment discipline of logic:CertifiedFragmentResource applied to process control flow rather than rule recursion; because resource is an execution policy holding independent properties together, a contract may carry both a DAG-workflow requirement and a budget at once. A plan outside the acyclic fragment is still valid canonically but resolves to `unsupported` under this policy, the offending edge disclosed by logic:dagCycleWitness — never silently truncated."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:DagWorkflowContract
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ReasoningContract ;
    logic:resourcePolicy logic:DagWorkflowResource ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "DAG-Workflow Contract"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The named ReasoningContract that requests the DAG-workflow resource policy (logic:resourcePolicy logic:DagWorkflowResource), under which a loop-free logic:Plan is topologically schedulable and complete-for-fragment. It is a CONTRACT (a standing request on the Resource facet), NOT a logic:ReasoningPreset: a preset bundles a model-semantics selection, whereas this fixes only the execution-discipline (Resource) facet — the contract-not-profile doctrine, applied so the resource discipline is a facet a contract requests, not a semantic profile. The dogfooded build pipeline runs logic:executedUnderContract this contract; a cyclic plan resolves to `unsupported` with its offending edge named rather than being approximated to a nearby supported semantics."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:dagCycleWitness
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "DAG cycle witness"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates an `unsupported` DAG-workflow verdict to a disclosed offending edge or cycle member — the back-edge or logic:Iteration that places a plan outside the certified acyclic fragment — MULTI-valued (a graph may carry several cycles). Mirrors the witness-disclosure discipline of logic:forbiddenViolationWitness: the certifier never silently truncates a cyclic plan, it surfaces exactly which edges break acyclicity while the plan stays valid under a non-DAG contract. Domain a reasoning result or coherence outcome carrying the `unsupported` evaluation status, range a flow edge or process node (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# =========================================================================== #
# === Enactment layer: prescription versions, enactments, the transition   === #
# === journal, checkpoints, continuation, and the prescription kind        === #
# =========================================================================== #
#
# The PRESCRIPTION -> ENACTMENT -> COMMITMENT kernel. A logic:Plan is the
# prescription: unchanged, it already reads as a workflow, a DAG, a repeatable
# guide, a curriculum (activities whose success criterion is competency
# attainment), a SCADA control plan (commands whose logic:planGoal is a
# logic:MaintenanceGoal over a setpoint), or an organizational guide. This layer
# mints VERSIONING, ENACTMENT and DURABILITY over that one plan concept and
# mints no second plan concept.
#
# The normative charter is design/LOGIC-ENACTMENT.md; the durable-session
# constructs it gives ontological twins to are specified in design/LOGIC-SESSION.md.

logic:TransitionJournal
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Transition Journal"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The hash-linked, append-only record of every transition an enactment has committed: an ordered chain of logic:JournalEntry nodes each naming its predecessor's head. It is the ontological twin of the runtime transition journal, and it is the ONE mechanism from which durability, idempotency, fencing and no-blind-retry all follow — a resubmitted operation carries a now-stale head, so double-apply is structurally impossible rather than guarded by a seen-set. Typed APART from logic:Path, which records the STATE sequence a transaction traversed: a journal records the COMMITTED-OPERATION sequence and survives crash and restart, whereas a path is the in-run state history."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:reviewJournal a logic:TransitionJournal ; logic:journalHead ex:entry7 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when an enactment must be resumable across a crash, or when an operation must be provably applied at most once."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid as a record of state history (that is logic:Path) and avoid minting one per step (a journal spans the whole enactment)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Mint one per logic:Enactment, append a logic:JournalEntry per committed transition, and carry the current head on logic:journalHead."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The ontological twin of the runtime transition journal, and the ONE mechanism by which an interrupted enactment can be told what it had already done. A journal that is not hash-linked records a sequence but cannot prove it was not rewritten."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:JournalEntry
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Journal Entry"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "One committed transition in a logic:TransitionJournal, binding the predecessor head it was applied against (logic:journalPrevHead), the content address of the delta applied (logic:journalDeltaIdentity), the typed outcome it produced (logic:journalOutcomeTag), and the head it establishes (logic:journalNewHead). The chain invariant is that an entry's logic:journalPrevHead equals its predecessor's logic:journalNewHead. A journal entry is not a logic:TransactionStep: a transaction step is an elementary state update inside one run, whereas a journal entry is the DURABLE, content-addressed commit record whose staleness a later submission is checked against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry7 a logic:JournalEntry ; logic:journalPrevHead \"blake3:a1b2\" ; logic:journalNewHead \"blake3:c3d4\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record that a specific delta was durably committed against a specific prior head."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid for an attempted-but-uncommitted operation (that has no entry until it commits) and avoid rewriting an entry in place."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Append one per commit; never mutate a landed entry; verify logic:journalPrevHead against the current head before accepting a submission."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Binds the predecessor head it was applied against, the content address of the delta, the typed outcome and the resulting head. Those four together are what make the chain verifiable; any one of them missing turns the journal into a log."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:journalPrevHead
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:JournalEntry ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "journal previous head"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The algorithm-prefixed content address of the journal head this entry was applied against. A submission whose logic:journalPrevHead does not equal the journal's current head is stale and is refused — the structural basis of the no-blind-retry law. Distinct from logic:journalNewHead, which is the head this entry establishes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry7 logic:journalPrevHead \"b3:0000…\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the head an entry was applied against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid omitting it: an entry naming only where it ended cannot be linked, and an unlinkable chain cannot detect a duplicate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "It must equal the predecessor entry's newHead; the chain-integrity law checks exactly that."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A submission whose value does not equal the journal's current head is stale and is refused, which is the structural form of no-blind-retry. The refusal needs no policy: the chain itself rejects the resubmission."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:journalNewHead
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:JournalEntry ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "journal new head"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The algorithm-prefixed content address this entry establishes as the journal head once it commits. The successor entry's logic:journalPrevHead must equal it; a gap or a fork is a chain-integrity violation. Distinct from logic:journalPrevHead, which names the head the entry was applied against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry7 logic:journalNewHead \"b3:7c05ea…\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the head an entry establishes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid reusing a newHead across entries; the head is a position, and two entries claiming the same one make the order unrecoverable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Pair with prevHead so each entry names both of its ends."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The successor entry's previous-head value must equal it; a gap or a fork is a chain-integrity violation. Distinct from the predecessor edge, which records succession, where this records the address that succession must reproduce."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:journalDeltaIdentity
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:JournalEntry ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "journal delta identity"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The content address of the logic:SnapshotDelta this entry committed — order-independent over the delta's additions and suppressions, so two submissions of the same change carry the same identity and are recognizably duplicate. This is the idempotency key: it is derived from content, never assigned by a caller."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry7 logic:journalDeltaIdentity \"b3:2e6a19…\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the content-addressed identity of the change an entry records."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a mutable identifier here; the point is that the same change always addresses the same way."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Address the delta's content, not the time it was written, so a replayed change is recognisable as the same change."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Order-independent over the delta's additions and suppressions, so two submissions of the same change carry the same identity and cannot both be committed. Order dependence here would make a re-ordered resubmission look new."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:journalOutcomeTag
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:JournalEntry ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "journal outcome tag"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:JournalEntry to the typed outcome its commit produced. Range logic:OperationOutcome (stated in prose). It records what the engine decided about the operation, never whether an external effect committed — that is the separate commitment-knowledge axis."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry7 logic:journalOutcomeTag \"accepted\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record how an entry's transition resolved."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid encoding rich outcome structure in the tag; a typed outcome record carries that, and a string cannot be reasoned over."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Keep the tag short and stable — it is a marker for scanning a chain, not the outcome itself."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Records what the ENGINE decided about the operation, never whether an external effect committed. Those are different questions with different evidence, and reading one for the other is how a journalled success hides a failed dispatch."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:journalHead
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:TransitionJournal ;
    rdfs:range logic:JournalEntry ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "journal head"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:TransitionJournal to its most recent committed logic:JournalEntry — the position a new submission must name in its logic:journalPrevHead to be accepted. Functional: a journal has exactly one head at a time, and advancing it is the commit, so a second head would be two accepted continuations — a fork rather than a chain."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:journal logic:journalHead \"b3:7c05ea…\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the current head of a transition journal — the position a new entry must apply against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid letting two writers advance the head independently; the chain is what makes a stale resubmission detectable, and a forked chain detects nothing."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Advance the head only by appending an entry whose prevHead equals it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional: a journal has exactly one head at a time, and that head is the position a new submission must name to be accepted. Two heads would mean two accepted continuations, which is a fork rather than a chain."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:SnapshotDelta
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Snapshot Delta"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A content-addressed, order-independent change between two logic:InputSnapshot generations: a set of additions and a set of suppressions. It is what makes 'what is new or changed since the previous occurrence?' answerable without diffing two whole snapshots — a recurring review discovers its newly added and changed inputs by reading a delta, and a curriculum's syllabus revision or a control recipe change is the same construct. Typed APART from logic:InputSnapshot, which is a closed generation: a delta is the difference BETWEEN two, never a generation itself."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:week12Delta a logic:SnapshotDelta ; logic:deltaAddition ex:adr-0042 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record what changed between two closed input generations, and to derive the idempotency key of the operation that applies it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid as a substitute for the snapshot itself, and avoid ordering-sensitive construction (the content address must not depend on enumeration order)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind logic:deltaAddition and logic:deltaSuppression, then content-address the pair; carry the address as the applying entry's logic:journalDeltaIdentity."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Content-addressed and order-independent, which is what makes what is new since the previous occurrence answerable without diffing two whole snapshots. Order independence matters because a delta assembled in a different order must still be the same delta."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:deltaAddition
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:SnapshotDelta ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "delta addition"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:SnapshotDelta to an input it introduces relative to the base generation. Multi-valued. Paired with logic:deltaSuppression, which names what the delta withdraws; an input that is neither added nor suppressed is carried forward unchanged."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Names only what the delta introduces. An input that is neither added nor suppressed is carried forward unchanged, which is why a delta can be small even when the generation it produces is large."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:delta-7 logic:deltaAddition ex:reading-2026-07-28 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to name each input a snapshot delta introduces relative to the base generation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid re-listing inputs the base already carries: the delta's value lies in being exactly the difference, and restating carried-forward inputs destroys that."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Pair it with logic:deltaSuppression so a reader can reconstruct the derived generation from the base plus the two lists alone."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:deltaSuppression
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:SnapshotDelta ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "delta suppression"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:SnapshotDelta to an input it withdraws relative to the base generation. Multi-valued. Suppression is not deletion: the withdrawn input remains readable in the base generation, and the delta records that it no longer participates."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Suppression is not deletion: the withdrawn input remains readable in the base generation, and the delta records only that it no longer participates. That distinction is what keeps a derived generation auditable against the one it came from."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:delta-7 logic:deltaSuppression ex:reading-2026-07-21 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to name each input a snapshot delta withdraws relative to the base generation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it to express that an input was wrong: withdrawal from a generation is not a retraction of the datum, and conflating the two loses the record."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record the suppression rather than editing the base, so the earlier generation stays reproducible exactly as it was enacted."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:PrescriptionVersion
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Prescription Version"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "An immutable, content-addressed version of a logic:Plan — the prescription an enactment runs against. Exactly one logic:prescriptionOf names the plan it versions. A revision never mutates a version: it mints a new one and supersedes the old, so a landed enactment's prescription can always be recovered exactly as it was executed. Typed APART from logic:Plan, which is the continuing program identity across revisions: a version is one frozen state of that program."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:adrReviewV3 a logic:PrescriptionVersion ; logic:prescriptionOf ex:adrReviewPlan ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use whenever a plan can be revised while earlier runs against it must stay reproducible — a versioned curriculum, a revised control recipe, a workflow definition under change control."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid mutating a landed version in place, and avoid minting one per enactment (many enactments share one version)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Content-address the plan's frozen form, bind logic:prescriptionOf, and record revision by minting a successor and superseding the predecessor."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A revision never mutates a version: it mints a new one and supersedes the old, so an enactment that pinned the old version keeps running exactly what it started with. Mutation in place would silently rewrite history."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:prescriptionOf
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:PrescriptionVersion ;
    rdfs:range logic:Plan ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "prescription of"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:PrescriptionVersion to the logic:Plan it is a frozen version of. Functional: a version versions exactly one plan. The inverse direction is deliberately absent — a plan does not carry a forward list of its versions, which would make the plan node mutable on every revision."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:reviewV4 logic:prescriptionOf ex:reviewProcess ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind an immutable version to the plan it is a version of."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid pointing at another version; a version is a version OF a plan, and chaining versions loses the plan."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Exactly one plan per version, so every version of a process is reachable from the process."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The inverse direction is deliberately absent: a plan does not carry a forward list of its versions, so adding a version never edits the plan. That asymmetry is what keeps versioning append-only in practice."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:prescriptionDigest
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:PrescriptionVersion ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "prescription digest"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The algorithm-prefixed content address of a logic:PrescriptionVersion's frozen form. It is the value a checkpoint restore and an approval binding are both checked against, so a revised prescription cannot silently satisfy an authorization issued for its predecessor. Not functional: several digest algorithms may coexist over one version."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:reviewV4 logic:prescriptionDigest \"b3:4c8a03…\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to content-address a prescription version, which is how its immutability is enforced."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a mutability flag in place of a digest: a flag can be set wrongly, whereas an unmatched digest cannot lie."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Address the version's bytes, so revising it in place becomes detectable rather than silent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The value a checkpoint restore and an approval binding are both checked against, so a revised prescription cannot satisfy either. One digest serving both checks is what keeps the two from drifting apart."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:InputSnapshot
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Input Snapshot"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed, content-addressed generation of inputs one enactment runs against — the corpus as of a moment, fixed for the whole run so the run is reproducible. Closedness is the point: an enactment against an open input set cannot be replayed and cannot support an honest 'what changed since last time' comparison. Typed APART from logic:SnapshotDelta, which is the difference between two generations rather than a generation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:adrCorpusW12 a logic:InputSnapshot ; logic:snapshotDigest \"blake3:9f8e\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to freeze the inputs of one enactment so the run is replayable and comparable to its predecessors."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid sharing one snapshot across enactments that ran at different times, and avoid treating a live query result as a snapshot."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Content-address the closed input set, bind logic:snapshotDigest, and name it from the enactment via logic:enactmentInputSnapshot."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Closedness is the point: an enactment against an open input set cannot be replayed and cannot support comparison across occurrences. Content addressing is what makes the closure checkable rather than merely intended."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:snapshotDigest
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:InputSnapshot ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "snapshot digest"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The algorithm-prefixed content address of a logic:InputSnapshot's closed generation. A restore whose snapshot digest differs from the checkpoint's is refused: the run would otherwise resume against inputs it never started with. Not functional."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:corpusW13 logic:snapshotDigest \"b3:93e5c1…\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to content-address an input snapshot or a snapshot delta."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a timestamp in place of a digest; two snapshots taken at the same moment may differ, and the same snapshot re-taken later is still the same snapshot."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Address the closed content, so 'what changed since last time' is derivable rather than guessed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A restore whose snapshot digest differs from the checkpoint's is refused: the run would otherwise resume against inputs it never started with. The digest addresses a CLOSED generation, so an open input set cannot produce one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:Enactment
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Enactment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "One immutable occurrence of a logic:PrescriptionVersion against one logic:InputSnapshot, carrying its own logic:TransitionJournal. It reads uniformly as a workflow run, a walkthrough of a repeatable guide, a batch, one cohort's term, a control session, or one learner's enrolment — the occurrence, not the thing occurring. Typed APART from logic:Plan and logic:PrescriptionVersion, which are prescriptive and may be enacted many times, and APART from a calendar occurrence, which is the scheduling event that GENERATES an enactment rather than being one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:adrReviewW12 a logic:Enactment ; logic:enactsPrescriptionVersion ex:adrReviewV3 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for one durable, resumable occurrence of a prescription that must be replayable and separately auditable from its siblings."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid identifying an enactment with the scheduled occurrence that generated it, and avoid reusing one enactment for a repeat (a repeat is a new enactment)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind exactly one logic:enactsPrescriptionVersion and one logic:enactmentInputSnapshot, attach a logic:TransitionJournal, and record progress as journal entries and checkpoints."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Immutable and singular: one occurrence of one prescription version against one input snapshot. Reading it uniformly as a workflow run, a walkthrough, a batch, a cohort term, a control session or a learner path is the point of the abstraction, not a coincidence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:enactsPrescriptionVersion
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Enactment ;
    rdfs:range logic:PrescriptionVersion ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "enacts prescription version"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Enactment to the exactly-one logic:PrescriptionVersion it runs. Pinned at start: if the governing pointer moves to a newer version mid-run, this binding does not follow it, so the run stays reproducible against what it actually executed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:weekThirteen logic:enactsPrescriptionVersion ex:reviewV4 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to pin the exact version an occurrence runs."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid pointing at the plan rather than a version; without the version, two occurrences are not comparable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Pin at start and never move it — the cluster's active-version pointer moves independently, which is what stops a mid-flight revision rewriting this run."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Pinned at start: if the governing pointer moves to a newer version mid-run, this binding does not follow it, so the run stays what it began as. That non-following is the whole reason the binding exists separately from the pointer."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:enactmentInputSnapshot
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Enactment ;
    rdfs:range logic:InputSnapshot ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "enactment input snapshot"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Enactment to the exactly-one closed logic:InputSnapshot it runs against. Two enactments of one prescription differ precisely here, which is what makes a prior-occurrence comparison meaningful. The bound snapshot is frozen at start and is not whatever the inputs happen to be now, so the comparison is between two pinned worlds rather than between a run and live data."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:weekThirteen logic:enactmentInputSnapshot ex:corpusW13 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind an enactment to the closed input generation it ran against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid leaving it unbound: a run whose inputs are 'whatever was current' cannot be reproduced or compared to its siblings."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind exactly one snapshot; together with the version pin it is what makes the occurrence reproducible."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Two enactments of one prescription differ precisely here, which is what makes a prior-occurrence comparison meaningful. Without a pinned snapshot the comparison would be between a run and whatever the inputs happen to be now."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:enactmentJournal
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Enactment ;
    rdfs:range logic:TransitionJournal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "enactment journal"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Enactment to its logic:TransitionJournal — the durable commit chain that makes the enactment resumable. Functional: one journal per enactment, so the head is unambiguous. An enactment cannot carry a second journal for a retry or a sub-plan, because two journals would give an interrupted run two candidate positions to resume from and no principled way to choose."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:weekThirteen logic:enactmentJournal ex:journal ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to attach the transition journal recording how an enactment progressed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid sharing one journal across enactments; the chain would interleave and stop meaning anything."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "One journal per enactment, from which durability, idempotency and no-blind-retry all follow."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional: one journal per enactment, so the head is unambiguous. Two journals would give an interrupted run two candidate positions to resume from and no principled way to choose between them."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:EnactmentCohort
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Enactment Cohort"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A set of logic:Enactment occurrences of one prescription that share a schedule or input generation — a teaching cohort, a batch, a shift, a scheduled sweep across many targets. It groups siblings for comparison and for cohort-wide policy without making them one enactment. Typed APART from logic:Enactment, which is a single occurrence, and from a recurrence rule, which generates occurrences rather than collecting them."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:autumnCohort a logic:EnactmentCohort ; logic:cohortMember ex:enrolment-014 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when many enactments must be reasoned about together — a cohort's progression, a batch's throughput, a shift's handover."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid as a substitute for the schedule that generated the members, and avoid grouping enactments of different prescriptions."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind logic:cohortMember per enactment; carry the shared generation on the members' own logic:enactmentInputSnapshot rather than on the cohort."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Groups siblings for comparison and for cohort-wide policy without making them one enactment. Each member keeps its own journal and its own terminal state, so a cohort never has a single outcome to report."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:cohortMember
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:EnactmentCohort ;
    rdfs:range logic:Enactment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "cohort member"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:EnactmentCohort to one of the logic:Enactment occurrences it groups. Multi-valued; membership is a grouping claim and carries no authority over the member's own bindings. A cohort-wide policy reaches a member because that member is in scope, not because the cohort overrode the prescription version or input snapshot the enactment pinned for itself."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:autumnCohort logic:cohortMember ex:enrolment ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to group enactments sharing one prescription version and schedule."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid treating a cohort statement as an enactment statement: 'the class is behind' and 'this learner is behind' are different claims."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Membership is by shared version and schedule, not by convenience grouping."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Membership is a grouping claim and carries no authority over the member's own bindings. A cohort-wide policy applies to members because they are in scope, never because the cohort overrode what each enactment pinned."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:EnactmentCheckpoint
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Enactment Checkpoint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A durable, content-addressed resume point for a whole logic:Enactment, binding the seven identity axes (data, rules, slice, contract, engine, annotation algebra, fragment) and the journal head it was taken at. Restore is identity-gated: a mismatch on ANY axis is refused rather than reconciled, because resuming a run against drifted rules or a drifted engine silently produces a result the run never would have produced. Typed APART from logic:StepCheckpoint, which resumes one step rather than the enactment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ckptW12 a logic:EnactmentCheckpoint ; logic:checkpointDescriptorHash \"blake3:5a6b\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to make a long or interruptible enactment resumable across a crash or a deliberate pause."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid restoring across an identity mismatch, and avoid treating a checkpoint as a snapshot of engine state (it re-materializes deterministically instead)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind all seven axis digests plus the folded logic:checkpointDescriptorHash and the logic:journalHead; on restore, check integrity, re-materialize, gate on identity, then gate on data generation, in that order."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Binds all seven identity axes plus the journal head, and restore is identity-gated on them. A checkpoint that binds fewer axes still restores, but it restores into an engine that may no longer compute what the checkpoint assumed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:StepCheckpoint
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Step Checkpoint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A durable resume point for one step of a logic:Enactment rather than the whole run — the granularity at which a long step with expensive prefix work can restart without redoing it. It carries the same identity gate as logic:EnactmentCheckpoint but is scoped to the step it names, and a step checkpoint is never a valid restore point for the enactment as a whole."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ckptOcrStep a logic:StepCheckpoint ; logic:checkpointOfStep ex:ocrStep ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when one step is long enough that restarting it from the beginning is materially wasteful."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using a step checkpoint to resume the enactment, and avoid minting one per trivial step."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind logic:checkpointOfStep alongside the seven axis digests, and restore it only within the enactment whose journal head it names."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The granularity at which a long step with expensive prefix work can restart without redoing it. It carries the same identity gate as the enactment-scoped checkpoint but is scoped to one step, so it never licenses resuming the run as a whole."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:checkpointDescriptorHash
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "checkpoint descriptor hash"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The single folded content address over all seven checkpoint identity axes — the value a restore compares first, so a mismatch on ANY axis surfaces as one identity fault rather than requiring seven separate comparisons. Domain a checkpoint (stated in prose). It never replaces the per-axis digests: those name WHICH axis drifted once the fold has said that one did."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:midterm logic:checkpointDescriptorHash \"b3:e58021…\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the folded identity over all seven checkpoint axes — the single value a restore gate compares first."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid treating it as a substitute for the individual axes: the fold tells you the checkpoint does not match, and only the axes tell you why."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Compute it over the same seven axes in the same order every time, or two runs of the same state will disagree."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The single folded address a restore compares first, so a mismatch on ANY axis surfaces as one identity fault rather than seven comparisons. It answers THAT something differs; the per-axis digests answer which, and both are needed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:checkpointDataDigest
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "checkpoint data digest"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Identity axis one of seven: the content address of the authorized data generation the checkpoint was taken over. A restore against a different generation is refused even when every other axis matches, because the run would resume over inputs it never saw. Domain a checkpoint (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:midterm logic:checkpointDataDigest \"b3:a17f0c…\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to pin the asserted data a checkpoint was taken under — one of the seven axes a restore is gated on."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid digesting a filtered or projected view of the data; the restore compares this against what was actually loaded, so a narrowed digest matches a state that never existed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Digest the asserted quads as the run saw them, before any view or projection is applied."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A restore against a different generation is refused even when every other axis matches, because the run would resume over inputs it never read. It is the one axis a caller may legitimately intend to cross, by explicitly re-authorizing."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:checkpointRulesDigest
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "checkpoint rules digest"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Identity axis two of seven: the content address of the rule set in force when the checkpoint was taken. Drifted rules would make the resumed run derive conclusions the original could not, so a mismatch is a refusal rather than an upgrade. Domain a checkpoint (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:midterm logic:checkpointRulesDigest \"b3:3e9b1d…\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to pin the rule set in force a checkpoint was taken under — one of the seven axes a restore is gated on."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid omitting rules that were compiled but never fired: a rule set that could have fired differently is a different rule set, even when the run's output was identical."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Digest every rule in force at checkpoint time, firing or not."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Drifted rules would make the resumed run derive conclusions the original could not, so a mismatch is a refusal rather than a warning. The digest is over the rule set in force at checkpoint time, not over the rules currently loaded."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:checkpointSliceDigest
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "checkpoint slice digest"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Identity axis three of seven: the content address of the vocabulary selection in force when the checkpoint was taken. A restore under a different selection may find terms undefined or newly defined, so the axis is gated rather than reconciled. Domain a checkpoint (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:midterm logic:checkpointSliceDigest \"b3:6d2a58…\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to pin the slice inventory a checkpoint was taken under — one of the seven axes a restore is gated on."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid digesting only the slices the run touched; a slice that was present and unused still changes what the next step could have seen."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Digest the whole loaded slice inventory, including versions."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A restore under a different vocabulary selection may find terms undefined or newly defined, so the axis is gated rather than advisory. The failure is quiet in either direction: a missing term errors, but a newly added one just changes what matches."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:checkpointContractDigest
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "checkpoint contract digest"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Identity axis four of seven: the content address of the logic:ReasoningContract the checkpointed run executed under. Resuming under a different contract changes what counts as a complete answer, so the axis is part of identity rather than of configuration. Domain a checkpoint (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:midterm logic:checkpointContractDigest \"b3:8f41c0…\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to pin the reasoning contract a checkpoint was taken under — one of the seven axes a restore is gated on."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid treating two contracts as equal because they produced the same answer here; a contract governs what CAN be concluded, not only what was."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Digest the declared reasoning contract — profile, budget policy and completeness semantics together."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Resuming under a different reasoning contract changes what counts as a complete answer, so the axis is part of identity rather than configuration. A restore that crossed it would report completeness under a definition the original run never used."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:checkpointEngineDigest
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "checkpoint engine digest"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Identity axis five of seven: the logic:EngineContract descriptor hash of the engine that took the checkpoint. It is not a digest of data or of rules: identical inputs under an identical rule set but a drifted engine can still derive a different closure, which is exactly the case a purely data-level check silently accepts and this axis refuses. Domain a checkpoint (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:midterm logic:checkpointEngineDigest \"b3:15c9e0…\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to pin the engine build a checkpoint was taken under — one of the seven axes a restore is gated on."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid pinning a version string in place of the engine's content: two builds of the same version can differ, and the restore would accept a build that reasons differently."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Digest the engine's own source-content identity, which is what moves when its behaviour can move."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The axis that refuses a restore under a drifted engine — the case a purely data-level check would silently accept. Identical inputs and rules under a changed engine can still derive a different closure, which is exactly what this gate exists to catch."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:checkpointAnnotationDigest
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "checkpoint annotation digest"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Identity axis six of seven: the content address of the tuple-annotation algebra in force when the checkpoint was taken. It addresses the algebra, not the annotations the algebra produced: a different algebra changes how derived annotations combine, so a resumed run would fold evidence differently from the run it claims to continue. Domain a checkpoint (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:midterm logic:checkpointAnnotationDigest \"b3:c07b2e…\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to pin the annotation algebra a checkpoint was taken under — one of the seven axes a restore is gated on."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid ignoring the annotation algebra because it looks like metadata; provenance and attribution decide which derivations are admissible, so changing it changes the closure."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Digest the annotation semantics in force, not merely the annotations present."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A different annotation algebra changes how derived annotations combine, so a resumed run would fold evidence differently from the run it claims to continue. The digest gates that rather than leaving the divergence to be discovered in the output."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:checkpointFragmentDigest
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "checkpoint fragment digest"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Identity axis seven of seven: the content address of the certified decidable fragment the checkpointed run was admitted under. A restore outside that fragment would trade a certified-complete answer for an uncertified one without saying so, and the substitution is not visible in the output: both runs return answers, and only one of them is warranted. Domain a checkpoint (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:midterm logic:checkpointFragmentDigest \"b3:24a90f…\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to pin the declared fragment a checkpoint was taken under — one of the seven axes a restore is gated on."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid restoring across a fragment change: a result that was complete for a narrower fragment is merely incomplete for a wider one, and nothing in the data says so."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Digest the declared decidable fragment, so a completeness claim is only reused where it still holds."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A restore outside the certified fragment would trade a certified-complete answer for an uncertified one without saying so. The axis is gated because the substitution is invisible in the output: both runs return answers, and only one of them is warranted."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:checkpointOfEnactment
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:EnactmentCheckpoint ;
    rdfs:range logic:Enactment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "checkpoint of enactment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:EnactmentCheckpoint to the logic:Enactment it can resume. Functional: a checkpoint resumes exactly one enactment, which is what makes a cross-enactment restore detectable rather than merely discouraged."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:midterm logic:checkpointOfEnactment ex:enrolment ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind a checkpoint to the enactment whose state it captures."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a floating checkpoint: one that names no enactment can never be restored into anything."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Exactly one enactment per checkpoint; a checkpoint spanning two occurrences would restore neither."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional, so a checkpoint resumes exactly one enactment. That is what makes a cross-enactment restore detectable rather than merely discouraged: a restore naming a different run fails a comparison instead of violating a convention."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:checkpointOfStep
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:StepCheckpoint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "checkpoint of step"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:StepCheckpoint to the plan step it can resume. Range a plan step node (stated in prose). Functional. Paired with logic:checkpointOfEnactment on the enactment-scoped sibling: a step checkpoint additionally names its enactment through that step's own run, so it cannot be restored into a run it did not come from — which naming only the step would allow by accident."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:extractCheckpoint logic:checkpointOfStep ex:extractStep ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a per-step checkpoint, so a resumed run can skip completed steps rather than restarting."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using a step checkpoint as an enactment checkpoint; the state it captures is narrower than the run."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Carry the same seven identity axes as an enactment checkpoint — a step result reused under changed rules is the same defect at smaller scale."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Paired with the enactment-scoped sibling: a step checkpoint additionally names its enactment, so it can never be restored into a run it did not come from. Naming only the step would make it portable across enactments by accident."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:ContinuationKind
    a logic:AbstractIndividualType ,
        logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:QualityValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Continuation Kind"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed three-valued vocabulary distinguishing the three ways work continues after it has already begun: resuming the same enactment, repeating under a new input generation, or revising the prescription itself. They are pairwise disjoint and carry different required bindings, so conflating them — the common failure in which 'run it again' silently means any of the three — is structurally detectable. Its members are individuals, never subclasses."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:w12continuation logic:continuationKind logic:ContinuationResume ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to say which of the three continuation operations an act is, whenever an enactment is picked up after an interruption."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid inventing a fourth value and avoid leaving the kind unstated when work continues."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Select exactly one member and supply that member's required bindings; the disjointness constraint rejects a record carrying two."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The three values are pairwise disjoint and carry different identity consequences, so choosing between them is not a matter of emphasis. A resume keeps one enactment, a repeat mints another, and a revision changes what future occurrences will do."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ContinuationResume
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ContinuationKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "resume"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Continuation of the SAME logic:Enactment from a logic:EnactmentCheckpoint: the prescription version and the input snapshot are unchanged, and the journal continues rather than restarting. Requires a checkpoint whose identity gate passes. Distinguished from repeat, which mints a new enactment, and from revise, which mints a new prescription version."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Continues one enactment across a gap; it is not a repair. The seven identity axes of the checkpoint must all match, so a resume that would cross a changed engine, rule set or data generation is refused rather than silently promoted into a repeat."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:enactment-88 logic:continuationKind logic:ContinuationResume ; logic:restoresCheckpoint ex:ckpt-12 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a paused or crashed enactment is picked up from a durable checkpoint whose identity gates all pass, so the journal continues from where it stopped."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when any identity axis has moved: the resume would be reading a state the current engine did not produce, which is the drifted-fold error the checkpoint gate exists to catch."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the checkpoint with logic:restoresCheckpoint and let the four integrity faults be evaluated in order, so a refusal reports which gate stopped it rather than a bare failure."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ContinuationRepeat
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ContinuationKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "repeat"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A NEW logic:Enactment of the same logic:PrescriptionVersion against a NEW logic:InputSnapshot — next week's occurrence of a recurring review, the next batch, the next cohort. The prior enactment stays closed and comparable. It is not a resume, which continues the existing enactment against the snapshot already pinned, nor a revise, which mints a new prescription version."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Covers the case where the prescription and the world are both re-entered afresh; it does not cover retrying a failed step, which stays inside the original enactment. The test is whether a new input snapshot is pinned: if the same snapshot is being worked again, the continuation is a resume and not a repeat."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:review-2026-w31 logic:continuationKind logic:ContinuationRepeat ; logic:enactmentInputSnapshot ex:snapshot-w31 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the next occurrence of a recurring prescription, or the next batch or cohort, where the previous run is finished and is meant to stay comparable rather than be extended."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it to restart a run that merely stalled: repeating discards the prior journal position, and a stalled run that holds a checkpoint should resume so its history stays continuous."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Mint a fresh enactment, pin the new snapshot with logic:enactmentInputSnapshot, and record the continuation kind so the recurrence series reads as a sequence of comparable occurrences."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ContinuationRevise
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ContinuationKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "revise the prescription"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Minting a NEW logic:PrescriptionVersion that supersedes its predecessor, because the prescription itself was wrong or has changed. In-flight enactments keep the version they pinned at start, so a revision never rewrites a running occurrence. It is not a resume or a repeat: both of those leave the prescription untouched and settle only which run is under way."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Changes the prescription, not the run. Enactments already in flight keep the version they pinned at start, so revision never rewrites what a running occurrence is doing; it only governs occurrences that begin afterwards."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:protocol-v4 logic:continuationKind logic:ContinuationRevise ; logic:supersededTerm ex:protocol-v3 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the prescription itself was wrong or has changed, so a new content-addressed version supersedes its predecessor for all later occurrences."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it to patch a running enactment: editing a version under a live run is what the kernel forbids, and the counter-example for it is a revised version observed beneath a running occurrence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Mint the new version with its own digest, link the predecessor with logic:supersededTerm, and leave in-flight enactments pinned to the version they started under."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:continuationKind
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:ContinuationKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has continuation kind"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a continuation record to the exactly-one logic:ContinuationKind it is. Domain a continuation record (stated in prose). Functional: a record carrying two kinds is the conflation the disjointness constraint rejects, because resuming and repeating make incompatible identity claims and a record asserting both cannot say which enactment is running."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:returnEntry logic:continuationKind logic:ContinuationResume ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to say which of resume, repeat or revise actually happened."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid claiming two kinds: they carry different required bindings and different consequences, so a record claiming both has not said what happened."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Resume continues a run, repeat mints a new one, revise supersedes the prescription — pick the one that matches the bindings present."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional, because a record carrying two kinds is the conflation the disjointness constraint rejects. Resuming and repeating make incompatible identity claims, so a record asserting both says nothing determinate about which enactment is running."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:PriorEnactmentRequirement
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Prior Enactment Requirement"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A precondition over OTHER enactments rather than over world state: this enactment may not start until a named prior enactment reached a named state. It is what a curriculum prerequisite, an operator certification, a prior-shift handover and an upstream-DAG dependency all are. Typed APART from logic:precondition, which gates on the situation obtaining at a state: a prior-enactment requirement gates on the RUN HISTORY of a different occurrence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:calc2Prereq a logic:PriorEnactmentRequirement ; logic:requiredPriorPrescription ex:calc1 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when eligibility to begin depends on another occurrence having completed — prerequisites, certifications, handovers, upstream stages."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid for a condition on world state (use logic:precondition) and avoid encoding a mere ordering preference as a requirement."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the required prescription and the state its enactment must have reached; the frontier reads unmet requirements as a blocking reason."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A precondition over OTHER enactments rather than over world state, which is why it cannot be expressed as an ordinary guard. A curriculum prerequisite, an operator certification and a prior-shift handover are all this shape."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:requiredPriorPrescription
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:PriorEnactmentRequirement ;
    rdfs:range logic:Plan ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "required prior prescription"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:PriorEnactmentRequirement to the logic:Plan whose enactment must already have reached the required state. It names the plan rather than a specific enactment, so the requirement is stated once and satisfied by whichever occurrence the subject actually ran."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Names the plan rather than a specific enactment, so the requirement is stated once and satisfied by whichever occurrence the subject actually ran. Pointing at an enactment instead would make the requirement unstatable in advance and unshareable across learners or teams."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:prereq-1 logic:requiredPriorPrescription ex:stats-101 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to name the plan whose enactment must already have reached a required state before this one may proceed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid naming a particular enactment: the requirement would then apply to one subject's history and could not be reused."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Pair it with logic:requiredPriorState, so the requirement says both which plan and how far it had to get."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:requiredPriorState
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:PriorEnactmentRequirement ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "required prior state"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:PriorEnactmentRequirement to the enactment state the prior occurrence must have reached. Range logic:EnactmentState (stated in prose). Naming the state rather than assuming completion lets a requirement be satisfied by, for example, a prior run that reached a reviewed-but-not-closed position."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Naming the state rather than assuming completion lets a requirement be satisfied by, for example, a prior run that reached a reviewed-but-not-closed position. Assuming completion would silently exclude every legitimate partial prerequisite."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:prereq-1 logic:requiredPriorState logic:EnactmentCompleted ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to state which enactment state a prior occurrence must have reached for the requirement to be met."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid omitting it and assuming completion: many real prerequisites are met by a run that stopped short deliberately."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Choose the weakest state that genuinely suffices, so the prerequisite gates on what matters rather than on how far someone happened to go."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:Deviation
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Deviation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A recorded, authorized departure from the prescription during an enactment — a control-plan deviation, a curriculum accommodation, an organizational exception, a workflow manual override. It records WHAT differed, WHO authorized it, and against WHICH step, so the run stays auditable rather than merely non-conforming. Typed APART from logic:Constraint violation, which is an integrity finding nobody authorized: a deviation is a sanctioned difference."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrManualOverride a logic:Deviation ; logic:deviationFromStep ex:ocrStep ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when an enactment legitimately departs from its prescription and the departure must be visible and attributed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid as a way to silence a constraint violation, and avoid recording a deviation with no authorizing party."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the step departed from and the authorizing party; the review projection surfaces deviations alongside the steps they alter."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Records WHAT differed, WHO authorized it, and against WHICH step, so a departure remains a departure rather than becoming the new prescription. A deviation that is not recorded against a step cannot be told apart from a revision of the plan."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:deviationFromStep
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Deviation ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "deviation from step"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Deviation to the prescribed step it departs from. Range a plan step node (stated in prose). Functional: a deviation names one step and not a span of them, so a departure covering several steps is several deviations, each carrying its own authorization and its own justification."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:accommodation logic:deviationFromStep ex:syllabus ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record an authorized departure from the prescription without editing it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid editing the prescription instead; that destroys the immutability the version pin depends on."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record the deviation against the step it departs from, so it is auditable alongside what it departed from."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional: a deviation names one step, and a departure spanning several steps is several deviations. Recording it once against a range would leave each step's authorization and justification unattributable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:PrescriptionKind
    a logic:AbstractIndividualType ,
        logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:QualityValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Prescription Kind"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The open classification of what KIND of prescription a logic:Plan is — a data pipeline, a lab protocol, a recipe, a curriculum, a control plan, an organizational guide. It is a VALUE carried on the plan, never a subclass of logic:Plan: minting a subclass per genre would fork the one canonical plan concept the kernel exists to keep single. Its members are individuals and more than one may co-apply."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:adrReviewPlan logic:prescriptionKind logic:prescriptionKindOrganizationalGuide ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record the genre of a plan for routing, display and profile selection."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid minting a logic:Plan subclass per genre, and avoid treating the kind as licensing different execution semantics."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach one or more members through logic:prescriptionKind; extend the vocabulary with a new individual rather than a new class."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A VALUE carried on the plan, never a subclass of the plan class. Minting a subclass per kind would put an open, evolving classification into the class hierarchy, where every addition becomes a schema change instead of a data one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:prescriptionKind
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Plan ;
    rdfs:range logic:PrescriptionKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has prescription kind"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Plan to a logic:PrescriptionKind classifying its genre. Non-functional: a plan may be both a data pipeline and an ingestion, and the co-application is meaningful rather than a modelling error."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:reviewProcess logic:prescriptionKind logic:prescriptionKindRepeatableGuide ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to classify what kind of prescription a plan is — a workflow, a curriculum, a control plan, a guide."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid reading the kind as changing the plan's semantics; it classifies, and the kernel treats all kinds alike."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Pick the closed value that matches how the plan is used, so consumers can filter without inspecting the body."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Non-functional: a plan may be both a data pipeline and an ingestion, and the co-application is meaningful rather than a modelling error. Forcing a single kind would make an author choose between two true statements."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:prescriptionKindAgentFlow
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:PrescriptionKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "agent flow"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A prescription whose steps are the moves of an autonomous agent — tool calls, retrievals, decisions. Distinguished from a business process, whose steps are organizational rather than agentic."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Classifies whose moves the steps are, not how automated they look. A business process executed entirely by software is still a business process; this kind is for prescriptions whose steps are the agent's own choices of tool, retrieval and decision."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:triage-flow logic:prescriptionKind logic:prescriptionKindAgentFlow ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a prescription whose steps are an autonomous agent's own moves — tool calls, retrievals and decisions it selects at run time."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for an organizational workflow that happens to be automated: the steps there are roles and handoffs, and the agent is an implementation detail."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:prescriptionKind on the plan, so the frontier's approval axis can be read against an agent's dispatches rather than a person's."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:prescriptionKindBusinessProcess
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:PrescriptionKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "business process"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A prescription describing an organizational workflow with roles, handoffs and approvals. Distinguished from an organizational guide, which is advisory rather than an executed process."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "An executed instance flow with roles, handoffs and approvals, as opposed to advisory guidance about how work ought to be done. The test is whether occurrences are enacted and journalled, not whether the document reads like a procedure."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:onboarding logic:prescriptionKind logic:prescriptionKindBusinessProcess ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for an organizational workflow whose occurrences are enacted with named roles, handoffs and approval points."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for normative guidance nobody enacts as a tracked occurrence: that is an organizational guide, and enactment records would be fabricated."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:prescriptionKind and expect the approval axis to carry real commitments at the named steps."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:prescriptionKindCiBuild
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:PrescriptionKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "CI build"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A prescription describing an automated build or integration pipeline whose steps compile, test and publish. Distinguished from a data pipeline, whose steps transform data rather than produce artifacts."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Steps produce artifacts rather than transform data, which is what separates it from a data pipeline. The distinction matters to compensation: a published artifact usually cannot be withdrawn, whereas a derived dataset can be recomputed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:release-build logic:prescriptionKind logic:prescriptionKindCiBuild ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for an automated build or integration pipeline whose steps compile, test and publish artifacts."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a pipeline whose products are datasets: the compensation story differs, and the kind is what tells a reader which one applies."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:prescriptionKind and treat publication steps as external effects with their own receipts rather than as internal work."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:prescriptionKindDataPipeline
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:PrescriptionKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "data pipeline"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A prescription whose steps transform data from sources to derived products, as opposed to an ingestion, which is the narrower act of bringing external material in under a canonical layout. Its outputs are recomputable, which is what makes compensation cheap here and expensive for a build."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Transforms already-available material into derived products, as opposed to bringing material in under a canonical layout. Its outputs are recomputable, which is what makes compensation cheap here and expensive for a build."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:nightly-etl logic:prescriptionKind logic:prescriptionKindDataPipeline ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the steps transform data from sources into derived products that could be recomputed from the same inputs."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for the narrower act of bringing external material in: ingestion names that, and it carries its own posture and unresolved-claim steps."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:prescriptionKind and pin the input snapshot, so a repeat over the same generation is genuinely comparable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:prescriptionKindIngestion
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:PrescriptionKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "ingestion"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A prescription that brings external material into a canonical layout — copy, extract, assess posture, derive claims, record what stayed unresolved. It is not a data pipeline, which transforms already-ingested material: what an ingestion records about material that stayed unresolved is as much its product as the bytes it brought in."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The narrower act of bringing external material in — copy, extract, assess posture, derive claims, record what stayed unresolved. What it records about unresolved material is as much its product as the ingested bytes, which is why it is not just a pipeline stage."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:corpus-intake logic:prescriptionKind logic:prescriptionKindIngestion ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a prescription that brings external material into a canonical layout and records the posture and residue of doing so."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for transformations over material already inside the canonical layout: that is a data pipeline, and the ingestion posture steps would be vacuous."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:prescriptionKind and keep the unresolved-claim record as a first-class output rather than a log line."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:prescriptionKindLabProtocol
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:PrescriptionKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "lab protocol"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A prescription describing a laboratory or field procedure whose steps must be executed and recorded exactly for the result to be admissible. Unlike a recipe, it tolerates no substitution: the same swap a recipe absorbs invalidates a protocol result outright, so the identical deviation carries entirely different consequences under the two kinds."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Admissibility is gated on exact execution, which is what separates it from a recipe. A substitution that a recipe tolerates invalidates a protocol result outright, so the same deviation carries entirely different consequences under the two kinds."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:assay-b logic:prescriptionKind logic:prescriptionKindLabProtocol ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a laboratory or field procedure whose steps must be executed and recorded exactly for the result to be admissible."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it where substitution is acceptable: a recipe says so honestly, and a protocol would make ordinary flexibility look like a violation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:prescriptionKind and record every deviation, since deviation is what determines admissibility rather than merely quality."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:prescriptionKindRecipe
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:PrescriptionKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "recipe"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A prescription with ingredients, steps and a yield, tolerant of substitution and scaling. Distinguished from a lab protocol, whose steps are not discretionary."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Tolerant of substitution and scaling, and that tolerance is a property of the kind rather than of any particular step. A recipe whose steps genuinely may not vary is a lab protocol, and typing it as a recipe understates what a deviation costs."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:sourdough logic:prescriptionKind logic:prescriptionKindRecipe ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a prescription with ingredients, steps and a yield, where substitution and scaling are legitimate rather than deviations."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it where exact execution determines whether the result may be used at all: that is a lab protocol."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:prescriptionKind and model substitutions as ordinary alternatives rather than as recorded deviations."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:prescriptionKindResearchPlan
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:PrescriptionKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "research plan"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A prescription organizing an open inquiry — themes to pursue, sources to consult, questions whose answerhood conditions are not yet settled. Distinguished from the other kinds by having an epistemic rather than a productive goal."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Has an epistemic rather than a productive goal, which is what marks it out from every other kind. Its questions may have unsettled answerhood conditions, so an occurrence can be entirely successful and still leave the plan open."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:inquiry-2026 logic:prescriptionKind logic:prescriptionKindResearchPlan ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a prescription organizing an open inquiry — themes to pursue, sources to consult, questions whose answerhood conditions are not yet settled."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for an inquiry with a fixed deliverable and settled acceptance criteria: that is productive work, and one of the other kinds describes it better."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:prescriptionKind and expect goal evaluation to stay open across successful occurrences."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:prescriptionKindCurriculum
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:PrescriptionKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "curriculum"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A prescription whose steps are learning activities and whose success criterion is competency attainment, typically decomposed by logic:DecompositionMethod into modules and gated by logic:PriorEnactmentRequirement prerequisites. Distinguished from a repeatable guide by carrying attainment criteria rather than only instructions."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Carries attainment criteria, which is what separates it from a repeatable guide. Prerequisites are modelled as prior-enactment requirements rather than as ordering, so a learner who already attained a module satisfies it without re-walking it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:stats-101 logic:prescriptionKind logic:prescriptionKindCurriculum ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the steps are learning activities and success is judged by competency attainment rather than by execution."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a walkthrough with no attainment criteria: a repeatable guide describes that honestly, and a curriculum would imply an assessment that does not exist."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:prescriptionKind and express prerequisites as prior-enactment requirements, so attainment rather than sequence gates progression."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:prescriptionKindControlPlan
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:PrescriptionKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "control plan"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A prescription whose primitive operations are commands to a physical process and whose logic:planGoal is characteristically a logic:MaintenanceGoal over a setpoint — so a successful occurrence never closes the goal. Distinguished from the other kinds by irreversible external effect and by requiring exclusive control of its actuators."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Marked out from the other kinds by irreversible effects on a physical process and by a maintenance goal over a setpoint, so a successful occurrence never closes the goal. Completion here is expected to recur indefinitely, and reading it as goal satisfaction is always wrong."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:reactor-hold logic:prescriptionKind logic:prescriptionKindControlPlan ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the primitive operations are commands to a physical process and the governing goal is a maintenance goal over a setpoint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a one-shot physical procedure with an achievement goal: that closes on success, and this kind asserts the opposite."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:prescriptionKind and pair the plan with a maintenance goal, so completion and goal satisfaction stay visibly separate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:prescriptionKindOrganizationalGuide
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:PrescriptionKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "organizational guide"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A prescription stating how an organization expects work to be done, governed by policy and typically requiring approval at named steps. Distinguished from a business process by being normative guidance rather than an executed instance flow."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Normative guidance rather than an executed instance flow. It states how work is expected to be done and is cited by processes; enactments belong to the processes that follow it, not to the guide itself."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:change-guide logic:prescriptionKind logic:prescriptionKindOrganizationalGuide ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a document stating how an organization expects work to be done, governed by policy and requiring approval at named steps."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for something whose occurrences are tracked and journalled: that is a business process, and calling it a guide loses the enactment record."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:prescriptionKind and let processes cite it as their governing norm rather than enacting the guide directly."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:prescriptionKindRepeatableGuide
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:PrescriptionKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "repeatable guide"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A prescription a human follows step by step and may walk through many times, each walkthrough its own logic:Enactment. Distinguished from a curriculum by carrying no attainment criteria, and from a lab protocol by not gating admissibility on exact execution."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Carries no attainment criteria and gates nothing on exactness, which places it between a curriculum and a lab protocol. Each walkthrough is its own enactment, so repetition is expected and never signals that something went wrong."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:oncall-runbook logic:prescriptionKind logic:prescriptionKindRepeatableGuide ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a prescription a human follows step by step and may walk through many times, each walkthrough its own enactment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it where competency attainment is assessed: that is a curriculum, and this kind would drop the criteria that make progression meaningful."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:prescriptionKind and expect a recurrence series of comparable occurrences rather than a single closing run."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

# =========================================================================== #
# === Enactment layer: the seven orthogonal lifecycle axes                 === #
# =========================================================================== #
#
# SEVEN axes, deliberately not collapsed into one status slot. FIVE closed value
# classes are minted here. The SIXTH is carried by the property
# logic:effectKnowledge over the EXISTING logic:InformationState - the epistemic
# reading of whether an external effect committed - and mints nothing. The
# SEVENTH reuses logic:GoalEvaluationStatus unchanged and mints nothing.
#
# Protocol position and commitment knowledge are the pair most often conflated:
# an attempt can sit at protocol position "receipted" while its commitment
# knowledge is InfoBoth (the provider reports committed, the local ledger does
# not). They are independent, and logic:LifecycleAxisNonConflationConstraint
# rejects a property carrying values drawn from two axes.

logic:EnactmentState
    a logic:AbstractIndividualType ,
        logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:QualityValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Enactment State"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Axis one of seven: the closed lifecycle position of a whole logic:Enactment. It says where the OCCURRENCE stands, never whether its goal is met (that is the goal-evaluation axis) and never whether an external effect committed (that is the commitment-knowledge axis). Its members are individuals, never subclasses; a completed enactment of a maintenance prescription leaves its goal open."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:adrReviewW12 logic:enactmentState logic:EnactmentRunning ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record where one occurrence stands as a whole."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid reading a completed state as goal satisfaction, and avoid using it for a single step."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach exactly one member; transitions are governed by the axis transition law rather than by free assignment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Says where the OCCURRENCE stands, never whether its goal is met and never whether an external effect committed. Those are the goal-evaluation and commitment-knowledge axes, and reading this one for either is the most common misreading of the kernel."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:EnactmentPending a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:EnactmentState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "enactment pending"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The enactment exists and has pinned its prescription version and input snapshot, but no step has begun. It is not a queue position and not the running state: the two facts that make the run comparable and reproducible are already fixed, and all that is outstanding is that no step has started."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Pending is not a queue position: the enactment has already pinned its prescription version and its input snapshot, which are the two facts that make it comparable and reproducible. What is outstanding is only that no step has begun."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:enactment-88 logic:enactmentState logic:EnactmentPending ; logic:enactsPrescriptionVersion ex:protocol-v3 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use between minting the enactment with its pinned version and snapshot and the start of its first step."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for an enactment that has not yet pinned a version: an unpinned run is not pending, it is not yet an enactment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Pin logic:enactsPrescriptionVersion and logic:enactmentInputSnapshot at creation, so the pending state already carries everything a later comparison needs."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:EnactmentRunning a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:EnactmentState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "enactment running"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "At least one step has begun and the enactment has neither paused nor reached a terminal position. Distinguished from paused, which is a deliberate suspension with a checkpoint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Running says at least one step has begun and none of the terminal or suspended conditions hold. It makes no claim about progress: an enactment all of whose steps are blocked is still running, and it is the frontier rather than this state that reveals that."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:enactment-88 logic:enactmentState logic:EnactmentRunning ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use once the first step has begun and until the enactment pauses at a checkpoint or reaches a terminal state."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid reading it as healthy: use the frontier to distinguish a running enactment that can act from one whose every entry is blocked."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:enactmentState and pair every operator view of it with logic:hasFrontierEntry, so running is always reported together with what can actually be done next."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:EnactmentPaused a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:EnactmentState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "enactment paused"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The enactment is suspended at a durable checkpoint and may be resumed as the SAME enactment. It is not cancelled, which is terminal, nor a step waiting on its own axis; and without a durable checkpoint there is nothing to resume from, so what is claimed as a pause is an abandonment waiting to be discovered."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Pausing is suspension of the SAME run, so it is only meaningful where a durable checkpoint exists. Without one there is nothing to resume from, and what looks like a pause is really an abandonment waiting to be discovered."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:enactment-88 logic:enactmentState logic:EnactmentPaused ; logic:checkpointOfEnactment ex:ckpt-12 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the run is deliberately suspended at a checkpoint that carries a folded identity, so a later resume can be gated on that identity."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a step that is merely waiting on an external signal: that is a step-level position, and raising it to the enactment hides which step is blocked."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Take the checkpoint first and link it with logic:checkpointOfEnactment, so the paused state always has a restorable point behind it rather than a promise of one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:EnactmentCompleted a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:EnactmentState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "enactment completed"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Every step reached a terminal position and the prescription was carried out. Terminal. It does NOT assert that the governing goal is satisfied: a successful occurrence of a maintenance prescription leaves the goal open by construction."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Completion is about the prescription, not about the goal. A maintenance prescription completes every occurrence and leaves its goal permanently open by construction, so reading completion as goal satisfaction is a category error the two axes exist to prevent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:enactment-88 logic:enactmentState logic:EnactmentCompleted ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when every step has reached a terminal position and the prescription was carried out as written."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it to assert that the governing goal is now satisfied: goal evaluation is a separate judgment with its own status vocabulary."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:enactmentState and evaluate the goal separately with logic:goalEvaluationStatus, so a completed occurrence of an unmet goal reads correctly."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:EnactmentCancelled a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:EnactmentState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "enactment cancelled"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The enactment was deliberately stopped before completing. Terminal. It records a decision to stop and does not assert that the world was left quiet: cancellation while an effect is still unresolved is a distinct and harder position than cancellation from a quiescent state, and the frontier labels the two separately."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Records a decision to stop, and says nothing about what the world was left holding. Cancelling while an effect is unresolved leaves obligations the frontier labels separately, so a cancelled enactment is not automatically a quiet one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:enactment-88 logic:enactmentState logic:EnactmentCancelled ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when an operator or policy deliberately stops the run before it completed, so the stop is attributable to a decision rather than to a fault."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when a step failed terminally with no continuation available: that is failure, and reporting it as cancellation invents a decider who never acted."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:enactmentState and read the frontier afterwards, since cancellation with in-flight effects carries its own label and its own remaining obligations."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:EnactmentFailed a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:EnactmentState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "enactment failed"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The enactment stopped because a step failed terminally and no continuation is available. Terminal. Distinguished from cancelled, which is a decision rather than a failure."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Failure is terminal only because no continuation was available. It is a statement about the options that remained, not about the severity of the defect, so a small error with no fallback fails the enactment while a large one with a compensation path does not."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:enactment-88 logic:enactmentState logic:EnactmentFailed ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a step failed terminally and the frontier offers no remaining continuation, compensation or skip."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it while any frontier entry is still actionable: an enactment with a live compensation path has not failed, it has more work to do."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Derive it from an empty actionable frontier plus a terminally failed step, so the state is a consequence of the frontier rather than a parallel assertion about it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:enactmentState
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Enactment ;
    rdfs:range logic:EnactmentState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has enactment state"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Enactment to its exactly-one lifecycle position on axis one. It is not the position of any step — a running enactment holds steps at many positions at once, and those live on axis two — and carrying a value from any other axis here is the conflation the non-conflation constraint rejects."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:weekThirteen logic:enactmentState logic:EnactmentRunning ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the enactment's position on its own lifecycle axis."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid carrying a step's state or an approval's state here; each axis has its own property precisely so they cannot blur."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Exactly one value from the enactment-state class."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Carrying a value from any other axis here is the conflation the non-conflation constraint rejects. The relation is functional, so an enactment has one lifecycle position and its steps' positions live on their own axis."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:StepState
    a logic:AbstractIndividualType ,
        logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:QualityValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Step State"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Axis two of seven: the closed lifecycle position of ONE step within an enactment, never the position of the enactment as a whole. Kept separate from the enactment axis because a running enactment contains steps in many different positions at once, and collapsing the two loses exactly the information a next-action frontier is derived from."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrStep logic:stepState logic:StepWaiting ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record where a single prescribed step stands."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid promoting one step's state to the enactment, and avoid using it to express approval or effect position."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach exactly one member per step per enactment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Kept separate from the enactment axis because a running enactment contains steps in many different positions at once. Collapsing the two loses exactly the information a next-action frontier is built from."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:StepPending a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:StepState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "step pending"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The step is part of the enactment but its preconditions have not been evaluated as met. Distinguished from ready, where they have."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Preconditions have not been evaluated as met, which is not the same as having been evaluated and found false. Pending is therefore an absence of judgment, and a blocked-precondition frontier label is a judgment that was made."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:step-4 logic:stepState logic:StepPending ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a step that belongs to the enactment but whose preconditions have not yet been evaluated."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a step whose preconditions were evaluated and found false: that step is blocked, and pending would hide a known obstruction."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:stepState at enactment start, and let precondition evaluation move it to ready or produce a blocked frontier entry."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:StepReady a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:StepState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "step ready"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Preconditions are met and the step could begin. Readiness is a step-state claim only and is never sufficient on its own for an external effect to leave the system; whether the step may ALSO dispatch depends on the independent approval and effect axes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A step-state claim only. Whether the step may ALSO dispatch depends on the independent approval and effect axes, so readiness is necessary and never sufficient for an external effect to leave the system."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:step-4 logic:stepState logic:StepReady ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the step's preconditions have been evaluated and hold, so the step could begin as far as the state is concerned."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid treating it as a dispatch licence: the frontier label, not the step state, says whether an effect may leave unattended."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:stepState and let the frontier combine it with the approval and effect axes before anything is dispatched."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:StepRunning a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:StepState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "step running"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The step has begun and has not reached a terminal position. Distinguished from waiting, in which it has begun but is blocked on an external signal, time or input."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Begun and not terminal. It is distinguished from waiting, which has also begun but is blocked on something external, and the distinction matters because only one of the two can make progress unaided."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:step-4 logic:stepState logic:StepRunning ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use once the step has begun and while it is making progress under its own power."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a step blocked on an external signal: waiting is what lets an operator see that nothing will change until something arrives."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:stepState at the start of work and move it to waiting the moment the step blocks on something outside the enactment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:StepWaiting a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:StepState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "step waiting"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The step is blocked pending an event, a time, or an input that has not arrived. Load-bearing: a waiting step is NOT a failed step, and the distinction is what lets a run be resumed rather than restarted."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A waiting step is not a failed step, and that distinction is what lets a run be resumed rather than restarted. It covers only arrivals from outside the enactment: an internal dependency is sequencing, and modelling it as waiting would hide the step that will satisfy it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:step-4 logic:stepState logic:StepWaiting ; logic:awaitsSignal ex:signal-lab-result ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the step has begun and is blocked pending an event, a time or an input that must arrive from outside."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a step that has not begun at all: pending covers that, and waiting implies work already under way."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:stepState and name the awaited signal, so a resume can be triggered by the arrival rather than by a timer."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:StepCompleted a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:StepState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "step completed"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The step carried out its prescribed work and reached a terminal position. Terminal. It asserts nothing about whether an external effect the step dispatched actually committed: a completed step sitting over an effect not yet known to have committed is an ordinary combination rather than a contradiction."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Asserts that the step's own work finished, and nothing about whether an external effect it dispatched actually committed. Those are separate axes, so a completed step over an undetermined effect is an ordinary and expected combination."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:step-4 logic:stepState logic:StepCompleted ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the step carried out its prescribed work and reached a terminal position."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid reading it as a guarantee about the external world: the effect protocol position is what says whether anything committed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:stepState, and report it alongside the effect position so a reader never mistakes a finished step for a settled effect."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:StepSkipped a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:StepState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "step skipped"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The step was deliberately not executed, and a typed reason records why. Terminal. Distinguished from cancelled, which stops a step already begun, and from pending, which has simply not started."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Terminal and deliberate, with a typed reason recording why. It differs from cancelled, which stops a step already begun, and from pending, which has simply not started; the typed reason is what keeps a skip from being read as an oversight."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:step-4 logic:stepState logic:StepSkipped ; logic:skipReason logic:SkipNotApplicable ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the step was deliberately not executed and one of the typed skip reasons applies."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it without a typed reason: an unexplained skip is indistinguishable from a step nobody noticed, which is the failure mode the typing exists to prevent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:stepState together with logic:skipReason, so skips can be counted by cause across a recurrence series."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:StepCancelled a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:StepState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "step cancelled"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A step that had begun was deliberately stopped. Terminal. It stops the step and not the effects the step already dispatched: commitment knowledge for an in-flight effect is untouched by the cancellation and must still be resolved, so a cancelled step can leave the enactment with more work rather than less."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Stops the step, never the effects it already dispatched. Commitment knowledge for an in-flight effect is untouched by the cancellation and still has to be resolved, which is why a cancelled step can leave the enactment with more work rather than less."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:step-4 logic:stepState logic:StepCancelled ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a step that had already begun is deliberately stopped before reaching its own terminal position."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a step that never started: that is a skip, and cancelling implies work was under way that had to be interrupted."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:stepState and check the effect protocol positions afterwards, since an in-flight effect survives the cancellation and still owes reconciliation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:StepFailed a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:StepState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "step failed"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The step attempted its work and terminally failed. Terminal. Distinguished from waiting, which is an unresolved but non-failing position, and from an unknown external outcome, which is an epistemic position rather than a failure."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A failure of the step's own work, distinguished both from waiting, which is unresolved but not failing, and from an unknown external outcome, which is epistemic. Collapsing any of the three would let an open question be closed as a defeat."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:step-4 logic:stepState logic:StepFailed ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the step attempted its work and terminally failed with no continuation available to it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for an external effect whose commitment is merely unknown: that is a knowledge gap closed by reconciliation, not a failure."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:stepState only once the frontier offers the step no remaining move, so failure follows from exhausted options rather than from an error message."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:stepState
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:StepState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has step state"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a step occurrence within an enactment to its exactly-one position on axis two. Domain a step occurrence (stated in prose). It is a position on that one axis and nothing more: the approval and effect axes are independent, so this value cannot by itself say whether the step may dispatch."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Relates a step occurrence within an enactment to its exactly-one position on the step axis. It is a position on one axis only: the approval and effect axes are independent, so this value never by itself says whether the step may dispatch."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:step-4 logic:stepState logic:StepRunning ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use on each step occurrence to state its single position on the step lifecycle axis."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it on the plan step rather than the occurrence: a plan step is a specification and has no lifecycle position of its own."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it on the occurrence and read dispatch readiness from the frontier, which combines this axis with approval and effect."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:ApprovalState
    a logic:AbstractIndividualType ,
        logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:QualityValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Approval State"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Axis three of seven: the closed lifecycle of an authorization treated as a social commitment rather than a boolean flag. Modelling approval as a commitment with a DETACHMENT lifecycle is what makes revocation, delegation, escalation and expiry first-class instead of absent, and what turns exact-binding into a detachment condition rather than a comparison."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrApproval logic:approvalState logic:ApprovalDetached ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record where an authorization stands in its own lifecycle, independently of whether the work it authorizes has begun."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid collapsing to approved/not-approved, and avoid reading a detached commitment as a completed one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach exactly one member; a commitment moves null to created to detached, then to exactly one of the four terminal positions."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Modelling approval as a commitment with a detachment lifecycle is what makes revocation, delegation, escalation and expiry first-class instead of side effects of flipping a flag. A boolean can only be true or false; a commitment can lapse, be released, or be violated."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ApprovalNull a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:ApprovalState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "approval null"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "No commitment exists. The absence of an authorization, made explicit so that 'unapproved' is a recorded position rather than missing data."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The explicit absence of a commitment, deliberately materialized so that unapproved is a recorded position rather than a missing triple. It is not a denial: a denial is a violated or cancelled commitment that once existed, whereas null says no one ever undertook anything here."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:step-4 logic:approvalState logic:ApprovalNull ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a step or intent that requires no authorization at all, so that the reader sees a decided absence instead of wondering whether the commitment was merely not modelled."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it to stand in for an authorization that exists but is not yet detached: that is logic:ApprovalCreated, and flattening the two hides an approval that is about to become live."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:approvalState on the record whose approval axis is genuinely vacuous, so the frontier can label the step ready-authorized without inventing a commitment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ApprovalCreated a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:ApprovalState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "approval created"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A commitment exists but its antecedent has not yet been met, so it authorizes nothing yet. Distinguished from detached, at which point it does."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The position in which the commitment is real but inert. It is not a queue or a draft: a created commitment is fully authored and binding on its issuer, and only the antecedent is outstanding, so nothing about it may be edited to make detachment easier."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:approval-31 logic:approvalState logic:ApprovalCreated ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a commitment that has been issued and whose triggering condition has not yet been observed, so that a reader can distinguish an authorization that exists from one that is currently licensing an act."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it once the antecedent has been met: leaving a detached commitment marked created hides live authority, which is worse than showing none at all."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:approvalState at issue time, and let the antecedent observation drive the move to logic:ApprovalDetached rather than editing the state by hand at dispatch."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ApprovalDetached a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:ApprovalState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "approval detached"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The commitment's antecedent is met and the authorization is live for the exact intent it names. Detachment is the moment an approval starts authorizing; an intent whose digest differs from the one named never detaches this commitment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Authorizes exactly the one intent whose digest the commitment names, and only inside the validity window. It says nothing about whether the act is a good idea, whether resources exist, or whether preconditions hold — those are separate frontier axes, so a detached approval alone never makes a step ready."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:approval-31 logic:approvalState logic:ApprovalDetached ; logic:approvalIntentDigest \"b3:7f2c\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use at the moment the commitment's antecedent is observed to hold, which is the only moment at which a dispatch may cite the commitment as its licence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for an intent whose digest differs from the one named, however small the revision: a re-digested intent is a different intent and needs its own commitment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:approvalState and check it against the four binding faults before dispatch, so a mismatch of run, digest, window or operator-policy is reported as the specific fault rather than as a bare refusal."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ApprovalDischarged a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:ApprovalState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "approval discharged"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The authorized act occurred and the commitment is satisfied. Terminal. It does not authorize a second act: a repeat needs its own commitment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Records that the authorization was consumed, not that the act succeeded. An act that was authorized, attempted and failed still discharges the commitment: success and failure of the effect live on the effect protocol axis, and conflating them would let a failed act silently retain its licence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:approval-31 logic:approvalState logic:ApprovalDischarged ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use once the authorized act has been performed, so that the commitment stops licensing anything and a repeat of the act is visibly unauthorized."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid reusing a discharged commitment as the licence for a retry: a retry is a fresh act and needs a fresh commitment, which is precisely the borrowed-licence pattern the kernel refuses."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:approvalState from the receipt that records the authorized act, so the discharge is derived from evidence rather than asserted alongside it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ApprovalExpired a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:ApprovalState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "approval expired"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The validity window closed before the authorized act occurred. Terminal. Expiry is evaluated against an attributed clock, so this is not a claim about wall-clock reality but about the time source the enactment named, and a dispatch whose expiry check names no clock source is refused rather than assumed fresh."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A clock-driven terminal position: the commitment lapses whether or not anyone noticed. Expiry is judged against an attributed clock, so it is not a claim about wall-clock reality but about the time source the enactment named, and an unattributed check is refused rather than assumed fresh."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:approval-31 logic:approvalState logic:ApprovalExpired ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the validity window closed with the authorized act still unperformed, so that a later dispatch cannot cite a stale commitment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when a party ended the commitment deliberately: cancellation and release are decisions and expiry is the absence of one, and reporting a decision as a lapse loses the accountable actor."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Derive it from the commitment window and the attributed clock, and report the corresponding logic:FaultExpiredWindow on any dispatch that tried to use it, so the refusal names its reason."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ApprovalViolated a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:ApprovalState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "approval violated"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The authorized act was not performed although the commitment was live and its window open, or an act was performed outside what it authorized. Terminal, and a finding rather than a routine outcome."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A finding, not a routine outcome: it says an obligation was live and open and was still not met, or that an act exceeded what was authorized. It does not assign blame or damages — the governing norm does that — and it is never inferred merely from a step failing, since a failed attempt discharges rather than violates."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:approval-31 logic:approvalState logic:ApprovalViolated ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a live commitment lapsed unmet inside its own open window, or when an act was performed beyond the intent the commitment named."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when the window had already closed, which is expiry: a commitment cannot be violated after it stopped obliging anyone."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Derive it from the commitment window, the attributed clock and the intent digest, and expect it to be read as a finding by the review projection rather than as a step outcome."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ApprovalCancelled a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:ApprovalState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "approval cancelled"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The authorizing party withdrew the commitment before it was discharged. Terminal. It is not a release, in which the party who BENEFITED from the commitment gave it up, and it is not a judgment that the authorized act was wrong: the kernel records who ended the commitment and when, never whether ending it was correct."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A withdrawal position, not a judgment that the act was wrong. The kernel records who ended the commitment and when, never whether ending it was correct; a cancellation that ought not to have happened is still a cancellation and is challenged through the governing norm, not by retyping the state."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:approval-31 logic:approvalState logic:ApprovalCancelled ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the authorizing party ends a commitment it issued, before the authorized act happened, so the intent it covered loses its licence from that moment on."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when the commitment simply ran out of time — that is expiry — and when the beneficiary rather than the issuer gave it up, which is release."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it through logic:approvalState on the commitment and leave the dispatch intent it covered intact: the intent stays recorded and unauthorized, which is what lets a later audit see that something was proposed and refused rather than never proposed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ApprovalReleased a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:ApprovalState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "approval released"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The party the commitment was owed to gave it up, so it lapses without being discharged or violated. Terminal. It is not a cancellation, which is withdrawal by the authorizing party, and it leaves no violation behind: a released commitment is evidence that the authorized act was not needed, not that it was refused or that it failed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Ends the commitment from the beneficiary's side. It leaves no violation and no discharge, so a released commitment is evidence that the authorized act was not needed, not that it was refused or that it failed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:approval-31 logic:approvalState logic:ApprovalReleased ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the party owed the authorized act gives it up, for instance because the underlying need disappeared while the commitment was still live."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a withdrawal by the issuer, which is cancellation: the two differ in who acted, and that is exactly what an accountability trail must retain."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:approvalState and keep the intent it covered in the journal, so the record shows an authorized act that was deliberately not taken."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:approvalState
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:ApprovalState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has approval state"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates an authorization record to its exactly-one position on axis three. Domain an authorization record (stated in prose). It is not itself a permission check: a detached commitment is a necessary input to one, and the frontier is what actually performs it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:approval logic:approvalState logic:ApprovalDetached ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the approval's position on its own lifecycle axis."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid collapsing created and detached: a commitment that exists is not yet one that has attached to a specific intent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "One value per approval; revocation, expiry and discharge are distinct terminal states."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Relates an authorization record to its exactly-one position on the approval axis, and to nothing else. It is not a permission check: a detached commitment is a necessary input to one, and the frontier is what performs it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:EffectProtocolPosition
    a logic:AbstractIndividualType ,
        logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:QualityValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Effect Protocol Position"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Axis four of seven: WHERE an external-effect exchange stands in its protocol - intended, attempted, receipted, reconciling, reconciled, compensating, compensated, abandoned. It is a position in a labelled transition system and not a claim about whether the effect actually committed; that is axis five. The pair is the most consequential non-conflation in the kernel: an exchange can be receipted while its commitment knowledge is contradictory."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:payDispatch logic:effectProtocolPosition logic:EffectReconciling ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record how far an external exchange has proceeded through its protocol."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid reading a position as evidence that the effect committed, and avoid using it to express epistemic state."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach exactly one member and carry the epistemic reading separately on logic:effectKnowledge."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A position in a labelled transition system, and it says nothing about whether the effect committed. Commitment knowledge is a separate axis, so an effect can be receipted and still not known to have committed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:EffectIntended a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:EffectProtocolPosition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "effect intended"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A dispatch intent is durably recorded but no attempt has been made. It records that a dispatch was decided on, not that anything left the process, so it never implies external change. That is the position which makes a crash at the boundary analyzable: an intent with no attempt is recoverable without ambiguity."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The position that makes a crash at the boundary analyzable: an intent with no attempt is unambiguously recoverable. It records that a dispatch was decided on, not that anything left the process, so it never implies external change."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:effect-19 logic:effectProtocolPosition logic:EffectIntended ; logic:intentDigest ex:digest-3a ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use after the dispatch intent is durably recorded and before any call is made, so a recovery sweep can tell decided-but-not-sent from possibly-sent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid leaving a record here once a call has been made: the whole value of this position is that it excludes external change, and an attempted call breaks that."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record the intent with its digest before the call, and let the transition to attempted be written by the caller at the boundary, not inferred afterwards."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:EffectAttempted a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:EffectProtocolPosition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "effect attempted"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "An attempt was made against the provider but no authoritative receipt has been durably recorded. This is the position at which commitment knowledge is characteristically undetermined, and reaching it does not license a retry: the retry licence comes from the idempotency contract, never from the position itself."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Marks the window in which the external world may already have changed while the local record does not yet know it. Nothing about this position licenses a retry on its own; the retry licence comes from the idempotency contract, and this position is precisely where that distinction bites."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:effect-19 logic:effectProtocolPosition logic:EffectAttempted ; logic:attemptOfIntent ex:intent-77 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use from the moment a call leaves the boundary until an authoritative receipt is durably recorded, which is the interval a crash makes ambiguous."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it once any authoritative determination exists, whether receipt or probe verdict: leaving the record here understates what is known."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record the attempt against its intent with logic:attemptOfIntent before the call, so an interrupted enactment can find the attempt and reconcile rather than guess."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:EffectReceipted a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:EffectProtocolPosition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "effect receipted"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "An authoritative receipt was durably recorded. Being receipted does NOT by itself settle commitment knowledge: a receipt that contradicts the local ledger moves knowledge to the contradictory value rather than to supported."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Receipted is about evidence, not about truth. A receipt that contradicts the local ledger moves commitment knowledge to the contradictory value rather than to supported, so this position never licenses treating the effect as settled."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:effect-19 logic:effectProtocolPosition logic:EffectReceipted ; logic:receiptOfAttempt ex:attempt-5 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use once an authoritative receipt has been durably recorded against the attempt it answers."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid recording a receipt that names no attempt: a receipt without an attempt is a claim about an exchange that was never begun, and the kernel rejects it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the receipt to its attempt with logic:receiptOfAttempt, and read commitment knowledge from the combination of receipt and ledger rather than from the receipt alone."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:EffectReconciling a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:EffectProtocolPosition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "effect reconciling"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A probe is in flight to establish whether the effect committed. Reconciliation is the only knowledge-increasing move available from an undetermined position; retrying the effect is not."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The only knowledge-increasing move available from an undetermined position. Retrying is not: a retry changes the world again without telling you what the first attempt did, which is why this position and a retry licence are governed separately."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:effect-19 logic:effectProtocolPosition logic:EffectReconciling ; logic:probesAttempt ex:attempt-5 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use while a probe is in flight to establish whether an attempted effect committed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it as a substitute for a retry decision: reconciling answers what happened, and only the idempotency contract answers whether the act may be repeated."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Point the probe at the attempt with logic:probesAttempt so the verdict, when it arrives, attaches to the exchange it actually investigated."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:EffectReconciled a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:EffectProtocolPosition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "effect reconciled"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A probe returned an authoritative verdict and commitment knowledge advanced. Distinguished from receipted, which is the provider's own report at dispatch time rather than an after-the-fact determination."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The verdict came from an after-the-fact probe rather than from the provider's report at dispatch time. That difference is what makes it authoritative about the current external state, and it is why reconciliation and receipting are separate positions rather than one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:effect-19 logic:effectProtocolPosition logic:EffectReconciled ; logic:reconciliationVerdict logic:ReconciledCommitted ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use once a probe has returned an authoritative verdict and commitment knowledge has moved off the undetermined value."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a probe that came back still unknown: that verdict exists, and collapsing it into reconciled would claim knowledge the probe did not deliver."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record the verdict with logic:reconciliationVerdict so the three outcomes committed, not committed and still unknown remain distinguishable downstream."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:EffectCompensating a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:EffectProtocolPosition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "effect compensating"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A compensating action is in flight against a forward effect known to have committed. Compensation is a new action with its own preconditions and its own possible failure, not an erasure of the forward effect."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A compensation in flight is an ordinary effect with its own preconditions, its own approval question and its own possible failure. Treating it as a guaranteed rollback is the mistake this position exists to prevent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:effect-19 logic:effectProtocolPosition logic:EffectCompensating ; logic:compensatesEffect ex:effect-18 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use while a counteracting action is running against a forward effect already known to have committed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it against an effect whose commitment is still unknown: compensating an effect that may never have happened can create the very change it was meant to reverse."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Point at the forward effect with logic:compensatesEffect, and let the compensation carry its own approval and receipt rather than inheriting the forward action's."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:EffectCompensated a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:EffectProtocolPosition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "effect compensated"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A compensating action completed and produced its own receipt. The forward effect remains recorded as having committed; the history is corrected forward rather than rewritten."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The forward effect stays recorded as having committed. This position asserts that a counteracting action completed, not that the original was undone: history is corrected forward, so a query for what happened must still see both actions."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:effect-19 logic:effectProtocolPosition logic:EffectCompensated ; logic:compensationReceipt ex:receipt-20b ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a compensating action has completed and produced its own receipt, so the pair of forward effect and compensation is fully evidenced."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it without a compensation receipt: an outcome asserted with no receipt behind it is the compensation-outcome-without-receipt error the kernel rejects."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach the compensation receipt with logic:compensationReceipt and keep it typed distinctly from the forward receipt it counteracts, since typing it as its forward receipt is itself a rejected pattern."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:EffectAbandoned a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:EffectProtocolPosition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "effect abandoned"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The exchange was given up without an authoritative determination, because no reconciliation semantics remain available. Terminal and deliberately uncomfortable: it records an unresolved external state rather than pretending to a verdict."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The honest terminal position for an exchange whose truth cannot be established. It is not a failure verdict: abandoning records that the external world may or may not have changed, and any downstream step that treats it as a clean failure is reading it wrongly."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:effect-19 logic:effectProtocolPosition logic:EffectAbandoned ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when every reconciliation avenue is exhausted or expired and no authoritative verdict is reachable, so the enactment stops pretending it will learn more."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it while a probe is still possible: abandoning early destroys the one route by which commitment knowledge could still increase."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:effectProtocolPosition and leave the unknown commitment knowledge in place, so the review projection surfaces an unresolved external state instead of a resolved one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:effectProtocolPosition
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:EffectProtocolPosition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has effect protocol position"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates an external-effect exchange record to its exactly-one position on axis four. Domain an effect exchange record (stated in prose). It carries a protocol position and never a commitment-knowledge value: a record carrying one here is the axis conflation the non-conflation constraint rejects."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeAttempt logic:effectProtocolPosition logic:EffectAttempted ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for where an effect sits in the dispatch protocol."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid conflating it with commitment knowledge: an effect can be at position 'receipted' while knowledge is contradictory."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "One position per effect record; the two axes move independently by design."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Relates an exchange record to its exactly-one position on the protocol axis, and to nothing about commitment. A record carrying a knowledge value here is the axis conflation the non-conflation constraint rejects."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:effectKnowledge
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:InformationState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "effect knowledge"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Axis five of seven, carried by a PROPERTY rather than a new value class: what is KNOWN about whether an external effect committed, read off the existing logic:InformationState rather than a second status enum. It admits logic:InfoSupported, logic:InfoOpposed, logic:InfoBoth, logic:InfoUndetermined and logic:InfoNotEvaluated. logic:InfoNeither is BANNED here and the ban is enforced by constraint: that value is the conclusive closed-world reading, and external commitment is never closed-world, so admitting it would let a consumer read 'conclusively no effect' from a provider that was merely unreachable. Domain an effect exchange record (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeAttempt logic:effectKnowledge logic:InfoUndetermined ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for what is KNOWN about an external effect, kept separate from where it sits in the protocol."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid asserting conclusive-absence for an external effect: the provider's state is not in our records, so 'conclusively no effect' is a claim we cannot make."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Move only upward in the knowledge order as evidence arrives; a contradiction routes to adjudication rather than overwriting."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Carried by a PROPERTY rather than a new value class, reading off the existing information-state vocabulary rather than a second status enum. Minting a parallel enum here would put two orderings on the same question and let them disagree."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:RecurrenceState
    a logic:AbstractIndividualType ,
        logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:QualityValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Recurrence State"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Axis seven of seven as counted in the charter, and the last minted value class: the position of a recurring prescription's SCHEDULE, distinct from the position of any occurrence it generates. A suspended schedule with a running occurrence is an ordinary state, which is why the two cannot share a slot. Kept distinct from calendar-level task status and from occurrence-exception typing, which classify the scheduled event rather than the generating schedule."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:weeklyAdrSchedule logic:recurrenceState logic:RecurrenceActive ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record whether a recurring prescription is still generating occurrences."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid conflating with the state of a generated occurrence, and avoid using it to record that one occurrence was skipped."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach exactly one member to the schedule; a skipped occurrence is recorded on the occurrence, not by suspending the schedule."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The position of a recurring prescription's SCHEDULE, distinct from the position of any occurrence it generates. A suspended schedule with a running occurrence is an ordinary state, and a vocabulary that could not express it would force a false report."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:RecurrenceScheduled a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:RecurrenceState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "recurrence scheduled"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The recurrence is defined and will generate occurrences, but none has been generated yet. It is not yet active, which requires at least one generated occurrence; a scheduled recurrence therefore has nothing to compare against and no history to read."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Defined but not yet productive. The distinction from active is exactly whether any occurrence exists, which matters because a scheduled recurrence with no occurrences has nothing to compare against and no history to read."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:weekly-review logic:recurrenceState logic:RecurrenceScheduled ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use from the moment the recurrence is defined until it generates its first occurrence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it once an occurrence exists, even a skipped one: the recurrence has begun acting, and scheduled would understate what has already happened."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:recurrenceState at definition time and let the first generation move it to active."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:RecurrenceActive a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:RecurrenceState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "recurrence active"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The recurrence is generating occurrences on schedule. It describes the generator and not the work the generator schedules: an active recurrence can be producing occurrences that are all failing, so it says nothing about the state of any particular occurrence it generated."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Describes the generator, never a generated occurrence. An active recurrence can be producing occurrences that are all failing, so reading health off this state confuses the schedule with the work it schedules."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:weekly-review logic:recurrenceState logic:RecurrenceActive ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when at least one occurrence has been generated and the recurrence continues to generate on schedule."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it to report that the latest occurrence is going well: the occurrence carries its own enactment state, and this one would mask it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:recurrenceState on the recurrence, and read occurrence health from the enactments it generated rather than from here."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:RecurrenceSkipped a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:RecurrenceState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "recurrence skipped"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A scheduled generation point passed without producing an occurrence, and the omission is recorded rather than silent. Distinguished from suspended, which stops generation until resumed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Concerns a single generation point, not the recurrence as a whole. Recording the omission is the point: a silently missed occurrence is indistinguishable from a scheduler that never ran, and only one of those is a defect."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:weekly-review logic:recurrenceState logic:RecurrenceSkipped ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a scheduled generation point passed without producing an occurrence and the omission is to be recorded."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when generation was deliberately halted across many points: that is suspension, and per-point skips would obscure the standing decision."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record the skip against the generation point so the series shows a gap with a reason rather than an unexplained absence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:RecurrenceSuspended a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:RecurrenceState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "recurrence suspended"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Generation is deliberately paused and may resume. It is not ended, which is terminal, nor a skip, which concerns a single generation point; and it governs the generator only, so occurrences already running are unaffected."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A standing decision to stop generating, which distinguishes it from a skip at one point and from an ending that is terminal. Occurrences already running are unaffected: suspension governs the generator, not the work in flight."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:weekly-review logic:recurrenceState logic:RecurrenceSuspended ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when generation is deliberately paused with the expectation that it may resume."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when no resumption is contemplated: ended is the honest terminal value, and suspension would leave a reader waiting for a restart that will not come."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:recurrenceState and leave in-flight occurrences to finish under their own enactment states."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:RecurrenceEnded a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:RecurrenceState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "recurrence ended"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The recurrence will generate no further occurrences. Terminal for the generator only: it does not close the enactments it already generated, which keep their own states and remain readable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Terminal for the generator only. Occurrences already generated keep their own states and remain readable, so ending a recurrence never closes the enactments it produced."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:weekly-review logic:recurrenceState logic:RecurrenceEnded ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the recurrence will generate no further occurrences at all."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a pause that may be lifted: suspension exists for that, and ending discards the expectation of resumption."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it with logic:recurrenceState and leave the generated occurrences untouched, so historical runs stay queryable in their own right."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:recurrenceState
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:RecurrenceState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has recurrence state"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a recurring prescription's schedule to its exactly-one position on the recurrence axis, never an occurrence to its own state. Domain a schedule record (stated in prose). A schedule and the enactments it generates are different subjects, and reading this axis for the other is what makes an active recurrence look healthy."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Relates a schedule record to its position on the recurrence axis, never an occurrence to its own state. A schedule and the enactments it generates are different subjects, and reading one axis for the other is what makes an active recurrence look healthy."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:weekly-review logic:recurrenceState logic:RecurrenceActive ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use on a recurring prescription's schedule record to state its exactly-one position on the recurrence axis."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid putting it on an enactment: an occurrence carries an enactment state, and this axis governs the generator instead."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Keep the schedule and its occurrences as separate subjects, so a review can report generator health and occurrence health independently."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:knowledgePrecedes
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:InformationState ;
    rdfs:range logic:InformationState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "knowledge precedes"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The explicit knowledge order the kernel's monotonicity law ranges over: a is below b when b settles strictly more than a does. Authored explicitly because the runtime enumeration order of logic:InformationState is DECLARATION order, not the knowledge order, and reading one for the other inverts the lattice. The admitted chain is logic:InfoNotEvaluated below logic:InfoUndetermined below each of logic:InfoSupported and logic:InfoOpposed, both below logic:InfoBoth. A reconciliation may only move effect knowledge upward along it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Authored explicitly because the runtime enumeration order of the information states is DECLARATION order, not the knowledge order. Reading one for the other inverts the monotonicity law, so this relation exists to keep the ordering out of accidental control of the file layout."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "logic:EffectAttempted logic:knowledgePrecedes logic:EffectReconciled ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to state that one information state settles strictly less than another, so the monotonicity law has an order to range over."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid relying on declaration order in its place: that order is an artifact of the file, and the law would silently follow whatever an editor last did."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Author the full order explicitly between the information states, and let the monotonicity check read it rather than an enumeration."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:AxisTransitionLaw
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Axis Transition Law"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The authored, machine-checkable statement of which transitions one lifecycle axis admits, carried as a logic:Formula rather than as prose. Making the admitted relation data is what lets deadlock-freedom and reachability be PROVED over an axis instead of asserted about it. Typed APART from logic:Constraint, which rejects an ill-formed record: a transition law describes the legal moves, and a constraint enforces one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:enactmentStateLaw a logic:AxisTransitionLaw ; logic:lawGovernsAxis logic:EnactmentState ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to declare the admitted transitions of a closed lifecycle axis."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid stating the transitions only in prose, and avoid one law spanning two axes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind logic:lawGovernsAxis and carry the admitted relation as a logic:Formula through logic:lawAdmits."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Carrying the admitted relation as data is what lets deadlock-freedom and reachability be PROVED over an axis instead of reviewed by eye. A prose statement of the same relation cannot be re-evaluated when a value is added."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:lawGovernsAxis
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:AxisTransitionLaw ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "law governs axis"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:AxisTransitionLaw to the closed value class whose transitions it governs. Range a lifecycle axis value class (stated in prose). Functional: one law per axis, so the admitted relation is unambiguous. Two laws over one axis cannot be reconciled — a transition admitted by one and refused by the other leaves no principled way to decide."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional by design: one law per axis, so the admitted relation is unambiguous. Two laws over one axis would leave a transition admitted by one and refused by the other with no principled way to decide."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:step-law logic:lawGovernsAxis logic:StepState ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to declare which closed value class an axis transition law governs."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid authoring a second law for an axis to cover a special case: fold the case into the one law's formula so the admitted relation stays single-valued."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the axis value class, and let the certificates range over exactly the values that class closes over."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:lawAdmits
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:AxisTransitionLaw ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "law admits"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:AxisTransitionLaw to the logic:Formula stating its admitted transition relation. Carrying the relation as a formula rather than as a table is what makes the liveness certificate derivable rather than hand-maintained."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Carries the admitted transition relation as a formula rather than as a table, which is what makes the liveness certificate derivable instead of hand-maintained. A table would have to be re-checked by a human whenever an axis value is added; a formula is re-evaluated."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:step-law logic:lawAdmits ex:step-transition-formula ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind an axis transition law to the formula stating which moves it admits."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid enumerating pairs in its place: an enumeration cannot be re-evaluated when a value is added, and the certificate would quietly go stale."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Author the relation as a formula and let the deadlock and reachability certificates be derived from it rather than asserted beside it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:LivenessCertificate
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Liveness Certificate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The record that the authored transition laws were checked for PROGRESS, not merely for safety: that every non-terminal axis tuple has at least one enabled next move (deadlock-freedom), that no state is unreachable and every state can reach a terminal one (reachability), and that no dispatch intent can sit past its deadline with no attempt, receipt or reconciliation (no orphan intent). Safety constraints alone prove that bad transitions are rejected; only this proves the system cannot get stuck, which is the dominant real failure of long-running work."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:kernelLiveness a logic:LivenessCertificate ; logic:certifiesDeadlockFreedom true ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record that the axis transition relations were proved live, with the property that was checked named."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid asserting liveness without a check, and avoid treating a safety constraint set as evidence of progress."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Emit one per checked axis set, binding the three property flags; a false flag names the offending state."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Records that the laws were checked for PROGRESS and not merely for safety. A safety-only check passes on an axis where nothing can ever move, which is exactly the failure a deadlock-freedom and reachability certificate is meant to catch."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:certifiesDeadlockFreedom
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:LivenessCertificate ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "certifies deadlock freedom"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "True when every non-terminal tuple of axis positions has at least one enabled next move under the authored transition laws. False names a stuck tuple rather than merely reporting failure."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A property of the authored transition laws, not of any particular run. False does not merely report a failure: it names the stuck tuple of axis positions, so the certificate is diagnostic rather than a bare verdict."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:cert-1 logic:certifiesDeadlockFreedom true ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record whether every non-terminal tuple of axis positions has at least one enabled next move under the authored laws."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid asserting it true by hand: the value is meant to be derived from the transition laws, and a hand-written true claims a check nobody ran."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Derive it from the laws and publish the stuck tuple alongside a false value, so an author can see which combination has no exit."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:certifiesReachability
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:LivenessCertificate ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "certifies reachability"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "True when no axis value is unreachable from an initial position and every value can reach a terminal one. An unreachable value is dead vocabulary; a value that cannot reach a terminal is a trap."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Two defects at once: an unreachable value is dead vocabulary, and a value that cannot reach a terminal is a trap. Both are properties of the authored axis rather than of data, so the certificate is about the model and not about any enactment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:cert-1 logic:certifiesReachability true ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record whether every axis value is reachable from an initial position and can reach a terminal one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid reading a false value as a data problem: it says the vocabulary itself has a dead or trapping value, which is fixed by editing the axis."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Derive it from the transition laws whenever an axis value is added, since a new value is exactly what most often introduces a trap."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:certifiesNoOrphanIntent
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:LivenessCertificate ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "certifies no orphan intent"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "True when no dispatch intent can remain past its deadline with no attempt, no receipt and no reconciliation. An orphan intent is the silent form of an unresolved external effect and is the case a purely per-record safety check does not catch."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Catches the case a per-record safety check cannot: an intent that simply sits past its deadline with no attempt, no receipt and no reconciliation. Every individual record is well formed there, and only the absence across records is the defect."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:cert-1 logic:certifiesNoOrphanIntent true ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record whether no dispatch intent can outlive its deadline with nothing attached to it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid treating a clean per-record validation as evidence for it: orphaning is a relation between records, and no single record shows it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Derive it over the whole journal and read a false value as a request for a sweep rather than for a schema fix."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# =========================================================================== #
# === Enactment layer: refinement — decomposition methods, episodes,       === #
# === closed candidate sets, pinned subgraphs, and capability gaps         === #
# =========================================================================== #
#
# Turning an abstract step into something executable is HTN decomposition, and
# the load-bearing part is the METHOD: a named, versioned, applicability-gated
# schema for how a task decomposes. Without methods every refinement is a
# one-off, so a continuing cluster RECORDS its history without ever USING it -
# which guts the point of continuity. A pinned subgraph is one instantiation of
# a method, never a substitute for having one.

logic:DecompositionMethod
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Decomposition Method"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A named, versioned, content-addressed schema for decomposing one abstract task into a subplan, gated by an applicability condition. It is the reusable unit means-end search selects among: a curriculum's course-into-modules rule, a control plan's phase-into-steps rule, an organizational guide's policy-into-procedure rule. It is not a logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph, which is one committed INSTANTIATION of a method against one situation; the method is the schema and outlives every pin made from it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrByPagePartition a logic:DecompositionMethod ; logic:methodDecomposes ex:ocrTask ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to declare a reusable way of decomposing an abstract task, so refinement can select among alternatives rather than improvising."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid inlining a decomposition into a single episode with no reusable schema, and avoid mutating a landed method in place."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind logic:methodDecomposes, logic:methodApplicability and logic:methodYields; version it like a prescription and supersede rather than edit."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Content-addressed and versioned, because it is the reusable unit means-end search selects among. Without the version a later edit silently re-interprets every pin that instantiated it, which is exactly what content addressing prevents."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:methodDecomposes
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DecompositionMethod ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "method decomposes"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:DecompositionMethod to the abstract task it knows how to break down. Range an abstract plan step (stated in prose). Functional: one method decomposes one task type, so several ways of breaking down the same task are several methods rather than one method with alternatives folded inside it, which is what lets a search rank them against each other."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrMethod logic:methodDecomposes ex:ocr ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to name the task a decomposition method applies to."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a method decomposing a task into itself, directly or transitively; that is a cycle and no expansion terminates."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "One task per method; several methods may decompose the same task, and each is a genuine alternative."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional: one method decomposes one task type, and several methods for the same task are several methods rather than one with alternatives. That is what lets the search rank them against each other."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:methodApplicability
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DecompositionMethod ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "method applicability"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The condition under which a logic:DecompositionMethod may be applied, as a logic:Formula over the situation. It is what makes the candidate roster DERIVED rather than asserted: a method whose applicability does not hold is not a candidate, and the rejection carries that formula as its witness."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrMethod logic:methodApplicability ex:hasOcrCapability ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the condition under which a method may be applied."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid leaving it unbound where the method is not universally applicable; an unconditioned method will be proposed in situations it cannot serve."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "State the condition so search can rule the method out rather than proposing it and failing later."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "What makes the candidate roster DERIVED rather than asserted: a method whose applicability formula does not hold in the snapshot is simply not a candidate, without anyone deciding so. Prose applicability would need a human to apply it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:methodYields
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DecompositionMethod ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "method yields"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:DecompositionMethod to the ORDERED sequence of subtasks its decomposition produces, carried as ONE rdf:List whose position is semantic. Single-valued; range an rdf:List of plan steps or action schemas (stated in prose, as for logic:joinPath). Repeated unordered triples are deliberately NOT the carrier: RDF multi-values are a set, so inspect-then-prepare-then-extract stated that way loses its sequence at the first reader, and a reader forced to impose one can only guess or alphabetise. The order is the plan, so it is carried where a reader can find it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrMethod logic:methodYields ( ex:extract ex:verify ) ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the ordered sequence of subtasks a method reduces its task to."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a second logic:methodYields list on one method, and avoid a chain terminating anywhere but rdf:nil; either makes the sequence depend on which triple a reader happened to see first."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Give exactly one rdf:List in execution order, yielding at least one subtask; NAME the list cells whenever the decomposition must be pinned or content-addressed, because a pin cannot freeze a cell it cannot name."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Single-valued and carried as ONE list whose position is semantic. Two lists would give one method two decompositions with no way to say which a pin instantiated, and an unordered set would lose the sequence entirely."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:methodDigest
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DecompositionMethod ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "method digest"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The algorithm-prefixed content address of a logic:DecompositionMethod's frozen form, so a pin made from it can name the exact method version it instantiated and a later method revision cannot silently re-interpret an existing pin."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrMethod logic:methodDigest \"b3:1a2b3c…\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to content-address a method, so a pinned decomposition names the exact method that produced it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid revising a method in place; supersede it, or past pins silently change meaning."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Address the method's content, which is what lets a refinement be replayed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Lets a pin name the exact method version it instantiated, so a later method revision cannot silently re-interpret an existing pin. Without it the pin's provenance is a name, and names are reused."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:RefinementEpisode
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Refinement Episode"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "One bounded, attributed attempt to turn an abstract step into something executable, against a stated world snapshot and under a declared resource policy. Boundedness is definitional: means-end search is undecidable in general, so an episode that cannot report the budget it ran under cannot report an honest closed roster either. Typed APART from logic:Enactment, which carries out a plan; an episode PRODUCES the plan fragment an enactment will later carry out."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrRefinement a logic:RefinementEpisode ; logic:refinesStep ex:ocrTask ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use whenever an abstract step must be made executable and the reasoning that did so must stay auditable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an unbounded episode, and avoid one that reports candidates without reporting what it searched under."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the step, the served goal, the world snapshot and a resource policy; produce a closed candidate set and either a pin or a capability-gap proposal."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Boundedness is definitional: means-end search is undecidable in general, so an episode that could run unbounded would be unable to report anything honest about completeness. The budget and the fragment are what make its roster a claim rather than a sample."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:refinesStep
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:RefinementEpisode ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "refines step"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:RefinementEpisode to the abstract step it is trying to make executable. Range a plan step (stated in prose). Functional: one episode refines one step, so a multi-step refinement is several episodes and each carries its own budget and outcome. One episode cannot be shared across steps, because that would make the budget's attribution meaningless."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:episode logic:refinesStep ex:ocrStep ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind a refinement episode to the abstract step it decomposes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid refining a step that is already primitive; there is nothing to decompose and the episode records nothing."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "One step per episode, so the decomposition is attributable to what it decomposed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional: one episode refines one step, so a multi-step refinement is several episodes and each carries its own budget and snapshot. Sharing one episode across steps would make the budget's attribution meaningless."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:refinementServesGoal
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:RefinementEpisode ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "refinement serves goal"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:RefinementEpisode to the goal the refined step contributes to. Range a goal expression (stated in prose). Naming it explicitly keeps a decomposition answerable to WHY it was undertaken, not merely to whether it produced runnable steps."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:episode logic:refinementServesGoal ex:textExtracted ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to name the goal a refinement is trying to reach."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid omitting it: a decomposition with no served goal cannot be judged better or worse than an alternative."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the goal the candidates are compared against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Keeps a decomposition answerable to WHY it was undertaken, not merely to whether it produced something executable. A refinement that serves no named goal has no criterion by which its candidates could be preferred to one another."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:refinementWorldSnapshot
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:RefinementEpisode ;
    rdfs:range logic:InputSnapshot ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "refinement world snapshot"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed generation of world, policy and capability facts the episode reasoned over. Without it a roster's closedness is unfalsifiable, because a later reader cannot tell which facts were visible when the search ran."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:episode logic:refinementWorldSnapshot ex:worldAtDecision ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to pin the world state a refinement reasoned over."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid re-deriving a past episode against today's world; the candidates would differ and the record would look wrong rather than historical."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the snapshot so a past decision stays interpretable against what was known then."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Without it a roster's closedness is unfalsifiable, because a later reader cannot tell which facts were visible when the search ran. The snapshot is what turns closedness from a claim into something a re-run can contradict."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:refinementBudget
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:RefinementEpisode ;
    rdfs:range logic:ResourcePolicy ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "refinement budget"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The declared resource policy the episode's search ran under, reusing the engine's existing budget vocabulary rather than minting a second one. A cut against this budget yields an honest incomplete result, never a roster claimed closed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:episode logic:refinementBudget ex:stepBudget ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bound a refinement episode's search."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an unbounded episode: hierarchical decomposition is undecidable in general, so an unbounded search has no honest completeness answer."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Pair the budget with the resulting completeness status, so a cut is reported rather than hidden."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Reuses the engine's existing budget vocabulary rather than minting a second one, so a cut against it yields an honest incomplete result and never a roster claimed closed. A local budget notion would let closedness be asserted under an unrelated limit."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:RefinementCandidateSet
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Refinement Candidate Set"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The roster of subplans and action schemas a logic:RefinementEpisode found applicable — CLOSED, meaning the episode ran to saturation within its budget and nothing applicable was omitted. Closedness is a claim that must be earned by a saturation witness, never asserted: a roster produced under a budget cut is incomplete and must say so, because presenting a truncated roster as complete is how a system silently picks a worse action."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrCandidates a logic:RefinementCandidateSet ; logic:refinementCandidate ex:ocrViaTesseract ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to carry the alternatives a refinement found, together with whether the roster is complete."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid claiming closure without a saturation witness, and avoid omitting rejected candidates (their rejections are the audit trail)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind every applicable candidate and every rejection; carry the episode's completeness status alongside."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "CLOSED means the episode ran to saturation within its budget and nothing applicable was omitted, and that closedness is a claim to be earned by a saturation witness. A roster without one records what was found, not what exists."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:refinementCandidate
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:RefinementCandidateSet ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "refinement candidate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:RefinementCandidateSet to one applicable subplan or action schema. Range a subplan or action schema (stated in prose). Multi-valued; membership means applicability held, not that the candidate was selected."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:episode logic:refinementCandidate ex:pinnedPlan ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for each candidate decomposition the episode produced."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid presenting a candidate list as closed without a completeness status; a cut search yields real candidates and an open roster."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "List every candidate considered, including rejected ones, so the choice is reviewable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Membership means applicability held, not that the candidate was selected. A roster is a record of what the search found admissible, and reading it as a recommendation skips the authorization step entirely."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:candidateInstantiatesMethod
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:DecompositionMethod ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "candidate instantiates method"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates one logic:refinementCandidate of a roster to the logic:DecompositionMethod whose application produced it. Domain a roster candidate — a subplan or action schema (stated in prose, as for logic:refinementCandidate itself). Functional: a candidate produced by two methods would have two step sequences and two provenances, so which decomposition was authorized would be undecidable at the moment of authorization. It is what makes a pin DERIVABLE rather than hand-authored: with the method named, the frozen sequence and the content address of an authorized candidate are read off the method it came from, and a pin whose steps its method never yielded becomes unconstructible instead of merely condemned after the fact."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrCandidate logic:candidateInstantiatesMethod ex:ocrByPagePartition ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use on every roster candidate, to name the method application that produced it, so the candidate's step sequence and content address have a source the reasoner can read."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid naming two methods on one candidate, and avoid leaving it unbound on a candidate that may be authorized: an authorized candidate with no method yields no pin at all, which reads exactly like an episode that found nothing."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind it as the search records each candidate; the pin-derivation rules join through it to reach logic:methodYields and logic:methodDigest."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The provenance edge from a roster candidate back to the schema that produced it. Functional, because a candidate reached by two methods would carry two possible step sequences and the authorization could not say which one it licensed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:producedCandidateSet
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:RefinementEpisode ;
    rdfs:range logic:RefinementCandidateSet ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "produced candidate set"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:RefinementEpisode to the roster it produced. Functional: one episode yields one roster, so two rosters for one step are two episodes rather than one episode with two results, each with its own budget and snapshot. That is what keeps a roster comparable against the exact conditions it was produced under."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional: one episode yields one roster, so two rosters for one step are two episodes with their own budgets and snapshots. That is what keeps a roster comparable against the exact conditions it was produced under."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:episode-3 logic:producedCandidateSet ex:roster-1 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind a refinement episode to the candidate roster it produced."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid attaching a second roster to one episode: re-running the search under different conditions is a new episode, and merging them hides which budget produced which candidate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it once per episode, alongside the episode's fragment, budget and world snapshot."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:RejectionKind
    a logic:AbstractIndividualType ,
        logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:QualityValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Rejection Kind"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed vocabulary of WHY a refinement candidate was rejected: an unmet precondition, an absent capability, an unavailable resource, or a missing authorization. Typing the reason is what lets an operator projection say why an option is unavailable and what would make it available, instead of reporting a bare absence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:tesseractRejection logic:rejectionKind logic:RejectedCapability ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to type the reason a candidate did not survive."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an untyped rejection and avoid using it for a candidate that was applicable but simply not preferred."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach exactly one member together with the witness that established it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Typing the reason is what lets an operator projection say why an option is unavailable and what would make it available. The four reasons have four different remedies, so an untyped rejection is advice to try again with no basis."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:RejectedPrecondition a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:RejectionKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "rejected on precondition"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The candidate's precondition did not hold in the episode's world snapshot. Remediable by changing the world; distinguished from a capability rejection, which no change of world state resolves."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Remediable by changing the world, which no capability rejection ever is. The judgment is made against the episode's world snapshot, so it is a claim about that snapshot rather than about the present."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:candidate-2 logic:rejectionKind logic:RejectedPrecondition ; logic:refinementWorldSnapshot ex:snap-14 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the candidate's precondition did not hold in the episode's world snapshot."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it as a general failure bucket: the other three kinds each name something a precondition change cannot fix."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:rejectionKind against the snapshot it was judged in, so a re-run over a newer snapshot is a meaningful retry."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:RejectedCapability a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:RejectionKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "rejected on capability"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "No actor available to the episode holds the capability the candidate requires. This is the rejection that yields a capability-gap proposal rather than a retry."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "No change of world state resolves it: the ability itself is absent from every actor the episode can reach. That is why it yields a capability-gap proposal rather than a retry, and why it must not be merged with a resource rejection."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:candidate-2 logic:rejectionKind logic:RejectedCapability ; logic:proposalMissingCapability ex:cap-notarize ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when no actor available to the episode holds the capability the candidate requires."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when the capability exists but its means are exhausted: that is a resource rejection, and the two point at different remedies."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:rejectionKind and name the gap with logic:proposalMissingCapability, so the search reports what would have to be acquired."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:RejectedResource a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:RejectionKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "rejected on resource"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A resource the candidate consumes is exhausted or unavailable. Distinguished from a capability rejection: the ability exists, the means to exercise it does not."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The ability exists and the means to exercise it do not. Separating this from a capability rejection is what keeps a temporary exhaustion from being reported as a permanent structural gap."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:candidate-2 logic:rejectionKind logic:RejectedResource ; logic:resourceExhausted ex:budget-gpu-hours ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a resource the candidate consumes is exhausted or unavailable while the capability itself is held."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when nobody holds the ability at all: that gap is structural, and a resource report would suggest waiting would help."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:rejectionKind and name the exhausted resource, so replenishment is an actionable remedy rather than a guess."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:RejectedApproval a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:RejectionKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "rejected on approval"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The candidate requires an authorization that is absent, undetached or expired. Unlike the other three rejection kinds, nothing about the world or the actor is lacking, only the permission — which is why the remedy is a decision rather than an action on the state."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Nothing about the world or the actor is lacking, only the permission. That is what separates it from the other three rejection kinds, and it is why the remedy is a decision rather than an action on the state."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:candidate-2 logic:rejectionKind logic:RejectedApproval ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a refinement candidate needs an authorization that is absent, not yet detached, or expired."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when policy forbids the action outright: that is not a missing permission but a standing exclusion, and no approver can grant it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:rejectionKind on the rejection, so the episode's report tells an operator which decision would unblock the search."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:RefinementRejection
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Refinement Rejection"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The record that a specific candidate was considered and did not survive, carrying its typed logic:RejectionKind and the witness that established it. Rejections are retained rather than discarded because 'why is this option not available?' is the question an operator actually asks, and a roster that lists only survivors cannot answer it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:tesseractRejection a logic:RefinementRejection ; logic:rejectionKind logic:RejectedCapability ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record a considered-and-declined candidate with its typed reason."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid discarding rejections, and avoid recording one without the witness that justifies it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the rejected candidate, the kind, and the witness; hang it off the candidate set the episode produced."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Retained rather than discarded, because why is this option not available is the question an operator actually asks. A roster that keeps only survivors answers what may be done but never what was ruled out and on what ground."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:rejectionKind
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:RefinementRejection ;
    rdfs:range logic:RejectionKind ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has rejection kind"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:RefinementRejection to its exactly-one typed reason. Functional: a candidate blocked on two grounds yields two rejections rather than one record naming both, so each remedy stays separately actionable; folding them together would leave an operator clearing a permission and finding the capability still absent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional by design: a candidate blocked on two grounds yields two rejections, so each remedy is separately actionable. Folding both grounds into one record would leave an operator clearing a permission and finding the capability still absent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:rejection-4 logic:rejectionKind logic:RejectedResource ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use on every refinement rejection to name its single typed reason."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid choosing the most severe of several grounds: the others then disappear, and the search report understates what would have to change."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Emit one rejection per ground with its own kind and witness, so remedies can be pursued in parallel."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:rejectedCandidate
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:RefinementRejection ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "rejected candidate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:RefinementRejection to the specific candidate it declined, not to the step in general. Range a subplan or action schema (stated in prose). Functional: a rejection covering two candidates would leave each of them with a reason that may fit only the other."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Names what was declined, so a rejection is always about a specific subplan or action schema rather than about the step in general. Functional: a rejection covering two candidates would leave each of them with a reason that may fit only the other."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:rejection-4 logic:rejectedCandidate ex:subplan-b ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to name the subplan or action schema a refinement rejection declined."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid grouping several declined candidates under one rejection: each needs its own kind and witness for its remedy to be actionable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Emit one rejection per declined candidate and attach it to the roster, so the deliberation is readable candidate by candidate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:rejectionWitness
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:RefinementRejection ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "rejection witness"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:RefinementRejection to the evidence that established it — the unsatisfied formula, the absent capability, the exhausted resource. Range a witness node (stated in prose). A rejection without a witness is an opinion, not a finding."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A rejection without a witness is an opinion, not a finding. The witness is the unsatisfied formula, the absent capability or the exhausted resource itself, so the rejection can be re-checked rather than merely believed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:rejection-4 logic:rejectionWitness ex:unsat-formula-9 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to attach the evidence that established a refinement rejection."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid substituting a prose explanation: prose cannot be re-evaluated against a newer world snapshot, and the witness is what makes a retry meaningful."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Point at the concrete unsatisfied formula, missing capability or exhausted resource, so the rejection can be re-derived rather than re-argued."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:hasRejection
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:RefinementCandidateSet ;
    rdfs:range logic:RefinementRejection ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has rejection"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:RefinementCandidateSet to a candidate it considered and declined. Multi-valued; together with logic:refinementCandidate it makes the roster's full deliberation readable rather than only its outcome."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Together with logic:refinementCandidate it makes the roster's full deliberation readable rather than only its outcome. Recording what was declined and why is what lets a later reader challenge the search instead of only its winner."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:roster-1 logic:hasRejection ex:rejection-4 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record each candidate a refinement candidate set considered and declined, with its own rejection record."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid omitting rejections to keep a roster tidy: a roster showing only what passed cannot be audited for what it failed to consider."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Emit one rejection per ground, so a candidate blocked on two counts yields two separately actionable remedies."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:PreparationStep
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Preparation Step"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Work carried out to make a selected action ready that is not itself the action — staging inputs, warming a cache, acquiring a lease, obtaining an authorization. Typed APART from the action it prepares for: preparation may complete while the action never runs, and conflating the two would report readiness work as the work itself."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:stageForOcr a logic:PreparationStep ; logic:preparationOf ex:ocrStep ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for work done before an action becomes ready, when that work must be visible and separately auditable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid folding preparation into the prepared action, and avoid preparation with no named beneficiary."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind logic:preparationOf and emit a logic:PreparationReceipt when it completes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Typed APART from the action it prepares for, because preparation may complete while the action never happens. Folding preparation into the action would make an acquired lease or a warmed cache invisible when the action is later skipped."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:preparationOf
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:PreparationStep ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "preparation of"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:PreparationStep to the step or enactment it prepares for. Range a plan step or enactment (stated in prose). Functional. It is the seam a domain slice specializes to bind preparation to its own activity vocabulary. A preparation with no target is exactly as suspect as an effect with no intent: work that cannot be attributed to what it was for is not preparation but unaccounted activity."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The seam a domain slice specializes to bind preparation to its own activity vocabulary. It relates the preparation to what it prepares for, so a preparation with no target is exactly as suspect as an effect with no intent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:prep-2 logic:preparationOf ex:step-4 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind a preparation step to the plan step or enactment it prepares for."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for work that is itself prescribed: a prescribed activity is a step, and modelling it as preparation removes it from the frontier."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Specialize it in the domain slice so preparation lands in that slice's activity vocabulary rather than staying a bare kernel edge."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:PreparationReceipt
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Preparation Receipt"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The durable record that a logic:PreparationStep completed and what it produced, so a resumed enactment does not redo preparation it already paid for. Typed APART from logic:ExternalEffectReceipt, which records an outcome observed OUTSIDE the system: a preparation receipt records internal readiness work whose outcome is not in doubt."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:stagedForOcr a logic:PreparationReceipt ; logic:receiptOfPreparation ex:stageForOcr ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to make completed preparation survive a pause or crash."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for an external outcome, and avoid emitting one for preparation that did not finish."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind logic:receiptOfPreparation; a restore reads it to skip already-completed readiness work."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Typed APART from an external effect receipt, which records an outcome observed OUTSIDE the system. Preparation is internal work, so its receipt is derivable evidence rather than an observation, and merging the two would blur the system boundary."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:receiptOfPreparation
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:PreparationReceipt ;
    rdfs:range logic:PreparationStep ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "receipt of preparation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:PreparationReceipt to the logic:PreparationStep it records. Functional: one receipt per completed preparation step, so a resumed enactment can tell paid-for preparation from preparation it must redo. The relation cannot be multi-valued, because two receipts for one step would leave that question unanswerable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:stagingReceipt logic:receiptOfPreparation ex:stageFiles ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to evidence that a preparation activity actually completed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid inferring preparation from the absence of an error; a preparation that silently did nothing looks identical."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the receipt to the preparation step, so readiness is evidenced rather than assumed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional: one receipt per completed preparation step, so a resumed enactment can tell paid-for preparation from preparation it must redo. Two receipts for one step would leave that question unanswerable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Pinned Executable Subgraph"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The immutable, content-addressed plan fragment a refinement committed to — one instantiation of a logic:DecompositionMethod against one situation, frozen so that what was authorized is exactly what runs. Immutability is a SECURITY property, not tidiness: a mutable pin means an approval was granted against a moving target, and the authorized argument set could differ from the dispatched one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrPin a logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph ; logic:pinInstantiatesMethod ex:ocrByPagePartition ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a refinement is selected and the resulting fragment must be exactly what executes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid editing a landed pin, and avoid pinning without recording which method version produced it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Freeze the ordered steps on logic:pinnedStepSequence, content-address them on logic:pinDigest, bind the method version it instantiates, and carry the proof term that authorized it on logic:pinAuthority."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Immutability is a SECURITY property: what was authorized must be exactly what runs, so a pin that could be edited after approval reduces the authorization to a gesture. Content addressing is what makes the identity between the two checkable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:pinInstantiatesMethod
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph ;
    rdfs:range logic:DecompositionMethod ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "pin instantiates method"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph to the logic:DecompositionMethod it instantiates. Functional. Naming the method version keeps a later method revision from silently re-interpreting an existing pin; a pin cannot claim two methods, because its frozen step sequence would then have two possible readings."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Naming the method version keeps a later method revision from silently re-interpreting an existing pin. Functional, because a pin that claimed two methods would leave the frozen step sequence with two possible readings."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:pin-3 logic:pinInstantiatesMethod ex:method-decompose-v2 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to name the decomposition method a pinned executable subgraph instantiates."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid naming a method the pin's frozen steps were never yielded by: a pin whose steps its method never produced is a rejected pattern."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record the method version, so a later revision of that method leaves existing pins interpretable as they were authorized."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:pinAuthority
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "pin authority"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph to what authorized the pin — a checkable proof term establishing that the pin was licensed, not a bare reference to whoever asked. Range an authorization proof (stated in prose). A logic:Advisory may never occupy this position: advice can motivate a pin but cannot license one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Takes a checkable proof term establishing that the pin was licensed, not a bare reference to whoever asked. A logic:Advisory may never occupy this position: advice can motivate a pin but cannot license one, and admitting it here is the laundering the kernel refuses."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:pin-3 logic:pinAuthority ex:auth-proof-11 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to name the authorization proof establishing that a pinned executable subgraph was licensed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid pointing it at an advisory or at an operator identity: neither is a proof, and both would let motivation stand in for licence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Mint the proof first and reference it here, so the pin's licence is checkable rather than attested."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:pinDigest
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "pin digest"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The algorithm-prefixed content address of the pinned fragment — the value an approval binds to and a dispatch is checked against, so a revised fragment cannot satisfy an authorization issued for its predecessor."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The value an approval binds to and a dispatch is checked against, so a revised fragment cannot satisfy an authorization issued for its predecessor. Algorithm-prefixed by design: a bare hex string would leave the comparison undefined across algorithm changes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:pin-3 logic:pinDigest \"blake3:2b41c9\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record the algorithm-prefixed content address of a pinned fragment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid recording an unprefixed digest: the comparison an approval performs is only well defined when the algorithm travels with the value."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Compute it over the pinned fragment and let the approval bind to it, so a revision invalidates the authorization automatically."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:pinnedStepSequence
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "pinned step sequence"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The ordered rdf:List of steps a logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph froze — the executable content its logic:pinDigest addresses and the content an approval was granted against. Single-valued; range an rdf:List of plan steps (stated in prose). It is what makes the pin a COMMITMENT rather than a name: a pin carrying only a label and a digest asserts an immutability it has no content to be immutable about, so 'what was validated is what executes' becomes a claim no reader can check and no law can test."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrPin logic:pinnedStepSequence ( ex:inspect ex:extract ex:verify ) ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to freeze the exact ordered steps a refinement committed to, so the dispatched fragment can be compared against the authorized one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid restating the steps in a label and calling the pin content-addressed; prose is not a fragment a digest can address or a law can join against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Give exactly one rdf:List of NAMED cells in execution order — the same list the instantiated method's logic:methodYields carries, since a pin whose steps differ from the method's is executing something other than what was validated."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The executable content the pin digest addresses and the content an approval was granted against, so editing it after approval breaks the identity the authorization rests on. Single-valued for the same reason the yielded list is."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:selectedPin
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:RefinementEpisode ;
    rdfs:range logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "selected pin"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:RefinementEpisode to the pin it committed to, when a candidate was authorized. Functional and OPTIONAL: an episode that found no authorized candidate produces a capability-gap proposal instead of a pin, and producing neither is an incomplete episode. Its absence is therefore informative rather than missing data."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional and OPTIONAL: an episode that found no authorized candidate produces a capability-gap proposal instead, and producing neither is an incomplete episode. Its absence is therefore informative rather than missing data."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:episode-3 logic:selectedPin ex:pin-3 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a refinement episode committed to a pin because a candidate was authorized."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid leaving both this and the capability-gap proposal absent: an episode that produced neither has not reported an outcome at all."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record the pin the episode committed to, or a capability-gap proposal in its place, so every episode ends with a readable result."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:OperationalCapabilityGap
    a logic:Class ;
    logic:disjointWith gmeow:CapabilityGap ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Operational Capability Gap"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The finding that no available actor holds a capability an otherwise-applicable action requires — a missing tool, an unprovisioned provider, an uncertified operator. It is DISJOINT from the reasoner-conformance capability-gap notion, which grades a divergence case the native reasoner could not classify: that one is about what the ENGINE can decide, this one about what the WORLD can do. Overloading either for the other makes a provisioning problem look like a reasoning defect."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:noOcrProvider a logic:OperationalCapabilityGap ; logic:gapBlockedStep ex:ocrStep ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when an action is blocked because no actor can perform it, not because the engine could not reason about it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid conflating with reasoner-fragment gaps, and avoid recording one without a proposal that says what would close it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the blocked step and pair it with a logic:CapabilityGapProposal naming what provisioning it would take."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "DISJOINT from the reasoner-conformance capability-gap notion: this one is about no actor holding a capability, and that one is about an engine not supporting a construct. Sharing a name across the two would make a staffing problem look like an engine limit."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:gapBlockedStep
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:OperationalCapabilityGap ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "gap blocked step"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:OperationalCapabilityGap to the one step it blocks, not to every step the missing capability affects. Range a plan step (stated in prose). Functional: the same absent capability blocking three steps yields three gap records, which is what lets each blocked step carry its own proposal and its own remedy."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrGap logic:gapBlockedStep ex:ocrStep ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind an operational capability gap to the step it blocks."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a gap with no blocked step: it is a general complaint about provisioning that no operator can act on."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the concrete blockage, which is what makes the gap answerable by a proposal."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional, so a gap names the one step it blocks. The same missing capability blocking three steps yields three gap records, which is what lets each blocked step carry its own proposal and its own remedy."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:CapabilityGapProposal
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Capability Gap Proposal"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The complete, actionable statement of what would close a logic:OperationalCapabilityGap: the missing capability, the contract it must satisfy, its expected inputs, outputs and effects, how its correctness would be verified, what security and lifecycle handling it needs, and the step it unblocks. All eight are mandatory, because a proposal missing any one of them is a complaint rather than a request — and the whole point of surfacing a gap instead of substituting a weaker tool is that the gap comes with its own remedy."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrProposal a logic:CapabilityGapProposal ; logic:proposalMissingCapability ex:pdfOcr ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use whenever a capability gap blocks an action, so the blockage carries its own remedy."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an incomplete proposal, and avoid substituting a weaker capability in place of raising one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind all eight fields; the completeness constraint rejects a proposal that omits any."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Complete and actionable by construction: it states the missing capability, the contract it must satisfy, its expected inputs, outputs and effects, how correctness would be verified and what security lifecycle applies. A partial proposal names a gap without saying what would close it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:proposalMissingCapability
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CapabilityGapProposal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "proposal missing capability"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Field one of eight: the capability that is absent. Range a capability (stated in prose). Functional. It is the only field of the eight that names what is missing rather than what would be required of a replacement; the other seven describe the shape of the thing that would close the gap."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:prop logic:proposalMissingCapability ex:ocrCap ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the capability that is absent — one of the eight fields a capability-gap proposal must bind."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid naming a product or a vendor here; the gap is a capability the deployment lacks, and pinning it to one implementation forecloses the alternatives an operator may prefer."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the capability abstractly enough that more than one implementation could close it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Field one of eight, and the only one that names what is absent rather than what would be required of a replacement. Everything else in the proposal describes the shape of the thing that would close the gap."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:proposalRequiredContract
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CapabilityGapProposal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "proposal required contract"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Field two of eight: the contract a provider of the missing capability must satisfy, as opposed to field one, which names what is absent. Range a contract descriptor (stated in prose). Functional. Stating the contract is what lets two different providers be judged against the same bar rather than against each other."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:prop logic:proposalRequiredContract \"Page image in, positioned text out.\"@x-gmeow-english ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the interface it must satisfy — one of the eight fields a capability-gap proposal must bind."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid describing the contract in terms of the current caller; a contract written around one call site cannot be satisfied by anything reusable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "State the interface as an obligation on any implementation: what it must accept and what it must guarantee."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Field two of eight: what a provider must satisfy, as distinct from what is missing. Stating the contract is what lets two different providers be judged against the same bar instead of against each other."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:proposalExpectedInputs
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CapabilityGapProposal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "proposal expected inputs"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Field three of eight: the inputs a provider would consume. Range an input descriptor (stated in prose). Functional. It is one half of a pair and is not sufficient alone: without the expected outputs stated alongside it, the verification method has nothing to check a candidate provider against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:prop logic:proposalExpectedInputs \"Rasterised page images, 300dpi or better.\"@x-gmeow-english ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for what it consumes — one of the eight fields a capability-gap proposal must bind."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid listing inputs the blocked step does not actually have; a proposal demanding data nobody can supply cannot be acted on."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "List what the deployment can already produce at the blocked step, with the formats and quality bounds that matter."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Field three of eight, and one half of the pair a verifier needs: without both inputs and outputs stated, the verification method has nothing to check the provider against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:proposalExpectedOutputs
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CapabilityGapProposal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "proposal expected outputs"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Field four of eight: the outputs a provider would produce — data the caller receives, not the world changes the caller is left holding after the call returns, which are field five. Range an output descriptor (stated in prose). Functional."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:prop logic:proposalExpectedOutputs \"Per-page text with confidence scores.\"@x-gmeow-english ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for what it produces — one of the eight fields a capability-gap proposal must bind."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid specifying outputs richer than the blocked step consumes: over-specifying rules out simpler implementations for no benefit."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Specify exactly what the blocked step needs downstream, and no more."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Field four of eight, and the other half of that pair. Outputs are data the caller receives, as distinct from effects, which are changes the world retains after the call returns."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:proposalExpectedEffects
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CapabilityGapProposal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "proposal expected effects"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Field five of eight: the world changes a provider would cause. Range an effect descriptor (stated in prose). Functional. Stated separately from outputs rather than folded into them, because an effect may be irreversible where an output is merely data: collapsing the two would let a proposal for something that changes the world read like a proposal for something that computes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:prop logic:proposalExpectedEffects \"Reads batch images; writes text alongside.\"@x-gmeow-english ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for what it changes outside itself — one of the eight fields a capability-gap proposal must bind."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid omitting effects outside the obvious data path — writes, deletions, external calls; an unstated effect is the one that surprises an operator in production."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "State every effect beyond returning a value, including what it must NOT touch."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Stated separately from outputs because an effect may be irreversible where an output is merely data. Collapsing the two would let a proposal for something that changes the world read like a proposal for something that computes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:proposalVerificationMethod
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CapabilityGapProposal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "proposal verification method"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Field six of eight: how a candidate provider's correctness would be established. Range a verification descriptor (stated in prose). Functional. Without it a gap can be closed by something that merely claims the capability rather than something shown to have it: the method is what converts a provider's assertion into a check somebody can run before depending on it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:prop logic:proposalVerificationMethod \"CER below 2% on the ground-truth set.\"@x-gmeow-english ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for how its correctness would be checked — one of the eight fields a capability-gap proposal must bind."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a verification method with no threshold: 'tested for accuracy' cannot be passed or failed, so it will be declared passed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Give a measurable criterion and the corpus it is measured on, so enabling the capability is a decidable event."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Without it a gap can be closed by something that merely claims the capability. The method is what converts a provider's assertion into a check somebody can run before depending on it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:proposalSecurityLifecycle
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CapabilityGapProposal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "proposal security and lifecycle needs"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Field seven of eight: the credential, isolation, retention and decommissioning handling a provider would require. Range a security-lifecycle descriptor (stated in prose). Functional. It is the field most often deferred, and deferring it is not free: it makes a gap look cheaper to close than it is, because the handling requirements arrive only after a provider has already been chosen."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:prop logic:proposalSecurityLifecycle \"Runs inside the data boundary; retains no page content.\"@x-gmeow-english ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for its security and retention handling — one of the eight fields a capability-gap proposal must bind."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid deferring security to the deployment: a capability whose data handling is unstated will be deployed with whatever handling is convenient."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "State the trust boundary, the retention, and the classification of what it processes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Field seven of eight, and the one most often deferred: credentials, isolation, retention and decommissioning. Deferring it makes a gap look cheaper to close than it is, because the handling requirements arrive only after a provider is chosen."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:proposalBlockedStep
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CapabilityGapProposal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "proposal blocked step"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Field eight of eight: the step that would be unblocked. Range a plan step (stated in prose). Functional. It is what makes a proposal answerable to a concrete blockage rather than a general wish."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:prop logic:proposalBlockedStep ex:ocrStep ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the step it would unblock — one of the eight fields a capability-gap proposal must bind."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid naming the plan rather than the step; a proposal answerable to a whole plan cannot be checked as having unblocked anything."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the exact step whose blockage this proposal would clear, which is how the gap and its remedy stay joined."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "What makes a proposal answerable to a concrete blockage rather than a general wish. A proposal naming no blocked step describes a capability someone would like to have, which no operator can prioritize."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:Advisory
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Model Advisory"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A model-generated decomposition, recommendation, summary or draft: attributed evidence that may inform a decision and may never constitute one. It is structurally barred from authorizing a pin, deciding an approval, or carrying a proof. Typed APART from logic:AdviceGuidance, which is authored usage prose attached to a TERM for a human author's benefit; an Advisory is generated content about a SITUATION, with a different subject, lifetime and consumer."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:suggestedOcrPlan a logic:Advisory ; logic:advisoryAbout ex:ocrTask ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for anything a model produced that a reader might otherwise mistake for a decision or a derivation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid letting an Advisory authorize, decide or prove; avoid confusing it with authored term guidance."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attribute it, bind what it is about, and keep it out of every authority position; the advisory constraint rejects the alternative."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Typed apart from evidence and proof so the bar is structural rather than a matter of care. It cannot authorize a pin, decide an approval or carry a proof, which means an advisory that is right about everything still licenses nothing."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:advisoryAbout
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Advisory ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "advisory about"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Advisory to what it advises on. Range any subject of advice (stated in prose). Multi-valued; one advisory may bear on several steps. It records what the advice is about and does not confer authority over any of it: an advisory reaches a step without governing it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:draftSummary logic:advisoryAbout ex:weekThirteen ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind model-generated advice to what it is about."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid letting an advisory occupy an authority position: advice is never authority, and a heuristic relabelled as proof is the failure this separation exists to prevent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attribute the advisory, so a later reader can weigh it as evidence rather than read it as a decision."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Multi-valued, since one advisory may bear on several steps. What it never does is confer authority on any of them: the relation records what the advice is about, not that the advice governs it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:SearchFragment
    a logic:AbstractIndividualType ,
        logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:QualityValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Search Fragment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The declared decidable fragment a means-end search runs within. Hierarchical decomposition is undecidable in general, so a search that names no fragment cannot honestly report completeness. Declaring the fragment is what separates two different honest answers that would otherwise collapse together: a budget cut (the search was correct but ran out) and an out-of-fragment method set (the search was never entitled to terminate)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrRefinement logic:searchFragment logic:FragmentAcyclicMethod ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to declare which decidable fragment a refinement search was admitted under."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an undeclared fragment, and avoid reporting an out-of-fragment method set as a budget exhaustion."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Select one member per episode; a method set outside it yields a typed unsupported refusal, not an incomplete result."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A search that names no fragment cannot honestly report completeness, because hierarchical decomposition is undecidable in general. Declaring the fragment is what separates two different honest answers: nothing applies, and nothing was found in time."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FragmentTotallyOrdered a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:SearchFragment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "totally-ordered fragment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Every method yields a totally ordered subtask sequence, so search never interleaves alternatives. The most restrictive admitted fragment and the cheapest to certify."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The most restrictive admitted fragment and the cheapest to certify, because no interleaving of alternatives is possible. Choosing it is a modelling commitment: a domain with genuinely independent subtasks is misdescribed here, and the search will refuse plans that are in fact valid."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:episode-3 logic:searchFragment logic:FragmentTotallyOrdered ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a domain in which every method yields a strictly sequenced subtask list, such as a lab protocol whose steps are ordered by construction."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it where subtasks may legitimately run in either order: the cheap certification is bought by excluding those plans, and the exclusion is silent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Declare it with logic:searchFragment when the sequencing is genuinely inherent, and move to the acyclic fragment as soon as a method yields an unordered set."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FragmentAcyclicMethod a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:SearchFragment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "acyclic-method fragment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The method-application graph is acyclic, so no task can decompose into itself transitively. Admits partial order among subtasks, unlike the totally-ordered fragment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A structural admission about the method graph, not about how long a search will run. Acyclicity bounds the shape of decomposition; it says nothing about breadth, so a search inside this fragment can still exhaust its budget without ever revisiting a task."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:episode-3 logic:searchFragment logic:FragmentAcyclicMethod ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the method-application graph is known to be acyclic and subtasks may be partially ordered, which is the common case for a workflow with independent branches."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when any method can reach itself transitively: the admission would then be false, and termination would be resting on a budget rather than on the fragment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Declare it with logic:searchFragment on the refinement episode, so a refusal outside the fragment is reported as an out-of-fragment condition rather than as exhaustion."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FragmentBoundedDepth a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:SearchFragment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "bounded-depth fragment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Decomposition is admitted to a declared maximum depth, which terminates even where the method graph has cycles. The depth bound is part of the fragment declaration, not a budget: exceeding it is an out-of-fragment refusal, not an exhaustion."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The depth bound belongs to the fragment declaration, not to the budget. Exceeding it is a refusal to answer outside the admitted fragment, which is a different claim from having run out of steps, and the two must not be reported alike."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:episode-3 logic:searchFragment logic:FragmentBoundedDepth ; logic:pathMaxDepth 4 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the method graph may contain cycles and termination is obtained by admitting decomposition only to a declared maximum depth."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it to express a performance limit: a budget is a resource policy, and stating it as a fragment makes an exhaustion look like an inadmissible question."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Declare the bound as part of the fragment and report a refusal beyond it as out-of-fragment, keeping the budget free to express what the engine could afford."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:searchFragment
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:RefinementEpisode ;
    rdfs:range logic:SearchFragment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has search fragment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:RefinementEpisode to the decidable fragment its search ran within. Functional; an episode without one cannot claim a complete roster."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "An episode without one cannot claim a complete roster: completeness is relative to the fragment searched, so a roster with no declared fragment is an unquantified claim. Functional, because two fragments would leave the completeness claim ambiguous."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:episode-3 logic:searchFragment logic:FragmentBoundedDepth ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use on every refinement episode to declare the decidable fragment its search ran within."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid omitting it on an exploratory search: the roster then cannot be read as complete for anything, which is worse than a narrow declared fragment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Declare the fragment before searching, so an out-of-fragment input is refused as inadmissible rather than reported as exhausted."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# --- RQ2/RQ3: means-end expansion is DERIVED, never computed beside the engine ---
#
# The relations below are the means-end search itself, expressed as ontology content:
# walking a method's ordered rdf:List into its steps, expanding a task through the
# methods that decompose it, closing that expansion transitively, and typing why a step
# is not freely executable. Every one of them is a conclusion the canonical reasoner
# draws over the same closure everything else reads.
#
# Authoring them here rather than in Rust is the whole point. A search that runs beside
# the engine has no derivation to show: its roster is a list somebody's code produced,
# its budget cut is a counter somebody's code kept, and "why is this candidate here"
# has no answer the graph can give. Derived, each roster row carries the firing rule and
# its premises, so the roster is auditable in the same way every other conclusion is.
#
# The relations are deliberately BINARY and positive, which keeps them inside the
# incrementally-maintainable fragment: a refinement is then one committed transaction
# with a real budget, a real cost vector, and a real per-fact witness, rather than a
# bespoke evaluator with a bespoke completeness story.
#
# What a CANDIDATE is here is also deliberate. It is an applicable
# logic:DecompositionMethod — the reusable schema logic:RefinementCandidateSet is
# defined over — and NOT a fully linearized total plan. Enumerating linearizations is a
# cross-product over every task's alternatives: it is exponential in the method set, and
# it is exactly what makes an unbounded search exhaust memory before any budget trips.
# The roster of methods carries the same information (each alternative is named, and
# logic:refinementReaches states what each expands into) in space linear in the method
# set, which is what lets boundedness be a property rather than a hope.

logic:methodYieldCell
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DecompositionMethod ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "method yield cell"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:DecompositionMethod to a cell of the ONE rdf:List its logic:methodYields names — the head cell and, transitively, every rdf:rest reachable from it. Range an rdf:List cell (stated in prose). Derived, never asserted: it is how the ordered carrier is walked by the reasoner instead of by a reader, so the sequence a method yields is reachable as ontology content rather than only as a parser's internal state."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrMethod logic:methodYieldCell ex:ocrCell3 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to reach every cell of a method's yielded sequence, including the ones past the head."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid asserting it: a hand-written cell membership that disagrees with the rdf:rest chain makes the plan depend on which of the two a reader trusts."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Let the reasoner derive it from logic:methodYields and rdf:rest; read it to find the cells, and rdf:first to find the step each carries."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Covers the head cell and, transitively, every rest cell reachable from it, so the whole list is addressable rather than only its first link. Deriving the cells is what lets a constraint quantify over the sequence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:methodStep
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DecompositionMethod ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "method step"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:DecompositionMethod to a subtask its yielded sequence contains, derived from the rdf:first of each logic:methodYieldCell. Range a plan step or action schema (stated in prose). Multi-valued and UNORDERED by construction: membership is what a join needs, and the ORDER is not carried here but stays where it was authored, in the rdf:List, so reading a sequence off this relation would silently invert a decomposition whose order is semantic."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrMethod logic:methodStep ex:verifyStep ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to join a method against the subtasks it yields, without regard to their order."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid reading an execution order off it: it is a set, and any order a reader imposed on it would be a plan nobody authored."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Join on it for membership; walk logic:methodYields and rdf:rest for the sequence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "UNORDERED by design: it is derived from the yielded list's cells, and order lives in the list rather than here. Reading order off this relation would silently invert a decomposition whose sequence is semantic."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:refinementExpands
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "refinement expands"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a task to a subtask ONE method application away: some logic:DecompositionMethod decomposes the task and yields the subtask. Domain and range a plan step or action schema (stated in prose). This is a single means-end step made explicit, so the expansion an operator sees is a derivation with a firing rule and premises rather than a frontier some evaluator held in memory."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrStep logic:refinementExpands ex:verifyStep ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the immediate subtasks a task decomposes into under the authored method set."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it where the TRANSITIVE reach is meant; one application is not the whole expansion."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Read it for one level; read logic:refinementReaches for the closure."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A single means-end step made explicit: some method decomposes the task and yields the subtask. Keeping the one-step relation separate from its closure is what lets a constraint speak about immediate decomposition without ranging over the whole tree."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:refinementReaches
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "refinement reaches"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The transitive closure of logic:refinementExpands: every subtask a task decomposes into through any chain of method applications. Domain and range a plan step or action schema (stated in prose). It is also the DECIDABILITY witness for logic:FragmentAcyclicMethod, because a task that reaches ITSELF is exactly a decomposition cycle — and the self-edge names WHICH task closes the loop, which a bare refusal cannot."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ingestStep logic:refinementReaches ex:verifyStep ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for everything a task decomposes into, at any depth, and to detect a decomposition cycle as a self-edge."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid reading a self-edge as an ordinary expansion: it means no expansion of that task terminates, which no budget increase repairs."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Read a self-edge as an out-of-fragment method set under logic:FragmentAcyclicMethod, and every other edge as reachable work."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The transitive closure of the one-step relation, and also the DECIDABILITY witness for the search: reachability computed over a declared fragment is what a completeness claim rests on. Without the fragment the closure is merely large."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:refinementCandidateMethod
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:DecompositionMethod ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "refinement candidate method"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a task to a logic:DecompositionMethod that decomposes it — one member of the roster a means-end search is choosing between. Domain a plan step or action schema (stated in prose). Multi-valued: two methods for one task are two genuine alternatives, and a roster that carried one of them would be picking a plan on the operator's behalf while presenting the choice as settled. It is not logic:refinementCandidate, which relates a logic:RefinementCandidateSet RECORD to what it contains; this is the derived roster of the task itself."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrStep logic:refinementCandidateMethod ex:ocrByPagePartition ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the derived roster of methods a task may be refined through."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid presenting the roster as closed without the operation outcome that says the derivation ran to completion."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Read every member as a real alternative, and read the rejections alongside it to see what stands between each and execution."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Multi-valued: two methods for one task are two candidates, not one method with alternatives. Keeping them separate is what lets the search rank them, reject them individually, and report why each was declined."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# The four typed rejections. They mirror logic:RejectionKind one for one, and each
# carries its WITNESS in the object position: a rejection whose reason cannot be named
# is an opinion. Four predicates rather than one reified node because a rejection is
# fully determined by the pair (step, witness) — reifying it would require the reasoner
# to invent a node, which puts the derivation outside the bounded, incrementally
# maintainable fragment for no information gained.

logic:refinementRejectedOnPrecondition
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "refinement rejected on precondition"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a step whose action gate was DENIED to the logic:precondition situation that gate was judging — the logic:RejectedPrecondition case, carrying the unmet condition as its witness. Domain a plan step or action schema, range the precondition situation (stated in prose). Remediable by changing the world, unlike a capability rejection, which no change of world state resolves; the witness it carries is a condition that can be re-evaluated against a newer world."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:extractTextStep logic:refinementRejectedOnPrecondition ex:pagesRasterised ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to say WHICH condition stands between a candidate and execution when an action gate denied it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for an absent capability: no change of world state supplies a capability the deployment does not have."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Read the witness as the condition to establish, and act on the world rather than on the deployment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Carries the precondition situation the denied gate was judging, so the witness is a condition that can be re-evaluated against a newer world. A rejection carrying only a verdict cannot be retried meaningfully."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:refinementRejectedOnCapability
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "refinement rejected on capability"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a step blocked by a logic:OperationalCapabilityGap to the capability the gap's logic:CapabilityGapProposal names as missing — the logic:RejectedCapability case, carrying the absent capability as its witness. Domain a plan step or action schema, range the missing capability (stated in prose). This is the rejection that yields a capability-gap proposal rather than a retry, and naming the capability is what makes the refusal actionable instead of merely honest."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrStep logic:refinementRejectedOnCapability ex:ocrCapability ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to name the capability whose absence removes a candidate from consideration."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a reasoning-profile gap: 'install an OCR service' and 'this needs full first-order logic' are not the same finding."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Read the witness as the capability to acquire, and the gap's proposal as what acquiring it must deliver."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Carries the capability the gap proposal names as missing, so the witness is the absent capability itself rather than a note that something was unavailable. That is what makes the rejection actionable as an acquisition."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:refinementRejectedOnResource
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:ResourceLease ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "refinement rejected on resource"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a waiting step to the logic:ResourceLease whose scope covers what it awaits — the logic:RejectedResource case, carrying the exclusive claim as its witness. Domain a plan step or action schema (stated in prose). Distinguished from a capability rejection: the ability exists, the means to exercise it is claimed by somebody else, and the remedy is to wait for or preempt the lease rather than to acquire anything."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:valveStep logic:refinementRejectedOnResource ex:valveLease ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when what blocks a candidate is an exclusive claim on a resource it needs."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it where the capability itself is absent; a lease on a capability nobody has is not what stands in the way."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Read the witness lease's owner and expiry to decide between waiting and escalating."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Carries the resource lease whose scope covers what the step awaits, so the witness is the exclusive claim rather than a note that something was busy. The lease names a holder, so the wait has an owner to escalate to."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:refinementRejectedOnApproval
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:FrontierEntry ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "refinement rejected on approval"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a step to the logic:FrontierEntry whose approval axis sits at logic:ApprovalCreated — created and NOT yet detached — the logic:RejectedApproval case, carrying that entry as its witness. Domain a plan step or action schema (stated in prose). Distinguished from the other three: nothing about the world or the actor is lacking, only the permission, so the remedy is a decision by somebody rather than a change to anything."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:storeReceiptStep logic:refinementRejectedOnApproval ex:storeReceiptEntry ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the only thing between a candidate and execution is an undetached authorization."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid reading it as a defect: an approval-gated step is working exactly as the policy intends, and only the roster's silence about it would be the failure."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Read the witness entry's axis positions to see that the step is simultaneously ready and gated — two axes, not one blended state."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Carries the frontier entry whose approval axis sits at created-and-not-yet-detached as its witness, so the rejection points at a live commitment rather than at an absence. A missing commitment is a different case and a different remedy."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# --- The eleven rules that ARE the search -------------------------------------
#
# All positive Horn and all binary, which is what admits them to the incrementally
# maintainable fragment: a refinement then runs as one budgeted transaction whose
# completeness is the engine's own outcome, not a status a search invented for itself.
#
# They are DERIVATION rules and deliberately not violation rules: their heads publish
# refinement structure, never logic:violatedLaw, so nothing here competes with the
# enactment-integrity laws for a head tuple.

logic:ruleMethodYieldCellHead
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "method yield cell: the head of the yields list"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:methodYieldCell for the cell a method's logic:methodYields names directly — the base case of the ordered-carrier walk. Positive Horn. Without it the walk has nowhere to start, and a method's sequence would be reachable only by a reader who already knew where the list began."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleMethodYieldCellHead ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?method" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:methodYieldCell ;
        rdf:object    "?cell"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?method" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:methodYields ;
        rdf:object    "?cell"
    ] .

logic:ruleMethodYieldCellRest
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "method yield cell: every rdf:rest of a cell already reached"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:methodYieldCell for the rdf:rest of any cell already reached — the recursive case that walks the ordered carrier to rdf:nil. Positive Horn and recursive. This is where the SEQUENCE stops being a parser's private business: the whole list is in the closure, so the steps past the head are as visible to a query as the first one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleMethodYieldCellRest ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?method" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:methodYieldCell ;
        rdf:object    "?next"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?method" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:methodYieldCell ;
        rdf:object    "?cell"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?cell" ;
        rdf:predicate rdf:rest ;
        rdf:object    "?next"
    ] .

logic:ruleMethodStep
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "method step: the rdf:first of every reached cell"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:methodStep for the subtask carried by each logic:methodYieldCell. Positive Horn. It projects the ordered carrier into the membership relation joins need, while leaving the order where it was authored — so a join never has to pretend a set is a sequence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleMethodStep ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?method" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:methodStep ;
        rdf:object    "?step"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?method" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:methodYieldCell ;
        rdf:object    "?cell"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?cell" ;
        rdf:predicate rdf:first ;
        rdf:object    "?step"
    ] .

logic:ruleRefinementExpands
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "refinement expands: one method application"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:refinementExpands from a method that decomposes a task and yields a step — ONE means-end application, made a fact. Positive Horn. This is the smallest unit of the search, and having it in the closure is what lets 'why is this subtask here' be answered by the graph rather than by reading an evaluator."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleRefinementExpands ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?task" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:refinementExpands ;
        rdf:object    "?step"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?method" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:methodDecomposes ;
        rdf:object    "?task"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?method" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:methodStep ;
        rdf:object    "?step"
    ] .

logic:ruleRefinementReachesBase
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "refinement reaches: one application is a reach"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:refinementReaches from a single logic:refinementExpands — the base case of the expansion closure. Positive Horn."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleRefinementReachesBase ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?task" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:refinementReaches ;
        rdf:object    "?step"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?task" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:refinementExpands ;
        rdf:object    "?step"
    ] .

logic:ruleRefinementReachesTransitive
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "refinement reaches: the transitive closure of expansion"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:refinementReaches transitively, so the whole expansion of a task is a single derived relation. Positive Horn and recursive, and it TERMINATES on every method set because the closure is a finite relation over the tasks the data names — a cyclic method set yields a self-edge rather than a divergence, which is precisely what makes the cycle reportable instead of fatal."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleRefinementReachesTransitive ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?task" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:refinementReaches ;
        rdf:object    "?far"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?task" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:refinementReaches ;
        rdf:object    "?mid"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?mid" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:refinementReaches ;
        rdf:object    "?far"
    ] .

logic:ruleRefinementCandidateMethod
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "refinement candidate: every method that decomposes the task"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:refinementCandidateMethod for each logic:DecompositionMethod decomposing a task, orienting the roster on the TASK so a refinement can be asked of the thing being refined. Positive Horn. Every method is a member: applicability is reported by the typed rejections alongside the roster rather than by silently shortening it, because a candidate removed without a reason is indistinguishable from one nobody thought of."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleRefinementCandidateMethod ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?task" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:refinementCandidateMethod ;
        rdf:object    "?method"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?method" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:methodDecomposes ;
        rdf:object    "?task"
    ] .

logic:ruleRefinementRejectedOnPrecondition
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "rejection: a denied action gate names the precondition it judged"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:refinementRejectedOnPrecondition for a step whose logic:GateProbe returned logic:GateDenied, witnessed by the step's own logic:precondition. Positive Horn: the gate is the authority on whether the situation obtained, and this rule projects its verdict onto the refinement roster rather than re-deciding it. The witness is the CONDITION, not the probe, because 'establish this' is the actionable form and 'a probe was denied' is not."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleRefinementRejectedOnPrecondition ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?step" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:refinementRejectedOnPrecondition ;
        rdf:object    "?situation"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?probe" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:probesSchema ;
        rdf:object    "?step"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?probe" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:gateVerdict ;
        rdf:object    logic:GateDenied
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?step" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:precondition ;
        rdf:object    "?situation"
    ] .

logic:ruleRefinementRejectedOnCapability
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "rejection: an operational capability gap names the missing capability"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:refinementRejectedOnCapability for a step a logic:OperationalCapabilityGap blocks, witnessed by the logic:proposalMissingCapability of the proposal standing on the SAME step. Positive Horn. Joining gap and proposal on the blocked step is what keeps the answer specific: a deployment with several gaps must not attribute one gap's missing capability to another gap's step."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleRefinementRejectedOnCapability ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?step" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:refinementRejectedOnCapability ;
        rdf:object    "?capability"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?gap" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:gapBlockedStep ;
        rdf:object    "?step"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?proposal" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:proposalBlockedStep ;
        rdf:object    "?step"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?proposal" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:proposalMissingCapability ;
        rdf:object    "?capability"
    ] .

logic:ruleRefinementRejectedOnResource
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "rejection: an awaited resource is under an exclusive lease"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:refinementRejectedOnResource for a step whose frontier entry awaits something a logic:ResourceLease scopes, witnessed by that lease. Positive Horn. The join is on the AWAITED thing rather than on the step, because a lease knows nothing about plans: what makes it the obstruction is that its scope covers precisely what the step is waiting for."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleRefinementRejectedOnResource ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?step" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:refinementRejectedOnResource ;
        rdf:object    "?lease"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAction ;
        rdf:object    "?step"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAwaits ;
        rdf:object    "?resource"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?lease" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:leaseScope ;
        rdf:object    "?resource"
    ] .

logic:ruleRefinementRejectedOnApproval
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "rejection: an approval exists and has not been detached"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:refinementRejectedOnApproval for a step whose frontier entry sits at logic:ApprovalCreated, witnessed by that entry. Positive Horn. Created-and-undetached is the one approval position that blocks WITHOUT anything being wrong, which is why it must be reported rather than left to a reader to notice that a ready step never ran."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleRefinementRejectedOnApproval ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?step" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:refinementRejectedOnApproval ;
        rdf:object    "?entry"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAction ;
        rdf:object    "?step"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:ApprovalCreated
    ] .

# ── The pin half of refinement: validate the selection, and PIN it ────────────────────
#
# Three laws already read an authored logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph — its completeness,
# its steps against its method, and its membership in the roster it was chosen from. Every
# one of them presupposes a pin somebody typed by hand, which is the defect this kernel
# condemns everywhere else: a record whose whole content is asserted is a record whose
# content nothing can be wrong about, and the laws over it only ever check that an author
# was self-consistent.
#
# The six rules below derive the pin instead. What stays AUTHORED is exactly what cannot be
# derived and should not be: the roster the search produced, the provenance edge from each
# candidate back to the method that produced it, and the logic:AuthorizationProof — the
# organizational act of licensing one candidate. Everything the pin laws read is then read
# off the method: the frozen sequence IS logic:methodYields, the content address IS
# logic:methodDigest, the instantiated method IS the candidate's own provenance, and the
# authority IS the proof that established the candidate.
#
# That construction is what makes the pin laws bite on real data rather than on fixtures.
# logic:PinStepsMatchInstantiatedMethodConstraint can no longer be violated by a DERIVED
# pin at all, because the sequence and the method come from the same join — which is the
# right relationship between a derivation and a law: the law now governs the pins an author
# writes by hand, and the derived ones are correct by construction.
#
# Six rules rather than one conjunctive head, because the authored rule surface is binary
# Horn: one head triple per rule. They share one guard, so they fire together or not at all,
# and each names itself in logic:provenance so a derived pin's every triple carries the rule
# that concluded it.

logic:rulePinnedSubgraphFromAuthorizedCandidate
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "pin: an authorized roster candidate IS a pinned executable subgraph"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Types a logic:refinementCandidate of an episode's roster as a logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph once a logic:AuthorizationProof establishes it. Positive Horn. The authorization is the act that turns a candidate into a commitment, so it is the guard rather than an annotation: a roster member nobody licensed stays a candidate, and typing it a pin would assert a freeze that never happened."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:rulePinnedSubgraphFromAuthorizedCandidate ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?candidate" ;
        rdf:predicate rdf:type ;
        rdf:object    logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?episode" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:producedCandidateSet ;
        rdf:object    "?roster"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?roster" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:refinementCandidate ;
        rdf:object    "?candidate"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?proof" ;
        rdf:predicate rdf:type ;
        rdf:object    logic:AuthorizationProof
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?proof" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:proofEstablishes ;
        rdf:object    "?candidate"
    ] .

logic:rulePinInstantiatesAuthorizedMethod
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "pin: the instantiated method is the candidate's own provenance"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:pinInstantiatesMethod for an authorized candidate from the logic:candidateInstantiatesMethod edge the search recorded when it produced the candidate. Positive Horn. The method is not chosen at pin time — it is the one whose application produced this candidate — so deriving it closes the gap a hand-authored pin leaves open: naming a method the frozen steps never came from."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:rulePinInstantiatesAuthorizedMethod ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?candidate" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:pinInstantiatesMethod ;
        rdf:object    "?method"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?episode" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:producedCandidateSet ;
        rdf:object    "?roster"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?roster" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:refinementCandidate ;
        rdf:object    "?candidate"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?proof" ;
        rdf:predicate rdf:type ;
        rdf:object    logic:AuthorizationProof
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?proof" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:proofEstablishes ;
        rdf:object    "?candidate"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?candidate" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:candidateInstantiatesMethod ;
        rdf:object    "?method"
    ] .

logic:rulePinnedStepSequenceFromMethod
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "pin: the frozen sequence IS the method's yielded sequence"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:pinnedStepSequence for an authorized candidate as the very logic:methodYields list of the method that produced it. Positive Horn. It is the rule that makes logic:PinStepsMatchInstantiatedMethodConstraint unviolatable by a DERIVED pin: the frozen list and the instantiated method are read off one join, so the two cannot disagree. The law keeps its teeth against hand-authored pins, which is where the mismatch was ever possible."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:rulePinnedStepSequenceFromMethod ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?candidate" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:pinnedStepSequence ;
        rdf:object    "?cell"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?episode" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:producedCandidateSet ;
        rdf:object    "?roster"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?roster" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:refinementCandidate ;
        rdf:object    "?candidate"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?proof" ;
        rdf:predicate rdf:type ;
        rdf:object    logic:AuthorizationProof
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?proof" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:proofEstablishes ;
        rdf:object    "?candidate"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?candidate" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:candidateInstantiatesMethod ;
        rdf:object    "?method"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?method" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:methodYields ;
        rdf:object    "?cell"
    ] .

logic:rulePinDigestFromMethodDigest
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "pin: the content address is the method version's own"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:pinDigest for an authorized candidate from the logic:methodDigest of the method that produced it. Positive Horn. A derived pin freezes exactly one thing — the method's yielded sequence — so the address of that content is the address of the method version, and computing a second one would create two names for one fragment. A method carrying no digest therefore yields no pin digest and the pin is REFUSED by the completeness law rather than quietly addressed by a fabricated value, which is the honest failure: 'content-addressed' is a claim about the method, and a method that never made it cannot lend it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:rulePinDigestFromMethodDigest ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?candidate" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:pinDigest ;
        rdf:object    "?digest"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?episode" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:producedCandidateSet ;
        rdf:object    "?roster"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?roster" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:refinementCandidate ;
        rdf:object    "?candidate"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?proof" ;
        rdf:predicate rdf:type ;
        rdf:object    logic:AuthorizationProof
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?proof" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:proofEstablishes ;
        rdf:object    "?candidate"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?candidate" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:candidateInstantiatesMethod ;
        rdf:object    "?method"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?method" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:methodDigest ;
        rdf:object    "?digest"
    ] .

logic:rulePinAuthorityFromEstablishingProof
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "pin: the authority is the proof that established the candidate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:pinAuthority for an authorized candidate as the logic:AuthorizationProof whose logic:proofEstablishes named it. Positive Horn. The guard requires the licensing record to BE an authorization proof, so a logic:Advisory pointed at a candidate derives no authority at all — advice can motivate a pin and never licenses one. An advisory ALSO typed a proof still derives, and is still condemned, by logic:AdvisoryNeverAuthorityConstraint: the derivation does not launder the double typing, it inherits it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:rulePinAuthorityFromEstablishingProof ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?candidate" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:pinAuthority ;
        rdf:object    "?proof"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?episode" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:producedCandidateSet ;
        rdf:object    "?roster"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?roster" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:refinementCandidate ;
        rdf:object    "?candidate"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?proof" ;
        rdf:predicate rdf:type ;
        rdf:object    logic:AuthorizationProof
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?proof" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:proofEstablishes ;
        rdf:object    "?candidate"
    ] .

logic:ruleEpisodeSelectsItsAuthorizedPin
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "pin: the episode selected the pin its own roster carried"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:selectedPin from the episode that produced the roster to the authorized candidate inside it. Positive Horn. It is what makes logic:RefinementPinComesFromCandidateSetConstraint true by construction for a derived pin: the selection is read out of the roster rather than asserted next to it, so an episode cannot be recorded as committing to something its own search never found. The law keeps its teeth against an authored logic:selectedPin, which is the only place a pin from outside the roster could ever enter."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleEpisodeSelectsItsAuthorizedPin ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?episode" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:selectedPin ;
        rdf:object    "?candidate"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?episode" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:producedCandidateSet ;
        rdf:object    "?roster"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?roster" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:refinementCandidate ;
        rdf:object    "?candidate"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?proof" ;
        rdf:predicate rdf:type ;
        rdf:object    logic:AuthorizationProof
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?proof" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:proofEstablishes ;
        rdf:object    "?candidate"
    ] .

logic:CapabilityGapProposalCompletenessConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a capability-gap proposal binds all eight fields"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:CapabilityGapProposal that omits any of its eight mandatory fields. A partial proposal reports a blockage without its remedy, which is exactly the outcome raising a gap instead of substituting a weaker capability is meant to avoid."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:CapabilityGapProposal must bind all eight fields: missing capability, required contract, expected inputs, expected outputs, expected effects, verification method, security and lifecycle needs, and the blocked step." ;
    logic:integrity logic:cgpForall .
logic:cgpForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:cgpImpl .
logic:cgpImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:cgpGuard ; logic:consequent logic:cgpCons .
logic:cgpGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:CapabilityGapProposal ] .
logic:cgpCons a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:cgpCap , logic:cgpContract , logic:cgpIn , logic:cgpOut , logic:cgpEff , logic:cgpVerify , logic:cgpSec , logic:cgpStep .
logic:cgpCap a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:proposalMissingCapability ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "cap" ] .
logic:cgpContract a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:proposalRequiredContract ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "contract" ] .
logic:cgpIn a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:proposalExpectedInputs ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "inputs" ] .
logic:cgpOut a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:proposalExpectedOutputs ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "outputs" ] .
logic:cgpEff a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:proposalExpectedEffects ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "effects" ] .
logic:cgpVerify a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:proposalVerificationMethod ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "verify" ] .
logic:cgpSec a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:proposalSecurityLifecycle ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "sec" ] .
logic:cgpStep a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:proposalBlockedStep ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "step" ] .

logic:AdvisoryNeverAuthorityConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "an advisory never occupies an authority position"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:Advisory that occupies ANY of the three authority positions logic:Advisory's own definition bars it from: authorizing a pin (logic:pinAuthority), carrying the proof that licensed an intent (logic:proofEstablishes), or standing as the decision an approval reached (logic:approvalDecision). Model-generated content is attributed evidence and may never be the thing that licenses an action; the separation is enforced structurally rather than left to convention, because a convention is exactly what fails under time pressure. The three legs are one law rather than three because they are one prohibition read at three seams — and a law that guarded only the pin seam would leave the other two open while its name claimed all of them, which is how advice arrives as proof."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:Advisory may not authorize a pin (logic:pinAuthority), establish an intent's licensing (logic:proofEstablishes), or be an approval's decision (logic:approvalDecision): model-generated advice is evidence, never authority." ;
    logic:integrity logic:anaForall .
logic:anaForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:anaImpl .
logic:anaImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:anaGuard ; logic:consequent logic:anaCons .
logic:anaGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:Advisory ] .
logic:anaCons a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:anaNotAuthority , logic:anaNotProof , logic:anaNotDecision .
logic:anaNotAuthority a logic:Formula ; logic:not logic:anaAuthorizes .
logic:anaAuthorizes a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:pinAuthority ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "pin" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] .
logic:anaNotProof a logic:Formula ; logic:not logic:anaProves .
logic:anaProves a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:proofEstablishes ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "licensed" ] .
logic:anaNotDecision a logic:Formula ; logic:not logic:anaDecides .
logic:anaDecides a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:approvalDecision ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "approval" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] .

logic:RefinementEpisodeDeclaresSearchFragmentConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a refinement episode declares its search fragment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:RefinementEpisode that produced a candidate set without declaring the decidable fragment its search ran within. An undeclared fragment makes the roster's closedness unfalsifiable, since a reader cannot tell whether termination was earned or assumed. The law checks the DECLARATION, which is what the record can carry; the roster's closure itself is a property of a search that has already finished and left no other trace, so this is deliberately not named a closure law."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:RefinementEpisode that produced a candidate set must declare its logic:searchFragment; otherwise the roster's completeness cannot be judged." ;
    logic:integrity logic:rcsForall .
logic:rcsForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:rcsImpl .
logic:rcsImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:rcsGuard ; logic:consequent logic:rcsCons .
logic:rcsGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:producedCandidateSet ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "set" ] .
logic:rcsCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:searchFragment ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "frag" ] .

# The roster's closedness IS checkable in one respect the declaration cannot reach: a
# selected pin that was never in the roster means the roster was not the set the episode
# chose from, so its completeness claim is about a different search than the one that
# produced the commitment. That is a relation between three records and no field on any of
# them.
logic:RefinementPinComesFromCandidateSetConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a selected pin was in the roster it was selected from"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:RefinementEpisode whose logic:selectedPin is not a logic:refinementCandidate of the logic:RefinementCandidateSet it produced. A roster is a claim about what the search FOUND, and a commitment to something outside it says the roster describes some other search — after which its closedness, its budget and its declared fragment all certify a deliberation that did not produce this decision. The failure is invisible to any check over one record, since the episode, the roster and the pin are each individually well-formed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:RefinementEpisode's logic:selectedPin must be a logic:refinementCandidate of the roster it produced; committing to a candidate the roster never contained makes the roster's closure a claim about a different search." ;
    logic:integrity logic:rpcForall .
logic:rpcForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:rpcImpl .
logic:rpcImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:rpcGuard ; logic:consequent logic:rpcCons .
logic:rpcGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:rpcSet , logic:rpcPin .
logic:rpcSet a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:producedCandidateSet ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "roster" ] .
logic:rpcPin a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:selectedPin ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "pin" ] .
logic:rpcCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:refinementCandidate ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "roster" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "pin" ] .

# The pin's own two laws. Immutability is only a security property if the pin HAS content:
# a record carrying a label and nothing else is immutable the way an empty box is locked.
# The first law says the content exists; the second says it is the content that was
# validated. They are separate because a relational body needs the bindings in its GUARD,
# which puts the missing-binding case outside it.
logic:PinnedSubgraphCompletenessConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a pinned subgraph binds content, address and method"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph that omits its logic:pinnedStepSequence, its logic:pinDigest, or the logic:pinInstantiatesMethod it was produced from. Each omission empties the pin of the thing it exists to carry: with no step sequence there is no fragment, with no digest the fragment has no address an approval can bind to, and with no method the fragment cannot be checked against the schema it claims to instantiate. A pin missing all three is a label asserting that what was validated is what executes, which is precisely the claim the pin was introduced to make checkable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph must bind logic:pinnedStepSequence, logic:pinDigest and logic:pinInstantiatesMethod; a pin with no frozen content, no content address and no method is a label, not a commitment." ;
    logic:integrity logic:pscForall .
logic:pscForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:pscImpl .
logic:pscImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:pscGuard ; logic:consequent logic:pscCons .
logic:pscGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph ] .
logic:pscCons a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:pscSteps , logic:pscDigest , logic:pscMethod .
logic:pscSteps a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:pinnedStepSequence ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "steps" ] .
logic:pscDigest a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:pinDigest ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "digest" ] .
logic:pscMethod a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:pinInstantiatesMethod ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "method" ] .

logic:PinStepsMatchInstantiatedMethodConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a pin freezes the steps the method it names yields"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph whose logic:pinnedStepSequence is not the logic:methodYields sequence of the logic:DecompositionMethod its logic:pinInstantiatesMethod names. Naming a method and freezing some OTHER sequence is the exact failure pinning exists to prevent: the approval was reasoned about against the method's decomposition, and the dispatch runs the pin's, so the authorized argument set and the executed one differ while every record involved stays individually well-formed. Because both sides are one ordered rdf:List, the join is over the whole sequence at once — a pin that keeps the method's steps but reorders them is a different list and is condemned, which is the point of carrying order at all."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph's logic:pinnedStepSequence must be the logic:methodYields sequence of the logic:DecompositionMethod it instantiates; a pin freezing other steps, or the same steps in another order, executes something the validation never saw." ;
    logic:integrity logic:pmmForall .
logic:pmmForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:pmmImpl .
logic:pmmImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:pmmGuard ; logic:consequent logic:pmmCons .
logic:pmmGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:pmmIsPin , logic:pmmMethod , logic:pmmSteps .
logic:pmmIsPin a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:PinnedExecutableSubgraph ] .
logic:pmmMethod a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:pinInstantiatesMethod ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "method" ] .
logic:pmmSteps a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:pinnedStepSequence ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "steps" ] .
logic:pmmCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:methodYields ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "method" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "steps" ] .

# =========================================================================== #
# === Enactment layer: the external-effect commitment layer (the saga)     === #
# =========================================================================== #
#
# Everything here exists because the boundary between "we intended an effect" and
# "the world changed" is not crossable by assertion. The layer records intent,
# attempt, receipt, reconciliation and compensation as separate durable facts,
# keeps PROTOCOL POSITION apart from COMMITMENT KNOWLEDGE, and never lets the
# reasoner conclude that an effect happened.
#
# The engine describes, validates, derives and certifies these records. A
# consumer-owned organ performs persistence and physical dispatch: no derivation
# in this layer may ever emit an attempt or a receipt.

logic:DispatchIntent
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Dispatch Intent"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The durable pre-effect record: what is about to be done to the world, bound tightly enough that nothing else can be substituted for it. Nine bindings are mandatory - the enactment, the step, the action-schema version, the exactly normalized arguments, the target, the governing policy, the approval requirement, the content digest, and the journal head it is applied against. The digest is what an approval binds to; the journal head is what makes a resubmission after an interruption structurally detectable rather than a matter of remembering."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:payIntent a logic:DispatchIntent ; logic:intentDigest \"blake3:7f21\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use before any effect leaves the system, so a crash at the boundary leaves an analyzable record."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid dispatching without one, and avoid reusing an intent for a second dispatch (a repeat mints a new intent)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind all nine fields and persist before attempting; the completeness constraint rejects a partial intent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Nine bindings are mandatory together; the record exists to make substitution impossible, so a partially bound intent is not a weaker intent but an unusable one. Omitting any binding restores exactly the substitution the record was built to prevent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:intentOfEnactment
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DispatchIntent ;
    rdfs:range logic:Enactment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "intent of enactment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Binding one of nine: the logic:Enactment the intent belongs to. Functional. It is the binding that means an authorization issued for one run cannot license an effect in another, and scope is checked before anything else about the intent is worth comparing."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeIntent logic:intentOfEnactment ex:settlementRun ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind a dispatch intent to the enactment on whose behalf it acts."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an intent with no enactment: nothing owns it, so nothing is accountable for reconciling it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "One enactment per intent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "What stops an authorization issued for one run from licensing an effect in another. It is binding one of nine because scope is checked before anything else about the intent is worth comparing."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:intentOfStep
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DispatchIntent ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "intent of step"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Binding two of nine: the step the intent realizes, so a dispatch is always attributable to a place in the prescription. Range a plan step (stated in prose). Functional. An intent naming no step is an effect the plan never asked for, however well authorized it may otherwise be."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeIntent logic:intentOfStep ex:chargeStep ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind an intent to the step it realises."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid omitting the step; without it the intent cannot be placed on the frontier."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "One step per intent, so an effect is traceable to a position in the plan."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Names the step the intent realizes, so a dispatch is always attributable to a place in the prescription. An intent with no step is an effect the plan never asked for, however well authorized it may be."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:intentActionSchemaVersion
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DispatchIntent ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "intent action schema version"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Binding three of nine: the exact action-schema version being invoked. Range a versioned action schema (stated in prose). Functional. Naming the version, not the schema, stops a later schema revision from re-interpreting an authorized intent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeIntent logic:intentActionSchemaVersion ex:chargeSchemaV2 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to pin the exact action-schema version the dispatch uses."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid pointing at an unversioned schema; a schema that changes after approval changes what was approved."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Pin the version, not the schema, so the approved shape is the dispatched shape."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Naming the version, not the schema, stops a later schema revision from changing what an approved intent means. The approval was granted against one reading of the action, and only the version pins that reading."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:intentNormalizedArguments
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DispatchIntent ;
    rdfs:range logic:ArgumentNormalization ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "intent normalized arguments"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Binding four of nine: the arguments under the pinned normalization recipe. Functional. Normalization is part of the binding rather than an implementation detail: two normalizations of the same arguments would let an approval be obtained for one serialization and an effect dispatched under another."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeIntent logic:intentNormalizedArguments ex:chargeArgs ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the pinned normalization of the dispatch arguments."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid two normalizations of the same arguments: approve the benign serialization and dispatch the other, and the approval has been bypassed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Pin the recipe, not just the result, so the normalization is reproducible."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Normalization is part of the binding rather than an implementation detail: two normalizations of the same arguments produce two digests, and an approval obtained under one authorizes nothing under the other."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:intentTarget
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DispatchIntent ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "intent target"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Binding five of nine: the external resource or provider the effect is aimed at. Range a target descriptor (stated in prose). Functional. An approval for one target does not carry over to another, which is why the target is a binding rather than an annotation: the same action against a different provider is a different act with a different blast radius."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeIntent logic:intentTarget ex:paymentsEndpoint ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the external target the effect is dispatched against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a target that can be repointed after approval; approval binds by digest precisely because references move."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the resource the provider correlates against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "An approval for one target authorizes nothing against another, which is why the target is a binding rather than an annotation. The same action against a different provider is a different act with a different blast radius."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:intentPolicy
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DispatchIntent ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "intent policy"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Binding six of nine: the policy governing whether this dispatch is permitted. Range a governing policy (stated in prose). Functional. A domain slice specializes this to its own normative vocabulary; left at the kernel level the property is legal but inert, because an opaque reference cannot be reached by queries that range over norms."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeIntent logic:intentPolicy ex:settlementPolicy ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the policy governing whether this dispatch is permitted."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid leaving it unbound on an approval-gated dispatch; the approver needs to know what they are approving under."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the policy an auditor would check the dispatch against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A domain slice specializes this to its own normative vocabulary, which is what turns the governing policy from an opaque reference into something queries over norms can reach. The kernel property alone is legal but inert."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:intentApprovalRequired
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DispatchIntent ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "intent approval required"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Binding seven of nine: whether this dispatch needs an authorization before it may proceed. Functional. Recorded ON the intent so the requirement is fixed at intent time and cannot be relaxed afterwards by re-reading policy."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeIntent logic:intentApprovalRequired false ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to say whether the dispatch is approval-gated."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid defaulting everything to true: a system that gates everything trains its operator to approve without reading."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set it from policy, so policy-maximal automation is a derivation rather than a habit."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Recorded ON the intent so the requirement is fixed at intent time and cannot be relaxed afterwards by a policy edit. A requirement read live from policy would let a dispatch escape review by outliving the rule that demanded it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:intentDigest
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DispatchIntent ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "intent digest"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Binding eight of nine: the algorithm-prefixed content address over the whole intent. This is the value an authorization binds to, so a revised intent has a different digest and detaches no existing commitment. It is algorithm-prefixed because a bare digest cannot be compared across a change of hash function."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeIntent logic:intentDigest \"b3:6c1a80…\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to content-address the intent, which is what an approval binds to."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid binding an approval by reference instead; a reference can be repointed after approval and a digest cannot."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Address the whole intent, so approving it approves exactly it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The value an authorization binds to, so a revised intent has a different digest and detaches no existing commitment. Algorithm-prefixed, because a bare digest cannot be compared across a change of hash function."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:expectedJournalHead
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DispatchIntent ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "expected journal head"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Binding nine of nine: the journal head this intent is applied against. It is what makes no-blind-retry a property of the hash chain rather than a rule someone must remember to author - a resubmission after an interruption carries a head the journal has already advanced past, and is refused because it is stale rather than because a seen-set recognized it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Makes no-blind-retry a property of the hash chain rather than a rule someone must remember to author. A resubmission after an interruption carries a head the journal has already advanced past, so the refusal is structural and needs no separate policy."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:intent-77 logic:expectedJournalHead \"b3:9d41\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use on every dispatch intent to record the journal head the intent is applied against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid omitting it on a retry path: without it a resubmission is indistinguishable from a first submission, which is exactly the blind retry the binding forbids."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Read the head at intent time and refuse any dispatch whose recorded head no longer matches, so the chain rather than a policy enforces the ordering."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:ArgumentNormalization
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Argument Normalization"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The pinned recipe by which effect arguments are canonicalized before being digested, together with the resulting canonical form. Pinning it is a SECURITY requirement: with two normalizations in play an approval can be obtained for a benign serialization and a materially different one dispatched under the same digest. Only resolved surfaces enter the digest; runtime handles never do."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:payArgs a logic:ArgumentNormalization ; logic:normalizationRecipe \"canonical-nt-sorted\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use wherever effect arguments are digested for authorization or idempotency."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an unpinned recipe, and avoid admitting a runtime handle into a digested surface."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the recipe under a domain tag; semantically equal arguments must digest equal and materially different ones must not."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Pinning the recipe is a SECURITY requirement rather than a tidiness one: with two normalizations in play an approval obtained for a benign canonical form can be replayed against a different reading of the same arguments."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:normalizationRecipe
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ArgumentNormalization ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "normalization recipe"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The named, domain-tagged canonicalization the arguments were reduced under. Functional. Recording the recipe rather than only its output is what lets a verifier recompute the digest instead of trusting it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Recording the recipe rather than only its output is what lets a verifier recompute the digest instead of trusting it. A digest with no named canonicalization is unverifiable in principle, however strong the hash function is."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:intent-77 logic:normalizationRecipe \"urn:gmeow:norm:json-c14n-v2\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to name the domain-tagged canonicalization the intent arguments were reduced under before digesting."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid leaving it implicit because a single tool computes both sides: an independent verifier has no way to reproduce an unnamed reduction."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record the recipe identity beside the digest, so the digest can be recomputed by a party that shares no code with the producer."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:EffectAttempt
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Effect Attempt"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The record that a dispatch was actually made against a provider, carrying the provider's own correlation identity so a later probe can ask about THIS attempt rather than about the intent in general. An attempt is ASSERTED by the organ that performed it and may never be derived: a reasoner that could conclude an attempt happened could conclude the world changed, which is precisely the inference this layer exists to forbid."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:payAttempt1 a logic:EffectAttempt ; logic:providerCorrelationId \"txn-9f21ab\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record that an effect left the system, at the moment it left."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid deriving one, and avoid recording an attempt with no correlation identity (an uncorrelatable attempt cannot be reconciled)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the intent and the provider's correlation identity; the observed-not-derived constraint rejects an attempt carrying derivation provenance."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "ASSERTED by the organ that performed it and never derived: the presence of a derivation identifier on one is precisely what the observed-not-derived guard rejects. It carries the provider's own correlation identity so a later probe can ask about THIS attempt."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:attemptOfIntent
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:EffectAttempt ;
    rdfs:range logic:DispatchIntent ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "attempt of intent"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:EffectAttempt to the logic:DispatchIntent it carried out. Functional per attempt, and several attempts of one intent are legal only where the idempotency contract licenses them. The relation records that the attempt carried out this intent, never that it was entitled to."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeAttempt logic:attemptOfIntent ex:chargeIntent ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind an observed attempt to the intent it acted on."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid deriving an attempt: a reasoner that can conclude an attempt happened can conclude the world changed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Assert it from the organ that performed the dispatch, never from inference."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional per attempt, and several attempts of one intent are legal only where the idempotency contract licenses them. The relation records that the attempt carried out this intent, never that it was entitled to."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:providerCorrelationId
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:EffectAttempt ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "provider correlation identity"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The identity the provider itself assigned to this attempt, by which a later reconciliation probe asks about it. Functional. Without it an unknown outcome is unresolvable in principle, not merely in practice."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Without it an unknown outcome is unresolvable in principle, not merely in practice: there is no handle by which a probe can ask the provider about this attempt. It is therefore a precondition of reconciliation rather than a convenience."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:attempt-5 logic:providerCorrelationId \"req_8f31ab\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record the identity the provider itself assigned to an attempt, by which a later probe can ask about it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid substituting the local intent digest: the provider does not know it, and a probe carrying it can ask no answerable question."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Capture it at the boundary as the attempt is made, since it cannot be recovered afterwards if the call was interrupted."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:ExternalEffectReceipt
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "External Effect Receipt"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The durably recorded, authoritative report of what an attempt achieved, with the evidence supporting it. Like an attempt it is OBSERVED, never derived. A receipt settles the protocol position; it does not by itself settle commitment knowledge, because a receipt that contradicts the local ledger moves knowledge to the contradictory value rather than to supported."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:payReceipt a logic:ExternalEffectReceipt ; logic:receiptOfAttempt ex:payAttempt1 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a provider has authoritatively reported an outcome and the report is durably stored."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a receipt with no attempt behind it, and avoid deriving one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the attempt and the evidence; a receipt without an attempt is rejected."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Like an attempt it is OBSERVED, never derived. A receipt settles the protocol position; it does not by itself settle commitment knowledge, so a receipt contradicting the local ledger moves knowledge to the contradictory value rather than to supported."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:receiptOfAttempt
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ExternalEffectReceipt ;
    rdfs:range logic:EffectAttempt ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "receipt of attempt"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ExternalEffectReceipt to the logic:EffectAttempt it reports on. Functional and MANDATORY: a receipt naming no attempt asserts an outcome nobody caused, and the gate types such a record as faulty rather than leaving the dangling receipt readable as evidence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeReceipt logic:receiptOfAttempt ex:chargeAttempt ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind an observed outcome to the attempt that produced it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a receipt with no attempt; it asserts an outcome nobody caused, which is the shape of a fabricated or mis-imported record."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Assert receipts from observation, never derive them."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional and MANDATORY: a receipt with no attempt asserts an outcome nobody caused. It is one of the kernel's authored laws, so the gate types the offending record rather than leaving the dangling receipt readable as evidence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:receiptEvidence
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ExternalEffectReceipt ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "receipt evidence"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ExternalEffectReceipt to the evidence supporting its report — a provider response, a signed acknowledgement, an observed state change. Range an evidence node (stated in prose). Multi-valued. It carries why anyone should believe the report, not what the report says: stating what happened is the receipt's own job."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeReceipt logic:receiptEvidence ex:providerResponse ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for what makes a receipt believable — the provider's own response or record."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a receipt whose only evidence is our own log line; that records what we sent, not what happened."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Cite provider-side evidence where the provider supplies any."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Multi-valued, since a report may rest on a provider response, a signed acknowledgement and an observed state change at once. The receipt states what happened and this states why anyone should believe it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:ExternalOutcomeUnknown
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "External Outcome Unknown"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The crash-or-timeout boundary: a dispatch may have occurred and no authoritative outcome is known. It is the effect-boundary reading of an UNDETERMINED epistemic state - the engine did not reach a verdict - and never of the conclusive no-support reading, which is banned here because external commitment is never closed-world. Treating this as an ordinary failure and retrying is the single most damaging move available, which is why retry from here requires a discharged idempotency contract or a reconciliation verdict."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:payUnknown a logic:ExternalOutcomeUnknown ; logic:unknownOfAttempt ex:payAttempt1 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when an attempt was made and neither commitment nor non-commitment can be established."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid treating it as a failure, and avoid retrying from it without a licence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the attempt; the frontier derives reconciliation as the next action, never redispatch."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The effect-boundary reading of an UNDETERMINED epistemic state — the engine did not reach a verdict — and never of the conclusive negative. Treating it as a failure closes a question the evidence left open, which is how a committed effect gets dispatched twice."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:unknownOfAttempt
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ExternalOutcomeUnknown ;
    rdfs:range logic:EffectAttempt ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "unknown of attempt"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ExternalOutcomeUnknown to the attempt whose outcome is undetermined. Functional. It records a knowledge claim about that exchange and is not a failure claim: the attempt may well have committed, and the record says only that nobody knows."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeUnknown logic:unknownOfAttempt ex:chargeAttempt ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record that an attempt's outcome is undetermined."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an unknown naming no attempt: it is unresolvable in principle, so nothing could ever close it or license a retry from it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the attempt, which is what a reconciliation probe will ask about."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional, so an unknown-outcome record names one attempt. Its presence is a knowledge claim about that exchange and never a failure claim: the attempt may well have committed, and the record says only that nobody knows."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:ReconciliationImpossible
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Reconciliation Impossible"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The state in which no reconciliation semantics remain available: the provider is gone, exposes no query interface, or its retention window has elapsed. Distinct from an unknown outcome, which is not-yet-probed and still resolvable - this is the NOT-EVALUATED reading, where the engine could not look at all. It demands a different next action (escalation) and must never be silently folded into the resolvable case."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:payUnresolvable a logic:ReconciliationImpossible ; logic:impossibleOfAttempt ex:payAttempt1 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the means of determining what happened no longer exist."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid conflating with an unknown outcome, and avoid using it merely because a probe failed once."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the attempt and record why reconciliation is foreclosed; the frontier surfaces it for escalation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Distinct from an unknown outcome, which is not-yet-probed and still resolvable. Here the provider is gone, exposes no query interface, or its retention has elapsed, so the next move is escalation rather than another probe."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:impossibleOfAttempt
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ReconciliationImpossible ;
    rdfs:range logic:EffectAttempt ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "impossible of attempt"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ReconciliationImpossible to the attempt whose outcome can no longer be determined by any available means. Functional. It is a claim about the exhaustion of probes and not about the effect having failed, and the two must never be read alike."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Names the attempt whose outcome can no longer be determined by any available means. It is a claim about the exhaustion of probes, not about the effect having failed, and the two must not be read alike."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:impossible-1 logic:impossibleOfAttempt ex:attempt-5 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind a reconciliation-impossible record to the attempt it forecloses."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid recording it while a probe channel remains open, however slow: foreclosure is permanent and discards the only knowledge-increasing move."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it once every probe avenue is closed, and let the frontier derive the escalation label from it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:DeliverySemantics
    a logic:AbstractIndividualType ,
        logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:QualityValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Delivery Semantics"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "What a provider actually guarantees about duplicate delivery, not what a consumer's idempotency contract assumes of it. A retry licence is only sound RELATIVE to this: the same idempotency contract means something different against an at-most-once provider than against an at-least-once one, and assuming exactly-once where the provider offers at-least-once is how duplicate effects happen."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:payProvider logic:deliverySemantics logic:DeliveryAtLeastOnce ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to declare what a provider guarantees before relying on any retry."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid assuming exactly-once, and avoid omitting it from an idempotency contract."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Select one member per provider contract; the retry law reads it together with the retention window."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A retry licence is only sound RELATIVE to this value: the same idempotency contract means something different against an at-most-once provider than against an at-least-once one, and assuming exactly-once is how duplicate charges happen."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:DeliveryAtMostOnce a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:DeliverySemantics ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "at most once"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The provider may drop but never duplicate. A retry is safe from a duplication standpoint but may silently lose the effect entirely."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The dual hazard: no duplicate, but a silent drop. Under this value an absent receipt is genuinely ambiguous between never sent and sent and lost, so it is the value that most often forces reconciliation rather than retry."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:contract-notify logic:idempotencyDuplicateSemantics logic:DeliveryAtMostOnce ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a fire-and-forget provider that never re-sends, so the consumer must treat a missing receipt as a knowledge gap to be probed rather than a signal to resend."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a provider that merely lacks a documented guarantee: an unknown semantics is not at-most-once, and recording a guess here makes an undetermined position look settled."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it on the idempotency contract and expect the effect protocol to move through reconciliation, since retrying under this value risks losing the effect entirely rather than duplicating it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:DeliveryAtLeastOnce a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:DeliverySemantics ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "at least once"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The provider may duplicate but never drop. A retry is only safe under a live idempotency key, which is why the retention window is load-bearing rather than informational."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A statement about the provider's duplication behaviour only. It carries no claim that a duplicate is harmless: safety under retry comes from a live idempotency key inside its retention window, so this value plus an expired retention is not a licence to retry."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:contract-pay logic:idempotencyDuplicateSemantics logic:DeliveryAtLeastOnce ; logic:idempotencyRetention ex:window-24h ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a provider that acknowledges only after durable acceptance and therefore re-sends on doubt, so the consumer must be prepared for duplicates and never for loss."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it as shorthand for safe to retry: the retention window is what makes a retry safe, and this value alone leaves that window unstated."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it on the idempotency contract alongside logic:idempotencyRetention, so a retry decision can be checked against the window rather than against the provider's reputation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:DeliveryExactlyOnce a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:DeliverySemantics ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "exactly once"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The provider guarantees neither loss nor duplication for a given key within its retention window. The guarantee lapses with the window, so it is not a standing property of the provider."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Scoped to a key and a window, never to the provider as such. Outside the retention window the same provider offers no guarantee at all, so this value must always be read together with the retention it was granted under."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:contract-charge logic:idempotencyDuplicateSemantics logic:DeliveryExactlyOnce ; logic:idempotencyKey ex:key-9f ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the provider documents deduplication for a named key within a stated retention, and the enactment actually supplies that key on every attempt."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it as a standing property of the provider: once the retention lapses the guarantee is gone, and a stale claim here is what turns a safe retry into a double charge."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:idempotencyKey and logic:idempotencyRetention together, so the retry licence can be derived from the window rather than assumed from the label."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:IdempotencyContract
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Idempotency Contract"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The declared basis on which a repeated dispatch is safe: its scope, its key, its retention window, what the provider does with a duplicate, and the evidence that the provider actually supports it. All five are mandatory, and the retention window is not decoration - a retry after the provider has forgotten the key is exactly a duplicate effect, which is the classic production failure this record exists to prevent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:payIdempotency a logic:IdempotencyContract ; logic:idempotencyRetention \"P1D\"^^xsd:duration ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use wherever a retry might occur, before any retry occurs."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid asserting one without provider evidence, and avoid relying on it past its retention window."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind all five fields; the retry law reads the retention window and the delivery semantics together."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "All five parts are mandatory and the retention window is load-bearing rather than informational: outside it the provider guarantees nothing, so a contract quoted without its window supports no retry decision at all."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:idempotencyScope
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:IdempotencyContract ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "idempotency scope"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Field one of five: the scope within which the key is unique. Range a scope descriptor (stated in prose). Functional. A key unique per account is not the same claim as one unique per tenant, so the scope is what fixes whether two submissions can collide at all; omitting it leaves the key's uniqueness unquantified."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeIdem logic:idempotencyScope \"provider:acme:merchant-88\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the scope within which the key is unique."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid assuming global uniqueness; the same key under a different merchant or controller is a different key."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "State the scope the provider actually enforces uniqueness within."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A key unique per account means something different from one unique per tenant, so the scope is what fixes whether two submissions can collide at all. Omitting it leaves the key's uniqueness claim unquantified."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:idempotencyKey
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:IdempotencyContract ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "idempotency key"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Field two of five: the key the provider deduplicates on. Functional. Derived from the delta content address rather than assigned, so two submissions of the same change carry the same key by construction."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeIdem logic:idempotencyKey \"invoice:90233:charge:1\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the key the provider deduplicates on."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a key derived from the time of sending; a retry would carry a different key and duplicate the effect."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Derive it from the intent, so a retry of the same intent carries the same key."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Derived from the delta content address rather than assigned, so two submissions of the same change carry the same key by construction. An assigned key makes deduplication depend on the caller remembering to reuse it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:idempotencyRetention
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:IdempotencyContract ;
    rdfs:range xsd:duration ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "idempotency retention"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Field three of five: how long the provider remembers the key. Functional and LOAD-BEARING: past this window a retry is a duplicate effect, so the retry law reads it rather than treating the contract as timeless."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeIdem logic:idempotencyRetention \"PT24H\"^^xsd:duration ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for how long the provider honours the key."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid retrying after it elapses: that is not an idempotent repeat, it is a second effect, and it is the classic production bug."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Read the window before licensing a retry, not after the duplicate appears."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "LOAD-BEARING rather than informational: past this window a retry is a duplicate effect, so the retry law reads it rather than treating the contract as timeless. A contract quoted without its retention supports no retry at all."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:idempotencyDuplicateSemantics
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:IdempotencyContract ;
    rdfs:range logic:DeliverySemantics ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "idempotency duplicate semantics"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Field four of five: what the provider guarantees about duplicates. Functional. It is what makes the contract's safety claim relative to a real provider rather than an assumption."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeIdem logic:idempotencyDuplicateSemantics \"Repeated key within the window returns the original result.\"@x-gmeow-english ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to state what the provider does with a duplicate key."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid assuming collapse-to-original; some providers error, and the retry policy differs accordingly."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "State the observed behaviour, so the retry licence is sound relative to the provider rather than to hope."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "What makes the contract's safety claim relative to a real provider rather than an assumption. A contract that omits it is a statement about how the caller would like duplicates to behave, not about how they will."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:idempotencyProviderEvidence
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:IdempotencyContract ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "idempotency provider evidence"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Field five of five: the evidence that the provider actually implements this contract — documentation, a conformance probe, a signed statement. Range an evidence node (stated in prose). Functional. It is what makes the other four checkable: without it the contract records what someone believes the provider does rather than what has been established."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeIdem logic:idempotencyProviderEvidence \"Provider API v3 idempotency spec, section 4.2.\"@x-gmeow-english ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to evidence that the provider actually supports the contract claimed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid claiming idempotency the provider never documented or was never probed for; the whole retry policy rests on it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Cite the specification or the conformance probe, so the claim is checkable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Field five of five, and the one that makes the other four checkable: documentation, a conformance probe or a signed statement. Without it the contract records what someone believes the provider does rather than what has been established."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:intentIdempotency
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DispatchIntent ;
    rdfs:range logic:IdempotencyContract ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "intent idempotency"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:DispatchIntent to the contract under which a repeat of it would be safe. Optional: an intent with no idempotency contract simply may not be retried."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeIntent logic:intentIdempotency ex:chargeIdem ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to attach the idempotency contract governing retries of this dispatch."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid retrying without one; a retry with no idempotency contract is a second effect, not a repeat of the first."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the contract before dispatch, not after an outcome goes undetermined."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Optional, and its absence is meaningful: an intent with no idempotency contract simply may not be retried. Reading the absence as permission is exactly how a single charge becomes two."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:ReconciliationVerdict
    a logic:AbstractIndividualType ,
        logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:QualityValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Reconciliation Verdict"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed three-valued outcome of asking a provider what actually happened: committed, not committed, or still unknown. The third value is not a failure of the probe but a real answer, and keeping it means an unresolved effect stays visible instead of collapsing into one of the two comfortable answers."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:payProbe1 logic:reconciliationVerdict logic:ReconciledCommitted ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record what a reconciliation probe established."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid forcing a still-unknown result into committed or not-committed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach exactly one member to the probe result; still-unknown leaves the effect in its prior epistemic position."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Three-valued, and the third value is a real answer rather than a probe failure. Keeping it means an unresolved effect stays visible instead of being closed as a failed query, which is how an unresolved effect quietly disappears."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ReconciledCommitted a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:ReconciliationVerdict ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "committed"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The provider confirms the effect took place. This is the verdict that makes compensation eligible and retry forbidden, in one move. It is a statement about the external world and not about the local ledger, so it stands even where the local record holds no receipt."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The verdict that makes compensation eligible and retry forbidden, in one move. It is a statement about the external world, not about the local ledger, so it stands even where the local record has no receipt."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:probe-8 logic:reconciliationVerdict logic:ReconciledCommitted ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the provider authoritatively confirms the effect took place."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid inferring it from a receipt alone: a receipt is the provider's report at dispatch time, and reconciliation is the after-the-fact determination that outranks it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:reconciliationVerdict and let the frontier derive compensation eligibility from it rather than from an operator's judgment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ReconciledNotCommitted a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:ReconciliationVerdict ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "not committed"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The provider confirms the effect did not take place. This is the verdict that makes a fresh dispatch safe and compensation meaningless."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Makes a fresh dispatch safe and compensation meaningless at the same time. Both consequences follow from the same fact, which is why the verdict is recorded once rather than split into a retry permission and a compensation prohibition."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:probe-8 logic:reconciliationVerdict logic:ReconciledNotCommitted ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the provider authoritatively confirms the effect did not take place."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a probe that simply found no record: absence of evidence at the provider is the still-unknown verdict, not a confirmation of non-occurrence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:reconciliationVerdict, and let the retry licence be derived from it together with the idempotency contract."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ReconciledStillUnknown a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:ReconciliationVerdict ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "still unknown"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The probe completed and the provider could not say. A real answer, not a probe failure: it leaves the effect exactly where it was and licenses neither retry nor compensation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A real answer rather than a probe failure. The probe ran and the provider could not say, which leaves the effect exactly where it was and licenses neither retry nor compensation — a distinction lost if it is recorded as an error."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:probe-8 logic:reconciliationVerdict logic:ReconciledStillUnknown ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the probe completed and the provider could give no determination either way."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when the probe itself failed to run: an unexecuted probe leaves no verdict, and recording one would claim an answer nobody gave."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:reconciliationVerdict and keep the effect in its undetermined position, so a later probe under better conditions remains licensed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ReconciliationProbe
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Reconciliation Probe"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A query to a provider asking what became of a specific attempt, addressed by that attempt's correlation identity. It is the only knowledge-increasing move available from an undetermined position - retrying the effect is a truth-order move the epistemic position does not license."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:payProbe1 a logic:ReconciliationProbe ; logic:probesAttempt ex:payAttempt1 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to resolve an unknown outcome before considering any retry or compensation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid probing without a correlation identity, and avoid substituting a retry for a probe."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the attempt; record the outcome as a logic:ReconciliationResult carrying one verdict."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Addressed by the attempt's correlation identity, which is why an attempt lacking one is unresolvable in principle. It is the only knowledge-increasing move from an undetermined position; retrying is a truth-order move that changes the world instead."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:probesAttempt
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ReconciliationProbe ;
    rdfs:range logic:EffectAttempt ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "probes attempt"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ReconciliationProbe to the one attempt it is asking about. Functional: a probe cannot span several attempts, because it would then return a single verdict for exchanges whose outcomes may genuinely differ, which is how a committed effect gets recorded as uncommitted."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:probe logic:probesAttempt ex:chargeAttempt ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind a reconciliation probe to the attempt whose outcome it is asking about."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid probing an intent rather than an attempt; the provider correlates on what was actually sent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "One attempt per probe, so the verdict resolves a specific undetermined outcome."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional, so a probe asks about one attempt. A probe covering several attempts would return one verdict for exchanges whose outcomes may genuinely differ, which is how a committed effect gets recorded as not committed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:ReconciliationResult
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Reconciliation Result"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "What a logic:ReconciliationProbe established, carrying exactly one logic:ReconciliationVerdict. A result may only move commitment knowledge UP the knowledge order; a probe that appears to move it down has not disproved the earlier reading but contradicted it, which is a real-world disagreement to adjudicate rather than a record to correct."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:payProbe1Result a logic:ReconciliationResult ; logic:reconciliationVerdict logic:ReconciledCommitted ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record a probe's answer and the knowledge move it licenses."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid recording a downward knowledge move as a correction; route disagreement to adjudication."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the probe and one verdict; the monotonicity constraint governs the resulting knowledge move."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "May only move commitment knowledge UP the knowledge order. A probe that appears to move it down has not disproved the earlier reading but contradicted it, and the contradiction is adjudicated rather than silently applied."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:resultOfProbe
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ReconciliationResult ;
    rdfs:range logic:ReconciliationProbe ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "result of probe"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ReconciliationResult to the probe that produced it, so a reconciliation verdict always has an identifiable investigation behind it rather than standing as a bare assertion. Functional: a result attributed to two probes could not be re-run to check it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Binds the result to the probe that produced it, so a reconciliation verdict always has an identifiable investigation behind it. Functional, because a result attributed to two probes could not be re-run to check it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:recon-result-2 logic:resultOfProbe ex:probe-8 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind a reconciliation result to the probe that produced it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid recording a verdict with no probe behind it: an unattributed verdict claims an answer nobody asked for and cannot be re-checked."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Emit the probe first and the result against it, so a later reader can repeat the same investigation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:reconciliationVerdict
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ReconciliationResult ;
    rdfs:range logic:ReconciliationVerdict ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has reconciliation verdict"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ReconciliationResult to its exactly-one verdict. Functional: two verdicts on one probe would leave commitment knowledge with two candidate values and no rule for choosing between them, which is not a reconciliation but the very trap adjudication exists to handle."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:probeResult logic:reconciliationVerdict logic:ReconciledCommitted ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for what a reconciliation actually determined."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a completed probe that records no verdict; it is indistinguishable from a probe that never ran, and the difference decides what happens next."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record committed, not-committed, or still-unknown — still-unknown is a real answer and must not be rounded to either side."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional, so a result carries exactly one verdict. Two verdicts on one probe would leave commitment knowledge with two candidate values and no rule for choosing, which is precisely the trap adjudication exists to handle."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:ContradictionAdjudication
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Contradiction Adjudication"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The attributed operator decision that resolves a contradictory commitment state — the provider reporting committed where the local ledger reports otherwise. It exists because that state is REACHABLE and would otherwise be a trap: compensation requires proof of commitment, retry requires proof of non-commitment, and a contradictory reading supplies neither. Adjudication is a standpoint-indexed decision, never a derivation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:payAdjudication a logic:ContradictionAdjudication ; logic:adjudicatesAttempt ex:payAttempt1 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when commitment knowledge is contradictory and an authorized human must decide which reading governs."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid deriving an adjudication, and avoid resolving a contradiction by discarding one side silently."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the attempt and the resolved reading; keep both original observations, since the adjudication settles what governs, not what was observed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Exists because the contradictory state is REACHABLE and would otherwise be a trap. It is an attributed operator decision rather than a derivation: no rule can settle which of two authoritative sources to believe, and pretending otherwise hides the judgment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:adjudicatesAttempt
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ContradictionAdjudication ;
    rdfs:range logic:EffectAttempt ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "adjudicates attempt"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ContradictionAdjudication to the attempt whose contradictory commitment state it resolves, not to the contradiction itself. Functional: an adjudication that would settle two attempts at once must be split, so each attempt gets its own resolution and its own audit trail."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Points at the attempt whose commitment state is contradictory, never at the contradiction itself. Functional, so an adjudication that would settle two attempts at once must be split; each attempt gets its own resolution and its own audit trail."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:adjudication-2 logic:adjudicatesAttempt ex:attempt-5 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind a contradiction adjudication to the single attempt whose receipt and ledger disagree."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for an attempt whose outcome is merely unknown: an undetermined position is not a contradiction, and adjudicating it would settle a question no evidence answered."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach one adjudication per contradictory attempt, so the resolution of each disagreement is separately readable and separately challengeable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:CompensationAttempt
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Compensation Attempt"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A NEW action taken to counteract a forward effect known to have committed — with its own preconditions, its own effects, its own attempt and receipt, and its own possibility of failing. It is emphatically NOT an undo: the forward effect remains in the record, history is corrected forward rather than rewritten, and a compensation that fails leaves the system in a genuinely inconsistent state that needs escalation rather than retry."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:refundAttempt a logic:CompensationAttempt ; logic:compensatesEffect ex:payReceipt ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to counteract a committed forward effect, when the forward effect is proved to have committed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid treating it as a guaranteed inverse, avoid marking it successful without its own receipt, and avoid compensating an effect not known to have committed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the exact forward receipt it addresses; emit its own attempt and receipt as any other effect would."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Emphatically NOT an undo: the forward effect remains committed and the compensation is a new action with its own preconditions, effects, receipt and possibility of failing. Reading it as a rollback is what makes a failed compensation invisible."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:compensatesEffect
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CompensationAttempt ;
    rdfs:range logic:ExternalEffectReceipt ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "compensates effect"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:CompensationAttempt to the EXACT forward receipt it addresses — not the action schema, not the intent. Functional. Binding to the receipt is what stops one compensation from being read as covering every effect of its kind."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:closeValve logic:compensatesEffect ex:valveResult ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind a compensation to the exact forward effect it counteracts."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid typing the compensation as the forward receipt; that collapses the counteraction into the thing counteracted and makes a FAILED compensation invisible."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Compensation is a new action with its own attempt, receipt and possibility of failing."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Binds to the EXACT forward receipt, not the action schema and not the intent. That is what stops one compensation from being read as covering every effect of its kind, which would let a single counteraction discharge many committed effects."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:CompensationOutcome
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Compensation Outcome"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "What a logic:CompensationAttempt achieved, established by its own receipt exactly as a forward effect's outcome is. A compensation may not be recorded as successful on the strength of having been attempted: the asymmetry between attempting and achieving is the whole reason the saga exists, and it does not stop applying just because the action is corrective."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:refundOutcome a logic:CompensationOutcome ; logic:outcomeOfCompensation ex:refundAttempt ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record what a compensation actually achieved, with its receipt."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid inferring success from attempt, and avoid omitting the receipt."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the compensation attempt and its receipt; a failed compensation over a committed forward effect escalates."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Established by its own receipt exactly as a forward effect's outcome is. A compensation may not be recorded as successful on the strength of having been attempted, because the asymmetry between attempting and achieving is the whole reason receipts exist."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:outcomeOfCompensation
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CompensationOutcome ;
    rdfs:range logic:CompensationAttempt ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "outcome of compensation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:CompensationOutcome to the compensation attempt it records, which is what keeps a compensation outcome from being asserted with no attempt behind it. Functional: one attempt yields one outcome, and a second attempt is a second record rather than a revision of the first."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Binds the outcome record to the compensation attempt it reports on, which is what keeps a compensation outcome from being asserted with no attempt behind it. Functional: one attempt yields one outcome, and a second attempt is a second record."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:comp-outcome-2 logic:outcomeOfCompensation ex:comp-attempt-2 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind a compensation outcome to the attempt whose result it records."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid recording an outcome for a compensation nobody attempted: that is the outcome-without-receipt pattern the kernel rejects."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Emit the attempt first and the outcome against it, so the pair reads as an action and its result rather than as a claim."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:compensationReceipt
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CompensationOutcome ;
    rdfs:range logic:ExternalEffectReceipt ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "compensation receipt"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:CompensationOutcome to the receipt establishing what the compensation achieved. Functional and MANDATORY for a successful outcome: without it the record asserts an achievement nobody observed. A compensation that was attempted and produced no receipt has an unknown outcome, not a successful one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:closeOutcome logic:compensationReceipt ex:closeReceipt ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to evidence that a compensation succeeded."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid marking a compensation successful without one; 'we sent the reversal' is not 'the reversal took effect'."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Require the receipt before treating the forward effect as undone."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional and MANDATORY for a successful outcome: without it the record asserts an achievement nobody observed. A compensation that was attempted and produced no receipt has an unknown outcome, not a successful one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:ResourceLease
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Resource Lease"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Time-bounded exclusive claim on a resource, carrying an owner, a scope, a monotonically increasing fencing identity and an expiry. The expiry alone does not stop a stale writer: a holder whose lease lapsed while it was paused can still attempt a write, and only a fencing check at the resource rejects it, so the fencing identity rather than the deadline is what carries the safety. In a control plan this is literal exclusive control of an actuator; in a curriculum a seat; in a workflow a distributed lock."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:valveLease a logic:ResourceLease ; logic:fencingIdentity 47 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use wherever concurrent enactments could otherwise act on one resource at once."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a lease without a fencing identity, and avoid treating expiry alone as sufficient protection."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind owner, scope, fencing identity and expiry; the resource rejects any write bearing a fencing identity below the one it has seen."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The fencing identity is what makes the lease actually safe: a holder whose lease expired while it was paused can still attempt, and only a monotonically increasing fence lets the resource refuse a stale writer."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:leaseOwner
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ResourceLease ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "lease owner"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ResourceLease to the enactment or actor holding it. Range an enactment or actor (stated in prose). Functional: a lease has one holder, because two holders would make the exclusivity claim vacuous — a failure the fencing identity cannot detect, since both writers would be legitimate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:valveLease logic:leaseOwner ex:supervisorA ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for who holds exclusive control."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an ownerless lease: exclusivity with no holder excludes nobody."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "One owner per lease."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional, so a lease has one holder. Two holders would make the exclusivity claim vacuous, which is the failure the fencing identity is then unable to detect because both writers would be legitimate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:leaseScope
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ResourceLease ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "lease scope"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ResourceLease to the resource and extent it covers. Range a resource descriptor (stated in prose). Functional. Exclusivity is asserted within the scope, not globally."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:valveLease logic:leaseScope \"actuator:FV-104\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for exactly what the lease covers."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a vague scope; two leases whose scopes cannot be compared cannot be checked for overlap."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the resource precisely enough that exclusivity is decidable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Exclusivity is asserted within the scope, not globally. A lease whose scope is left unstated claims either nothing or everything depending on the reader, and both readings are wrong."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:fencingIdentity
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ResourceLease ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "fencing identity"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The monotonically increasing token a lease carries, which the resource compares against the highest it has seen. Functional. Monotonicity is the whole mechanism: expiry alone cannot stop a paused holder from writing after its lease lapsed, but a stale token can be rejected at the resource."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:valveLease logic:fencingIdentity \"fence:FV-104:epoch-41\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the monotonic token the target rejects stale writers on."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid omitting it; expiry alone cannot stop a holder that lost its lease without noticing."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "The token must only ever increase, so a stale writer is recognisable without a clock comparison."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Monotonicity is the whole mechanism: expiry alone cannot stop a paused holder from writing after its lease lapsed, and only a strictly increasing token lets the resource recognize the stale writer as stale."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:leaseExpiry
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ResourceLease ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "lease expiry"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "When the lease lapses, evaluated against an attributed clock. Functional. Skew between the holder's clock and the resource's is the canonical lease failure, which is why the clock source is recorded rather than assumed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:valveLease logic:leaseExpiry \"2026-07-25T06:30:00Z\"^^xsd:dateTime ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for when the lease lapses."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid relying on expiry alone for safety: a paused holder can wake after expiry and still write, which is what fencing prevents."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Evaluate expiry against an attributed clock, since skew is the canonical fencing failure."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Skew between the holder's clock and the resource's is the canonical lease failure, which is why the clock source is recorded rather than assumed. Expiry alone never makes a lease safe: the fencing identity does that."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:ClockAttribution
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Clock Attribution"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The record of WHOSE clock a time-bounded judgment was made against. Expiry, validity windows and retention are all evaluated against somebody's notion of now, and provider-consumer skew is the canonical failure of every one of them. A dispatch whose expiry evaluation names no clock source is refused, because an unattributed 'still valid' is unfalsifiable. It records whose now was used and never that the reading was correct."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:payClock a logic:ClockAttribution ; logic:clockObservedAt \"2026-07-25T04:00:00Z\"^^xsd:dateTime ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use wherever an expiry, window or retention judgment is made."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid evaluating a window against an unattributed clock."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the source and the observed instant; the dispatch gate reads it before honouring any validity window."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Provider-consumer skew is the canonical failure of every time-bounded judgment, so a dispatch whose expiry check names no clock source is refused rather than assumed timely. It records whose now was used, never that the reading was correct."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:clockSource
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ClockAttribution ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "clock source"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ClockAttribution to whose clock was read, which is what turns a time-bounded judgment into an auditable one rather than a bare assertion. Range a clock-bearing agent or service (stated in prose). Functional: a judgment made against two clocks has no single now to be judged against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:plantClock logic:clockSource ex:historianNtp ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to attribute a time judgement to whose clock made it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an unattributed 'still valid': it is unfalsifiable, which makes it worse than no judgement because it reads as one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the source so provider-consumer skew is visible rather than assumed away."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Names whose clock was read, which is what turns a time-bounded judgment from an assertion into an auditable one. Functional: a judgment made against two clocks has no single now to be judged against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:clockObservedAt
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ClockAttribution ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "clock observed at"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The instant the attributed clock reported. Functional. Recorded rather than inferred so a later reader can detect skew instead of inheriting it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:plantClock logic:clockObservedAt \"2026-07-25T06:12:00Z\"^^xsd:dateTime ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for when the attributed clock was read."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid reusing an old reading for a new judgement; a stale reading makes an expired lease look live."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Read the clock at the moment of judgement, and record both together."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Recorded rather than inferred so a later reader can detect skew instead of inheriting it. The value is what that clock reported, never a claim that the reading was correct."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:ApprovalCommitment
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Approval Commitment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "An authorization modelled as a social commitment with a detachment lifecycle rather than as a boolean. Six bindings are mandatory: the exact dispatch-intent digest, the enactment and step, the authorized operator identity, the governing policy, the decision, and the validity window. Exact binding then falls out as a DETACHMENT condition — an intent whose digest differs simply never detaches this commitment — and revocation, delegation, escalation and quorum become expressible instead of absent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:payApproval a logic:ApprovalCommitment ; logic:approvalIntentDigest \"blake3:7f21\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use wherever an effect requires authorization before dispatch."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a boolean approved flag, and avoid an approval bound to anything looser than the exact intent digest."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind all six fields and carry a checkable authorization proof; the completeness constraint rejects a partial commitment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Six bindings are mandatory together, so a commitment missing any one of them authorizes nothing at all rather than authorizing loosely. Partial binding is what turns an authorization for one act into a licence for a family of them."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:approvalIntentDigest
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ApprovalCommitment ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "approval intent digest"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Binding one of six: the exact logic:intentDigest this commitment authorizes. Functional. A revised intent digests differently and detaches nothing, which is what makes approval-for-one-thing unable to license another. The binding is to the digest and not to the intent node, so identity cannot be reassigned after the fact."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:approval logic:approvalIntentDigest \"b3:7c30a9…\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind an approval to the exact intent it authorizes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid binding by reference: a reference can be repointed after approval, so the approved thing and the dispatched thing could differ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the digest, which is what makes 'approve this' mean this and not its neighbour."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A revised intent digests differently and detaches nothing, which is what makes approval-for-one-thing unable to license another. The binding is to the digest rather than to the intent node, so identity cannot be reassigned after the fact."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:approvalEnactment
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ApprovalCommitment ;
    rdfs:range logic:Enactment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "approval enactment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Binding two of six: the enactment and, through it, the step the authorization is scoped to. Functional. An approval does not travel to another run."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:approval logic:approvalEnactment ex:settlementRun ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind an approval to the enactment it applies within."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an approval that floats across enactments; authorization for one run is not authorization for another."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "One enactment per approval."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Scopes the authorization to one run and, through it, to one step. An approval does not travel to another run, so the same act in the next occurrence of a recurring prescription needs its own commitment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:approvalOperator
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ApprovalCommitment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "approval operator"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Binding three of six: the identity authorized to make this decision. Range an operator identity (stated in prose). Functional. A different operator-policy binding is a different commitment, not the same one exercised by somebody else."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:approval logic:approvalOperator ex:releaseManager ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for who granted the approval."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an unattributed approval; nobody is accountable for it and it cannot be checked against authority."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the operator whose authority the policy recognises."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A different operator-policy binding is a different commitment, not the same one exercised by somebody else. Delegation is therefore modelled as a new commitment naming the delegate rather than as a substitution here."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:approvalPolicy
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ApprovalCommitment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "approval policy"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Binding four of six: the policy under which the authorization is granted. Range a governing policy (stated in prose). Functional. A domain slice specializes this onto its own normative vocabulary so the policy is a real norm rather than an opaque reference; leaving it at the kernel level is legal but gives up every query that ranges over norms."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:approval logic:approvalPolicy ex:publicationPolicy ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the policy the approval was granted under."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid omitting it: when the policy changes, an approval with no policy cannot be re-examined."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the governing norm, so the approval is reviewable rather than merely recorded."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A domain slice specializes this onto its own normative vocabulary, so the policy is a real norm rather than an opaque reference. Leaving it at the kernel level is legal but gives up every query that ranges over norms."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:approvalDecision
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ApprovalCommitment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "approval decision"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Binding five of six: the decision reached. Range a decision value (stated in prose). Functional. A logic:Advisory may never occupy this position: model output can inform a decision and can never be one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:approval logic:approvalDecision \"granted\"@x-gmeow-english ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for what was actually decided."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid inferring a grant from the presence of an approval record; a refusal is also a decision worth recording."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record refusals as well as grants, so a dispatch blocked by refusal is explicable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A logic:Advisory may never occupy this position: model output can inform a decision and can never be one. That bar is structural, so it holds however carefully the advisory was reviewed before being attached."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:approvalValidityWindow
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ApprovalCommitment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "approval validity window"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Binding six of six: the interval within which the authorization is live, evaluated against an attributed clock. Range a time interval (stated in prose). Functional. An expired request does not license the act even where every other binding matches, and the window is judged against the named clock rather than against ambient time, which together make expiry auditable instead of arguable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:approval logic:approvalValidityWindow \"2026-07-25T00:00:00Z/2026-07-25T23:59:59Z\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bound when an approval may be acted on."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an open-ended approval; authorization granted once should not license a dispatch a year later."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Evaluate the window against an attributed clock, as with lease expiry."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "An expired request authorizes nothing even where every other binding matches, and the window is judged against an attributed clock rather than ambient time. That combination is what makes expiry auditable instead of arguable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:AuthorizationProof
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Authorization Proof"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A checkable proof term establishing that a dispatch or a pin was licensed — one whose stated conclusion is RECOMPUTED by the checker rather than trusted. This is strictly stronger than a faithfulness check over cited evidence: a fabricated authorization citing entirely real identifiers passes the weaker check and fails this one. It is what makes 'advice is never authority' an unforgeable property rather than a convention."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:payAuthProof a logic:AuthorizationProof ; logic:proofEstablishes ex:payIntent ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use wherever an authorization decision must be verifiable after the fact by someone who does not trust its author."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid substituting a citation list for a proof term, and avoid accepting a proof whose conclusion was not recomputed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind what it establishes; the checker re-derives the conclusion from the premises and rejects a mismatch."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Strictly stronger than a faithfulness check over cited evidence: the checker RECOMPUTES the stated conclusion rather than confirming the citations look right. A fabricated proof that cites real evidence passes a faithfulness check and fails this one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:proofEstablishes
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:AuthorizationProof ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "proof establishes"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:AuthorizationProof to the intent or pin whose licensing it establishes, in that direction, so a proof is always readable as an argument about a specific subject. Range an intent or pin (stated in prose). Functional: a proof establishing two things would let one of them inherit an argument that was not made for it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Relates the proof to what it licenses, in that direction, so a proof is always readable as an argument about a specific intent or pin. Functional: a proof establishing two things would let one of them inherit an argument made for the other."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:auth-proof-11 logic:proofEstablishes ex:pin-3 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to name the intent or pin whose licensing an authorization proof establishes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid reusing one proof across intents: each intent digests differently, and a shared proof would license an act nobody argued for."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Mint one proof per licensed object and reference it from the pin or dispatch, so the licence is checkable at the point of use."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:ApprovalBindingFault
    a logic:AbstractIndividualType ,
        logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:QualityValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Approval Binding Fault"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed vocabulary of WHY an authorization failed to detach: a digest mismatch, a different enactment, an elapsed window, or a different operator-policy binding. Typing the fault separately for each is what lets a conformance corpus prove the four are detected independently rather than collapsing into one undifferentiated refusal."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:payRefusal logic:approvalBindingFault logic:FaultDigestMismatch ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to type why an approval did not authorize a dispatch."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid one undifferentiated refusal code for the four cases."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach exactly one member to the refusal record."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Typing each of the four reasons separately is what lets a conformance corpus prove the four cases are genuinely distinguished. A single generic refusal would pass the same tests while telling an operator nothing about what to fix."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FaultDigestMismatch a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:ApprovalBindingFault ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "digest mismatch"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The intent presented digests differently from the one authorized — a revised intent. The commitment never detaches."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Any revision of the intent, however cosmetic, produces a different digest and therefore a different intent. The fault is not a warning that the intent changed; it is the statement that the commitment never detached for the intent now being presented."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:dispatch-6 logic:approvalBindingFault logic:FaultDigestMismatch ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the dispatch intent digests differently from the digest the commitment names, so the authorization covers a different act."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when the digests agree and something else diverged: the other three binding faults name those cases precisely."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Compare logic:intentDigest against logic:approvalIntentDigest and report the fault rather than re-approving in place, so the revised intent gets its own decision."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FaultDifferentEnactment a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:ApprovalBindingFault ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "different enactment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The intent belongs to a different run from the one the authorization is scoped to. Authorizations do not travel between occurrences."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "An authorization is scoped to one run. This fault is what makes that explicit: it fires even when the operator, the policy, the digest and the window all match, because sameness of the act is not sameness of the occurrence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:dispatch-6 logic:approvalBindingFault logic:FaultDifferentEnactment ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a dispatch cites an authorization issued against another run, including a previous occurrence of the same recurring prescription."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when the run matches but the intent was revised: that is a digest mismatch, and the two faults tell an operator to do quite different things."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Report it with logic:approvalBindingFault, and expect the remedy to be a new commitment for this run rather than a widening of the existing one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FaultExpiredWindow a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:ApprovalBindingFault ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "expired window"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The validity window closed before dispatch, judged against an attributed clock and not against ambient time. Every other binding may match and the authorization still licenses nothing; a dispatch whose expiry check names no clock source falls here too, rather than being assumed timely."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Judged against an attributed clock, never against ambient time. A dispatch whose expiry check names no clock source is refused rather than assumed timely, so this fault covers both a genuinely closed window and an unauditable claim that it was open."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:dispatch-6 logic:approvalBindingFault logic:FaultExpiredWindow ; logic:clockSource ex:ntp-pool-a ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the validity window had closed at the attributed clock reading taken for the dispatch, or when no clock source was attributed at all."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a commitment that was ended by a party: cancellation and release are decisions, and this fault records the absence of one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the clock with logic:clockSource on the check, so the refusal can be re-derived later against the same time source rather than re-argued."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FaultOperatorPolicyMismatch a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:ApprovalBindingFault ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "operator-policy mismatch"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The operator or the governing policy differs from the one the commitment names. A different operator-policy pairing is a different commitment, not the same one exercised elsewhere."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Treats the operator and the governing policy as one binding, because a commitment is an undertaking by a particular party under a particular norm. The same person acting under a different policy is a different commitment, which is why one fault covers both rather than two."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:dispatch-6 logic:approvalBindingFault logic:FaultOperatorPolicyMismatch ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when either the operator or the governing policy on the dispatch differs from the pairing the commitment names."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it to record a delegation: a delegated authority is a new commitment naming the delegate, not the old commitment exercised by someone else."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Compare logic:approvalOperator and logic:approvalPolicy together as one binding, and report the fault rather than accepting a partial match."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:approvalBindingFault
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:ApprovalBindingFault ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has approval binding fault"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a refusal record to the typed reason an authorization did not detach. Domain a refusal record (stated in prose). Functional."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:fault logic:approvalBindingFault logic:FaultDifferentEnactment ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to type WHY an approval failed to bind."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid one generic binding failure; an operator debugging a refused dispatch needs to know which of the four ways it failed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Pick the specific fault — wrong intent, wrong enactment, expired window, unauthorized operator."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional, so a refusal names one reason. Where several bindings diverge at once the first checked is reported, because a refusal listing four faults tells an operator to fix four things when correcting one may be enough."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:DeadlineConsumption
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Deadline Consumption"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "How much of a declared deadline an enactment has used and whether it is exhausted — the time-shaped sibling of the engine's existing step and budget accounting, which this layer reuses rather than duplicating for every other resource. Recorded so that 'we ran out of time' is a derivable state with a witness rather than an operator's impression."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:reviewDeadline a logic:DeadlineConsumption ; logic:deadlineExhausted true ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use where an enactment or dispatch is bounded by wall-clock time."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid duplicating step or answer budgets here; those already have carriers."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the consuming enactment and the exhaustion flag; evaluate against an attributed clock."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The time-shaped sibling of the engine's existing step and budget accounting, reused rather than duplicated for every other resource. It records consumption against a run, so the remaining deadline is recomputed from records rather than trusted from a running total."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:deadlineOfEnactment
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DeadlineConsumption ;
    rdfs:range logic:Enactment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "deadline of enactment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:DeadlineConsumption to the enactment consuming the deadline, and not the enactment to its deadline. Functional. That direction is what lets several consumption records accumulate against one run while the run's own record stays uncluttered."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Points from the consumption record to the enactment, not from the enactment to its deadline. That direction is what lets several consumption records accumulate against one run while the run itself stays uncluttered."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:consumption-3 logic:deadlineOfEnactment ex:enactment-88 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind a deadline-consumption record to the enactment whose deadline it is drawing down."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it to give an enactment its deadline: the deadline belongs to the plan or the contract, and this relates the consumption of it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach one consumption record per draw-down, so the remaining deadline can be recomputed from the records rather than trusted from a running total."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:deadlineExhausted
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DeadlineConsumption ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "deadline exhausted"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Whether the declared deadline has elapsed. Functional. Exhaustion blocks dispatch rather than merely annotating it, so an orphaned intent past its deadline is a detectable finding."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Blocks dispatch rather than merely annotating it, which is what makes an orphaned intent past its deadline a detectable finding instead of a stale row. Functional, so the answer is a single fact rather than a history of checks."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:enactment-88 logic:deadlineExhausted true ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record whether the declared deadline has elapsed for the enactment consuming it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it as a soft warning flag: the value blocks dispatch, so setting it true on a run that should continue stops work rather than annotating it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Derive it against the attributed clock, so exhaustion is re-checkable later rather than resting on when someone happened to look."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:ReceiptRequiresAttemptConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a receipt names the attempt it reports on"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:ExternalEffectReceipt with no logic:receiptOfAttempt. A receipt without an attempt asserts an outcome nobody caused, which is the shape a fabricated or mis-imported effect record takes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:ExternalEffectReceipt must name the logic:EffectAttempt it reports on; a receipt with no attempt asserts an outcome nobody caused." ;
    logic:integrity logic:rraForall .
logic:rraForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:rraImpl .
logic:rraImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:rraGuard ; logic:consequent logic:rraCons .
logic:rraGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ExternalEffectReceipt ] .
logic:rraCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:receiptOfAttempt ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "attempt" ] .

logic:CompensationSuccessRequiresReceiptConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a compensation outcome carries its own receipt"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:CompensationOutcome with no receipt. Compensation is a new action, so the gap between attempting and achieving applies to it exactly as to a forward effect; recording success on the strength of having tried is the failure this constraint exists to prevent. Coded separately from the not-an-inverse constraint because the two are independent obligations and a corpus must be able to trip each alone."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:CompensationOutcome must carry its own logic:compensationReceipt; a compensation is not successful merely because it was attempted." ;
    logic:integrity logic:csrForall .
logic:csrForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:csrImpl .
logic:csrImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:csrGuard ; logic:consequent logic:csrCons .
logic:csrGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:CompensationOutcome ] .
logic:csrCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:compensationReceipt ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "receipt" ] .

logic:CompensationNamesForwardEffectConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a compensation names a forward effect"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:CompensationAttempt that names no forward effect at all. It is the PRESENCE half of the exactness discipline, and it says only that: whether the named thing is the exact forward receipt rather than an action schema or an intent is decided by logic:CompensationBindsExactForwardEffectConstraint, which dereferences the binding. Coded apart so a corpus can trip the empty binding and the loose binding independently."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:CompensationAttempt must bind the forward effect it addresses with logic:compensatesEffect; a compensation naming nothing counteracts nothing." ;
    logic:integrity logic:cbeForall .
logic:cbeForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:cbeImpl .
logic:cbeImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:cbeGuard ; logic:consequent logic:cbeCons .
logic:cbeGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:CompensationAttempt ] .
logic:cbeCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:compensatesEffect ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "receipt" ] .

# The RELATIONAL half: the thing a compensation binds must itself BE a receipt of an
# observed attempt. The presence law above cannot tell an exact forward receipt from an
# action schema, an intent, or a reconciliation result — every one of those satisfies
# "logic:compensatesEffect is bound" — and it is precisely the loose binding that lets one
# compensation read as covering every effect of its kind. Dereferencing the binding is what
# makes "the EXACT forward effect" checkable rather than merely asserted.
logic:CompensationBindsExactForwardEffectConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a compensation binds the exact forward receipt"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:CompensationAttempt whose logic:compensatesEffect names something that is not a receipt of an observed attempt. Exactness is a property of what the binding REACHES, not of the binding existing: an action schema, an intent, or a reconciliation result all satisfy a presence check while naming a CLASS of effects rather than the one committed effect, which is the reading under which a single refund appears to undo many charges. Only a logic:ExternalEffectReceipt carries logic:receiptOfAttempt, so requiring the target to carry it is exactly the exactness test."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:CompensationAttempt's logic:compensatesEffect must reach a logic:ExternalEffectReceipt reporting a specific logic:EffectAttempt; an action schema, an intent, or a reconciliation result names a class of effects, not the one committed effect being counteracted." ;
    logic:integrity logic:cbxForall .
logic:cbxForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:cbxImpl .
logic:cbxImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:cbxGuard ; logic:consequent logic:cbxCons .
logic:cbxGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:cbxIsCompensation , logic:cbxBinds .
logic:cbxIsCompensation a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:CompensationAttempt ] .
logic:cbxBinds a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:compensatesEffect ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "forward" ] .
logic:cbxCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:receiptOfAttempt ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "forward" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "attempt" ] .

# The compensation's OWN receipt may not be the forward receipt it counteracts. A
# compensation outcome that evidences itself with the very receipt of the effect it was
# supposed to reverse records an achievement that never happened — the strictly relational
# twin of logic:CompensationNotInverseConstraint, which forbids the same collapse when it
# is expressed by TYPING rather than by pointing.
logic:CompensationOutcomeReceiptIsNotTheForwardReceiptConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a compensation outcome is not evidenced by the receipt it counteracts"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:CompensationOutcome whose logic:compensationReceipt is the very logic:ExternalEffectReceipt its compensation addresses. Reusing the forward receipt as the compensation's evidence is the pointing form of the guaranteed-inverse fallacy: nothing new was observed, so a compensation that never took effect reads as successful. Coded apart from the typing form because a corpus must be able to trip each alone."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:CompensationOutcome's logic:compensationReceipt must not be the forward logic:ExternalEffectReceipt its logic:CompensationAttempt counteracts; a compensation evidenced by the effect it was undoing evidences nothing." ;
    logic:integrity logic:corForall .
logic:corForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:corImpl .
logic:corImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:corGuard ; logic:consequent logic:corCons .
logic:corGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:corIsOutcome , logic:corOfCompensation , logic:corForward .
logic:corIsOutcome a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:CompensationOutcome ] .
logic:corOfCompensation a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:outcomeOfCompensation ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "compensation" ] .
logic:corForward a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:compensatesEffect ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "compensation" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "forward" ] .
logic:corCons a logic:Formula ; logic:not logic:corReuses .
logic:corReuses a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:compensationReceipt ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "forward" ] .

logic:DispatchIntentCompletenessConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a dispatch intent binds all nine fields"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:DispatchIntent that omits any of its nine mandatory bindings. The journal head is gated alongside the other eight deliberately: it is the field that makes no-blind-retry structural, so leaving it optional would quietly demote that law back to a rule someone must remember."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:DispatchIntent must bind all nine fields: enactment, step, action-schema version, normalized arguments, target, policy, approval requirement, content digest, and expected journal head." ;
    logic:integrity logic:diForall .
logic:diForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:diImpl .
logic:diImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:diGuard ; logic:consequent logic:diCons .
logic:diGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:DispatchIntent ] .
logic:diCons a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:diEnact , logic:diStep , logic:diSchema , logic:diArgs , logic:diTarget , logic:diPolicy , logic:diApproval , logic:diDigest , logic:diHead .
logic:diEnact a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:intentOfEnactment ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "enactment" ] .
logic:diStep a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:intentOfStep ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "step" ] .
logic:diSchema a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:intentActionSchemaVersion ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "schema" ] .
logic:diArgs a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:intentNormalizedArguments ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "args" ] .
logic:diTarget a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:intentTarget ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "target" ] .
logic:diPolicy a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:intentPolicy ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "policy" ] .
logic:diApproval a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:intentApprovalRequired ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "approval" ] .
logic:diDigest a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:intentDigest ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "digest" ] .
logic:diHead a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:expectedJournalHead ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "head" ] .

logic:IdempotencyContractCompletenessConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "an idempotency contract binds all five fields"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:IdempotencyContract that omits any of its five fields. Provider evidence and the retention window are gated alongside the rest because a contract asserting safety without evidence records a hope, and one without a window claims a guarantee that in reality lapses."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:IdempotencyContract must bind all five fields: scope, key, retention window, duplicate semantics, and provider support evidence." ;
    logic:integrity logic:icForall .
logic:icForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:icImpl .
logic:icImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:icGuard ; logic:consequent logic:icCons .
logic:icGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:IdempotencyContract ] .
logic:icCons a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:icScope , logic:icKey , logic:icRetention , logic:icDup , logic:icEvidence .
logic:icScope a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:idempotencyScope ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "scope" ] .
logic:icKey a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:idempotencyKey ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "key" ] .
logic:icRetention a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:idempotencyRetention ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "retention" ] .
logic:icDup a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:idempotencyDuplicateSemantics ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "dup" ] .
logic:icEvidence a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:idempotencyProviderEvidence ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "evidence" ] .

logic:ApprovalCommitmentCompletenessConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "an approval commitment binds all six fields"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:ApprovalCommitment that omits any of its six bindings. Each omission is a distinct way an authorization could authorize more than it was meant to, which is why they are gated together rather than individually optional."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:ApprovalCommitment must bind all six fields: exact intent digest, enactment, authorized operator, governing policy, decision, and validity window." ;
    logic:integrity logic:acForall .
logic:acForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:acImpl .
logic:acImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:acGuard ; logic:consequent logic:acCons .
logic:acGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ApprovalCommitment ] .
logic:acCons a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:acDigest , logic:acEnact , logic:acOperator , logic:acPolicy , logic:acDecision , logic:acWindow .
logic:acDigest a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:approvalIntentDigest ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "digest" ] .
logic:acEnact a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:approvalEnactment ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "enactment" ] .
logic:acOperator a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:approvalOperator ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "operator" ] .
logic:acPolicy a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:approvalPolicy ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "policy" ] .
logic:acDecision a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:approvalDecision ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "decision" ] .
logic:acWindow a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:approvalValidityWindow ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "window" ] .

# The two RELATIONAL approval laws. Completeness above says the six fields are bound; it
# cannot say that the digest addresses anything real, nor that the thing it addresses
# belongs to the run the authorization is scoped to. Those are exactly logic:
# FaultDigestMismatch and logic:FaultDifferentEnactment — two of the four authored binding
# faults — and until they are checked, "an approval binds the EXACT intent" is a claim the
# corpus makes and nothing enforces.

logic:ApprovalDigestBindsDispatchIntentConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "an approval's digest addresses a real dispatch intent"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:ApprovalCommitment whose logic:approvalIntentDigest is not the logic:intentDigest of any logic:DispatchIntent. Exact binding is a RELATION between two records, not a field on one: a commitment addressed to a digest nothing carries detaches against nothing, which is indistinguishable — to a completeness check — from one addressed to the intent actually about to be dispatched. This is the machine-checkable form of logic:FaultDigestMismatch."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:ApprovalCommitment's logic:approvalIntentDigest must equal the logic:intentDigest of an actual logic:DispatchIntent; an authorization addressed to a digest nothing carries authorizes nothing." ;
    logic:integrity logic:adbForall .
logic:adbForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:adbImpl .
logic:adbImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:adbGuard ; logic:consequent logic:adbCons .
logic:adbGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:adbIsApproval , logic:adbDigest .
logic:adbIsApproval a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ApprovalCommitment ] .
logic:adbDigest a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:approvalIntentDigest ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "digest" ] .
logic:adbCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:intentDigest ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "intent" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "digest" ] .

logic:ApprovalScopedToIntentEnactmentConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "an approval does not authorize another run's intent"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:ApprovalCommitment whose logic:approvalEnactment is not the logic:intentOfEnactment of the intent its digest addresses. An authorization does not travel between occurrences, and the digest alone cannot stop it: two runs of one prescription can produce byte-identical intents, so the digest match is satisfied while the authorization licenses an effect in a run nobody approved. This is the machine-checkable form of logic:FaultDifferentEnactment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:ApprovalCommitment's logic:approvalEnactment must be the logic:intentOfEnactment of the logic:DispatchIntent its digest addresses; authorization granted in one run licenses nothing in another." ;
    logic:integrity logic:asiForall .
logic:asiForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:asiImpl .
logic:asiImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:asiGuard ; logic:consequent logic:asiCons .
logic:asiGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:asiIsApproval , logic:asiDigest , logic:asiIntentDigest , logic:asiEnactment .
logic:asiIsApproval a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ApprovalCommitment ] .
logic:asiDigest a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:approvalIntentDigest ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "digest" ] .
logic:asiIntentDigest a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:intentDigest ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "intent" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "digest" ] .
logic:asiEnactment a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:approvalEnactment ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "enactment" ] .
logic:asiCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:intentOfEnactment ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "intent" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "enactment" ] .

logic:EffectRecordsAreObservedNotDerivedConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "effect attempts and receipts are observed, never derived"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:EffectAttempt that carries derivation provenance. The engine describes, validates, derives and certifies effect records; it never causes them. Carrying a derivation identifier is exactly what a record produced by inference rather than by observation looks like, so that is what the constraint keys on — and a reasoner that could conclude an attempt happened could conclude the world changed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:EffectAttempt must be asserted by the organ that performed it, never derived: an attempt carrying derivation provenance means the engine concluded an external effect occurred." ;
    logic:integrity logic:eodForall .
logic:eodForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:eodImpl .
logic:eodImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:eodGuard ; logic:consequent logic:eodCons .
logic:eodGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:EffectAttempt ] .
logic:eodCons a logic:Formula ; logic:not logic:eodDerived .
logic:eodDerived a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:derivationIdentifier ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "derivation" ] .

logic:derivationIdentifier
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "derivation identifier"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The identifier a derived record carries naming the derivation that produced it. Domain any derived record (stated in prose). It is legitimate on a derived record and must never appear on an effect attempt or receipt: its presence there is what the observed-not-derived constraint rejects, because it is the machine-checkable mark that a record was computed by the engine rather than observed at the boundary."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Its PRESENCE on an effect attempt or receipt is what the observed-not-derived constraint rejects. The identifier is legitimate on a derived record and is precisely the machine-checkable mark that a record was computed rather than observed at the boundary."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:closure-row-4 logic:derivationIdentifier \"chase-run-0f3a\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use on a record the reasoner produced, to name the derivation run that produced it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid putting it on an effect attempt or receipt: those record what the world reported, and a derivation identifier there claims the boundary was computed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Let the derivation write it, and let the observed-not-derived guard read its presence as the test of which side of the boundary a record came from."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:LeaseCarriesFencingIdentityConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a lease carries a fencing identity"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:ResourceLease with no fencing identity. Expiry alone does not make a lease safe: a holder paused past its expiry can still attempt a write, and only a monotonic token checked at the resource rejects it. A lease without one records an intention to be exclusive rather than a mechanism for it. This is the MECHANISM half; exclusivity itself — one holder at a time over one scope — is logic:LeaseExclusivityConstraint, which is a relation between leases and could never be decided by looking at one lease's fields."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:ResourceLease must carry a logic:fencingIdentity; expiry alone cannot stop a paused holder from writing after its lease lapsed." ;
    logic:integrity logic:lexForall .
logic:lexForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:lexImpl .
logic:lexImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:lexGuard ; logic:consequent logic:lexCons .
logic:lexGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ResourceLease ] .
logic:lexCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:fencingIdentity ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "fence" ] .

logic:scopeExclusiveHolder
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:ResourceLease ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "scope exclusive holder"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a leased scope to the ONE logic:ResourceLease currently holding it. Domain a resource descriptor — the same term a lease names in logic:leaseScope (stated in prose, since a scope is whatever the deployment addresses exclusivity over). Functional, and that is the whole point: exclusivity is a property of the SCOPE, not of any lease, so it is recorded once at the scope and every claimant is checked against it. Without it 'exclusive' is an adjective on a lease that no second lease can contradict."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:actuatorFV104 logic:scopeExclusiveHolder ex:valveLease ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record which lease currently holds a scope, so a second claimant over that scope is detectable rather than merely undesirable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid binding two holders to one scope, and avoid leaving it unbound on a contended scope: an unheld scope excludes nobody and the exclusivity law has nothing to compare a claimant against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Advance it exactly when the fencing identity advances, so the holder pointer and the monotonic token tell the same story to the resource."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Relates a leased scope to the ONE lease currently holding it, which is the inverse view the resource itself needs. A scope with two holders is the second-lease violation the kernel's laws reject."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# Exclusivity is a RELATION between leases, so it is decided at the scope. A scope naming
# its holder condemns every OTHER lease over that scope — which is exactly "two overlapping
# leases on one scope", stated in a form the Horn+NAF fragment can decide. The alternative
# reading (`leaseScope(a,s) ∧ leaseScope(b,s) ∧ a ≠ b`) needs an inequality guard, and the
# logic:Formula AST carries no inequality construct at all: logic:distinctBody is a
# logic:Rule surface and never reaches a logic:Constraint's integrity formula. Recording the
# holder at the scope is not a workaround for that — it is the mechanism a real fencing
# implementation already has (the resource knows the highest token it has seen), lifted into
# the record so the ontology can check it.
logic:LeaseExclusivityConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "one lease at a time over one scope"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:ResourceLease that claims a scope some OTHER lease holds. Exclusivity is not a field a lease can carry: two leases each carrying a fencing identity, an owner and an expiry are individually impeccable and jointly a double-claim over one actuator, one seat, one lock. The scope's logic:scopeExclusiveHolder is the single arbiter, so any lease whose logic:leaseScope names a held scope without BEING the holder is the overlap."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:ResourceLease over a scope that already names a logic:scopeExclusiveHolder must BE that holder; two live leases over one scope is the overlap exclusivity exists to forbid." ;
    logic:integrity logic:lxoForall .
logic:lxoForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:lxoImpl .
logic:lxoImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:lxoGuard ; logic:consequent logic:lxoCons .
logic:lxoGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:lxoIsLease , logic:lxoScope , logic:lxoHeld .
logic:lxoIsLease a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ResourceLease ] .
logic:lxoScope a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:leaseScope ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "scope" ] .
logic:lxoHeld a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:scopeExclusiveHolder ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "scope" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "holder" ] .
logic:lxoCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:scopeExclusiveHolder ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "scope" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] .

# =========================================================================== #
# === Enactment layer: lens laws, the actionable frontier, derivation      === #
# === contracts, the total operation outcome, and context assembly         === #
# =========================================================================== #

logic:RevisionLens
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Revision Lens"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Revising a prescription to accommodate an observed state, and restoring a checkpoint, are the two legs of one bidirectional transformation, so they are governed by lens laws rather than by ad-hoc rules. Revise is a PUT: it pushes observed state back into a prescription. Restore is GET after PUT: it recovers what was pushed. Stating them this way buys two machine-checkable properties that three separate hand-authored constraints would only approximate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:adrRevisionLens a logic:RevisionLens ; logic:lensGoverns ex:adrReviewPlan ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to govern how observed execution state flows back into a prescription and how a checkpoint recovers it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid replacing the laws with per-case revision rules, and avoid a lens whose legs are not both executable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind what it governs and assert the two laws; discharge them the way any correspondence law is discharged."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Revise and restore are the two legs of one bidirectional transformation, so they are governed by lens laws rather than by ad-hoc rules. Governing them separately is what lets a revision be accepted that does not survive re-derivation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:lensGoverns
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:RevisionLens ;
    rdfs:range logic:Plan ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "lens governs"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:RevisionLens to the plan whose revision and restore it governs, so the two lens laws are always evaluated against a specific plan rather than in the abstract. Functional: a plan with two governing lenses would have two answers to the same question."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Points at the plan whose revision and restore the lens governs, so the two lens laws are always evaluated against a specific plan rather than in the abstract. Functional: a plan with two governing lenses would have two answers to the same question."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:lens-1 logic:lensGoverns ex:protocol-v3 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind a revision lens to the plan whose revision and restore behaviour it governs."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid sharing one lens across unrelated plans: the laws are checked per plan, and a shared lens would report one plan's result for another."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach one lens per plan and record both law results on it, so revision behaviour is auditable plan by plan."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:lensPutGetHolds
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:RevisionLens ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "lens put-get law holds"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Whether revising to accommodate an observed state and then re-deriving yields that state — the law saying a revision actually incorporated the change it was made for. A revision that does not survive re-derivation changed the prescription without changing what it prescribes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Says a revision actually incorporated the change it was made for: revising to accommodate an observed state and then re-deriving yields that state. A revision that does not survive re-derivation changed the prescription without changing what it prescribes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:lens-1 logic:lensPutGetHolds true ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record whether revising for an observed state and re-deriving reproduces that state."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid reading a false value as a bug in the observation: it says the revision failed to take, which is a defect in the revision rather than in what was seen."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Check it by round-tripping a real observed state through revision and re-derivation, and record the result on the lens."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:lensGetPutHolds
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:RevisionLens ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "lens get-put law holds"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Whether a revision that changes nothing declines to mint a new version — the law against spurious version churn. Without it every no-op review produces a new prescription version and the revision lineage stops meaning anything. It is a property of the revision machinery, not a verdict on any single revision."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The law against spurious version churn. Without it every no-op review produces a new prescription version and the revision lineage stops meaning anything, so this is a property of the revision machinery rather than of any one revision."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:lens-1 logic:lensGetPutHolds true ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record whether a revision that changes nothing declines to mint a new version."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid asserting it true without checking a no-op revision: the law is exactly about the case nobody tests, and an untested true is a claim about the machinery rather than a report on it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Check it by revising with an unchanged input and confirming no new version appears, then record the result on the lens."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:recoveryWitness
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:EnactmentCheckpoint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "recovery witness"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The witness a checkpoint carries of the state it was taken from, so restoring is RECOVERY rather than reconstruction. Range a witness node (stated in prose). It is strictly stronger than comparing digests and refusing on mismatch: a digest says the state differs, a recovery witness says what the state WAS."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Strictly stronger than comparing digests and refusing on mismatch: a digest says the state differs, a recovery witness says what the state was. That is what makes restoring a recovery rather than a reconstruction, and it is why the witness cannot be replaced by a hash."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ckpt-12 logic:recoveryWitness ex:witness-state-12 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to carry, on a checkpoint, the witness of the state it was taken from."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid treating a stored digest as one: a digest supports refusal but not recovery, and a checkpoint carrying only a digest cannot restore, only reject."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record the witness when the checkpoint is taken, since it cannot be reconstructed afterwards from the digest alone."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:ActionableFrontier
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Actionable Frontier"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The derived set of what could happen next in an enactment, together with why each item can or cannot proceed. Its CLOSEDNESS is certified, not asserted: the frontier carries a saturation witness showing the derivation ran to a fixed point, because a roster truncated by a budget cut and presented as complete is how a system silently omits the action it should have taken. Typed APART from a work queue, which lists what someone intends to do; a frontier is derived from state and carries its own completeness status."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:w12Frontier a logic:ActionableFrontier ; logic:frontierOfEnactment ex:adrReviewW12 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to derive and present what an enactment can do next, with reasons."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid claiming closure without a saturation witness, and avoid hand-maintaining a frontier as data."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Derive it, bind the saturation witness, and carry one entry per candidate action with its axis tuple and label."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Its closedness is certified rather than asserted: without a saturation witness the frontier is a snapshot of whatever had been derived when someone looked, and an operator cannot tell a genuinely empty frontier from an unfinished derivation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:frontierOfEnactment
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ActionableFrontier ;
    rdfs:range logic:Enactment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "frontier of enactment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ActionableFrontier to the enactment it was derived for. Functional: a frontier belongs to one enactment and cannot span runs, because a saturation witness over several runs would make closedness relative to a set nobody declared."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:frontier logic:frontierOfEnactment ex:run884 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind a derived frontier to the enactment it describes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a frontier spanning enactments; what is actionable is relative to one run's state."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "One enactment per frontier."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional, so a frontier belongs to one enactment. A frontier spanning runs could not carry a saturation witness that means anything, because closedness would then be relative to a set nobody declared."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:SaturationWitness
    a logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:ReasoningResult ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Saturation Witness"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The evidence that a derivation reached a FIXED POINT rather than a budget cut: the predicates whose extensions it proved final (logic:settledPredicate) and the budget they were proved under (logic:consumedBudget). It is a logic:ReasoningResult, so its computation axis is the kernel's existing logic:resultEvaluation rather than a second, witness-private notion of having finished. Typed APART from logic:FrontierEntry, and the distinction is operational, not tidiness: an entry is a candidate ACTION offered to an operator, a witness is a claim ABOUT the roster of them, so a witness typed as an entry becomes a phantom row an operator can be asked to perform."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:run78Saturation a logic:SaturationWitness ; logic:settledPredicate logic:entryLabel ; logic:consumedBudget \"64\"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ; logic:resultEvaluation logic:EvaluationCompleted ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to certify that a derivation whose closedness matters — most characteristically a logic:ActionableFrontier's roster — settled rather than stopped."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a witness carrying neither settled predicates nor a budget: a citation with no content reads as certification and is strictly worse than no witness at all. Avoid typing one as a logic:FrontierEntry."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name every predicate proved final, record the budget it was proved under, and carry the logic:EvaluationStatus the run reached; a witness naming no predicate certifies the closure of nothing."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Evidence of a FIXED POINT rather than a budget cut, carrying the predicates proved final and the budget they were proved under. Without the budget the witness cannot be distinguished from a run that simply stopped early and looked settled."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:settledPredicate
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:SaturationWitness ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "settled predicate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:SaturationWitness to one predicate whose extension the derivation proved FINAL — no further rule application adds a tuple to it. Multi-valued and deliberately enumerated rather than summarized: the predicates a witness does not name are exactly the ones whose closure it does not certify, so 'everything settled' is a claim that must be spelled out predicate by predicate to be falsifiable. Range a predicate (stated in prose)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:run78Saturation logic:settledPredicate logic:hasFrontierEntry , logic:entryLabel ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to name each predicate a saturation run drove to a fixed point."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid naming a predicate the run never reached, and avoid leaving the witness empty; an unnamed predicate is an uncertified one, and no named predicate is no certification."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "List every predicate the closure claim depends on — for a frontier, at minimum the roster edge and the label the roster is read through."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Deliberately enumerated rather than summarized: the predicates a witness does NOT list are exactly the ones whose extensions may still grow. A summary would hide which part of the derivation is final."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:frontierSaturationWitness
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ActionableFrontier ;
    rdfs:range logic:SaturationWitness ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "frontier saturation witness"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The evidence that the frontier derivation reached a fixed point rather than a budget cut — the settled predicate set and the budget consumed. Range logic:SaturationWitness, declared rather than left to prose: an undeclared range is how the position came to be filled by a logic:FrontierEntry, which put a phantom action on the very roster the witness was certifying. Functional and MANDATORY for any frontier claiming closure: without it, closedness is an assertion the reader cannot check."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:frontier logic:frontierSaturationWitness ex:saturation ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to certify that a frontier's roster is genuinely closed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid claiming closure without one: an unwitnessed claim is exactly how a roster truncated by a budget cut passes for exhaustive, after which the system confidently omits the action it should have taken."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind exactly one logic:SaturationWitness carrying the predicates proved final and the budget they were proved under."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Declared rather than left to convention, because a frontier whose closedness rests on convention cannot be audited. The witness carries both the settled predicate set and the budget consumed, so a fixed point and an early stop are distinguishable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:FrontierEntry
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Frontier Entry"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "One candidate action on a logic:ActionableFrontier, carrying the axis tuple that positions it and the label derived from that tuple. Keeping the tuple alongside the label is what stops the two drifting apart: the label is a projection for readers, and the tuple is the thing it is a projection OF. An entry carrying only a label cannot be re-derived and cannot be challenged."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrEntry a logic:FrontierEntry ; logic:entryLabel logic:FrontierReadyApprovalRequired ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for each candidate action a frontier derivation produced."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an entry carrying a label with no axis tuple behind it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the action, the axis positions, and exactly one derived label."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Keeps the axis tuple alongside the label so the two cannot drift apart: the label is a projection for readers and the tuple is the ground truth. An entry carrying only a label cannot be re-derived and cannot be challenged."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:hasFrontierEntry
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ActionableFrontier ;
    rdfs:range logic:FrontierEntry ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has frontier entry"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ActionableFrontier to one of its entries and to nothing else: the labels, the witnesses and the proposals hang off the entries, not off the frontier. Multi-valued, and an empty frontier is a meaningful answer rather than missing data."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Relates the frontier to its entries and nothing else: the entries carry the labels, the witnesses and the proposals. Multi-valued, and an empty frontier is a meaningful answer rather than missing data."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:frontier-5 logic:hasFrontierEntry ex:entry-9 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to attach each derived entry to the actionable frontier it belongs to."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid hand-authoring entries onto a frontier: the frontier is derived, and a hand-added entry claims an action the derivation did not license."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Let the frontier derivation emit the entries, and read an empty frontier as the assertion that nothing is currently actionable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:entryAction
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FrontierEntry ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "entry action"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:FrontierEntry to the one candidate action it positions. Range a plan step or action schema (stated in prose). Functional: an entry cannot cover several actions, because it would then carry a single label for positions that may genuinely differ, which is exactly what the per-entry axis tuple exists to prevent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrEntry logic:entryAction ex:ocrStep ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind a frontier entry to the candidate action it positions."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an entry with no action; there is nothing for an operator to do or decline."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "One action per entry; the same action under two policies is two entries."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional, so an entry positions one candidate action. An entry covering several actions would carry one label for positions that may genuinely differ, which is exactly what the per-entry axis tuple is meant to prevent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:entryAxisWitness
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FrontierEntry ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "entry axis witness"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:FrontierEntry to an axis position that contributed to its label. Range a lifecycle axis value (stated in prose). Multi-valued: the label is a function of the whole tuple, and carrying every contributing position is what lets an operator ask why an action carries the label it does. A single witness would show one reason among several rather than the ground of the label, leaving a reader to take it on trust."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrEntry logic:entryAxisWitness logic:StepReady , logic:ApprovalCreated ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for each axis position that contributed to an entry's label."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid recording only one axis: the label is a function of the whole tuple, and a partial witness cannot explain it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Carry every contributing position, which is what lets an operator ask why an action carries the label it does."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Multi-valued because the label is a function of the whole tuple, and carrying every contributing position is what lets a reader re-derive the label rather than take it on trust. A single witness would show one reason among several."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:entryAwaits
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FrontierEntry ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "entry awaits"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a waiting logic:FrontierEntry to the event, instant or input it is blocked pending. Range an event, an instant or an input descriptor (stated in prose). The step axis records THAT a step waits; without this the kernel cannot say WHAT it waits for, and 'waiting for event, time or input' would be a label naming three causes none of which is recorded. It is also what separates waiting from a capability block: what a step awaits is something that arrives, and a capability the deployment does not have never does."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrEntry logic:entryAwaits ex:scannerCallback ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use whenever a frontier entry sits at the waiting step position."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a waiting entry with nothing awaited: an unattributed wait is indistinguishable from a stall, and it cannot be told from a capability block either."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the awaited event, instant or input precisely enough that its arrival is recognisable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The step axis records THAT a step waits; without this the kernel cannot say what would unblock it. Naming the awaited event, instant or input turns a waiting entry from a status into something an operator can act on."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:entryExcludes
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FrontierEntry ;
    rdfs:range logic:FrontierEntry ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "entry excludes"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:FrontierEntry to another entry it cannot proceed together with. Multi-valued. Exclusivity is a RELATION and not a property of either action, so it cannot be carried on a lifecycle axis: it becomes visible only when local frontiers are glued, and it is recorded on the candidate the glue withheld. An entry the glue withheld carries no readiness position, because being ready is a claim about a frontier on which the other candidate was not taken."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:reprocessEntry logic:entryExcludes ex:archiveEntry ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when gluing local frontiers reveals that two candidates cannot both be taken."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid expressing exclusivity as a per-action flag; a flag cannot name the other candidate, so an operator cannot see what taking this one costs."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it on the withheld candidate and name the candidate that excludes it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Exclusivity is a RELATION and not a property of either action, so it cannot be carried on a lifecycle axis. It becomes visible only when local frontiers are glued, which is why it lives on the entry rather than on the step."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:entryLabel
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FrontierEntry ;
    rdfs:range logic:FrontierLabel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "entry label"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:FrontierEntry to its exactly-one derived label. Functional. The label is computed by a total function from the axis tuple and is never authored directly, so every reachable tuple carries exactly one label and the frontier is answerable for every entry rather than only for the ones someone thought to cover."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrEntry logic:entryLabel logic:FrontierReadyApprovalRequired ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a frontier entry's exactly-one derived label."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid asserting it by hand: the label is a total function of the axis tuple, and a hand-written one that disagrees with the derivation is a silent lie about what the system concluded."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Let the rule set derive it, and read the axis witnesses to see why it came out that way."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional and derived by a total function from the axis tuple, so no reachable tuple is unlabelled and none carries two. Totality is what makes the frontier answerable for every entry rather than for the ones someone thought to cover."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:FrontierLabel
    a logic:AbstractIndividualType ,
        logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:QualityValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Frontier Label"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The operator-facing name for a position on the actionable frontier, DERIVED from the axis tuple by a total, disjoint function rather than assigned. Deriving rather than assigning is what stops the labels drifting from the axes they summarize, and it is what lets a totality proof show every reachable tuple has exactly one label and every label is reachable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrEntry logic:entryLabel logic:FrontierBlockedCapabilityOrResource ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to present a frontier position to an operator."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid assigning a label directly, and avoid adding one without extending the derivation that produces it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Let the derivation assign it from the axis tuple; carry the tuple alongside as the witness."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "DERIVED from the axis tuple by a total, disjoint function rather than assigned. Totality and disjointness are what let a reader trust that two entries with the same tuple always read the same, and that no tuple is left unlabelled."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FrontierReadyAuthorized a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:FrontierLabel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "ready and authorized"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Preconditions hold and policy permits dispatch without further authorization. The only label under which an effect may leave the system unattended, which makes it the highest-consequence label in the vocabulary: it asserts all four axes are clear at once, so it must be derived from the tuple and never asserted by an author."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The only label under which an effect may leave the system unattended, which makes it the highest-consequence label in the vocabulary. It asserts all four axes are clear at once, so it must be derived rather than asserted by an author."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry-9 logic:entryLabel logic:FrontierReadyAuthorized ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when preconditions hold, capability and resources are available, and policy permits dispatch with no further authorization outstanding."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid asserting it by hand on an entry: it is the label that licenses unattended external effects, so a hand-written one claims a clearance no derivation gave."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Let the frontier derivation emit it from the four axes, and treat any hand-asserted occurrence as the observed-not-derived violation the kernel guards against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FrontierReadyApprovalRequired a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:FrontierLabel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "ready but approval required"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Preconditions hold but an authorization must detach before dispatch. It differs from ready-and-authorized on the approval axis alone: preconditions, capability and resources are all satisfied, so the operator action is to authorize rather than to change anything about the world."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Differs from ready-and-authorized on the approval axis alone: preconditions, capability and resources are all satisfied. The remaining gap is a commitment that must detach, so the operator action is to authorize rather than to change anything about the world."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry-9 logic:entryLabel logic:FrontierReadyApprovalRequired ; logic:intentApprovalRequired true ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when everything except an authorization is in place, so the single outstanding act is a human or policy decision."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when a precondition is also unmet: showing an approval prompt for a step that could not run even once approved wastes the approver's decision."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Derive it from the four axes together, and surface the commitment that must detach so the approver knows exactly what they are authorizing."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FrontierBlockedPrecondition a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:FrontierLabel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "blocked by unmet precondition"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A precondition does not hold in the current state. Remediable by changing the world, unlike a capability block."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Remediable by changing the world rather than by changing who acts. That is the whole force of the distinction from a capability block: the same actor can clear this one by making the precondition true."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry-9 logic:entryLabel logic:FrontierBlockedPrecondition ; logic:guardsPrecondition ex:invoice-approved ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a named precondition does not hold in the current state and would let the step proceed if it did."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a step waiting on an external signal that no local action can produce: that is waiting, and it is resumed rather than remedied."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Emit it with logic:entryLabel and name the failing precondition, so the operator sees a fact to establish rather than an unexplained refusal."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FrontierBlockedCapabilityOrResource a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:FrontierLabel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "blocked by missing capability or resource"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "No available actor holds the capability, or a required resource is unavailable. This is the label that carries a capability-gap proposal rather than a retry suggestion."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Distinguished from a precondition block by what would fix it: here no reachable actor holds the capability, so the remedy is to acquire or delegate it, and the label carries a capability-gap proposal rather than a suggestion to try again."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry-9 logic:entryLabel logic:FrontierBlockedCapabilityOrResource ; logic:proposalMissingCapability ex:cap-sign-release ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when no available actor holds the capability the step needs, or a required resource is unavailable, so the block cannot be cleared by changing the world state alone."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when the capability exists but a precondition is false: that block is cleared by acting on the world, and mislabelling it sends the operator hunting for a permission they already have."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Emit it with logic:entryLabel and attach the gap through logic:proposalMissingCapability, so the entry names what is missing rather than merely that something is."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FrontierWaitingOnEventTimeOrInput a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:FrontierLabel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "waiting for event, time or input"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The action is blocked pending something that has not arrived. Not a failure: the distinction is what lets the enactment be resumed rather than restarted."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Not a failure, and that distinction is what lets the enactment be resumed rather than restarted. It covers only arrivals from outside: a step waiting on a fact some other step will establish is blocked on a precondition, not waiting."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry-9 logic:entryLabel logic:FrontierWaitingOnEventTimeOrInput ; logic:entryAwaits ex:signal-invoice-cleared ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the action is blocked pending an event, a time or an input that must arrive from outside the enactment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a precondition another step in the same enactment will establish: that dependency is internal and should be visible as sequencing."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Emit it with logic:entryLabel and name the awaited signal with logic:entryAwaits, so an operator can see what would unblock it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FrontierMutuallyExclusive a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:FrontierLabel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "mutually exclusive with another candidate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The action cannot proceed together with another frontier candidate. A derived obstruction rather than a per-action property: exclusivity is a relation, and it becomes visible only when local frontiers are glued."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A relation made visible only when local frontiers are glued together; no single action carries it as a property. Reading it as a per-action attribute would make it disappear from any view that looks at one step at a time, which is precisely when it matters."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry-9 logic:entryLabel logic:FrontierMutuallyExclusive ; logic:competesForResource ex:release-window ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a candidate cannot proceed together with another candidate on the glued frontier, for instance because both claim a scope only one may hold."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a step blocked by a precondition that another step happens to establish: that is ordinary sequencing, not exclusion."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Derive it when the local frontiers are glued and name the contested resource, so the operator can see which pair is in conflict rather than only that one exists."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FrontierReconciliationRequired a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:FrontierLabel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "reconciliation required"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "An effect's outcome is undetermined and must be resolved before anything else. The only licensed next action is a probe: neither redispatch nor compensation is permitted from here, because each of them acts on the world while the question of what the world already holds is still open."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The only licensed next action is a probe. Redispatch and compensation are both unlicensed from here, because each of them acts on the world while the question of what the world already holds is still open."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry-9 logic:entryLabel logic:FrontierReconciliationRequired ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when an effect's outcome is undetermined and must be resolved before any other frontier entry may act."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it once a verdict has arrived, including a verdict of still unknown: that verdict changes what is licensed next and should not be hidden behind an unchanged label."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Derive it from the undetermined commitment knowledge, and let the frontier refuse to offer redispatch or compensation while it holds."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FrontierCompensationEligible a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:FrontierLabel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "compensation eligible"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A forward effect is proved committed and compensation is permitted but not demanded. Distinguished from compensation-required, where policy or a failed enactment makes it obligatory."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Permission, not obligation. Declining is unremarkable here, which is exactly what distinguishes it from compensation-required, where declining is itself a decision with consequences the review projection will record."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry-9 logic:entryLabel logic:FrontierCompensationEligible ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a forward effect is proved committed and a compensation path exists but neither policy nor a failed enactment demands taking it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when the enactment cannot reach a consistent terminal state without compensating: that is the required label, and eligible understates the obligation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Derive it from a committed forward effect plus the absence of a policy demand, so eligibility is read off the evidence rather than chosen by an author."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FrontierCompensationRequired a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:FrontierLabel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "compensation required"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A committed forward effect must be counteracted for the enactment to reach a consistent terminal state. It is an obligation and not a permission, unlike the eligible label: without the counteracting action the enactment has no consistent terminal state to reach, so declining is itself a decision with recorded consequences."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Obligation rather than permission: without the counteracting action the enactment has no consistent terminal state to reach. Declining is therefore a decision with recorded consequences, not a neutral choice as it is under the eligible label."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry-9 logic:entryLabel logic:FrontierCompensationRequired ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when policy or a failed enactment makes counteracting a committed forward effect obligatory for a consistent close."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when the forward effect's commitment is still undetermined: reconciliation comes first, because compensating an effect that may never have happened can create it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Derive it from a committed forward effect plus the policy or failure that demands the counteraction, so the obligation has a stated source."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FrontierCompleted a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:FrontierLabel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "completed"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The action reached its terminal position successfully and needs nothing further. It is terminal for the action and silent about the enactment: it does not assert that the goal the action served has been met, which is a far wider claim than that nothing more is owed on this entry."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Terminal for the action and silent about the enactment's goal. It asserts that nothing further is owed on this entry, which is a much narrower claim than that the reason for doing it has been met."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry-9 logic:entryLabel logic:FrontierCompleted ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the action reached its terminal position successfully and no compensation, reconciliation or follow-up is owed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it to signal that the goal is met: goal evaluation is a separate judgment, and a maintenance goal stays open across completed occurrences."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Emit it with logic:entryLabel and let goal status be read from the goal evaluation, so a completed frontier over an unmet goal remains visible."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FrontierSkippedWithTypedReason a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:FrontierLabel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "skipped with a typed reason"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The action was deliberately not taken and the reason is typed rather than free text. The typing is the point: an untyped skip is indistinguishable from an oversight."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The typing is the substance of the label. An untyped skip is indistinguishable from an oversight, so this label asserts not merely that the action was not taken but that a recorded, queryable reason accompanies it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry-9 logic:entryLabel logic:FrontierSkippedWithTypedReason ; logic:skipReason logic:SkipAlreadySatisfied ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when an action was deliberately not taken and one of the typed skip reasons applies to it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it with a free-text justification only: the label claims a typed reason, and prose in its place makes the skip unqueryable while looking accounted for."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Emit it with logic:entryLabel and attach the typed reason with logic:skipReason, so a later sweep can count skips by cause."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FrontierCancelled a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:FrontierLabel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "cancelled"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The action was stopped by decision. Distinguished from terminally-failed, which is an outcome rather than a choice."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A choice, not an outcome. The label is reserved for a stop somebody decided on, so an enactment that stopped because nothing further was possible is terminally failed instead; keeping them apart is what preserves the accountable actor."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry-9 logic:entryLabel logic:FrontierCancelled ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the action was stopped by a decision and no effect of it remains outstanding."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when an effect was still in flight at the moment of cancellation: that case has its own label precisely because a plain cancelled reading hides the outstanding obligation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Emit it with logic:entryLabel only after checking the effect axis, so a cancellation over an unresolved effect is escalated to the in-flight label instead."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FrontierTerminallyFailed a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:FrontierLabel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "terminally failed"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The action failed and no continuation is available. Distinguished from an undetermined external outcome, which is epistemic rather than a failure."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A failure claim, not an epistemic one. An external outcome that is merely undetermined is not a failure however long it stays unknown, and collapsing the two would let an unresolved effect be closed as if it had been answered."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry-9 logic:entryLabel logic:FrontierTerminallyFailed ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the action failed and the frontier offers no continuation, compensation or skip for it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for an effect whose commitment is undetermined: that is a knowledge gap, and it is closed by reconciliation rather than by declaring failure."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Derive it from the exhausted continuation set, so the label reports what remains available rather than an author's reading of the error."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FrontierCompensationFailedForwardCommitted a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:FrontierLabel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "compensation failed with forward effect committed"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Compensation was required, attempted, and failed, while the forward effect remains committed — a genuinely inconsistent state needing escalation, not retry. Homeless in a labelling that stops at compensation-required, and the case an operator most needs surfaced."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Names a genuinely inconsistent state: the forward effect stands and the action meant to counteract it did not. The next move is escalation rather than retry, and the label exists because a labelling that stops at compensation-required leaves this case homeless."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry-9 logic:entryLabel logic:FrontierCompensationFailedForwardCommitted ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when compensation was required, attempted, and failed, while the forward effect remains proved committed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it while another compensation attempt is genuinely licensed: the label asserts that automatic recovery is exhausted, which a live retry path would contradict."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Derive it from the failed compensation outcome plus the committed forward receipt, and route it to escalation rather than to the retry path."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FrontierReconciliationImpossible a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:FrontierLabel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "reconciliation foreclosed"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The label DERIVED from the reconciliation-impossible class: no means of determining what happened remains. Distinct from reconciliation-required, where probing is still available; the next action here is escalation, not another probe."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Derived from the reconciliation-impossible class rather than chosen: no means of determining what happened remains. The next action is escalation, and a further probe is not merely useless here but unavailable, which is what separates it from reconciliation-required."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry-9 logic:entryLabel logic:FrontierReconciliationImpossible ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when every probe avenue for an undetermined effect is closed, so the outcome can no longer be established by any available means."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it while any probe remains available, even an expensive one: foreclosing reconciliation early discards the only knowledge-increasing move."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Derive it from the impossibility record on the attempt, and route the entry to escalation rather than leaving it in the probe loop."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FrontierCancelledWithInFlightEffects a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:FrontierLabel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "cancelled with in-flight effects"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The enactment was cancelled while an effect remained unresolved. Cancellation does not resolve commitment knowledge, so the outstanding effect still demands reconciliation — arguably the hardest real case and one a plain cancelled label hides."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Cancellation does not resolve commitment knowledge. This label exists because the plain cancelled reading silently discards an outstanding external obligation, which is the hardest real position an operator can be left in and the one most worth surfacing."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry-9 logic:entryLabel logic:FrontierCancelledWithInFlightEffects ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the enactment was cancelled while at least one effect remained in an undetermined position, so reconciliation is still owed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it once every outstanding effect has been reconciled or abandoned: at that point the cancellation is quiet and the plain label is the honest one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Derive it from the cancellation together with the effect protocol positions, so the label follows from the effects rather than from an operator's recollection of them."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

# --- RQ1/RQ5/RQ6/RQ7/RQ9: the frontier label is DERIVED, never asserted ---------
#
# SIXTEEN rules for the SIXTEEN labels: the function from the lifecycle-axis tuple to
# the operator-facing label is TOTAL over the roster, so no label is reachable only by
# hand-typing it, and every label an operator reads is a conclusion the reasoner drew.
# Authoring them as logic:Rule rather than as Rust means the canonical reasoner
# computes the frontier, the derivation is inspectable as ontology content, and the
# axis tuple that produced a label is recoverable from the same graph — which is what
# makes "why does this action carry this label" answerable without reading the engine.
#
# The rules are positive Horn, and their guards are disjoint in two different ways.
#
# Where two guards constrain the SAME axis they pin DIFFERENT positions on it, and an
# entry carries at most one position per axis, so at most one guard can match. That is
# the whole separation between ready-authorized and ready-approval-required (the
# approval axis), between cancelled and cancelled-with-in-flight-effects (the
# commitment-knowledge axis), and between terminally-failed and
# compensation-failed-forward-committed (the enactment axis).
#
# Where two guards constrain DISJOINT axes they are separated by reachability under the
# axis transition laws together with one modelling discipline: a frontier entry
# positions ONE candidate action and carries only the witnesses that position IT. A
# pending step's entry therefore carries no effect position, because a step that has
# not begun has dispatched nothing; and an external exchange's entry carries no step
# position, because the step that dispatched it is positioned by its own entry.
#
# Three labels are refinements of a coarser obstruction rather than of an axis value,
# and in each the refining datum is a witness the entry carries rather than a position
# it occupies: blocked-on-capability names the operational capability gap standing on
# its action, waiting-on-event-time-or-input names the awaited thing through
# logic:entryAwaits, and skipped-with-a-typed-reason names the logic:SkipReason. The
# first two are disjoint because a step blocked by a capability the deployment does not
# have awaits nothing — what is absent does not arrive. Mutually-exclusive is the one
# guard that is purely relational, because exclusivity is a relation between two
# candidates and belongs on no axis at all.

logic:ruleFrontierReadyAuthorized
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "frontier label: ready and authorized"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:FrontierReadyAuthorized for a frontier entry whose step axis is ready and whose approval axis is null — the policy already authorizes the effect, so no operator gate stands between the action and its dispatch. Positive Horn. This is the rule that makes policy-maximal automation a derivation rather than a habit: an entry is authorized because its axis tuple says so, not because nobody remembered to gate it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleFrontierReadyAuthorized ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryLabel ;
        rdf:object    logic:FrontierReadyAuthorized
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:StepReady
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:ApprovalNull
    ] .

logic:ruleFrontierReadyApprovalRequired
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "frontier label: ready but approval required"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:FrontierReadyApprovalRequired for a frontier entry whose step axis is ready and whose approval axis is created but not yet detached. Readiness and approval are separate axes, and this rule is where that separation earns its keep: the same entry is simultaneously ready to run and gated, which a single blended state could only express by minting a combined value for every such pairing."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleFrontierReadyApprovalRequired ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryLabel ;
        rdf:object    logic:FrontierReadyApprovalRequired
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:StepReady
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:ApprovalCreated
    ] .

logic:ruleFrontierReconciliationRequired
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "frontier label: reconciliation required"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:FrontierReconciliationRequired for a frontier entry whose effect axis sits at attempted — an effect was dispatched and no receipt settled it. This is the no-blind-retry law stated positively: rather than only forbidding a retry, the frontier NAMES the one action that is licensed from an undetermined position, so an operator reading it is told what to do instead of merely what not to do."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleFrontierReconciliationRequired ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryLabel ;
        rdf:object    logic:FrontierReconciliationRequired
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:EffectAttempted
    ] .

logic:ruleFrontierBlockedCapability
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "frontier label: blocked on capability or resource"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:FrontierBlockedCapabilityOrResource for a frontier entry whose step axis is waiting and whose action is the blocked step of an operational capability gap. The gap is an operational one — a capability the deployment does not have — and never a reasoning-profile gap, so this rule is also where the non-conflation is enforced by construction rather than by a reviewer noticing."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleFrontierBlockedCapability ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryLabel ;
        rdf:object    logic:FrontierBlockedCapabilityOrResource
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:StepWaiting
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAction ;
        rdf:object    "?step"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?gap" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:gapBlockedStep ;
        rdf:object    "?step"
    ] .

logic:ruleFrontierCompensationEligible
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "frontier label: compensation eligible"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:FrontierCompensationEligible for a frontier entry whose effect axis sits at receipted — a forward effect is known to have committed, so a counteraction is now permissible. Eligibility is all this derives: it asserts no compensation attempt, no compensation outcome, and no claim that compensating will succeed, because compensation is a new action with its own possibility of failing."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleFrontierCompensationEligible ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryLabel ;
        rdf:object    logic:FrontierCompensationEligible
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:EffectReceipted
    ] .

logic:ruleFrontierBlockedPrecondition
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "frontier label: blocked on an unmet precondition"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:FrontierBlockedPrecondition for a frontier entry whose step axis is pending — the step belongs to the enactment and its preconditions have not been evaluated as met, which is exactly the position the step axis mints for an unmet precondition. Positive Horn, and disjoint from every other rule by the step axis alone. The label matters because its remedy differs from every neighbouring obstruction: a precondition is remediable by changing the world, where a missing capability is not, and telling an operator to wait when the answer is to change the world is the failure this separation prevents."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleFrontierBlockedPrecondition ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryLabel ;
        rdf:object    logic:FrontierBlockedPrecondition
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:StepPending
    ] .

logic:ruleFrontierWaitingOnEventTimeOrInput
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "frontier label: waiting on an event, a time or an input"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:FrontierWaitingOnEventTimeOrInput for a frontier entry whose step axis is waiting and which names, through logic:entryAwaits, the thing it is blocked pending. The awaited witness is what makes the label a derivation rather than a shrug: the step axis says THAT the step waits, and only the witness says what would end the wait. It is also what keeps this rule disjoint from the capability-gap rule, which shares the waiting position — a step blocked by a capability the deployment does not have awaits nothing, because what is absent does not arrive, and its remedy is a capability-gap proposal rather than patience."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleFrontierWaitingOnEventTimeOrInput ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryLabel ;
        rdf:object    logic:FrontierWaitingOnEventTimeOrInput
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:StepWaiting
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAwaits ;
        rdf:object    "?awaited"
    ] .

logic:ruleFrontierMutuallyExclusive
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "frontier label: mutually exclusive with another candidate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:FrontierMutuallyExclusive for a frontier entry that logic:entryExcludes another entry. This is the one guard in the set that reads no axis, and deliberately: exclusivity is a relation between two candidates and not a position either of them occupies, so putting it on an axis would be the conflation the seven-axis discipline exists to refuse. It becomes visible only when local frontiers are glued, which is also why the withheld candidate carries no readiness position — being ready is a claim about a frontier on which the excluding candidate was not taken."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleFrontierMutuallyExclusive ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryLabel ;
        rdf:object    logic:FrontierMutuallyExclusive
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryExcludes ;
        rdf:object    "?other"
    ] .

logic:ruleFrontierCompensationRequired
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "frontier label: compensation required"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:FrontierCompensationRequired for a frontier entry whose effect axis sits at reconciled, whose commitment knowledge is supported, and whose enactment axis is failed — a probe settled that the forward effect committed, and the occurrence it belonged to will not complete, so the committed effect has to be counteracted for the run to reach a consistent terminal position. Three axes carry this and none of them could alone: the effect position says the question was answered, the knowledge value says which way, and the enactment position is what turns permission into obligation. That is precisely what separates it from compensation-eligible, where declining costs nothing."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleFrontierCompensationRequired ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryLabel ;
        rdf:object    logic:FrontierCompensationRequired
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:EffectReconciled
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:InfoSupported
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:EnactmentFailed
    ] .

logic:ruleFrontierCompensationFailedForwardCommitted
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "frontier label: compensation failed with the forward effect committed"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:FrontierCompensationFailedForwardCommitted for a frontier entry whose exchange sits in its compensating leg, whose step axis is failed, whose commitment knowledge is still supported, and whose enactment is still running. Read together those four positions say the whole thing: a compensating action was launched, the step carrying it terminally failed, the forward effect it was launched against remains committed, and the occurrence has not given up. The last position is what separates this from terminally-failed — the enactment is still running because escalation is a continuation, and escalation rather than retry is the only move this position licenses."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleFrontierCompensationFailedForwardCommitted ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryLabel ;
        rdf:object    logic:FrontierCompensationFailedForwardCommitted
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:StepFailed
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:EffectCompensating
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:InfoSupported
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:EnactmentRunning
    ] .

logic:ruleFrontierReconciliationImpossible
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "frontier label: reconciliation foreclosed"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:FrontierReconciliationImpossible for a frontier entry whose effect axis sits at abandoned — the exchange was given up without an authoritative determination because no reconciliation semantics remain available. The effect axis mints exactly one position for that, so the guard needs nothing else. The label is the honest counterpart of reconciliation-required: there, probing is still available and the frontier names it; here it is foreclosed, and naming another probe would send an operator after evidence that cannot be obtained."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleFrontierReconciliationImpossible ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryLabel ;
        rdf:object    logic:FrontierReconciliationImpossible
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:EffectAbandoned
    ] .

logic:ruleFrontierCompleted
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "frontier label: completed"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:FrontierCompleted for a frontier entry whose step axis is completed — the step carried out its prescribed work and reached a terminal position, so nothing further is owed on it. The guard reads that one axis and stops there on purpose: the label asserts nothing about whether the enactment's goal is met, which is the goal-evaluation axis, and nothing about whether an external effect the step dispatched actually committed, which is the commitment-knowledge axis carried by the exchange's own entry."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleFrontierCompleted ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryLabel ;
        rdf:object    logic:FrontierCompleted
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:StepCompleted
    ] .

logic:ruleFrontierSkippedWithTypedReason
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "frontier label: skipped with a typed reason"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:FrontierSkippedWithTypedReason for a frontier entry whose step axis is skipped and which carries a logic:skipReason. The typed reason is a guard and not a decoration: the label's whole content is that the omission was deliberate and attributable, so an entry claiming a skip without one derives nothing rather than deriving a label it cannot support — which is the correct outcome, because an untyped skip is indistinguishable from an oversight and the frontier must not present the two alike."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleFrontierSkippedWithTypedReason ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryLabel ;
        rdf:object    logic:FrontierSkippedWithTypedReason
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:StepSkipped
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:skipReason ;
        rdf:object    "?reason"
    ] .

logic:ruleFrontierCancelled
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "frontier label: cancelled"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:FrontierCancelled for a frontier entry whose step and enactment axes are both cancelled and whose commitment knowledge was never evaluated — the quiescent cancellation, where stopping by decision settles the matter because no external effect was ever in question. The knowledge position is the load-bearing conjunct: it is what distinguishes this from cancellation with in-flight effects, and reading a bare cancelled position as quiescent is exactly how an outstanding exchange gets closed on paper while remaining open at the provider."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleFrontierCancelled ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryLabel ;
        rdf:object    logic:FrontierCancelled
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:StepCancelled
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:EnactmentCancelled
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:InfoNotEvaluated
    ] .

logic:ruleFrontierCancelledWithInFlightEffects
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "frontier label: cancelled with in-flight effects"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:FrontierCancelledWithInFlightEffects for a frontier entry whose step and enactment axes are both cancelled while its commitment knowledge remains undetermined. Cancellation is a decision about the occurrence and it does not resolve knowledge about the world, so the outstanding exchange still demands reconciliation; the sibling entry that positions that exchange reads reconciliation-required. It differs from plain cancelled on the commitment-knowledge axis and on nothing else, which is exactly why the two cannot share a slot — and it is arguably the hardest real position an operator meets, which a bare cancelled label hides."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleFrontierCancelledWithInFlightEffects ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryLabel ;
        rdf:object    logic:FrontierCancelledWithInFlightEffects
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:StepCancelled
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:EnactmentCancelled
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:InfoUndetermined
    ] .

logic:ruleFrontierTerminallyFailed
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "frontier label: terminally failed"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:FrontierTerminallyFailed for a frontier entry whose step axis is failed and whose enactment axis is failed — the step attempted its work and failed, and the occurrence stopped because no continuation is available, which is the enactment axis's own definition of failure. Both conjuncts are needed: a failed step inside a still-running enactment has a continuation somewhere, and the enactment position is what says there is none. That is also what separates it from compensation-failed-forward-committed, where the run is still going because escalation remains open."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleFrontierTerminallyFailed ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryLabel ;
        rdf:object    logic:FrontierTerminallyFailed
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:StepFailed
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?entry" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:entryAxisWitness ;
        rdf:object    logic:EnactmentFailed
    ] .

logic:SkipReason
    a logic:AbstractIndividualType ,
        logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:QualityValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Skip Reason"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed vocabulary of why an action was deliberately not taken: it was not applicable, it was already satisfied, policy excluded it, or an operator declined it. Typed so that a skip is auditable and distinguishable from an oversight, which an untyped skip is not."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:ocrSkip logic:skipReason logic:SkipAlreadySatisfied ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use whenever an action is skipped."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid free-text skip reasons and avoid skipping without one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach exactly one member to the skipped entry."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Typed so a skip is auditable and distinguishable from an oversight, which an untyped skip is not. The four values differ in who or what decided, so counting skips by reason tells an operator whether the prescription or the world is the problem."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:SkipNotApplicable a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:SkipReason ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "not applicable"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The action's applicability condition did not hold for this occurrence. Distinguished from already-satisfied: the action was never called for, rather than already done."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The action was never called for in this occurrence, so nothing about the world made it redundant. A sweep of these skips is evidence about the prescription's applicability conditions, not about the state."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:step-4 logic:skipReason logic:SkipNotApplicable ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the action's applicability condition did not hold for this occurrence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when an operator simply chose not to act: that decision has an attributed reason of its own, and this value would erase the decider."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:skipReason, and read a high rate of these as a signal that the prescription may be carrying steps for a different case."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:SkipAlreadySatisfied a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:SkipReason ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "already satisfied"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The action's intended effect already obtains, so taking it would be redundant. Unlike a not-applicable skip, the action was genuinely called for and has simply been achieved already, so it is evidence about the world rather than about the prescription: a sweep of these skips reveals redundant steps, not misapplied ones."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The action was called for and is already achieved, which is what distinguishes it from not-applicable. It is therefore evidence about the world, not about the prescription, and a sweep of these skips reveals redundant steps rather than misapplied ones."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:step-4 logic:skipReason logic:SkipAlreadySatisfied ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the action's intended effect already obtains, so taking it would be redundant rather than wrong."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when the applicability condition never held: the action was not called for at all, and recording it as already done misdescribes the occurrence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:skipReason, and treat a recurring pattern of these skips as evidence for revising the prescription rather than as noise."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:SkipPolicyExcluded a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:SkipReason ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "policy excluded"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Governing policy excluded the action for this occurrence. Distinguished from an approval block: policy forbids it outright rather than requiring authorization."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Policy forbids the action outright rather than requiring authorization for it, which is what separates it from an approval block. No approver can grant what policy has excluded, so surfacing an approval prompt here would waste a decision."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:step-4 logic:skipReason logic:SkipPolicyExcluded ; logic:approvalPolicy ex:embargo-policy ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when governing policy excluded the action for this occurrence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when policy merely demands an authorization: that is a live approval requirement, not an exclusion."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:skipReason and name the governing policy, so a reader can challenge the policy rather than the omission."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:SkipOperatorDeclined a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:SkipReason ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "operator declined"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "An authorized operator chose not to take the action. Attributed, because a declined action is a decision somebody made and not a property of the state."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Attributed by construction, because a declined action is a decision somebody made and not a property of the state. That attribution is what makes it reviewable later, and it is the reason this reason is not merged with policy exclusion."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:step-4 logic:skipReason logic:SkipOperatorDeclined ; logic:approvalOperator ex:operator-ling ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when an authorized operator chose not to take an action that was available to them."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it where governing policy removed the option: the operator had no choice to exercise, and attributing the omission to them is unfair as well as inaccurate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:skipReason together with the operator, so the decision remains attributable at review time."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:skipReason
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FrontierEntry ;
    rdfs:range logic:SkipReason ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has skip reason"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a skipped logic:FrontierEntry to its typed reason. Functional: a skip carries one ground, not a list. A step apparently skipped for two reasons is a modelling smell, since one of them is the real ground and the other its consequence, and choosing between them is part of documenting the skip honestly."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional, so a skip has one typed reason rather than a list. A step skipped for two reasons is a modelling smell: one of them is the real ground and the other is a consequence, and choosing which is part of documenting the skip honestly."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry-9 logic:skipReason logic:SkipPolicyExcluded ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use on every skipped frontier entry or step to name its typed reason."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it on an entry that was blocked rather than skipped: a block is an obstruction the frontier may clear, and a skip is a decision not to act."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Choose the ground that actually governed the omission, so a sweep by reason counts causes rather than symptoms."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:frontierVantage
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FrontierEntry ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "frontier vantage"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The standpoint a frontier entry's label is relative to. Range a standpoint (stated in prose). Whether an action requires approval is a claim UNDER a policy, and two governing policies can disagree, so classifications coexist as attributed observations rather than collapsing to one global verdict."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:frontier logic:frontierVantage ex:opsStandpoint ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to attribute a frontier classification to the standpoint that made it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid presenting a policy-relative classification as absolute; two policies can disagree about whether an action requires approval."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attribute it, so disagreeing classifications coexist as claims rather than conflicting facts."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Whether an action requires approval is a claim UNDER a policy, and two governing policies can disagree, so classifications coexist rather than one overwriting the other. Omitting the vantage presents one policy's reading as the situation itself."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:OperationOutcome
    a logic:AbstractIndividualType ,
        logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:QualityValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Outcome"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The total six-way outcome of an engine operation. It is not a restatement of the existing evaluation-status axis, which is three-valued with no value for cancellation and none for engine failure, and which leaves invalid input on a different axis entirely. Collapsing those into 'incomplete' loses the distinction between a run that was cut short, one that was asked something malformed, and one where the engine itself broke."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:frontierRun logic:operationOutcome logic:OutcomeApplied ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to report what became of one engine operation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid collapsing cancellation, invalidity and engine failure into a single incomplete result."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach exactly one member, plus the typed cause enumeration its value calls for."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Closes a real gap rather than restating an existing axis: the evaluation-status axis is three-valued with no value for cancellation and none for engine failure, and invalid input lives on a different axis entirely."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:OutcomeApplied a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:OperationOutcome ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "applied"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The operation completed and its effect on the engine state was applied. It is the only outcome under which a result may be read as complete: every other outcome leaves some part of the answer unknown, so a consumer that does not separate them will report the absence of a fact as its falsity."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The only outcome under which a result may be read as complete. Every other outcome leaves some part of the answer unknown, so a consumer that treats them alike will report absence of a fact as its falsity."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:op-52 logic:operationOutcome logic:OutcomeApplied ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the operation completed and its effect on the engine state was applied in full."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a sound but partial run: incomplete exists so a consumer can tell a closed-world reading from an open one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:operationOutcome and let a closed-world reading of the result be conditioned on this value alone."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:OutcomeRequiresFullRebuild a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:OperationOutcome ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "requires full rebuild"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The operation cannot be applied incrementally and the state must be rebuilt from scratch. A recoverable outcome, distinguished from failure: nothing is wrong, the incremental path simply does not apply."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A recoverable outcome rather than an error: nothing is wrong, the incremental path simply does not apply. Treating it as a failure would send an operator hunting for a defect where there is only a changed entitlement."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:op-52 logic:operationOutcome logic:OutcomeRequiresFullRebuild ; logic:rebuildReason logic:RebuildRuleSetChanged ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the operation cannot be applied incrementally and the closure must be recomputed from scratch, for one of the typed rebuild reasons."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it as a catch-all for an incremental step that merely failed: the outcome asserts that the incremental algebra does not cover the change, which is a claim about the change and not about the run."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:operationOutcome and name the reason with logic:rebuildReason, so the cost of the rebuild is attributable to a specific change."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:OutcomeUnsupportedFragment a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:OperationOutcome ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "unsupported fragment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The input lies outside the fragment the engine is certified for. Distinguished from incomplete: no budget would have sufficed, because the engine was never entitled to terminate on this input."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "No budget would have sufficed: the engine was never entitled to terminate on this input. That is what separates it from incomplete, where the entitlement held and only the allowance ran out."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:op-52 logic:operationOutcome logic:OutcomeUnsupportedFragment ; logic:unsupportedCondition logic:UnsupportedNonTerminating ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the input lies outside the fragment the engine is certified for, so termination was never guaranteed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for an input inside the fragment that merely ran long: raising a budget can fix that, and this outcome tells the caller not to bother trying."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:operationOutcome and name the condition with logic:unsupportedCondition, so the caller can see which construct took the input out of the fragment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:OutcomeIncomplete a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:OperationOutcome ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "incomplete operation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The operation was sound but did not finish, for a typed cause. Sound-but-partial: what it did derive is correct, and what it did not derive is unknown rather than false."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Sound but partial: what was derived is correct and what was not derived is unknown rather than false. It always carries a typed cause, because a partial result whose limit is unstated cannot be acted on responsibly."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:op-52 logic:operationOutcome logic:OutcomeIncomplete ; logic:incompleteCause logic:IncompleteStepBudget ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the operation was sound but stopped short for one of the typed incompleteness causes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it without naming the cause: the four causes differ in what would fix them, and an unattributed incompleteness cannot be remedied."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:operationOutcome together with logic:incompleteCause, and forbid closed-world readings of the result downstream."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:OutcomeInvalid a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:OperationOutcome ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "invalid"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The operation was rejected because its input was malformed or its precondition failed, for a typed integrity fault. Nothing was attempted, so no partial result exists. The distinction from incomplete is not one of degree: an invalid operation produced no derivation at all, and a consumer must never mine it for whatever it managed to compute."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Nothing was attempted, so no partial result exists. The distinction from incomplete is not one of degree: an invalid operation produced no derivation at all, and a consumer must not mine it for whatever it managed to compute."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:op-52 logic:operationOutcome logic:OutcomeInvalid ; logic:integrityFault logic:FaultContentIntegrity ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the request was rejected up front because its input was malformed or a precondition failed, under a named integrity fault."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when work began and then stopped: that leaves a sound partial result, and invalid would discard evidence that exists."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:operationOutcome and name the fault with logic:integrityFault, so the caller learns which precondition to fix."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:OutcomeEngineFailure a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:OperationOutcome ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "engine failure"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The engine itself failed. It is the only outcome that says nothing whatever about the input: no conclusion about the query may be drawn from it, and retrying the identical request may succeed, which is why it must never be folded into invalid or unsupported."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The only outcome that says nothing whatever about the input. No conclusion about the query may be drawn from it, and retrying the identical request may succeed, which is why it must never be folded into invalid or unsupported."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:op-52 logic:operationOutcome logic:OutcomeEngineFailure ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the engine itself failed, so the request was neither rejected on its merits nor answered in part."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a malformed request: invalid names an input defect, and reporting it as an engine failure invites an endless retry of something that can never succeed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:operationOutcome and draw no inference about the query, since the input was never given a verdict."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:operationOutcome
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:OperationOutcome ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has operation outcome"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates an operation record to its exactly-one outcome. Domain an operation record (stated in prose). Functional, and functionality is the point: an operation recorded as both incomplete and unsupported would leave a consumer unable to decide whether raising a budget helps. One outcome, one remedy — never two."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Relates an operation record to its exactly-one outcome, and functionality is the point: an operation that was both incomplete and unsupported would leave a consumer unable to decide whether raising a budget helps. One outcome, one remedy."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:op-52 logic:operationOutcome logic:OutcomeApplied ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use on every operation record to state which of the six outcomes it reached."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid leaving it absent and inferring the outcome from the presence of results: an empty result set is ambiguous between applied-with-no-answers and never attempted."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it together with the outcome's own typed detail, so the remedy follows from the record without a second query."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:IncompleteCause
    a logic:AbstractIndividualType ,
        logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:QualityValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Incomplete Cause"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Why an operation was incomplete: a step budget, a cancellation, a deadline, or a page budget. Typed because the remedies differ — a step budget suggests raising it, a cancellation suggests no remedy at all, a deadline suggests scheduling, a page budget suggests paging — and an untyped incompleteness leaves all four indistinguishable rather than merely imprecise."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:frontierRun logic:incompleteCause logic:IncompleteStepBudget ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use alongside an incomplete outcome."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an untyped incomplete result."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach exactly one member whenever the outcome is incomplete."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Typed because the remedies differ: a step budget suggests raising it, a cancellation suggests nothing, a deadline suggests scheduling and a page budget suggests paging. An untyped incompleteness leaves all four indistinguishable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:IncompleteStepBudget a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:IncompleteCause ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "step budget"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The declared derivation budget was exhausted. Remediable by raising the budget, unlike a cancellation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The one cause a caller can straightforwardly remedy by raising the declared budget, because the engine was entitled to terminate and simply ran out of allowance. It is not an admissibility claim: the input stayed inside the certified fragment throughout."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:op-52 logic:incompleteCause logic:IncompleteStepBudget ; logic:consumedBudget 100000 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the declared derivation budget was exhausted on an input the engine is certified to terminate on."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for an input outside the certified fragment: no budget would have sufficed there, and the honest outcome is unsupported fragment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:incompleteCause and report the consumed budget, so raising the allowance is an informed decision rather than a guess."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:IncompleteCancellation a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:IncompleteCause ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "cancellation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The caller cancelled the operation before it finished. Not a resource limit: the engine would have completed given the chance."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Not a resource limit. The engine would have completed given the chance, so raising a budget will not change the outcome and the remedy is entirely on the caller's side."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:op-52 logic:incompleteCause logic:IncompleteCancellation ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the caller stopped the operation before it finished, so the partial result reflects an interruption rather than a limit."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a deadline the caller set: an elapsed deadline is a limit the caller chose, and reporting it as cancellation hides the budget that was actually binding."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:incompleteCause on the incomplete outcome, so an operator reading the result knows re-running unchanged may well succeed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:IncompleteDeadline a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:IncompleteCause ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "deadline"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A wall-clock deadline elapsed. It is not a step budget: the same work under identical step limits may complete on a less loaded host, so the cause is machine-dependent by construction and a reproducibility claim must never rest on a deadline-limited result."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "A wall-clock cause, so it is machine-dependent by construction: the same work under identical step limits may complete on a less loaded host. Reproducibility claims must therefore never rest on a deadline-limited result."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:op-52 logic:incompleteCause logic:IncompleteDeadline ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a wall-clock deadline elapsed while the derivation was still sound and in progress."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it where the declared step budget was the binding limit: only the step budget gives a machine-independent account of what was affordable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:incompleteCause, and prefer a step budget wherever the result is meant to be comparable across runs."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:IncompletePageBudget a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:IncompleteCause ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "page budget"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A page or byte budget over the answer set was exhausted. The derivation may have been complete; the delivery was not."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Concerns delivery rather than derivation. The closure may have been complete and only the answer set truncated, so this cause never licenses the conclusion that anything is underived."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:op-52 logic:incompleteCause logic:IncompletePageBudget ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a page or byte budget over the answer set was exhausted while the derivation itself ran to completion."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when the derivation stopped early: that is a step budget or a deadline, and confusing the two hides whether more reasoning remains to be done."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:incompleteCause and page the request rather than raising the derivation budget, since the derivation was not the limit."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:incompleteCause
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:IncompleteCause ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has incomplete cause"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates an incomplete operation record to its typed cause, so an incompleteness is never reported bare. Domain an operation record (stated in prose). Functional: the four causes differ in what would fix them, and one incompleteness has one binding limit."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Attaches the typed cause to the record, so an incompleteness is never reported bare. The four causes differ in what would fix them, which is why the relation is functional: one incompleteness has one binding limit."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:op-52 logic:incompleteCause logic:IncompleteDeadline ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use on every operation record whose outcome is incomplete, to name which limit bound it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it on a record that is invalid or unsupported: those carry their own typed vocabularies, and reusing this one would blur what is remediable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Emit it together with the incomplete outcome, so a consumer can decide between raising a budget, paging, and rewriting the input."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:IntegrityFault
    a logic:AbstractIndividualType ,
        logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:QualityValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Integrity Fault"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Why an operation was rejected as invalid, in the ORDER the restore gate checks them: corrupt content, failed re-materialization, identity mismatch, then data-generation mismatch. The ordering is part of the contract and not an implementation choice — a corrupt checkpoint must be rejected before an identity comparison is even attempted, because a fault derived from identities read out of corrupt bytes misnames its own cause."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:restoreAttempt logic:integrityFault logic:FaultIdentityMismatch ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use alongside an invalid outcome, especially on a checkpoint restore."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid reporting an identity mismatch for input that failed the content-integrity check."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach exactly one member, respecting the gate order."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The ORDER is part of the contract, not an implementation choice: a corrupt checkpoint must be rejected before any identity comparison, because comparing identities read out of corrupt bytes produces a fault that misnames its own cause."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FaultContentIntegrity a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:IntegrityFault ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "content integrity"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Gate one: the stored bytes do not match their own digest. Checked first, because every later comparison would be reading corrupt data."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The first of the four checkpoint gates, and the only one that is about the bytes rather than about identity. It is checked first because every later comparison would otherwise be reading corrupt data and reporting a mismatch that is really corruption."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:restore-4 logic:integrityFault logic:FaultContentIntegrity ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the stored checkpoint bytes do not match their own recorded digest, so no further gate result can be trusted."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for a state that read back intact but replayed differently: that is re-materialization, and the distinction is what tells storage failure from engine drift."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Report it with logic:integrityFault and stop the restore there, since running the identity and generation gates over corrupt bytes produces misleading faults."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FaultRematerialization a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:IntegrityFault ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "re-materialization"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Gate two: the state could not be deterministically re-derived from its recorded inputs. Distinguished from corruption: the bytes were intact and the replay still diverged."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Separates storage failure from engine drift. The bytes were intact and the replay still diverged, which means the recorded inputs no longer determine the recorded state — a stronger and more alarming claim than corruption."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:restore-4 logic:integrityFault logic:FaultRematerialization ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the checkpoint bytes verified against their digest but the state could not be deterministically re-derived from its recorded inputs."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when the digest itself failed: that is content integrity, and the ordering of the gates exists so the two are never confused."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Report it with logic:integrityFault and treat it as an engine or rule-set investigation, not a storage one, since the bytes were already shown intact."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FaultIdentityMismatch a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:IntegrityFault ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "identity mismatch"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Gate three: one of the seven identity axes differs from the checkpoint's. It reports THAT one differs and not WHICH: the folded descriptor hash is a single comparison by design, and the per-axis digests are what a reader consults next, so this fault points at the finer question rather than answering it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Reports THAT one of the seven identity axes differs, not WHICH. The folded descriptor hash is a single comparison by design; the per-axis digests are what a reader consults next, so this fault is a pointer to that finer question rather than an answer to it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:restore-4 logic:integrityFault logic:FaultIdentityMismatch ; logic:checkpointDescriptorHash ex:hash-7c ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the folded descriptor hash of the restore differs from the checkpoint's, after content integrity and re-materialization have both passed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when only the data generation moved: that has its own fault precisely because it is the one a caller may re-authorize."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Report it with logic:integrityFault and publish the per-axis digests alongside, so the operator can see which axis drifted without re-running the restore."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FaultDataGeneration a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:IntegrityFault ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "data generation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Gate four: the authorized data generation differs from the one the checkpoint was taken over. Checked last because it is the only one of the four gates a caller may legitimately intend to cross, by explicitly re-authorizing against a newer generation: crossing it is a decision, whereas the earlier three are not decisions but always errors."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The only one of the four gates a caller may legitimately intend to cross, by explicitly re-authorizing against a newer generation. That is why it is checked last: crossing it is a decision, whereas the earlier three are always errors."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:restore-4 logic:integrityFault logic:FaultDataGeneration ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the authorized data generation differs from the one the checkpoint was taken over, and the caller has not re-authorized the newer generation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when an identity axis other than the data generation moved: those are identity mismatches, and merging them would lose which axis actually drifted."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Report it with logic:integrityFault after the first three gates pass, and let an explicit re-authorization rather than a relaxed gate be the way a caller crosses it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:integrityFault
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:IntegrityFault ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has integrity fault"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates an invalid operation record to its typed fault. Domain an operation record (stated in prose). It serves the checkpoint gates and the invalid-operation record alike, so one vocabulary answers which precondition failed in either setting. Functional: a record that trips two gates is reported at the first, because the later gates read state the first has already shown untrustworthy and their verdicts cannot be relied on."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Serves both the checkpoint gates and the invalid-operation record, so one vocabulary answers which precondition failed in either setting. Functional: a record that trips two gates is reported at the first, because later gates read state the first has already shown untrustworthy."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:restore-4 logic:integrityFault logic:FaultRematerialization ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to name the typed fault on a rejected restore or an invalid operation record."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid recording a second fault for the same record: the gate ordering means only the first is a sound observation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Evaluate the gates in their declared order and record the first fault reached, so the report names a cause rather than a consequence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:RebuildReason
    a logic:AbstractIndividualType ,
        logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:QualityValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Rebuild Reason"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Why an operation demanded a full rebuild rather than an incremental application: the rule set changed, a retraction crossed a non-incremental boundary, or the fragment changed. Typed so a rebuild is explainable rather than mysterious, and so a frequent cause can be measured and addressed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:applyDelta logic:rebuildReason logic:RebuildRuleSetChanged ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use alongside a requires-full-rebuild outcome."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an unexplained rebuild."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach exactly one member."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Typed so a rebuild is explainable rather than mysterious, and so a frequent cause can be attacked. An untyped rebuild rate tells an operator that recomputation is expensive without telling them which change to stop making."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:RebuildRuleSetChanged a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:RebuildReason ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "rule set changed"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The governing rules changed, so previously derived conclusions can no longer be trusted incrementally. It is not a claim that any particular conclusion is now wrong, only that none of them may be carried forward without re-derivation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Previously derived conclusions can no longer be trusted incrementally because the rules that produced them moved. It is not a claim that any particular conclusion is now wrong, only that none may be carried forward without re-derivation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:op-52 logic:rebuildReason logic:RebuildRuleSetChanged ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the governing rules changed between the prior closure and the current request."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when the fragment itself changed: that is a change of entitlement rather than of content, and the two are worth telling apart in a rebuild audit."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:rebuildReason, so repeated rebuilds can be attributed to rule churn rather than to data volume."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:RebuildNonIncrementalRetraction a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:RebuildReason ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "non-incremental retraction"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A retraction crossed a boundary the incremental algebra does not support, so the closure must be recomputed rather than adjusted."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Names a limit of the incremental algebra, not a defect in the retraction. The retraction is perfectly well formed; it simply crosses a boundary the incremental machinery cannot adjust across, so the closure is recomputed instead."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:op-52 logic:rebuildReason logic:RebuildNonIncrementalRetraction ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a retraction crosses a boundary the incremental algebra does not support, so adjustment is impossible and recomputation is required."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it to describe a rejected retraction: the retraction is accepted here, and only the way of applying it changes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:rebuildReason so a reader can tell an algebra limit from a governance change in the rules or the fragment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:RebuildFragmentChanged a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:RebuildReason ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "fragment changed"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The certified fragment changed, so the prior closure was computed under a different entitlement to terminate. That makes it not merely stale but derived under different guarantees, which is a stronger reason to discard it than a change of data would be."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The prior closure was computed under a different entitlement to terminate, so it is not merely stale but derived under different guarantees. That is a stronger reason to discard it than a change of data would be."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:op-52 logic:rebuildReason logic:RebuildFragmentChanged ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the certified fragment moved between the prior closure and the current request."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when only the rules inside an unchanged fragment were edited: that has its own reason, and the two carry different implications for what may be reused."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:rebuildReason so the rebuild cost is attributable to the fragment declaration rather than to the data."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:rebuildReason
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:RebuildReason ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has rebuild reason"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a requires-full-rebuild operation record to its typed reason, so a rebuild is always attributable to a specific change rather than reported bare. Domain an operation record (stated in prose). Functional: two reasons would leave a rebuild audit unable to say which change actually cost the recomputation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Attaches the typed reason to a requires-full-rebuild record, so a rebuild is always attributable to a specific change. Functional, because two reasons would leave a rebuild audit unable to say which change actually cost the recomputation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:op-52 logic:rebuildReason logic:RebuildFragmentChanged ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use on every requires-full-rebuild operation record to name which change forced the recomputation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it on an operation that failed: a rebuild is a recoverable outcome, and reporting a failure this way suggests a remedy that does not apply."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with the outcome so repeated rebuilds can be attributed to rule churn, fragment churn or retraction shape."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:UnsupportedFragmentCondition
    a logic:AbstractIndividualType ,
        logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:QualityValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Unsupported Fragment Condition"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "What placed an input outside the certified fragment: an unadmitted construct, a non-terminating pattern, or a method set outside the declared search fragment. Typed so an unsupported verdict names the offending condition instead of refusing opaquely — a refusal a caller cannot act on is barely better than a wrong answer."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:searchRun logic:unsupportedCondition logic:UnsupportedMethodSet ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use alongside an unsupported-fragment outcome."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an opaque unsupported refusal."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach exactly one member and name the offending construct alongside it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Typed so an unsupported verdict names the offending condition instead of refusing opaquely. The three conditions differ in what a caller should do next, and an unnamed one leaves rewriting, contract selection and giving up equally plausible."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:UnsupportedConstruct a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:UnsupportedFragmentCondition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "unadmitted construct"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The input uses a construct the certified fragment does not admit. Remediable by rephrasing within the fragment or selecting a different contract."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Remediable by rephrasing within the fragment or selecting a different contract, which makes it the mildest of the three unsupported conditions. It is a statement about the wording of the input, not about its intent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:op-52 logic:unsupportedCondition logic:UnsupportedConstruct ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the input uses a construct the certified fragment does not admit, and an equivalent phrasing inside the fragment exists or a different contract would accept it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for an input with no termination certificate: rephrasing will not help there, and the honest condition is the non-terminating one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:unsupportedCondition and name the construct, so the caller can rewrite rather than guess at which contract to select."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:UnsupportedNonTerminating a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:UnsupportedFragmentCondition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "non-terminating pattern"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The input contains a pattern with no termination certificate. It is not a budget exhaustion: no budget makes it terminate, it simply runs longer, and reporting one as the other invites an operator to raise a limit that can never be high enough."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "No budget makes it terminate; it simply runs longer. That is the whole difference from an exhaustion, and reporting one as the other invites an operator to raise a limit that can never be high enough."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:op-52 logic:unsupportedCondition logic:UnsupportedNonTerminating ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the input contains a pattern carrying no termination certificate, so the engine was never entitled to stop on it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for an admitted pattern that was merely slow: an exhaustion is remediable and this is not, and the two must not be confused in an operator report."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:unsupportedCondition and refuse rather than budget, so the caller is told to change the input instead of the allowance."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:UnsupportedMethodSet a logic:NamedIndividual , logic:UnsupportedFragmentCondition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "method set outside fragment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The decomposition methods available fall outside the declared search fragment. This is a refusal about the methods themselves and not about a limit that was hit, which is what keeps an undecidable method set from masquerading as a budget exhaustion: no allowance would have changed the answer."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The refusal that keeps an undecidable method set from masquerading as a budget exhaustion. It reports that the decomposition methods themselves fall outside the declared search fragment, so no allowance would have changed the answer."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:episode-3 logic:unsupportedCondition logic:UnsupportedMethodSet ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the decomposition methods available to a refinement episode fall outside the declared search fragment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it when the methods are admitted and the search merely ran out of budget: that is an exhaustion, and it is remediable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with logic:unsupportedCondition so a reader can tell an inadmissible method set from an under-resourced search."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:unsupportedCondition
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:UnsupportedFragmentCondition ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has unsupported condition"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates an unsupported-fragment operation record to its typed condition, so a refusal names which construct took the input out of the fragment rather than reporting inadmissibility bare. Domain an operation record (stated in prose). Functional: an input outside the fragment on two counts is refused on the first, since checks past the first inadmissibility are not meaningful."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Attaches the typed condition to an unsupported-fragment record, so a refusal names which construct took the input out of the fragment. Functional: an input outside the fragment on two counts is refused on the first, since the later checks are not meaningful past the first inadmissibility."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:op-52 logic:unsupportedCondition logic:UnsupportedConstruct ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use on every unsupported-fragment operation record to name the condition that put the input outside the fragment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid using it for an admitted input that merely exhausted its budget: that is an incompleteness, and it is remediable where this is not."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record it with the outcome so the caller is told to rewrite the input or select another contract rather than raise an allowance."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:ContextAssembly
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Context Assembly"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The record of what was put in front of a decider: what was included, from where, under what budget, and — critically — what was EXCLUDED. Recording exclusions is what makes recorded dissent meaningful, because 'the operator saw the objection and proceeded' and 'the objection was never surfaced' are different situations that an inclusion-only record cannot tell apart. Typed APART from the decision itself: an assembly is what was available to decide with."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:w12Context a logic:ContextAssembly ; logic:assemblyForEnactment ex:adrReviewW12 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use wherever a decision was made against assembled material and the assembly must be auditable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid recording inclusions without exclusions, and avoid conflating the assembly with the decision."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the enactment served, every inclusion, every exclusion with its reason, and the governing guidance version."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Recording exclusions is what makes recorded dissent meaningful: the operator saw the objection and proceeded is a wholly different fact from the objection was filtered out before they looked, and only an assembly with exclusions can tell them apart."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:assemblyForEnactment
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ContextAssembly ;
    rdfs:range logic:Enactment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "assembly for enactment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ContextAssembly to the enactment it served. Functional and MANDATORY: an assembly with no named beneficiary cannot answer 'what was surfaced to THIS run', which is the question it exists for."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:week13Context logic:assemblyForEnactment ex:weekThirteen ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to bind a context assembly to the enactment it served."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an assembly with no enactment: without it, there is no answer to 'what was in front of the decider'."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Exactly one enactment per assembly."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Functional and MANDATORY: an assembly with no named beneficiary cannot answer what was surfaced to THIS run, which is the question it exists for. An unattached assembly is a context record about nobody."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:assemblyIncluded
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ContextAssembly ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "assembly included"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ContextAssembly to something surfaced to the decider. Range any surfaced item (stated in prose). Multi-valued. It records only what was shown and never what was withheld: without the matching exclusions an assembly shows what a decider saw but not what they were kept from seeing, which is what makes a deliberate omission look like an absence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:week13Context logic:assemblyIncluded ex:adrNote ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for what was actually surfaced to the decider."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid listing what was available rather than what was shown; availability is not visibility."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record what reached the decider, which is what a later dissent is weighed against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Records what was surfaced, and is only half the story: without the matching exclusions an assembly shows what a decider saw but not what they were not shown, which is what makes an omission look like an absence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:assemblyExcluded
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ContextAssembly ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "assembly excluded"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ContextAssembly to something available but NOT surfaced. Range any excluded item (stated in prose). Multi-valued. The half of the record that makes dissent interpretable: without it, silence is unreadable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:week13Context logic:assemblyExcluded ex:staleNote ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for what was deliberately withheld."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid recording only inclusions: 'I would have objected had I seen X' is checkable only if the record says whether X was shown."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Record exclusions alongside inclusions, with a reason."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The half of the record that makes dissent interpretable: without it, silence is unreadable. A decider who never saw an objection and one who saw and set it aside leave identical traces unless exclusions are recorded."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:assemblyExclusionReason
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ContextAssembly ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "assembly exclusion reason"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Why material available to the assembly was not surfaced — a budget, a relevance filter, a policy. Recording it distinguishes a deliberate omission from an accidental one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:week13Context logic:assemblyExclusionReason \"superseded by revision 4\"@x-gmeow-english ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to say why something was withheld."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid an unexplained exclusion; it is indistinguishable from an oversight."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Give a reason a reviewer could disagree with."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Distinguishes a deliberate omission from an accidental one. A budget, a relevance filter and a policy exclusion are three quite different justifications, and only the first two are cheap to revisit by re-running with more room."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:assemblyGuidanceVersion
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ContextAssembly ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "assembly guidance version"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The exact version of the guidance that governed the assembly. Range a versioned guidance record (stated in prose). Functional: 'which rules were we operating under' must be answerable for a past occurrence, not only the current one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:week13Context logic:assemblyGuidanceVersion ex:guidanceV2 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record which guidance revision governed this occurrence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid leaving it unbound; a decision made under revision 2 must not later be judged by revision 3."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind exactly one version, so the decision and its yardstick stay together."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Which rules were we operating under must be answerable for a PAST occurrence, not only the current one. Recording the version rather than pointing at the live guidance is what keeps that answer stable as guidance evolves."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:FrontierCarriesSaturationWitnessConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a frontier claiming closure carries a saturation witness"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:ActionableFrontier with no saturation witness. Closedness is the frontier's whole value, and an unwitnessed claim of it is exactly how a roster truncated by a budget cut passes for complete — after which the system confidently omits the action it should have taken. This law checks only that a witness is PRESENT; whether the witness actually certifies a fixed point rather than an exhaustion is logic:FrontierClosureRequiresSaturationConstraint, which reads it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:ActionableFrontier must carry a logic:frontierSaturationWitness; a roster without one cannot be claimed closed." ;
    logic:integrity logic:fcsForall .
logic:fcsForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:fcsImpl .
logic:fcsImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:fcsGuard ; logic:consequent logic:fcsCons .
logic:fcsGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ActionableFrontier ] .
logic:fcsCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:frontierSaturationWitness ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "witness" ] .

# A witness the frontier carries is not yet a certification. The failure the frontier layer
# exists to prevent is a roster CUT BY A BUDGET presented as complete, and a witness that
# records logic:BudgetExhausted says in so many words that the cut happened. Reading the
# witness — rather than counting it — is what makes "closure is certified" true. The
# evaluation axis is the kernel's existing one (logic:resultEvaluation over
# logic:EvaluationStatus); a second, frontier-private exhaustion flag would be a competing
# source of truth for the same fact.
#
# Reading it means reading its CONTENT, not only its status. A witness naming no settled
# predicate and no consumed budget records nothing that could be false, so it satisfies a
# not-exhausted test trivially — and a citation that cannot be wrong is a citation that
# certifies nothing while reading, to an operator, exactly like one that does.
logic:FrontierClosureRequiresSaturationConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a frontier's witness carries the closure it certifies"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:ActionableFrontier whose logic:frontierSaturationWitness fails to certify a fixed point — because it records logic:BudgetExhausted, because it names no logic:settledPredicate, or because it states no logic:consumedBudget. A witness is evidence, and evidence has content, so all three failures are one failure read three ways: a witness whose evaluation status says the search hit a declared bound is proof of the truncation the frontier claims not to have suffered, and a witness that names no settled predicate and no budget proves nothing at all while reading exactly like proof. That last case is the dangerous one, because it satisfies any law that merely COUNTS the witness — which is why this law reads it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:ActionableFrontier's logic:frontierSaturationWitness must name at least one logic:settledPredicate, state its logic:consumedBudget, and not carry logic:resultEvaluation logic:BudgetExhausted; a content-free witness certifies nothing and a budget-cut one certifies the truncation." ;
    logic:integrity logic:fclForall .
logic:fclForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:fclImpl .
logic:fclImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:fclGuard ; logic:consequent logic:fclCons .
logic:fclGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:fclIsFrontier , logic:fclWitness .
logic:fclIsFrontier a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ActionableFrontier ] .
logic:fclWitness a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:frontierSaturationWitness ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "witness" ] .
logic:fclCons a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:fclNotCut , logic:fclSettled , logic:fclBudget .
logic:fclNotCut a logic:Formula ; logic:not logic:fclCut .
logic:fclCut a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:resultEvaluation ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "witness" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:BudgetExhausted ] .
logic:fclSettled a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:settledPredicate ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "witness" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "settled" ] .
logic:fclBudget a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:consumedBudget ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "witness" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "budget" ] .

logic:ContextAssemblyNamesItsEnactmentConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a context assembly names the enactment it served"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:ContextAssembly with no named enactment. An assembly exists to answer what was surfaced to a PARTICULAR run; unbound, it answers nothing and the guidance-version record it carries has no subject. It says nothing whatever about exclusions — that is logic:ContextAssemblyRecordsExclusionsConstraint, which reads them."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:ContextAssembly must bind logic:assemblyForEnactment; an assembly with no beneficiary cannot say what was surfaced to which run." ;
    logic:integrity logic:careForall .
logic:careForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:careImpl .
logic:careImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:careGuard ; logic:consequent logic:careCons .
logic:careGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ContextAssembly ] .
logic:careCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:assemblyForEnactment ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "enactment" ] .

# The exclusion half, which the enactment-binding law above does not touch at all. An
# exclusion with no reason is indistinguishable from an oversight, which is precisely the
# distinction the assembly record exists to make: "the operator saw the objection and
# proceeded" and "the objection was never surfaced" are different situations, and an
# unexplained withholding collapses them again.
logic:ContextAssemblyRecordsExclusionsConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "an assembly that withholds material says why"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:ContextAssembly that names something in logic:assemblyExcluded and records no logic:assemblyExclusionReason. Recording exclusions is what makes recorded dissent meaningful, and a bare list of withheld items does not: 'I would have objected had I seen X' is answerable only if the record says whether X was shown AND on what grounds it was not. An unexplained exclusion reads, to every later reader, exactly like an oversight."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:ContextAssembly binding logic:assemblyExcluded must record a logic:assemblyExclusionReason; a deliberate omission with no stated grounds is indistinguishable from an accidental one." ;
    logic:integrity logic:careXForall .
logic:careXForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:careXImpl .
logic:careXImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:careXGuard ; logic:consequent logic:careXCons .
logic:careXGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:careXIsAssembly , logic:careXExcluded .
logic:careXIsAssembly a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ContextAssembly ] .
logic:careXExcluded a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:assemblyExcluded ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "withheld" ] .
logic:careXCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:assemblyExclusionReason ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "reason" ] .

logic:ContextAssemblyExclusionIsNotInclusionConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "nothing is both surfaced and withheld"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:ContextAssembly that lists one item under BOTH logic:assemblyIncluded and logic:assemblyExcluded. The two halves are the record's whole evidential value — one says what reached the decider, the other says what did not — and an item on both lists makes the assembly answer the audit question both ways at once, so no later dissent can be weighed against it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:ContextAssembly must not name one item in both logic:assemblyIncluded and logic:assemblyExcluded; availability and visibility are different claims and an item cannot honour both." ;
    logic:integrity logic:careIForall .
logic:careIForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:careIImpl .
logic:careIImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:careIGuard ; logic:consequent logic:careICons .
logic:careIGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:careIIsAssembly , logic:careIExcluded .
logic:careIIsAssembly a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ContextAssembly ] .
logic:careIExcluded a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:assemblyExcluded ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "withheld" ] .
logic:careICons a logic:Formula ; logic:not logic:careIAlsoIncluded .
logic:careIAlsoIncluded a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:assemblyIncluded ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "withheld" ] .


# =========================================================================== #
# === Enactment kernel: the remaining authored laws                        === #
# =========================================================================== #

logic:CheckpointCarriesFoldedIdentityConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a checkpoint carries its folded identity"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:EnactmentCheckpoint with no folded descriptor hash. Restore is identity-gated across seven axes, and the fold is the value the gate compares first; without it a restore would have to trust seven separate comparisons it cannot perform, which in practice means performing none. This law makes the fold AVAILABLE; the comparison itself is logic:CheckpointRestoreIdentityConstraint, which needs a restore to compare against and therefore could never be a property of a checkpoint alone."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:EnactmentCheckpoint must carry logic:checkpointDescriptorHash; a checkpoint with no folded identity cannot be identity-gated on restore." ;
    logic:integrity logic:criForall .
logic:criForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:criImpl .
logic:criImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:criGuard ; logic:consequent logic:criCons .
logic:criGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:EnactmentCheckpoint ] .
logic:criCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:checkpointDescriptorHash ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "fold" ] .

# ── Restore, as a first-class record ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# "Restore is identity-gated" was, until now, a claim with no subject: the kernel modelled
# the checkpoint and the continuation KIND but never the restore ACT, so there was nothing
# whose identity could disagree with the checkpoint's and nothing for a law to condemn. A
# checkpoint carrying a fold is not a gate; a restore carrying a fold that differs from the
# checkpoint's is what the gate exists to reject.

logic:CheckpointRestore
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Checkpoint Restore"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The act of resuming an enactment from a logic:EnactmentCheckpoint, recorded as its own durable fact: which checkpoint was restored, into which enactment, and under which folded identity the restoring engine BELIEVED itself to be operating. Recording the restorer's own fold rather than reading the checkpoint's is the entire mechanism — a restore that reports the checkpoint's value back to itself compares a number with itself and gates nothing. Typed APART from logic:EnactmentCheckpoint, which is the resume POINT: many restores may be attempted from one checkpoint, and each is separately auditable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:resumeW12 a logic:CheckpointRestore ; logic:restoresCheckpoint ex:ckptW12 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use whenever an enactment is resumed from a checkpoint and the resumption must be auditable after the fact — a crash recovery, a deliberate pause and continue, an operator-driven rollback."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid for a repeat or a revision (both mint new records rather than recovering an existing run), and avoid restoring without recording the act: an unrecorded restore is exactly the one whose identity nobody checked."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the checkpoint, the enactment being resumed, and the restoring engine's own logic:restoreDescriptorHash computed over its seven current axes; the identity laws compare that value with the checkpoint's."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Records the identity under which the restoring engine BELIEVED itself to be operating, not merely which checkpoint was read. That belief is what a later audit compares against reality, and without it a silent drift leaves no trace."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:restoresCheckpoint
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CheckpointRestore ;
    rdfs:range logic:EnactmentCheckpoint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "restores checkpoint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:CheckpointRestore to the exactly-one logic:EnactmentCheckpoint it recovers. It is the join the identity gate runs over: without it the restore has no counterparty and every comparison is vacuous. Functional: a restore cannot recover two checkpoints, because it would then have two identities to be gated against and no rule for choosing between them."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:resumeW12 logic:restoresCheckpoint ex:ckptW12 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to name the resume point a restore recovered from."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a restore naming a logic:StepCheckpoint as the resume point for a whole enactment; a step checkpoint is never a valid restore point for the run."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "One checkpoint per restore, so a disagreement names two specific records rather than two populations."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The join the identity gate runs over: without it the restore has no counterparty and every comparison is vacuous. Functional, because a restore recovering two checkpoints would have two identities to be gated against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:restoreDescriptorHash
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CheckpointRestore ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "restore descriptor hash"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The folded content address over the seven identity axes AS THE RESTORING ENGINE FINDS THEM — its own data, rules, slice, contract, engine, annotation algebra and fragment — computed at restore time and independently of the checkpoint. Functional. It is not a copy of logic:checkpointDescriptorHash, and its whole value is that it may DIFFER from it: equality is the gate passing and inequality is drift the run must be refused for, neither of which is statable if the restore simply echoes the checkpoint's value."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:resumeW12 logic:restoreDescriptorHash \"b3:e58021ca3f9d47b6018e5c27dab9403f1c68b0a597e2d413c6f8a705b19d2e83\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the folded identity the restoring engine computed over its own current axes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid copying the checkpoint's hash into it: a value read from the thing being compared cannot disagree with it, and the gate silently becomes a no-op."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Fold the same seven axes in the same order the checkpoint did, then let the identity law compare — never compare in the restoring code and record only the verdict."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Computed at restore time and INDEPENDENTLY of the checkpoint, over the seven axes as the restoring engine finds them. Independence is the point: a value copied from the checkpoint would compare equal to itself and gate nothing."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:restoreOfEnactment
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:CheckpointRestore ;
    rdfs:range logic:Enactment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "restore of enactment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:CheckpointRestore to the logic:Enactment being resumed. Functional. Resume continues the SAME occurrence, so this must be the enactment the restored checkpoint was taken from; restoring one run's checkpoint into another is not a resume but an undeclared repeat wearing a resume's clothes, and it carries the earlier run's journal into a run that never committed it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:resumeW12 logic:restoreOfEnactment ex:adrReviewW12 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to name the occurrence a restore resumes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid pointing it at a fresh enactment: that is a repeat, and repeat mints its own journal rather than inheriting one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the same enactment the checkpoint names in logic:checkpointOfEnactment; the identity law checks exactly that."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Resume continues the SAME occurrence, so this must be the enactment the restored checkpoint was taken from. Restoring one run's checkpoint into another is a cross-enactment restore and is refused rather than reinterpreted as a repeat."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:CheckpointRestoreIdentityConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a restore's folded identity must equal its checkpoint's"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:CheckpointRestore whose logic:restoreDescriptorHash is not the logic:checkpointDescriptorHash of the checkpoint it restores. This is the identity gate itself, and it is irreducibly a relation between two records: resuming a run against drifted rules, a drifted engine or a drifted slice silently produces a result the run never would have produced, and the fold is the single value that makes the drift visible before the first step executes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:CheckpointRestore's logic:restoreDescriptorHash must equal the logic:checkpointDescriptorHash of the logic:EnactmentCheckpoint it restores; a mismatch on ANY of the seven identity axes is refused, never reconciled." ;
    logic:integrity logic:crxForall .
logic:crxForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:crxImpl .
logic:crxImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:crxGuard ; logic:consequent logic:crxCons .
logic:crxGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:crxIsRestore , logic:crxOfCheckpoint , logic:crxFold .
logic:crxIsRestore a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:CheckpointRestore ] .
logic:crxOfCheckpoint a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:restoresCheckpoint ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "checkpoint" ] .
logic:crxFold a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:restoreDescriptorHash ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "fold" ] .
logic:crxCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:checkpointDescriptorHash ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "checkpoint" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "fold" ] .

logic:RestoreStaysWithinItsEnactmentConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a restore resumes the run its checkpoint came from"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:CheckpointRestore whose logic:restoreOfEnactment is not the logic:checkpointOfEnactment of the checkpoint it restores. The folded hash gates the ENGINE's identity and says nothing about WHOSE run is being continued: two occurrences of one prescription on one build fold identically, so a cross-run restore passes the fold gate while carrying one run's journal, approvals and effects into another. The second identity axis is therefore a separate law, not a detail of the first."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:CheckpointRestore's logic:restoreOfEnactment must be the logic:checkpointOfEnactment of the checkpoint it restores; resuming one run from another run's checkpoint is an undeclared repeat, not a resume." ;
    logic:integrity logic:rseForall .
logic:rseForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:rseImpl .
logic:rseImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:rseGuard ; logic:consequent logic:rseCons .
logic:rseGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:rseIsRestore , logic:rseOfCheckpoint , logic:rseEnactment .
logic:rseIsRestore a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:CheckpointRestore ] .
logic:rseOfCheckpoint a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:restoresCheckpoint ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "checkpoint" ] .
logic:rseEnactment a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:restoreOfEnactment ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "enactment" ] .
logic:rseCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:checkpointOfEnactment ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "checkpoint" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "enactment" ] .

logic:ContinuationKindDisjointnessConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "resume, repeat and revise are not conflated"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a continuation record that is typed as more than one logic:ContinuationKind. The three carry different required bindings and different consequences — resume continues a run, repeat mints a new one, revise supersedes the prescription — so a record claiming two of them has not said which actually happened, which is the conflation the closed vocabulary exists to make detectable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A continuation record must carry exactly one logic:ContinuationKind: resume, repeat and revise have different required bindings and different consequences." ;
    logic:integrity logic:ckdForall .
logic:ckdForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:ckdImpl .
logic:ckdImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:ckdResume ; logic:consequent logic:ckdCons .
logic:ckdResume a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:continuationKind ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ContinuationResume ] .
logic:ckdCons a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:ckdNotRepeat , logic:ckdNotRevise .
logic:ckdNotRepeat a logic:Formula ; logic:not logic:ckdRepeat .
logic:ckdRepeat a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:continuationKind ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ContinuationRepeat ] .
logic:ckdNotRevise a logic:Formula ; logic:not logic:ckdRevise .
logic:ckdRevise a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:continuationKind ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ContinuationRevise ] .

# The third pair. A universally-quantified implication has ONE antecedent, so the resume
# guard above can only exclude the two kinds resume conflicts with; a record carrying repeat
# AND revise satisfies it vacuously and passes. Three kinds have three unordered pairs, so
# pairwise disjointness needs the repeat/revise leg as its own law — and it is the pair that
# matters operationally, since repeat mints a new enactment while revise supersedes the
# prescription, and a record claiming both has just done neither reproducibly.
logic:ContinuationRepeatExcludesReviseConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "repeat and revise are not conflated"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a continuation record typed as BOTH logic:ContinuationRepeat and logic:ContinuationRevise. It completes the pairwise disjointness of the three continuation kinds, whose resume leg logic:ContinuationKindDisjointnessConstraint carries: repeat mints a new logic:Enactment against a new snapshot and leaves the prescription untouched, revise mints a new logic:PrescriptionVersion and leaves in-flight enactments on the version they pinned, and a record asserting both has said neither which occurrence continued nor which prescription governs it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A continuation record carrying logic:ContinuationRepeat must not also carry logic:ContinuationRevise: one mints a new enactment, the other supersedes the prescription, and no record does both." ;
    logic:integrity logic:ckrForall .
logic:ckrForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:ckrImpl .
logic:ckrImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:ckrRepeat ; logic:consequent logic:ckrCons .
logic:ckrRepeat a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:continuationKind ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ContinuationRepeat ] .
logic:ckrCons a logic:Formula ; logic:not logic:ckrRevise .
logic:ckrRevise a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:continuationKind ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ContinuationRevise ] .

logic:PrescriptionVersionIsContentAddressedConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a prescription version is content-addressed"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:PrescriptionVersion with no digest. Content-addressing is the PRECONDITION of immutability, not immutability itself: an unaddressed version cannot be shown to be the one an enactment ran, so nothing downstream can detect a revision in place. Whether a version actually stayed put is logic:PrescriptionVersionImmutabilityConstraint, which compares the address against what a run recorded."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:PrescriptionVersion must carry logic:prescriptionDigest; without a content address, revising a version in place is undetectable." ;
    logic:integrity logic:pviForall .
logic:pviForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:pviImpl .
logic:pviImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:pviGuard ; logic:consequent logic:pviCons .
logic:pviGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:PrescriptionVersion ] .
logic:pviCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:prescriptionDigest ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "digest" ] .

logic:enactedPrescriptionDigest
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Enactment ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "enacted prescription digest"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:prescriptionDigest of the version this enactment pinned, COPIED into the run's own record at start. Functional. It is the witness that makes in-place revision detectable: the version pointer follows the version wherever its bytes go, so comparing the version with itself can never disagree, and only a value the run froze at pin time can. This is the enactment-layer instance of the same discipline logic:approvalIntentDigest applies to authorization."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:adrReviewW12 logic:enactedPrescriptionDigest \"b3:4c8a03f7b25e91d6047ac35b8e1f20d9637b0ae514c92d87f30a6b15c8e04d29\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to freeze, at start, the content address of the prescription version an occurrence is running — so a later in-place edit of that version is detectable rather than invisible."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid recomputing it from the version at read time: a value derived from the thing under test cannot testify against it, and the check silently becomes a tautology."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Copy logic:prescriptionDigest once at pin time and never update it; the immutability law reports every enactment whose frozen value no longer matches."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "COPIED into the run's own record at start, which is what makes in-place revision detectable: the version pointer may move, but the copy does not, so a mismatch between them is evidence rather than an inference."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# Immutability is a claim about CHANGE OVER TIME, and a single record can never falsify one:
# a version and its own digest agree by construction, however many times the bytes were
# rewritten. The only witness is a value frozen elsewhere — the digest a run recorded when it
# pinned the version — so the law joins the version to its enactments and condemns the
# version whose current address no longer matches what a run is running against.
logic:PrescriptionVersionImmutabilityConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a landed version's address never moves"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:PrescriptionVersion whose logic:prescriptionDigest differs from the logic:enactedPrescriptionDigest an enactment of it froze at pin time. That divergence has exactly one cause — the version's bytes were edited after a run pinned them — and it is the failure the whole supersession discipline exists to make impossible: the run's recoverable account of what it executed now describes something that no longer exists, while its version pointer keeps resolving, so nothing else in the record looks wrong."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:PrescriptionVersion's logic:prescriptionDigest must equal the logic:enactedPrescriptionDigest every enactment of it froze at pin time; a divergence means the version was revised in place under a running occurrence." ;
    logic:integrity logic:pvmForall .
logic:pvmForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:pvmImpl .
logic:pvmImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:pvmGuard ; logic:consequent logic:pvmCons .
logic:pvmGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:pvmIsVersion , logic:pvmDigest , logic:pvmEnacted , logic:pvmPinned .
logic:pvmIsVersion a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:PrescriptionVersion ] .
logic:pvmDigest a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:prescriptionDigest ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "current" ] .
logic:pvmEnacted a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:enactsPrescriptionVersion ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "run" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] .
logic:pvmPinned a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:enactedPrescriptionDigest ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "run" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "pinned" ] .
logic:pvmCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:enactedPrescriptionDigest ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "run" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "current" ] .

logic:JournalEntryNamesBothHeadsConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a journal entry names both of its heads"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:JournalEntry missing either the head it was applied against or the head it establishes. An entry that names only one end cannot be LINKED at all, so it is the precondition of chain integrity rather than chain integrity itself: whether the links actually join up is logic:JournalChainIntegrityConstraint, which compares an entry's prior head against what its predecessor established."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:JournalEntry must carry both logic:journalPrevHead and logic:journalNewHead; an entry naming only one end cannot be chained." ;
    logic:integrity logic:jciForall .
logic:jciForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:jciImpl .
logic:jciImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:jciGuard ; logic:consequent logic:jciCons .
logic:jciGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:JournalEntry ] .
logic:jciCons a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:jciPrev , logic:jciNew .
logic:jciPrev a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:journalPrevHead ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "prev" ] .
logic:jciNew a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:journalNewHead ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "new" ] .

logic:journalPredecessor
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:JournalEntry ;
    rdfs:range logic:JournalEntry ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "journal predecessor"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:JournalEntry to the entry it was appended after — the chain's SUCCESSION edge, as distinct from logic:journalPrevHead, which is the content ADDRESS that succession is supposed to reproduce. Functional. The two are deliberately separate: a chain whose only link is a hash is a chain whose links can only be checked by finding some entry with a matching head, which silently accepts a fork (two entries claiming one predecessor) and cannot distinguish a genesis entry from a broken one. The genesis entry carries none, which is exactly what makes it the genesis entry."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entry8 logic:journalPredecessor ex:entry7 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to record which entry an entry was appended after, so the chain's links are checkable against the heads rather than reconstructed from them."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid binding one on the genesis entry (it succeeds nothing), and avoid pointing two entries at one predecessor — that is the fork the chain exists to make impossible."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Append it alongside logic:journalPrevHead at commit time; the chain-integrity law checks the two agree."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The chain's SUCCESSION edge, as distinct from the previous-head value, which is the content ADDRESS that succession is supposed to reproduce. Keeping both is what lets a broken chain be detected rather than merely traversed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# Chain integrity is the LINK, not the ends. Two entries can each name both of their heads
# and still form no chain — the whole point of the hash chain is that entry n's prior head
# reproduces entry n−1's new head, and a resubmission after an interruption is refused
# because that reproduction fails. Nothing checked it until now: the presence law above
# passes a journal whose entries all point into the void.
logic:JournalChainIntegrityConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "an entry's prior head is its predecessor's new head"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:JournalEntry whose logic:journalPrevHead is not the logic:journalNewHead of the entry it succeeds. This is the hash-chain invariant itself, and every durability property the layer claims rests on it: an entry applied against a head its predecessor never established was applied against a state the journal never reached, which is what a stale resubmission after a crash looks like from the inside. A genesis entry carries no logic:journalPredecessor and is therefore not governed — it is the one entry with nothing to reproduce."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:JournalEntry's logic:journalPrevHead must equal the logic:journalNewHead of its logic:journalPredecessor; an entry applied against a head nothing established is a stale or forked submission." ;
    logic:integrity logic:jchForall .
logic:jchForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:jchImpl .
logic:jchImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:jchGuard ; logic:consequent logic:jchCons .
logic:jchGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:jchIsEntry , logic:jchPredecessor , logic:jchPrev .
logic:jchIsEntry a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:JournalEntry ] .
logic:jchPredecessor a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:journalPredecessor ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "predecessor" ] .
logic:jchPrev a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:journalPrevHead ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "head" ] .
logic:jchCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:journalNewHead ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "predecessor" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "head" ] .

logic:UnknownOutcomeNamesItsAttemptConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "an unknown outcome names the attempt it is unknown about"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:ExternalOutcomeUnknown that names no attempt. An unknown outcome with no attempt is unresolvable in principle: nothing could ever probe it, and nothing could ever close it. It is a precondition of the no-blind-retry discipline rather than that discipline itself — whether a retry is actually licensed is logic:NoBlindRetryConstraint, which governs the retry record the kernel now carries."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:ExternalOutcomeUnknown must name the logic:EffectAttempt whose outcome is undetermined; without it neither reconciliation nor a licensed retry is reachable." ;
    logic:integrity logic:nbrForall .
logic:nbrForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:nbrImpl .
logic:nbrImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:nbrGuard ; logic:consequent logic:nbrCons .
logic:nbrGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ExternalOutcomeUnknown ] .
logic:nbrCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:unknownOfAttempt ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "attempt" ] .

# ── Retry, as a first-class record ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# "No blind retry" named an act the vocabulary could not express. The layer modelled the
# undetermined position (logic:ExternalOutcomeUnknown), the two things that resolve it
# (logic:IdempotencyContract, logic:ReconciliationResult) and the probe that reaches them —
# but never the RETRY, which is the move the law forbids. A law about an act nothing can
# record is a law about nothing, which is why the constraint bearing this name was in fact
# checking that an unknown outcome had a subject.

logic:RetryDispatch
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Retry Dispatch"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A re-dispatch of an effect whose earlier logic:EffectAttempt did not conclusively fail — the move that turns one charge into two, one valve command into two, one enrolment into two. It is recorded as its own durable fact BEFORE the re-dispatch, exactly as logic:DispatchIntent is, so that the licence under which it proceeded is auditable afterwards rather than reconstructed from a support ticket. Typed APART from logic:DispatchIntent, which is a first dispatch and needs no licence, and APART from logic:ReconciliationProbe, which is the knowledge-increasing move an undetermined position DOES license."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeRetry a logic:RetryDispatch ; logic:retryOfAttempt ex:chargeAttempt ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use whenever an effect is re-sent after an attempt whose outcome was not conclusively negative — the single most damaging move available at the effect boundary, and therefore the one that must leave a record."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid for the first dispatch of an intent, avoid for a reconciliation probe (that reads rather than acts), and above all avoid re-sending without minting one: an unrecorded retry is a blind retry by construction."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the attempt being retried and the licence that permits it; the no-blind-retry law checks the licence actually covers that attempt."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The move that turns one charge into two, one valve command into two, one enrolment into two. It is recorded as its own durable fact BEFORE the re-dispatch, so a crash during the retry leaves the same analyzable trace the first attempt did."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:retryOfAttempt
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:RetryDispatch ;
    rdfs:range logic:EffectAttempt ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "retry of attempt"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:RetryDispatch to the exactly-one earlier logic:EffectAttempt it re-sends. Functional. Naming the attempt rather than the intent is what makes the licence checkable: an idempotency contract and a reconciliation verdict both attach to a specific attempt through the provider's correlation identity, and a retry that names only the intent cannot be joined to either."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeRetry logic:retryOfAttempt ex:chargeAttempt ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to name the attempt a retry re-sends."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid naming the intent instead; the provider correlates on what was actually sent, and so must the licence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "One attempt per retry, so a second retry of the same attempt is a separate, separately-licensed record."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Naming the attempt rather than the intent is what makes the licence checkable: an idempotency contract and a reconciliation verdict both attach to an attempt, so a retry named against an intent has nothing to check against."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:retryLicence
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "retry licence"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:RetryDispatch to the record that permits it — a discharged logic:IdempotencyContract, or a logic:ReconciliationResult whose verdict settled the earlier attempt. Range either of those (stated in prose, because the two are genuinely alternative licences and forcing a common superclass would suggest they are interchangeable, which they are not: one makes a repeat harmless, the other makes it unnecessary). Functional."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeRetry logic:retryLicence ex:chargeIdempotency ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to name what makes a particular retry safe."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid a licence that names no attempt (it covers nothing), and avoid an operator's judgement in this position — a decision to retry is not a demonstration that retrying is safe."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the contract or result, and make sure it carries logic:licenceCoversAttempt for the attempt being retried; the law joins on exactly that."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Range is either licence kind, stated in prose because the two are genuinely alternative sources of the same permission. A retry with no licence of either kind is the unlicensed re-dispatch the kernel's counter-example corpus rejects."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:licenceCoversAttempt
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:EffectAttempt ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "licence covers attempt"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a retry licence — a discharged logic:IdempotencyContract or a settled logic:ReconciliationResult — to the exact logic:EffectAttempt it makes a retry safe from. Domain either licence kind (stated in prose). Multi-valued: one idempotency contract may cover several attempts against one key within its retention window, and its lapse is precisely the point at which it stops covering them. Naming the attempts is what means a licence issued for one dispatch cannot be borrowed for the next, which is the specific abuse the kernel's counter-example corpus rejects."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:chargeIdempotency logic:licenceCoversAttempt ex:chargeAttempt ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to say exactly which attempts a licence makes retryable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid extending it past the licence's own bounds — an idempotency contract whose retention window has elapsed covers nothing, and a retry after that is a second effect, not a repeat of the first."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Add the attempt when the contract is discharged or the reconciliation verdict lands, and never before: a licence recorded in advance licenses the failure it was supposed to prevent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Relates a retry licence to the EXACT attempt it makes a retry safe from, so a licence cannot be borrowed for a different attempt. Borrowing is the specific abuse the kernel's counter-example corpus rejects."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:RetryRequiresLicenceConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a retry names the licence it proceeded under"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:RetryDispatch that names no logic:retryLicence. Re-sending an effect whose outcome was never established is the most damaging move at the boundary, and 'we decided it was probably fine' is exactly the reasoning it must not be available under. This is the PRESENCE half; whether the named licence actually covers the attempt being retried is logic:NoBlindRetryConstraint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:RetryDispatch must name a logic:retryLicence — a discharged logic:IdempotencyContract or a settled logic:ReconciliationResult; an unlicensed retry is the blind retry itself." ;
    logic:integrity logic:rrlForall .
logic:rrlForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:rrlImpl .
logic:rrlImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:rrlGuard ; logic:consequent logic:rrlCons .
logic:rrlGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:RetryDispatch ] .
logic:rrlCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:retryLicence ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "licence" ] .

# The law the name has always claimed. A retry that cites SOME licence is not a licensed
# retry: the classic production failure is citing the idempotency contract of the previous
# invoice, or a reconciliation verdict about a different attempt, and both satisfy a
# presence check exactly. The licence must cover THIS attempt.
logic:NoBlindRetryConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a retry's licence covers the attempt being retried"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:RetryDispatch whose logic:retryLicence does not carry logic:licenceCoversAttempt for the attempt the retry re-sends. Licensing is a relation between a specific licence and a specific attempt, and nothing weaker will do: an idempotency contract covers the keys and the window it actually covers, and a reconciliation verdict settles the attempt it actually probed. A licence borrowed from a neighbouring dispatch — the same provider, the same step, the previous invoice — reads identically to a real one and permits exactly the double-effect the whole commitment layer exists to prevent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:RetryDispatch's logic:retryLicence must carry logic:licenceCoversAttempt for the exact logic:EffectAttempt being retried; a licence issued for a neighbouring dispatch licenses nothing here." ;
    logic:integrity logic:nblForall .
logic:nblForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:nblImpl .
logic:nblImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:nblGuard ; logic:consequent logic:nblCons .
logic:nblGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:nblIsRetry , logic:nblAttempt , logic:nblLicence .
logic:nblIsRetry a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:RetryDispatch ] .
logic:nblAttempt a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:retryOfAttempt ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "attempt" ] .
logic:nblLicence a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:retryLicence ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "licence" ] .
logic:nblCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:licenceCoversAttempt ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "licence" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "attempt" ] .

logic:CompensationNotInverseConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a compensation is not typed as its forward effect"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:CompensationAttempt that is also typed as a logic:ExternalEffectReceipt. Compensation is a NEW action with its own attempt, its own receipt and its own possibility of failing; typing it as the receipt of the effect it addresses collapses the counteraction into the thing counteracted, which is precisely the guaranteed-inverse reading that makes a failed compensation invisible."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:CompensationAttempt must not also be a logic:ExternalEffectReceipt: compensation is a new action, never the erasure of the effect it addresses." ;
    logic:integrity logic:cniForall .
logic:cniForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:cniImpl .
logic:cniImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:cniGuard ; logic:consequent logic:cniCons .
logic:cniGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:CompensationAttempt ] .
logic:cniCons a logic:Formula ; logic:not logic:cniIsReceipt .
logic:cniIsReceipt a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ExternalEffectReceipt ] .

logic:ClockAttributionRequiredConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a clock attribution names its source"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:ClockAttribution with no named source. Expiry, validity windows and retention are all judged against somebody's notion of now, and provider-consumer skew is the canonical failure of each; an unattributed 'still valid' is unfalsifiable, which makes it worse than an absent judgment because it reads as one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:ClockAttribution must name its logic:clockSource; an unattributed time judgment is unfalsifiable." ;
    logic:integrity logic:carForall .
logic:carForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:carImpl2 .
logic:carImpl2 a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:carGuard2 ; logic:consequent logic:carCons2 .
logic:carGuard2 a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ClockAttribution ] .
logic:carCons2 a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:clockSource ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "src" ] .

logic:ReconciliationResultCarriesVerdictConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a reconciliation result carries a verdict"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:ReconciliationResult with no logic:ReconciliationVerdict. A probe that completed and recorded nothing is indistinguishable from a probe that never ran, and the difference matters: the first leaves an effect where it was, the second leaves it unprobed and still owing a reconciliation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:ReconciliationResult must carry exactly one logic:reconciliationVerdict; a result with none is indistinguishable from a probe that never ran." ;
    logic:integrity logic:rrvForall .
logic:rrvForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:rrvImpl .
logic:rrvImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:rrvGuard ; logic:consequent logic:rrvCons .
logic:rrvGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:ReconciliationResult ] .
logic:rrvCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:reconciliationVerdict ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "verdict" ] .

logic:EnactmentPinsPrescriptionAndSnapshotConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "an enactment pins both a prescription version and an input snapshot"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:Enactment that does not bind both the prescription version it runs and the closed input generation it runs against. These two bindings are what make an occurrence reproducible and comparable to its siblings; an enactment missing either is a record that something happened without a recoverable account of what it was doing or over what."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:Enactment must bind both logic:enactsPrescriptionVersion and logic:enactmentInputSnapshot; without both, the occurrence is neither reproducible nor comparable." ;
    logic:integrity logic:epsForall .
logic:epsForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:epsImpl .
logic:epsImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:epsGuard ; logic:consequent logic:epsCons .
logic:epsGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:Enactment ] .
logic:epsCons a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:epsVersion , logic:epsSnapshot .
logic:epsVersion a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:enactsPrescriptionVersion ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "version" ] .
logic:epsSnapshot a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:enactmentInputSnapshot ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "snapshot" ] .

logic:OperationalGapNamesBlockedStepConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "an operational capability gap names the step it blocks"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:OperationalCapabilityGap that names no blocked step. A gap with no blocked step is a general complaint about provisioning, which no operator can act on. It says nothing about a REMEDY — whether the gap is actually answered by a logic:CapabilityGapProposal is logic:OperationalGapCarriesProposalConstraint, which joins the two through the step they share."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:OperationalCapabilityGap must name the step it blocks; a gap answerable to no concrete blockage cannot be closed." ;
    logic:integrity logic:ogpForall .
logic:ogpForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:ogpImpl .
logic:ogpImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:ogpGuard ; logic:consequent logic:ogpCons .
logic:ogpGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:OperationalCapabilityGap ] .
logic:ogpCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:gapBlockedStep ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "step" ] .

# The remedy leg. "Raise a gap instead of substituting a weaker capability" is only better
# than substitution if the gap ARRIVES WITH what would close it; a gap that names a blockage
# and nothing else is the complaint the discipline was supposed to replace. The proposal and
# the gap are joined through the step: logic:proposalBlockedStep is field eight of eight for
# exactly this reason, so no new predicate is needed to state the relation — only a law that
# reads it.
logic:OperationalGapCarriesProposalConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "an operational capability gap arrives with its remedy"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:OperationalCapabilityGap whose blocked step is the logic:proposalBlockedStep of no logic:CapabilityGapProposal. Surfacing a gap rather than substituting a weaker capability is a discipline that pays only when the blockage comes with the statement of what would close it: an unanswered gap is a provisioning complaint, and a backlog of them is indistinguishable from a system that silently degraded instead. The join is the step, which both records already name."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:OperationalCapabilityGap's blocked step must be the logic:proposalBlockedStep of some logic:CapabilityGapProposal; a gap nobody proposed a remedy for cannot be closed and is a complaint, not a request." ;
    logic:integrity logic:ogrForall .
logic:ogrForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:ogrImpl .
logic:ogrImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:ogrGuard ; logic:consequent logic:ogrCons .
logic:ogrGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:ogrIsGap , logic:ogrStep .
logic:ogrIsGap a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:OperationalCapabilityGap ] .
logic:ogrStep a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:gapBlockedStep ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "step" ] .
logic:ogrCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:proposalBlockedStep ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "proposal" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "step" ] .

# The SUBSTITUTION leg, focused on the record the substitution actually leaves behind. The
# two laws above both condemn the GAP, so between them they say a blockage was reported
# badly; neither says anything about the dispatch that went ahead anyway. That dispatch is
# the thing the discipline exists to forbid — an absent capability quietly replaced by a
# weaker one — and it is invisible to every presence check, because the intent that records
# it binds all nine of its mandatory fields. The join is the step: the intent names it
# through logic:intentOfStep and the gap through logic:gapBlockedStep, so the law needs no
# new predicate, only a body that reads both. The obligation the dispatch must meet is the
# remedy, which is why the green case differs by a logic:CapabilityGapProposal arriving
# rather than by the dispatch record being deleted.
logic:NoDispatchAgainstAnUnremediedGapConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "a blocked step is not dispatched against whatever capability is available"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:DispatchIntent whose logic:intentOfStep is the logic:gapBlockedStep of a logic:OperationalCapabilityGap that no logic:CapabilityGapProposal answers. This is the silent substitution itself, condemned at the record that carries it: the missing capability is not absent from the run, it has been replaced by whatever weaker thing was to hand, and the dispatch intent recording the replacement is complete, well-formed and individually blameless. Its siblings condemn the GAP — for naming no step, or for carrying no remedy — and so state only that a blockage was reported badly; none of them looks at the dispatch that proceeded regardless. The join is the step both records already name, and the obligation is the remedy: a blocked step whose gap has been answered is a step somebody is provisioning for, while a blocked step dispatched into an unanswered gap is an absent capability turned into a quality loss that surfaces much later as unexplained bad data."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:DispatchIntent must not name as its logic:intentOfStep a step some logic:OperationalCapabilityGap blocks and no logic:CapabilityGapProposal answers; dispatching a blocked step against whatever capability IS available is the silent substitution of a weaker one, and it leaves a complete, well-formed record behind it." ;
    logic:integrity logic:ndgForall .
logic:ndgForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:ndgImpl .
logic:ndgImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:ndgGuard ; logic:consequent logic:ndgCons .
logic:ndgGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:ndgIsIntent , logic:ndgOfStep , logic:ndgIsGap , logic:ndgGapStep .
logic:ndgIsIntent a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:DispatchIntent ] .
logic:ndgOfStep a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:intentOfStep ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "step" ] .
logic:ndgIsGap a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "gap" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:OperationalCapabilityGap ] .
logic:ndgGapStep a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:gapBlockedStep ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "gap" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "step" ] .
logic:ndgCons a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:proposalBlockedStep ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "proposal" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "step" ] .

# The recurrence law: a maintenance target is never CLOSED by an occurrence that went well.
#
# A continuing body of work exists because its goal is satisfied only WHILE its condition
# holds. A successful week of the recurring review is evidence about that week and says
# nothing conclusive about the standing goal, so the two are separate logic:GoalEvaluation
# records with separate conclusiveness: the week's own achievement goal reaches
# logic:GoalEvaluationCompleted, the cluster's maintenance goal stays
# logic:GoalEvaluationUndetermined. Letting one occurrence carry the maintenance goal to
# Satisfied AND Completed generates the conclusive gmeow:satisfiedBy edge, and the cluster
# then reads as finished — the recurrence keeps firing against a goal the record says was
# closed months ago, which is how a standing commitment quietly becomes a cron job nobody
# can justify.
#
# The guard is the BARE maintenance kind, and that is the discrimination the law rests on. A
# maintenance target with a closing window is authored as a logic:DeadlineWindowGoal whose
# operand carries the maintenance sub-expression, so the goal's own logic:hasGoalCondition
# names the windowed kind and falls outside this guard: a window that has closed may
# conclude, exactly as logic:GoalEvaluationCompleted's definition says. What may never
# conclude satisfied is the unbounded one — the goal whose interval is the life of the
# cluster, which no occurrence can reach the end of.
logic:MaintenanceGoalNeverConclusivelySatisfiedConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "an unbounded maintenance goal is never conclusively satisfied"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Constraint rejecting a logic:GoalEvaluation that judges a goal whose logic:hasGoalCondition is logic:MaintenanceGoal-kinded and carries logic:Satisfied together with logic:GoalEvaluationCompleted. A maintenance target holds only while its condition does, so a successful occurrence of a recurring prescription is evidence about that occurrence and never a closure of the continuing goal: the pair (Satisfied, Completed) is precisely the pair that generates a conclusive gmeow:satisfiedBy edge, and generating one here permanently retires a commitment that was supposed to keep recurring. The guard names the BARE maintenance kind on purpose — a maintenance target indexed to a closing interval is authored as a logic:DeadlineWindowGoal whose operand carries it, so a window that has closed reaches a conclusive judgment through a condition this law does not govern. Violation is Satisfied-and-Completed only: logic:Violated with logic:GoalEvaluationCompleted is the maintenance target FAILING, which is conclusive and must stay expressible."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:enforcesFailureClass logic:EnactmentIntegrityViolation ;
    logic:severity "Violation" ;
    logic:message "A logic:GoalEvaluation of a logic:MaintenanceGoal-kinded condition must not carry logic:satisfactionStatus logic:Satisfied together with logic:goalEvaluationStatus logic:GoalEvaluationCompleted; a successful occurrence never closes a continuing maintenance goal, and that pair is what generates the conclusive gmeow:satisfiedBy edge." ;
    logic:integrity logic:mgcForall .
logic:mgcForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:mgcImpl .
logic:mgcImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:mgcGuard ; logic:consequent logic:mgcCons .
logic:mgcGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:mgcIsEvaluation , logic:mgcOfGoal , logic:mgcGoalCondition , logic:mgcMaintenanceKind .
logic:mgcIsEvaluation a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:GoalEvaluation ] .
logic:mgcOfGoal a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:evaluatesGoal ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "goal" ] .
logic:mgcGoalCondition a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:hasGoalCondition ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "goal" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "condition" ] .
logic:mgcMaintenanceKind a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:goalExpressionKind ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "condition" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:MaintenanceGoal ] .
logic:mgcCons a logic:Formula ; logic:not logic:mgcTerminal .
logic:mgcTerminal a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:mgcSatisfied , logic:mgcCompleted .
logic:mgcSatisfied a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:satisfactionStatus ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:Satisfied ] .
logic:mgcCompleted a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:goalEvaluationStatus ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri logic:GoalEvaluationCompleted ] .

# =========================================================================== #
# The well-founded materializer's intra-engine phase plan — dogfooded as data. #
#                                                                             #
# This logic:Plan is the AUTHORED MODEL of the phase sequence the native       #
# well-founded-semantics evaluator runs per world in                          #
# crates/logic/src/wellfounded.rs (materialize()). It is the canonical-process #
# model applied ONE SCALE DOWN from the build pipeline (gmeow:pipeline-build): #
# where the pipeline is the inter-stage DAG, this plan is the intra-engine     #
# pass sequence of a single reasoning step. BOUNDARY (Principle 12,            #
# non-negotiable): this is authored SOURCE the parity test                     #
# crates/pipeline/tests/wellfounded_plan_parity.rs checks the Rust runtime     #
# against (gmeow_logic::WELL_FOUNDED_PHASES) — the reasoner NEVER parses this   #
# RDF at scheduling or runtime; the native loop is the runtime, the plan is    #
# its declared twin.                                                           #
#                                                                             #
# materialize() runs exactly three phases per world:                          #
#   1. wfMaterializeEdb   — seed the per-world EDB (world_edb_facts +          #
#                           echo_asserted + seed FactStore).                   #
#   2. wfResolveFixpoint  — the alternating fixpoint (van Gelder): the ITERATED #
#                           phase — loop { U = lmr(K); K2 = lmr(U); break when  #
#                           K2.key_set() == K.key_set() }. Modelled as a        #
#                           logic:Iteration whose logic:iterationCondition is   #
#                           the situation "the double-reduct fixpoint is not    #
#                           yet reached" (K2 != K).                             #
#   3. wfConstructModel   — final reduct against the well-founded model W,      #
#                           hard-fail on a partial model, emit derived rows.    #
#                                                                             #
# The phase order is the SerialConjunction (materialize ; (resolve ;          #
# construct)), reachable from the plan through logic:planBody. Because the     #
# middle phase is a logic:Iteration (a loop), this plan is OUTSIDE the         #
# certified acyclic DAG fragment BY CONSTRUCTION — that is correct and         #
# expected, so (unlike gmeow:pipeline-build) it does NOT run                    #
# logic:executedUnderContract logic:DagWorkflowContract.                       #
# =========================================================================== #

# Shared action-theory facets the three phase ActionSchemas carry to satisfy
# logic:ActionSchemaShape (logic:precondition + logic:capability are REQUIRED).
logic:wfWorldSeeded
    a logic:Situation ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "well-founded world seeded"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The situation gate the well-founded materializer's phase action schemas ground on: the per-world store is selected and its asserted EDB facts are reachable (the sorted worlds list of crates/logic/src/wellfounded.rs materialize()). The shared logic:precondition of the three intra-engine phase schemas — the elementary phase transition applies only once a world is in scope."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:wfReductEngineCapability
    a logic:EngineCapability ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "well-founded reduct-engine capability"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The executional precondition on the actor the well-founded materializer's phase action schemas carry as their logic:capability: the least-model-of-reduct engine (crate::rule_ir::least_model_of_reduct) is available to evaluate a reduct against a reference fact set. The generic ability the native engine must hold to run any of the three phases — distinct from logic:wfWorldSeeded, which is a precondition on the world state, not on the engine."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

# The loop condition of the alternating-fixpoint phase: the double-reduct
# fixpoint is NOT YET reached (K2.key_set() != K.key_set()). The logic:Iteration
# runs its body while this situation obtains; when it ceases (K2 == K) the loop
# terminates and W = K is the well-founded model.
logic:wfFixpointNotReached
    a logic:Situation ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "well-founded fixpoint not reached"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The loop-guard situation of the alternating-fixpoint phase: the double-reduct step has not yet converged — the new under-estimate K2 differs from the previous K by fact-key set (K2.key_set() != K.key_set() in crates/logic/src/wellfounded.rs). The logic:iterationCondition of logic:wfResolveLoop: the loop body re-runs while this obtains, and the iteration terminates when it ceases (K2 == K), at which point W = K is the well-founded model's set of true atoms."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

# Phase 1 — seed the per-world EDB.
logic:wfMaterializeEdb
    a logic:ActionSchema ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "well-founded materialize EDB"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Phase 1 of the well-founded materializer (crates/logic/src/wellfounded.rs materialize()): seed the per-world EDB — collect world_edb_facts, echo the asserted rows, and load them (sorted-key order) into the seed FactStore that the alternating fixpoint starts from. The first elementary phase transition of the intra-engine plan; its successor is the alternating-fixpoint resolution."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox ;
    logic:precondition logic:wfWorldSeeded ;
    logic:capability logic:wfReductEngineCapability .

# Phase 2 — the alternating fixpoint (the ITERATED phase).
logic:wfResolveFixpoint
    a logic:ActionSchema ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "well-founded resolve fixpoint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Phase 2 of the well-founded materializer (crates/logic/src/wellfounded.rs materialize()): the alternating fixpoint of van Gelder — the double-reduct step U = least_model_of_reduct(reference = K) then K2 = least_model_of_reduct(reference = U), repeated until K2 and K have the same fact-key set. The body of the logic:Iteration logic:wfResolveLoop; the loop runs while logic:wfFixpointNotReached obtains and converges on W = K, the well-founded model's set of true atoms. The single ITERATED phase of the plan — why the plan is outside the acyclic DAG fragment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox ;
    logic:precondition logic:wfWorldSeeded ;
    logic:capability logic:wfReductEngineCapability .

# Phase 3 — final reduct + emit.
logic:wfConstructModel
    a logic:ActionSchema ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "well-founded construct model"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Phase 3 of the well-founded materializer (crates/logic/src/wellfounded.rs materialize()): the final reduct against the converged well-founded model W — least_model_of_reduct(reference = W), a HARD FAIL if the result's fact-key set differs from W (an undefined/partial model, unsupported in v1), then emit the derived non-EDB rows stamped with the world graph. The last elementary phase transition of the intra-engine plan."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox ;
    logic:precondition logic:wfWorldSeeded ;
    logic:capability logic:wfReductEngineCapability .

# The combinator tree (program form an interpreter walks).
#   wfResolveLoop      = Iteration(body = wfResolveFixpoint,
#                                  condition = wfFixpointNotReached)
#   wfResolveThenBuild = SerialConjunction(left = wfResolveLoop,
#                                          right = wfConstructModel)
#   wfPhaseSequence    = SerialConjunction(left = wfMaterializeEdb,
#                                          right = wfResolveThenBuild)
# Phase order, left-first: materialize ; (resolve* ; construct).
logic:wfResolveLoop
    a logic:Iteration ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "well-founded resolve loop"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:Iteration combinator wrapping the alternating-fixpoint phase of the well-founded materializer: its logic:iterationBody is logic:wfResolveFixpoint (the double-reduct step) and its logic:iterationCondition is logic:wfFixpointNotReached (K2 != K). The loop is the program-level twin of the `loop { … break when K2.key_set() == K.key_set() }` of crates/logic/src/wellfounded.rs — the back-edge that places logic:wellFoundedMaterializerPlan outside the certified acyclic DAG fragment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox ;
    logic:iterationBody logic:wfResolveFixpoint ;
    logic:iterationCondition logic:wfFixpointNotReached .

logic:wfResolveThenBuild
    a logic:SerialConjunction ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "well-founded resolve then build"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The inner logic:SerialConjunction of the well-founded materializer's phase plan: do the alternating-fixpoint loop (logic:leftOperand logic:wfResolveLoop), THEN construct the final model (logic:rightOperand logic:wfConstructModel). The suffix program of the plan's outer serial conjunction."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox ;
    logic:leftOperand logic:wfResolveLoop ;
    logic:rightOperand logic:wfConstructModel .

logic:wfPhaseSequence
    a logic:SerialConjunction ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "well-founded phase sequence"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The ROOT logic:SerialConjunction of the well-founded materializer's phase plan: do the EDB-seeding phase (logic:leftOperand logic:wfMaterializeEdb), THEN the resolve-then-build suffix (logic:rightOperand logic:wfResolveThenBuild). Walked left-first, the combinator tree yields the execution order materialize ; resolve* ; construct — the program twin of crates/logic/src/wellfounded.rs materialize() and of gmeow_logic::WELL_FOUNDED_PHASES. The program root logic:wellFoundedMaterializerPlan reaches it through logic:planBody."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox ;
    logic:leftOperand logic:wfMaterializeEdb ;
    logic:rightOperand logic:wfResolveThenBuild .

# The plan's success criterion: the converged well-founded model emitted.
logic:goalWellFoundedModel
    a gmeow:Goal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "well-founded model goal"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The success criterion of the well-founded materializer's phase plan: the total (two-valued) well-founded model of the rules over every world is computed and its derived non-EDB rows emitted. The gmeow:Goal logic:wellFoundedMaterializerPlan is arranged to reach; a partial model is a hard failure, never a partial success."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

# The plan: the authored twin of crates/logic/src/wellfounded.rs materialize().
logic:wellFoundedMaterializerPlan
    a logic:Plan ,
        gmeow:SocialObject ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "well-founded materializer plan"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The AUTHORED MODEL of the intra-engine phase sequence the native well-founded-semantics evaluator runs per world (crates/logic/src/wellfounded.rs materialize()): the canonical process model applied one scale down from the build pipeline (gmeow:pipeline-build) — an intra-engine pass sequence rather than an inter-stage DAG. Its logic:planBody is the root combinator logic:wfPhaseSequence, whose left-first walk yields the three phases materialize (logic:wfMaterializeEdb) ; resolve* (the logic:Iteration logic:wfResolveLoop over logic:wfResolveFixpoint, gated by logic:wfFixpointNotReached) ; construct (logic:wfConstructModel) — matching gmeow_logic::WELL_FOUNDED_PHASES. Because the middle phase is a logic:Iteration (a loop), this plan is OUTSIDE the certified acyclic DAG fragment by construction; that is correct and expected, so it does NOT run logic:executedUnderContract logic:DagWorkflowContract. Principle 12 BOUNDARY: this plan is authored SOURCE the parity gate crates/pipeline/tests/wellfounded_plan_parity.rs checks the Rust runtime against — the reasoner NEVER parses this RDF at scheduling or runtime; the native loop is the runtime, the plan is its declared twin. As a logic:Plan it asserts its own gmeow:SocialObject sort directly, the prose grounding of logic:Plan licensing no DL inference of it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox ;
    logic:planBody logic:wfPhaseSequence ;
    logic:planGoal logic:goalWellFoundedModel ;
    logic:planSuccessMode logic:StrongPlanSuccess .

# --- Transaction outcome: the executional-entailment verdict surface ----------
# The recorded result of EXECUTING a transaction program (the combinators above)
# under executional entailment — the native engine's verdict, surfaced as quads
# so the success/failure decision and the executed path carry into the
# derivation graph rather than being discarded after evaluation.

logic:TransactionOutcome
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Transaction Outcome"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The recorded result of executing a transaction program from a start state under executional entailment: the program succeeds iff there exists a path from the start state along which it holds, and failure leaves the start state untouched. It carries logic:outcomeOfProgram (the program executed), logic:transactionStart (the state it started from), and logic:transactionSucceeds (the boolean verdict); on success it is linked by logic:executedAlongPath to the logic:Path of states the run traversed and the per-step support is recorded as supersession. Typed APART from logic:GoalEvaluation, which records at-a-state satisfaction of a goal, and from logic:PlanSuccessMode, which classifies a plan's success mode over its nondeterministic outcome set: a logic:TransactionOutcome records ONE concrete executed run's success or failure under executional entailment, not a satisfaction judgement and not a mode classification."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:outcomeOfProgram
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "outcome of program"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:TransactionOutcome to the transaction program whose execution it records — a transaction-program combinator node (logic:SerialConjunction, logic:Choice, logic:Fallback, or logic:Iteration). Domain logic:TransactionOutcome, range a transaction-program node (stated in prose). It ties the verdict back to the program that produced it without conflating the outcome (a run record) with the program (the structure that was run)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:transactionStart
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "transaction start"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:TransactionOutcome to the gmeow:State the program executed from — the start state of the executional-entailment run. Domain logic:TransactionOutcome, range gmeow:State (stated in prose). On failure the start state is left untouched (no successor is materialized); on success it is the first state of the logic:executedAlongPath path. The input side marks the executable program root with logic:transitionFromState; logic:transactionStart records, on the outcome, the start the verdict was computed from."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:transactionSucceeds
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "transaction succeeds"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The boolean executional-entailment verdict of a logic:TransactionOutcome: true iff there exists a path from logic:transactionStart along which the program holds, false otherwise. Domain logic:TransactionOutcome, range xsd:boolean (stated in prose). Kept distinct from goal satisfaction (logic:satisfactionStatus over a logic:GoalEvaluation) and from plan success mode (logic:planSuccessMode): it is the bare 'a path exists from the start' reading of the transaction layer, and a false verdict is honest total failure rather than a weak or partial success."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:executedHypotheticallyAs
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "executed hypothetically as"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The content-addressed witness (a digest) of a hypothetical (sandbox) transaction run: present on a logic:TransactionOutcome exactly when the program executed under logic:HypotheticalExecution, recording the identity of the discarded run whose verdict (logic:transactionSucceeds) was computed without committing its effects. The run's effect substrate — the executed logic:Path, the per-step logic:TransactionStep records, and the supersession quads — is intentionally ABSENT, because the sandbox operator tests without committing; this digest is therefore the sole standing trace that the run occurred and what it ranged over. Nothing is asserted to be erased, only never emitted, so suppression-never-erasure holds for free. Single-valued. Domain logic:TransactionOutcome, range xsd:string (stated in prose). The committed counterpart (logic:CommittedExecution) emits the full effect substrate and carries no such witness."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# --- Concurrency: three distinct, separately-declared concerns ----------------

logic:SerializabilityCriterion
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Serializability Criterion"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A correctness property OF A HISTORY — not a protocol and not a guarantee strength. The closed value class of serializability criteria for a concurrent transaction history. Its individuals are logic:ConflictSerializability (an equivalent serial order exists reachable with no swap of conflicting operations) and logic:ViewSerializability (some serial order has the same read-from and final-write relationships). The set is closed; a misspelt value is rejected. This is one of three distinct, separately-declared concurrency concerns that must NOT be conflated: a logic:SerializabilityCriterion is a property of a history, a logic:IsolationLevel is a declared guarantee strength, and a logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol is an implementation mechanism — keeping them apart is required because they are not interchangeable and an implicit assumption about any one of them is a defect."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ConflictSerializability
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:SerializabilityCriterion ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Conflict Serializability"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:SerializabilityCriterion value satisfied when an equivalent serial order exists that is reachable from the history by swapping only non-conflicting operations — no swap of a pair of conflicting operations is required. A history property, not a protocol. Contrast logic:ViewSerializability."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ViewSerializability
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:SerializabilityCriterion ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "View Serializability"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:SerializabilityCriterion value satisfied when some serial order has the same read-from and final-write relationships as the history — equivalent on what each read observed and which write was last. A history property, not a protocol. Strictly weaker than logic:ConflictSerializability is not implied; the two are distinct criteria a history may satisfy."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:IsolationLevel
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Isolation Level"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The declared GUARANTEE STRENGTH a schedule must meet — not a history property and not an implementation mechanism. The closed value class spanning the standard isolation spectrum up to and including full serializability and opacity. Its individuals are logic:ReadUncommittedIsolation, logic:ReadCommittedIsolation, logic:RepeatableReadIsolation, logic:SnapshotIsolation, logic:SerializableIsolation, and logic:OpacityIsolation — the strongest, under which even aborted or in-flight transactions never observe an inconsistent state, strictly stronger than serializability. The set is closed; a misspelt value is rejected. This is one of three distinct, separately-declared concurrency concerns that must NOT be conflated: a logic:IsolationLevel is a guarantee strength, a logic:SerializabilityCriterion is a property of a history, and a logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol is an implementation mechanism."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:ReadUncommittedIsolation
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:IsolationLevel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Read Uncommitted Isolation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:IsolationLevel guarantee under which a transaction may observe uncommitted writes of others — the weakest level of the standard spectrum, admitting dirty reads."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ReadCommittedIsolation
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:IsolationLevel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Read Committed Isolation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:IsolationLevel guarantee under which a transaction observes only committed writes, but successive reads of the same item may differ — dirty reads excluded, non-repeatable reads still admitted."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:RepeatableReadIsolation
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:IsolationLevel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Repeatable Read Isolation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:IsolationLevel guarantee under which re-reads of an item a transaction has read return the same value — non-repeatable reads excluded, phantom rows still admitted."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:SnapshotIsolation
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:IsolationLevel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Snapshot Isolation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:IsolationLevel guarantee under which a transaction reads from a consistent snapshot taken at its start — strong against most anomalies yet still admitting write skew, so strictly weaker than logic:SerializableIsolation."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:SerializableIsolation
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:IsolationLevel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Serializable Isolation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:IsolationLevel guarantee under which the schedule is equivalent to some serial execution of the committed transactions — full serializability. Strictly weaker than logic:OpacityIsolation, which additionally constrains aborted and in-flight transactions."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:OpacityIsolation
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:IsolationLevel ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Opacity Isolation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:IsolationLevel guarantee under which even aborted or in-flight transactions never observe an inconsistent state — the strongest level, STRICTLY STRONGER than serializability (logic:SerializableIsolation), which constrains only committed transactions. Engine note: under the committed-only, snapshot-isolation execution model of the native evaluator, no aborted or in-flight transactions arise; opacity's additional constraints are therefore vacuously satisfied, and the isolation-adequacy verdict for logic:OpacityIsolation coincides with that of logic:SerializableIsolation by construction — both are met exactly when the witnessed schedule is conflict-serializable."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Concurrency Control Protocol"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The IMPLEMENTATION MECHANISM that enforces a chosen isolation level — HOW a guarantee is achieved, never the guarantee itself. The closed value class of concurrency-control protocols. Its individuals are logic:StrictTwoPhaseLocking, logic:StrongStrictTwoPhaseLocking, logic:TimestampOrdering, logic:OptimisticValidation, and logic:MultiversionConcurrencyControl. The set is closed; a misspelt value is rejected. This is one of three distinct, separately-declared concurrency concerns that must NOT be conflated: a logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol is a mechanism, a logic:SerializabilityCriterion is a property of a history, and a logic:IsolationLevel is a guarantee strength — a protocol is how a guarantee is achieved, it is never the guarantee itself."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:StrictTwoPhaseLocking
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Strict Two-Phase Locking"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol that holds all EXCLUSIVE (write) locks until the transaction commits or aborts, preventing cascading aborts on writes. Contrast logic:StrongStrictTwoPhaseLocking, which additionally holds shared (read) locks to commit."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:StrongStrictTwoPhaseLocking
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Strong Strict Two-Phase Locking"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol that holds ALL locks — both shared (read) and exclusive (write) — until commit or abort, yielding the strongest, strictest two-phase-locking discipline. The strong-strict variant the design names distinctly from the strict variant (logic:StrictTwoPhaseLocking), which releases shared locks earlier."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:TimestampOrdering
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Timestamp Ordering"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol that assigns each transaction a timestamp and orders conflicting operations by it, aborting any operation that would violate the timestamp order. A mechanism, not a guarantee."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:OptimisticValidation
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Optimistic Validation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol that lets transactions run without locking and validates for conflicts at commit time, aborting and retrying on a detected conflict. A mechanism, not a guarantee."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:MultiversionConcurrencyControl
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Multiversion Concurrency Control"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol under which each transaction reads from a consistent version snapshot taken at its start and writes new versions independently, so readers never block writers. Its strongest enforced guarantee is logic:SnapshotIsolation — it still admits WRITE SKEW and so does NOT enforce logic:SerializableIsolation, which is exactly why declaring it under a serializable-or-stronger level is inadequate. This engine realizes snapshot isolation by construction, so a multiversion run needs no lock or timestamp events. A mechanism, not a guarantee."@x-gmeow-english .

# --- Serialization anomaly: a finding, never a contradiction witness ----------

logic:SerializationAnomaly
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Serialization Anomaly"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A HISTORY-LEVEL finding: a pattern of conflicting operations, read-from edges, and happens-before arcs in a transaction history that admits no equivalent serial execution under the declared policy. Lost updates, write skew, and read/write anomalies are findings of this kind. It is DISTINCT from a contradiction witness (logic:contradictionWitness, which asserts ⊥ within a single state) and must NOT be modelled as one: the final state after a non-serializable schedule may be perfectly logically consistent while the schedule still has no equivalent serial execution, so a serialization anomaly is a property of the history, never a logical contradiction inside a state. It is surfaced as a finding with its dependency cycle or violated isolation level described; a non-serializable schedule is never silently linearized."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

# --- Concurrent history: the derived conflict graph + anomaly surface ----------
# The transaction history of a concurrent composition (logic:ConcurrentComposition)
# and the conflict-edge / criterion / finding vocabulary the native concurrent
# evaluator DERIVES from an interleaved run and the serialization-anomaly engine
# classifies. A history is a property bearer kept APART from the program that gives
# rise to it (the composition) and from the executional-entailment verdict (the
# outcome). Normative in design/LOGIC-TRANSACTION.md §Concurrent transactions.

logic:ConcurrentHistory
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Concurrent History"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The transaction history of a concurrent composition: the node that carries the logic:precedes conflict edges whose cycle structure logic:SerializationAnomaly classifies. The native concurrent evaluator DERIVES it from the interleaved execution of a logic:ConcurrentComposition's two legs (it may also be authored directly as a conflict graph). It is a HISTORY-LEVEL property bearer, kept DISTINCT from the logic:ConcurrentComposition PROGRAM that gives rise to it and from the logic:TransactionOutcome VERDICT of running that program — a history records which conflicting operations were ordered against which, not whether a path exists. Its serializability is judged against the logic:serializabilityCriterion it carries (default logic:ConflictSerializability); a cyclic conflict graph is surfaced as a logic:SerializationAnomaly finding, never silently linearized and never a logical contradiction within a state."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:precedes
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "precedes"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A conflict (precedence) edge in a logic:ConcurrentHistory: 'from precedes to' orders the conflicting operation of transaction 'from' before that of transaction 'to' in the witnessed schedule. Two operations conflict when they touch the same item and at least one writes it; the edge direction records which came first in the interleaving. Domain and range are transaction-program nodes (stated in prose). A CYCLE over logic:precedes is a serialization anomaly (logic:SerializationAnomaly) — between two transactions it requires edges in BOTH directions, the write-skew / lost-update shape — and is NEVER a logical contradiction (logic:contradictionWitness): it is a property of the history, not of any single state."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:serializabilityCriterion
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "serializability criterion"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ConcurrentHistory to the logic:SerializabilityCriterion its schedule is classified against — logic:ConflictSerializability (the default when none is stated) or logic:ViewSerializability. Domain logic:ConcurrentHistory, range logic:SerializabilityCriterion (stated in prose). This is the history-property dimension only: it is kept SEPARATE from logic:IsolationLevel (a declared guarantee strength) and logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol (an implementation mechanism), the two other concurrency concerns the layer never conflates. The criterion a detected anomaly violates is recorded on the finding through logic:violatedCriterion."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:concurrentComposedFrom
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "concurrent composed from"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a DERIVED logic:ConcurrentHistory to each of the two transaction-program operands (the logic:leftOperand and logic:rightOperand of the logic:ConcurrentComposition) whose interleaved elementary steps produced its conflict edges. Domain logic:ConcurrentHistory, range a transaction-program node (stated in prose); multi-valued (one link per operand). It is the audit trail from the history back to the authored sub-programs that gave rise to it, kept distinct from logic:precedes (an edge BETWEEN the transactions within the history) — this link points OUT to the programs, not between operations."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:derivedHistory
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "derived history"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:TransactionOutcome of a logic:ConcurrentComposition run to the logic:ConcurrentHistory the native evaluator derived from the interleaved execution of its two legs. Domain logic:TransactionOutcome, range logic:ConcurrentHistory (stated in prose). It SEPARATES the executional-entailment verdict (the outcome — does a path exist) from the derived concurrency surface (the history — how the conflicting operations were ordered and whether that ordering is serializable): a concurrent run records BOTH, linked by this property, so neither the verdict nor the serializability finding is conflated with the other."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:violatedCriterion
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "violated criterion"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:SerializationAnomaly finding to the logic:SerializabilityCriterion it violates — the criterion (logic:ConflictSerializability or logic:ViewSerializability) under which the history admits no equivalent serial execution. Domain logic:SerializationAnomaly, range logic:SerializabilityCriterion (stated in prose). It names WHICH history property the anomaly breaches; it does not assert a logical contradiction (the schedule's final state may be perfectly consistent), keeping the finding a property of the history rather than of any state."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:anomalyCycle
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "anomaly cycle"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The detected dependency cycle of a logic:SerializationAnomaly, rendered as the canonical sequence of transaction IRIs joined by ' -> ' (rotated to start at the lexicographically smallest member and closed back to it, so the same history always yields the same description). Domain logic:SerializationAnomaly, range xsd:string (stated in prose). It is a human-readable DESCRIPTION of the history-level finding — the conflict-graph cycle that has no equivalent serial order — never a claim of ⊥ within a state; a non-serializable schedule is surfaced with its cycle described here, never silently linearized."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# --- View-serializability: the read-from / final-write history surface ---------
# The native concurrent evaluator classifies the witnessed binary interleaving
# against logic:ViewSerializability as well as logic:ConflictSerializability, and
# DERIVES the read-from and happens-before edges that classification rests on.
# View-equivalence compares, across the witnessed schedule and the two serial
# orders of its two legs, which write each read observed (logic:readsFrom) and
# which transaction wrote each situation last. A conflict-serializable schedule is
# always view-serializable; a conflict-CYCLIC schedule may still be view-
# serializable (a blind-write history), so the two criteria are recorded
# independently through logic:satisfiesCriterion.

logic:readsFrom
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "reads from"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A read-dependency edge of a logic:ConcurrentHistory: 'reader readsFrom writer' records that the reading logic:TransactionStep observed, for a situation in its read set (its schema's logic:precondition), the value written by the writer logic:TransactionStep — the last step to write that situation before the reader in the witnessed interleaving. Domain and range logic:TransactionStep (stated in prose). Emitted only for CROSS-transaction read dependencies (a step reading from the OTHER leg's write), which is where the read-from relation can differ from a serial order and therefore where it carries view-serializability information; intra-leg read dependencies are fixed by the leg's own order and carry none. Together with logic:happensBefore it is the view-serializability surface, kept DISTINCT from logic:precedes, which is the transaction-level conflict edge over which conflict-serializability is decided."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:happensBefore
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "happens before"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A schedule-order edge of a logic:ConcurrentHistory: 'earlier happensBefore later' records that the earlier logic:TransactionStep is scheduled before the later one in the witnessed index-order interleaving AND the two conflict (they share a situation that at least one writes). Domain and range logic:TransactionStep (stated in prose). Emitted only for CROSS-transaction conflicting pairs — the schedule order between the two legs' operations that view- and conflict-serializability both read. It is the step-level witnessed-order companion to logic:readsFrom; it is kept DISTINCT from logic:precedes, which lifts conflict order to the TRANSACTION level for cycle detection, and from logic:temporallySucceeds, which orders the states of a single executed path rather than operations across legs."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:satisfiesCriterion
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "satisfies criterion"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ConcurrentHistory to each logic:SerializabilityCriterion its witnessed schedule actually SATISFIES — the classification RESULT, as opposed to logic:serializabilityCriterion, which names the criterion the history is judged against. The native evaluator emits logic:ConflictSerializability when the logic:precedes conflict graph is acyclic and logic:ViewSerializability when the witnessed interleaving is view-equivalent to one of the two serial orders of its legs. Domain logic:ConcurrentHistory, range logic:SerializabilityCriterion (stated in prose); multi-valued. Because conflict-serializability is strictly stronger, a history that satisfies logic:ConflictSerializability always also satisfies logic:ViewSerializability; a conflict-CYCLIC history may satisfy logic:ViewSerializability alone. Absence of a criterion here is the honest record that the schedule does not satisfy it — the corresponding logic:SerializationAnomaly finding carries the violated criterion — never a silent linearization."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# --- Protocol soundness: the isolation guarantee + control mechanism surface ---
# A logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol is enforced by the protocol's OWN events —
# per-transaction timestamps and per-action lock acquire/release — which a run
# records so that "does this witnessed schedule respect the declared protocol?"
# is a polynomial WELL-FORMEDNESS classification of the schedule (Principle 12),
# never a search for a legal schedule (which would be NP-hard). The protocol is
# declared on the reasoning contract (logic:declaredProtocol); the verdict is
# recorded on the derived history (logic:protocolEnforced). A declared protocol
# whose required events are absent from the run is a HARD ERROR, never a silent
# pass — the run must carry the events its protocol is verified against.

logic:declaredProtocol
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "declared protocol"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ReasoningContract to the logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol its concurrent transactions are to be enforced under — the IMPLEMENTATION MECHANISM (strict / strong-strict two-phase locking, timestamp ordering, or optimistic validation) declared for the run. Domain logic:ReasoningContract, range logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol (stated in prose); at most one per contract, from the closed protocol set (a value outside it is a hard error). It is kept DISTINCT from the guarantee strength the mechanism is meant to achieve (logic:declaredIsolationLevel) and from the history property it produces (logic:serializabilityCriterion): a protocol is HOW a guarantee is enforced, never the guarantee itself. The native evaluator classifies whether the witnessed schedule respects this protocol's discipline over the run's recorded protocol events and records the verdict as logic:protocolEnforced."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:transactionTimestamp
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "transaction timestamp"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The start-order timestamp assigned to a transaction (a concurrent-composition leg) under a timestamp-ordering protocol — the recorded event a logic:TimestampOrdering verification classifies the witnessed schedule against. Domain a transaction-program node (a logic:ConcurrentComposition leg), range xsd:integer (stated in prose); single-valued. Under logic:TimestampOrdering the schedule is well-formed iff every logic:precedes conflict edge runs from the earlier timestamp to the later one; a run declaring logic:TimestampOrdering that omits a leg's timestamp is a hard error. It is a recorded protocol EVENT — an input the classifier reads — not a derived verdict, and it is distinct from logic:temporallySucceeds, which orders the STATES of an executed path rather than the transactions of a schedule."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:lockAcquired
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "lock acquired"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Records that the action a transaction step performs ACQUIRES an exclusive lock on a situation — a recorded event of a two-phase-locking protocol. Declared on the action schema (logic:ActionSchema) a step instantiates, range the locked situation (stated in prose); the native evaluator reads the acquire/release events in each leg's step order to classify the lock discipline. Under logic:StrictTwoPhaseLocking the schedule is well-formed iff each leg is two-phase (no acquire follows any release within the leg); under logic:StrongStrictTwoPhaseLocking, additionally, no lock is released before the leg's final (commit) step. It is a recorded protocol EVENT the classifier reads, kept distinct from logic:lockReleased (its releasing counterpart) and from the situation-validity vocabulary (logic:situationObtains), which is about what holds, not what is locked."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:lockReleased
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "lock released"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Records that the action a transaction step performs RELEASES its exclusive lock on a situation — the releasing counterpart of logic:lockAcquired and a recorded event of a two-phase-locking protocol. Declared on the action schema (logic:ActionSchema) a step instantiates, range the released situation (stated in prose). The native evaluator reads releases in each leg's step order: a release before any remaining acquire breaks the two-phase discipline (logic:StrictTwoPhaseLocking), and a release before the leg's final step breaks the held-to-commit discipline (logic:StrongStrictTwoPhaseLocking). It is a recorded protocol EVENT the classifier reads, never a derived verdict."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:protocolEnforced
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "protocol enforced"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The boolean verdict of classifying a logic:ConcurrentHistory's witnessed schedule against the logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol its run declared (logic:declaredProtocol): true iff the schedule respects that protocol's discipline over the run's recorded protocol events (timestamps for logic:TimestampOrdering; lock acquire/release events for the two-phase-locking protocols; cross-leg read-write disjointness for logic:OptimisticValidation). Domain logic:ConcurrentHistory, range xsd:boolean (stated in prose); present only when a protocol is declared. A false verdict carries a human-readable logic:protocolViolationReason. This is HOW-a-guarantee-is-enforced verification, kept distinct from logic:satisfiesCriterion (the history property the schedule satisfies) and logic:isolationLevelAdequacy (whether the schedule meets a declared guarantee strength); a declared protocol whose required events are absent is a hard error, never a false-by-default."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:protocolViolationReason
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "protocol violation reason"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A human-readable description of WHY a logic:ConcurrentHistory's witnessed schedule fails the concurrency-control protocol its run declared — present exactly when logic:protocolEnforced is false. Domain logic:ConcurrentHistory, range xsd:string (stated in prose). It names the discipline the schedule breaks (a non-two-phase lock order, a lock released before commit, a conflict edge against timestamp order, or a cross-leg read-write conflict that optimistic validation would abort), so a protocol failure is surfaced with its cause rather than as a bare boolean. It is a description of a mechanism-level finding, never a claim of logical contradiction within a state."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# --- Isolation-level adequacy: does the schedule meet the declared strength? ---
# A logic:IsolationLevel is a declared GUARANTEE STRENGTH; the native evaluator
# classifies whether a run's witnessed schedule MEETS it. The concurrent engine
# realizes snapshot isolation (each leg reads a consistent start snapshot and
# writes independently), so every level up to and including logic:SnapshotIsolation
# is met by construction; logic:SerializableIsolation and logic:OpacityIsolation
# are met only when the schedule is conflict-serializable (a write-skew cycle,
# which snapshot admits, fails them). The level is declared on the contract
# (logic:declaredIsolationLevel); the verdict is recorded on the history
# (logic:isolationLevelAdequacy).

logic:declaredIsolationLevel
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "declared isolation level"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ReasoningContract to the logic:IsolationLevel guarantee strength its concurrent transactions must meet. Domain logic:ReasoningContract, range logic:IsolationLevel (stated in prose); at most one per contract, from the closed isolation-level set (a value outside it is a hard error). It is kept DISTINCT from logic:declaredProtocol (the mechanism that would enforce the level) and from logic:serializabilityCriterion (a history property): an isolation level is the guarantee STRENGTH required, neither the mechanism that achieves it nor the property a particular history happens to have. The native evaluator classifies whether the witnessed schedule meets this declared strength and records the result as logic:isolationLevelAdequacy."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:isolationLevelAdequacy
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "isolation level adequacy"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The boolean verdict of classifying a logic:ConcurrentHistory's witnessed schedule against the logic:IsolationLevel its run declared (logic:declaredIsolationLevel): true iff the schedule meets that declared guarantee strength. Domain logic:ConcurrentHistory, range xsd:boolean (stated in prose); present only when a level is declared. Because the concurrent evaluator realizes snapshot isolation, every level up to logic:SnapshotIsolation is adequate by construction; logic:SerializableIsolation and logic:OpacityIsolation are adequate iff the schedule is conflict-serializable — a write-skew cycle (which snapshot admits) is adequate for snapshot yet INADEQUATE for serializability. A false verdict carries a logic:isolationInadequacyReason. It is guarantee-strength adequacy, kept distinct from logic:protocolEnforced (mechanism soundness) and logic:satisfiesCriterion (the history property)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:isolationInadequacyReason
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "isolation inadequacy reason"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A human-readable description of WHY a logic:ConcurrentHistory's witnessed schedule fails to meet its declared logic:IsolationLevel — present exactly when logic:isolationLevelAdequacy is false. Domain logic:ConcurrentHistory, range xsd:string (stated in prose). In the snapshot-isolation model this is the case of a schedule that is not conflict-serializable (a write-skew or lost-update cycle) declared under logic:SerializableIsolation or logic:OpacityIsolation, and the reason names that: the schedule is not conflict-serializable, so it does not meet the declared strength. It is a guarantee-level finding, never a claim of logical contradiction within a state."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# --- Protocol -> isolation-level adequacy: does the declared mechanism reach ---
# the declared strength? A logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol is a mechanism and a
# logic:IsolationLevel a guarantee strength; a run declares each separately, so
# it may pair them incoherently. Each protocol has a guaranteed CEILING (the
# strongest level it enforces when respected): logic:MultiversionConcurrencyControl
# reaches logic:SnapshotIsolation (write skew remains), while the four
# serializability-strength protocols reach logic:SerializableIsolation (coincident
# with logic:OpacityIsolation for committed-only histories). The pairing is
# adequate iff the ceiling is at least the declared level. This is a consistency
# cross-check between two declarations, never an inference of one from the other.

logic:protocolLevelAdequacy
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "protocol level adequacy"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The boolean verdict of classifying whether the logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol a run declares (logic:declaredProtocol) is strong enough to guarantee the logic:IsolationLevel it also declares (logic:declaredIsolationLevel): true iff the protocol's guaranteed ceiling level is at least the declared level. Domain logic:ConcurrentHistory, range xsd:boolean (stated in prose); present only when a run declares BOTH a protocol and a level — otherwise there is no pairing to judge. The guaranteed ceilings are logic:SnapshotIsolation for logic:MultiversionConcurrencyControl and logic:SerializableIsolation (coincident with logic:OpacityIsolation for committed-only histories) for logic:StrictTwoPhaseLocking, logic:StrongStrictTwoPhaseLocking, logic:TimestampOrdering, and logic:OptimisticValidation. The one inadequate pairing this vocabulary admits is logic:MultiversionConcurrencyControl declared under logic:SerializableIsolation or logic:OpacityIsolation, since its snapshot ceiling still admits write skew; a false verdict carries a logic:protocolLevelInadequacyReason. It is a CONSISTENCY CROSS-CHECK between two declarations — kept distinct from logic:protocolEnforced (whether the witnessed schedule respects the mechanism) and logic:isolationLevelAdequacy (whether the witnessed schedule meets the strength) — and never infers the level from the protocol nor the protocol from the level."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:protocolLevelInadequacyReason
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "protocol level inadequacy reason"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A human-readable description of WHY a run's declared logic:ConcurrencyControlProtocol cannot guarantee its declared logic:IsolationLevel — present exactly when logic:protocolLevelAdequacy is false. Domain logic:ConcurrentHistory, range xsd:string (stated in prose). In this vocabulary the case is logic:MultiversionConcurrencyControl declared under logic:SerializableIsolation or logic:OpacityIsolation, and the reason names it: the protocol's guaranteed ceiling is snapshot isolation, which admits write skew, so it does not reach the declared serializable-or-stronger level. It is a declaration-consistency finding, never a claim of logical contradiction within a state."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# ── The full first-order formula AST surface (TBox + RBox) ────────────────────
# The reified vocabulary the typed full-FOL formula core serializes into: a
# logic:Formula tree of quantifiers (logic:forall / logic:exists), connectives
# (logic:and / logic:or / logic:not / logic:antecedent+logic:consequent /
# logic:iff), and atomic predications (logic:relation over ordered term-carrier
# arguments). Horn+NAF rules remain the recognized sub-fragment carried by
# logic:Rule; this surface carries the formulas that exceed it. Relations are
# reified as logic:Type individuals (the HiLog reflection — see logic:instanceOf
# / logic:orderedType / logic:Type), so the object level stays first-order: there
# is no predicate-variable term.

logic:Formula
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Formula"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A first-order formula node: the reified AST node a non-Horn logic: statement compiles into. A logic:Formula is a quantifier (carrying logic:forall or logic:exists over a body and one or more logic:quantifiedVariable bindings), a connective (logic:and, logic:or, logic:not, logic:antecedent with logic:consequent, or logic:iff over sub-formulae), or an atomic predication (logic:relation over ordered logic:argument terms). It is the unified full-FOL core of which Horn+NAF derivation rules are a recognized sub-fragment; the canonical RDF 1.2 serialization round-trips it losslessly, while weaker projections carry any formula they cannot express as flagged unsupported residue."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:Rule
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Rule"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A productive Horn or stratified-negation derivation whose logic:head is entailed when every positive logic:body atom holds, every logic:negatedBody atom is absent in its declared closure, and every guard succeeds. It is not a general logic:Formula or an integrity logic:Constraint: constructs outside the rule fragment remain formula trees and are carried as projection residue rather than approximated."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:TermCarrier
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Term Carrier"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "An ordered slot in an atomic formula's logic:argument list, a function term's logic:argument list, or a quantifier's logic:quantifiedVariable block. It carries exactly one zero-based logic:termIndex and exactly one value kind: logic:termIri, logic:termVariable, logic:termLiteral, logic:termSequenceMarker, or logic:termApplication (a nested compound function term). It is not the term value itself; it is the RDF node that preserves the value's kind and position independently of triple order."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FunctionTerm
    a logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:ArgumentStructure ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Function Term"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A compound function-term application node: a single reified function symbol (logic:functionSymbol, a logic:Type individual) applied to a non-empty, ordered list of logic:argument term-carriers, denoting a first-order term such as cons(H, T) or s(X). It mirrors the atomic-predication pattern (logic:relation over ordered logic:argument), but predicates a FUNCTION symbol producing a term rather than a relation producing a truth value; reifying the symbol as a logic:Type individual keeps the object level first-order, so there is no function-variable. An argument carrier may itself carry logic:termApplication, so a nested term such as cons(H, cons(1, nil)) round-trips. It is not itself a logic:Formula: it is a term, referenced from a carrier's logic:termApplication, never asserted as a sentence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "cons(H, cons(1, nil)) is a logic:FunctionTerm whose logic:functionSymbol is ex:cons and whose second logic:argument carries logic:termApplication onto the inner cons(1, nil) function term."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use a function term for a nullary constant; a 0-ary function symbol is an individual and is carried by logic:termIri, so an empty-argument function term would give one constant two spellings."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Reify the function symbol as a logic:Type individual on logic:functionSymbol, place each operand in a zero-based logic:argument term-carrier, and reference the whole node from an argument carrier's logic:termApplication."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when an argument is itself a compound term built by applying a function symbol to sub-terms, rather than an IRI, variable, literal, or sequence marker."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ArgumentStructure
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Argument Structure"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A node that applies a reified operator to an ordered list of logic:argument term-carriers: an atomic-predication logic:Formula (operator = logic:relation) or a logic:FunctionTerm (operator = logic:functionSymbol). It is the shared rdfs:domain of logic:argument, so the ordered argument-carrier machinery is reused across both without asserting that a function term is a formula or that a formula is a term."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:SequenceMarker
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Sequence Marker"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A variadic argument placeholder (the Common Logic '…x' form) that binds a sequence of terms rather than a single term, generalizing the fixed arity-three atom to predications of any arity. A term-carrier argument whose value is a logic:termSequenceMarker denotes one; keeping it a distinct kind stops a single-term variable from being mistaken for a sequence one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

# ── The authored logic:ReasoningProgram surface (TBox + RBox) ─────────────────
# A general, mode-annotated authoring envelope over the EXISTING clause
# constructs above: logic:Formula atoms/Horn rules via logic:antecedent /
# logic:consequent, logic:not for negated body literals, and
# logic:termApplication / logic:FunctionTerm / logic:functionSymbol for
# compound terms. It mints no second clause language — a logic:ReasoningProgram
# is a named clause set plus a goal, evaluated under a selected strategy.

logic:ReasoningProgram
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Reasoning Program"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A named clause set evaluated under a selected strategy: the authored surface that drives the native reasoning engine directly from slice content. A logic:ReasoningProgram carries a set of logic:clause atoms — facts and Horn rules, the SAME logic:Formula clause shape the forward relational-core lane already consumes, so there is no separate clause language — exactly one logic:programQuery goal atom whose logic:termVariable term-carriers are the answer variables the engine binds, zero or more logic:verdictProbe atoms whose three-valued well-founded verdict is projected independently of the query's bindings, and exactly one logic:evaluationMode selecting the evaluation strategy. 'Backward' resolution is a STRATEGY over a clause set, not a kind of program: keeping the mode a selector on a shared clause set, rather than splitting programs into forward and backward kinds, is what lets the SAME authored clauses later be evaluated forward once a chase strategy is added, without re-authoring."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "A program with two logic:clause facts, a rule concluding path(X,Z) from edge(X,Y) and path(Y,Z), one logic:programQuery path(a, V), and logic:evaluationMode logic:BackwardEvaluation resolves V by goal-directed SLG-WFS backward resolution over the clause set."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not author a separate forward-only or backward-only program class; author one logic:ReasoningProgram and select the strategy on logic:evaluationMode, since the clause set itself is strategy-neutral."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Author one or more logic:clause facts/rules using the existing logic:Formula / logic:antecedent / logic:consequent / logic:not shape, attach exactly one logic:programQuery goal atom, optionally attach logic:verdictProbe atoms, and select exactly one logic:evaluationMode."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a slice needs to author a normal logic program — a clause set plus a goal query — directly as data the native engine evaluates, rather than describing reasoning behavior only in prose."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:EvaluationMode
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Evaluation Mode"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed value class of the evaluation strategy a logic:ReasoningProgram selects via logic:evaluationMode — which procedure resolves the SAME authored clause set. Its sole individual today is logic:BackwardEvaluation, the goal-directed SLG-WFS backward-resolution strategy; the set is closed and a misspelt or otherwise-unrecognized value is rejected. A forward chase over the identical clause shape is a future member of this same closed set, not a competing program kind, which is why evaluation strategy is carried as a selector value rather than a class distinction on logic:ReasoningProgram."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:BackwardEvaluation
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:EvaluationMode ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Backward Evaluation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:EvaluationMode value selecting goal-directed SLG-WFS backward resolution: subsumptive demand-driven answer keying (SLG tabling), structured-term unification over logic:FunctionTerm arguments, explicit proof objects for each derived answer, and three-valued well-founded negation for logic:not-negated body literals. The one evaluation strategy this vocabulary supports today; contrast a future forward-chase member of logic:EvaluationMode over the identical clause set."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:evaluationMode
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ReasoningProgram ;
    rdfs:range logic:EvaluationMode ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "evaluation mode"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ReasoningProgram to the single logic:EvaluationMode strategy it selects to resolve its clause set. Functional: a program selects exactly one strategy, never a mix. An unrecognized or unsupported mode value is a HARD FAIL at compile time — explicit feature selection under the closed logic:EvaluationMode set, never a silent fallback to a weaker or default strategy."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:clause
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ReasoningProgram ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "clause"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ReasoningProgram to each authored clause: an atomic fact or a Horn rule whose logic:antecedent body atoms entail its logic:consequent head, with any body atom optionally logic:not-negated. Reuses the SAME logic:Formula clause shape the forward relational-core lane already lowers from — there is no separate, program-specific clause language. Multi-valued; a program with no clauses is malformed (logic:ReasoningProgramClauseConstraint)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:programQuery
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ReasoningProgram ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "program query"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ReasoningProgram to the single goal atom it resolves against its clause set. Functional: a program declares exactly one goal (logic:ReasoningProgramQueryConstraint requires it; the OWL FunctionalProperty declaration caps it at one). The goal's logic:termVariable term-carriers are the answer variables the engine binds and returns, distinct from a logic:verdictProbe atom's three-valued verdict projection."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:verdictProbe
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ReasoningProgram ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "verdict probe"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ReasoningProgram to zero or more atoms whose three-valued (true/false/undefined) well-founded verdict is projected as a standalone win/move-style check, rather than an answer-variable binding. Distinct from logic:programQuery: a probe reports which of the three verdicts an atom carries under the well-founded model, while the query resolves variable bindings for its single goal. Multi-valued; a program may carry no probes at all."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:variableSort
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:TermCarrier ;
    rdfs:range logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "variable sort"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Assigns an order-sort to a variable term-carrier: the reasoned class (any math:, logic:, or domain-slice OWL Class, not only the numeric tower) the term-carrier's bound variable ranges over, consumed to build the order-sorted unification context a backward-resolution engine unifies structured terms under. It is ONE case of a general sort-assignment pattern, not a variable-only special case: logic:variableSort is the named extension point where a future logic:functionSort (assigning a rank/sort signature to a function symbol rather than a variable) slots in, so nothing here precludes rank-sorted compound terms later. It is not a value restriction on an ordinary OWL property; it is metadata the typed-IR frontend reads to seed sort-checked unification."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:hasFormula
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "has formula"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a program or container to a top-level logic:Formula it asserts. Top-level formulae are reached by this property; sub-formulae are reached by the connective and quantifier properties, so a formula referenced as another's component is not also a top-level assertion."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:integrity
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "integrity"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Constraint to its closed-world integrity condition: the reified logic:Formula whose violation is a finding, not a derivation. Reuses the realized full-FOL formula core verbatim — the condition is an outer range-restricted universal (logic:forall over a bound focus) whose logic:antecedent guard names the class or predicate the focus ranges over and whose logic:consequent is the per-focus condition — so a constraint needs no bespoke shape DSL. The focus target (a class, or the subjects/objects of a predicate) is derived from that guard, never authored twice."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:severity
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "severity"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The severity a logic:Constraint violation reports at, as one of the SHACL local names 'Violation', 'Warning', or 'Info'. Absent means the SHACL default 'Violation'. Carried on the canonical constraint so the projected sh:SPARQLConstraint reproduces the authored sh:severity rather than re-deriving one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:message
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "message"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The advisory human-readable message a logic:Constraint violation attaches (projected to sh:message). Presentation-only and load-bearing-false: it carries no identity, so two constraints differing only in their message are the same constraint. Absent means no message."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Compact constraint-sugar records + the aggregate-comparison satellite.
#
# A few authoring shortcuts the compiler EXPANDS into a canonical logic:Constraint
# + integrity logic:Formula, so an author never hand-writes a formula tree for the
# common closed-world patterns. Sugar is frontend-only: each record expands to
# exactly one logic:Constraint (carrying an honest reified FOL integrity + a
# required logic:formalizes), so there is one canonical home per authored
# constraint. The aggregate satellite carries a reduce-style comparison the
# realized FOL formula core has no node for, lowered to a GROUP BY/HAVING
# sh:SPARQLConstraint (design/LOGIC-VALIDATION.md).
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #

logic:ConstraintSugar
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Constraint Sugar"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "An authoring descriptor that the logic frontend expands into one canonical logic:Constraint with a reified integrity formula. A constraint-sugar record names a closed validation pattern and its parameters; it is not itself a logic:Formula AST node, so formula constructor checks never mistake validation metadata for an object-level sentence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "A logic:ChoiceGroupConstraint record can require exactly one of two predicates without being treated as either operand of the formula it compiles."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use a sugar class when the integrity condition needs a formula shape the selected closed pattern cannot preserve."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Select the narrowest sugar subclass, provide its target and pattern parameters, and inspect the compiled logic:Constraint projection."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a supported closed-world validation pattern whose canonical expansion is deterministic."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ChoiceGroupConstraint
    a logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:ConstraintSugar ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Choice Group Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Compact sugar for a choice-group cardinality constraint (P1): a logic:onClass target, a set of logic:choicePredicate predicates, and a logic:choiceMode ('exactly-one', 'at-most-one', or the 'exactly-one-of-N' alias of 'exactly-one'). The compiler expands it into a canonical logic:Constraint whose integrity is the XOR / at-most formula over the ∃-requiredness of each predicate. Frontend-only: it is not a new node kind, only an authoring shortcut."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:contactChoice a logic:ChoiceGroupConstraint ; logic:onClass ex:Contact ; logic:choicePredicate ex:email, ex:phone ; logic:choiceMode \"exactly-one\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use a choice group when several members may coexist and at least one, rather than exactly or at most one, is required."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Supply one target class, at least two choice predicates, and the exact cardinality mode the compiled XOR or at-most formula must enforce."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a closed validation boundary permits exactly one or at most one member of a finite predicate group."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:GuardedImplicationConstraint
    a logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:ConstraintSugar ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Guarded Implication Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Compact sugar for a guarded-implication constraint (P2): a logic:onClass target, a logic:trigger predicate (optionally pinned to a logic:triggerValue), and one or more logic:requires companion predicates. Expands to a canonical logic:Constraint ∀this. C(this) ∧ trigger(this, …) → ∃ companion. Frontend-only authoring shortcut."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:datedApproval a logic:GuardedImplicationConstraint ; logic:onClass ex:Submission ; logic:trigger ex:approved ; logic:requires ex:approvalDate ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use guarded implication for an unconditional required property or for a trigger whose value needs an open-ended expression."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the target class, trigger predicate, optional trigger value, and every companion predicate whose existence the guard requires."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the presence of a predicate, optionally at one fixed value, conditionally requires companion data."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:DisjunctiveRequirednessConstraint
    a logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:ConstraintSugar ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Disjunctive Requiredness Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Compact sugar for a disjunctive-requiredness constraint (P3): a logic:onClass target and a set of logic:anyOf predicates, at least one of which is required. Expands to a canonical logic:Constraint ∀this. C(this) → (∃p1 ∨ … ∨ ∃pn). Frontend-only authoring shortcut."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:contactRoute a logic:DisjunctiveRequirednessConstraint ; logic:onClass ex:Contact ; logic:anyOf ex:email, ex:phone ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use disjunctive requiredness when the alternatives are mutually exclusive; use a choice-group cardinality constraint instead."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Provide one target class and at least two alternative predicates; the compiled condition succeeds when any alternative has a value."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a focus node must carry at least one of several substitutable properties and may legitimately carry more than one."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:PathValueTypeConstraint
    a logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:ConstraintSugar ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Path Value Type Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Compact sugar for a path-value type-membership constraint (P4): a logic:onClass target, a logic:valuePath predicate, and a required logic:valueClass. Expands to a canonical logic:Constraint ∀this. C(this) → ∀v. P(this,v) → D(v). Frontend-only authoring shortcut."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:memberType a logic:PathValueTypeConstraint ; logic:onClass ex:Team ; logic:valuePath ex:member ; logic:valueClass ex:Person ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use a path-value type constraint to require that the path exist; it constrains every present value but does not add minimum cardinality."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the focus class, one value path, and the class every object reached through that path must instantiate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when all values of one direct predicate must belong to a declared class under closed-world validation."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:CrossNodeConstraint
    a logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:ConstraintSugar ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Cross Node Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Compact sugar for a cross-node co-occurrence / inequality constraint (P5): a logic:onClass target and two roles (logic:roleA, logic:roleB) that must, per logic:crossMode, either 'co-occur' (be present together) or 'differ' (never bind the same value). Expands to a canonical logic:Constraint whose integrity is the bi-implication (co-occur) or the join + inequality (differ, via the logic:termEqual filter relation). Frontend-only authoring shortcut."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:reviewRoles a logic:CrossNodeConstraint ; logic:onClass ex:Review ; logic:roleA ex:author ; logic:roleB ex:reviewer ; logic:crossMode \"differ\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use a cross-node constraint for independent cardinalities; it compares the presence or values of two named roles on the same focus."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Provide the target class, two role predicates, and either co-occur or differ as the comparison mode."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when two roles must appear together or must never resolve to the same value."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ForbiddenPatternConstraint
    a logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:ConstraintSugar ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Forbidden Pattern Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Compact sugar for a forbidden-pattern constraint (P7): a logic:onClass target and a logic:forbiddenPredicate (optionally pinned to a logic:forbiddenValue). Expands to a canonical logic:Constraint ∀this. C(this) → ¬∃b. forbidden(this,b) (or the pinned-value form ¬ forbidden(this, value)). Frontend-only authoring shortcut."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:noDeprecatedCode a logic:ForbiddenPatternConstraint ; logic:onClass ex:Submission ; logic:forbiddenPredicate ex:deprecatedCode ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use a forbidden pattern to express an open-world negation claim; this is a closed validation prohibition over observed data."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the target class and forbidden predicate, adding a fixed forbidden value only when other values on that predicate remain legal."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a focus must not carry a predicate at all or must exclude one specific predicate value."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ValueRangeConstraint
    a logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:ConstraintSugar ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Value Range Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Compact sugar for a numeric value-range constraint (P8): a logic:onClass target, a logic:valuePath predicate, and an inclusive lower (logic:minInclusiveBound) and/or upper (logic:maxInclusiveBound) literal bound every value on that path must satisfy. Expands to a canonical logic:Constraint ∀this,v. C(this) ∧ P(this,v) → min ≤ v ∧ v ≤ max and lowers declaratively to sh:minInclusive/sh:maxInclusive on the target class shape. It is the decidable, validation-only home of a bounded range — NOT an OWL faceted-datatype axiom, which the native reasoner cannot decide once a literal is asserted on the constrained path: it validates and never entails. Frontend-only authoring shortcut."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:unitInterval a logic:ValueRangeConstraint ; logic:onClass ex:Probability ; logic:valuePath ex:magnitude ; logic:minInclusiveBound 0 ; logic:maxInclusiveBound 1 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use a value-range constraint for an exclusive endpoint, a string-length or pattern facet, or a comparison between two properties of one focus; those need the string-pattern or comparison forms."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the target class, the value path, and at least one inclusive literal bound; the compiled condition flags any value outside the closed interval."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when every numeric value of one direct predicate on a class must lie in a closed interval under closed-world validation."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:StringPatternConstraint
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf logic:ConstraintSugar ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "String Pattern Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Compact sugar for a per-value string-pattern constraint: a logic:onClass target (optional — omitted, the guard is the value path's own presence), a logic:valuePath predicate, a logic:stringOp mode (regexRequired / regexForbidden / prefixRequired / prefixForbidden), and a literal logic:stringPattern every (or no) value on that path must satisfy. Expands to a canonical logic:Constraint carrying an honest reified FOL integrity using logic:termRegex / logic:termStrStarts, lowered to a sh:SPARQLConstraint — regex is out of the EL/OWL restriction fragment, so this stays procedural rather than a native declarative sh:pattern facet. Frontend-only authoring shortcut."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:blake3Head a logic:StringPatternConstraint ; logic:onClass ex:Segment ; logic:valuePath ex:headId ; logic:stringOp \"regexRequired\" ; logic:stringPattern \"^blake3:[0-9a-f]{64}$\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use it for a numeric bound (use logic:ValueRangeConstraint) or a fixed forbidden value (use logic:ForbiddenPatternConstraint); this is a regex/prefix test over every present value."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the target class (or omit it for a domain-scoped guard), the value path, the string-op mode, and the literal pattern; the compiled condition flags any value failing the regex or prefix test."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when every (or no) value of one direct predicate must match a regex or share a required/forbidden prefix under closed-world validation — the canonical-identifier and codepoint-pattern idiom."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:UniqueLangConstraint
    a logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:ConstraintSugar ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Unique Language Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Compact sugar for a per-property unique-language constraint: a logic:onClass target and a logic:valuePath predicate whose language-tagged literal values must each carry a DISTINCT language tag (no two values on that path share a tag). Expands declaratively to a sh:uniqueLang true property component on the target class node shape; a language-tagged prose predicate localizes at most once per language. Frontend-only authoring shortcut with no ShEx form (the uniqueLang facet is a disclosed ShEx drop)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:oneNamePerLang a logic:UniqueLangConstraint ; logic:onClass ex:Place ; logic:valuePath ex:localName ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use it to cap total values (that is a cardinality restriction) or to constrain a non-language-tagged predicate; uniqueLang only bites language-tagged literals."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the target class and the localizable-prose value path; the derived sh:uniqueLang true flags any focus carrying two values that share a language tag."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a language-tagged prose predicate on a class must localize at most once per language tag (the localizable-prose convention)."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:AggregateConstraint
    a logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:ConstraintSugar ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Aggregate Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Compact sugar for an aggregate-comparison constraint (P6): a logic:onClass target, an aggregate over a path (logic:aggFunction, optional logic:aggDistinct, logic:aggPath), a logic:aggComparator, and a logic:aggCompareTo right-hand side (a compared property IRI, or a literal). Expands to a canonical logic:Constraint carrying both the honest reified FOL integrity and a structured aggregate satellite, lowered to a SELECT $this … GROUP BY $this HAVING(…) sh:SPARQLConstraint. Frontend-only authoring shortcut."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:minimumMembers a logic:AggregateConstraint ; logic:onClass ex:Team ; logic:aggFunction \"COUNT\" ; logic:aggPath ex:member ; logic:aggComparator \">=\" ; logic:aggCompareTo 2 ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use an aggregate constraint for a per-value condition that needs no grouping; use the narrower path-value or forbidden-pattern sugar."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Specify the target, aggregate function, path, comparator, and literal or property-valued right-hand side; request DISTINCT only when duplicate values must collapse."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when validity depends on COUNT, SUM, MIN, or MAX over the values reached by one predicate."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:JoinAggregateConstraint
    a logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:ConstraintSugar ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Join Aggregate Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Compact sugar for a multi-hop-join product-aggregate constraint (P9): a logic:onClass focus, an ordered logic:joinPath of at least two logic:JoinLeg records (each a reified relation with a logic:legSource / logic:legTarget / logic:legValue predicate and an optional logic:legRecordType), a logic:aggFunction (SUM for ∂²), a logic:aggComparator invariant, and a fixed literal logic:aggThreshold. Each leg hops from an already-bound endpoint through a record to the next endpoint, chaining leg[k].target into leg[k+1].source (the shared join variable); the aggregate is applied to the PRODUCT of the joined leaf values, GROUPED BY the (focus, far-endpoint) tuple, and a violation fires when a group value fails the invariant. It GENERALIZES logic:AggregateConstraint from a single-predicate focus aggregate to a chained-join product aggregate, and is the sanctioned home of the general-CW ∂²=0 conformance check (a SUM of incidence-sign products over composable cells that must equal 0). Expands to a canonical logic:Constraint carrying both the honest reified FOL integrity and a structured join-aggregate satellite, lowered to a SELECT $this ?far … GROUP BY $this ?far HAVING(…) sh:SPARQLConstraint whose join order uses the incidence index and scales with the number of records, never cells². It is not a single-predicate aggregate (use logic:AggregateConstraint), not a derivation rule (a reduce head belongs on logic:aggregateFunction), and not a cartesian product over cells. Frontend-only authoring shortcut."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:boundarySquareZero a logic:JoinAggregateConstraint ; logic:onClass ex:TopCell ; logic:aggFunction \"SUM\" ; logic:aggComparator \"=\" ; logic:aggThreshold 0 ; logic:joinPath ( [ logic:legRecordType ex:Incidence ; logic:legSource ex:incidenceCoface ; logic:legTarget ex:incidenceFace ; logic:legValue ex:incidenceSign ] [ logic:legRecordType ex:Incidence ; logic:legSource ex:incidenceCoface ; logic:legTarget ex:incidenceFace ; logic:legValue ex:incidenceSign ] ) ; logic:formalizes ex:BoundaryOperator ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use a join-aggregate constraint for a single-predicate focus aggregate (use logic:AggregateConstraint) or for a per-value condition that needs no join; and do not use it to derive triples (a reduce head belongs on a logic:Rule)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the focus class, an ordered joinPath of two or more JoinLeg records whose target/source predicates chain the endpoints, the aggregate function, the invariant comparator, and the fixed literal threshold; each leg's legValue predicate carries the numeric leaf multiplied into the group product."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when validity depends on an aggregate (typically SUM) of a PRODUCT of leaf values gathered over a multi-hop JOIN and grouped by a pair of endpoints — most canonically the ∂²=0 boundary-composition law of a chain complex."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:JoinLeg
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Join Leg"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "One hop (leg) of a logic:JoinAggregateConstraint's logic:joinPath: a reified relation record whose logic:legSource predicate reaches this leg's source endpoint, whose logic:legTarget predicate reaches its target endpoint, and whose logic:legValue predicate carries the numeric leaf value multiplied into the group product; an optional logic:legRecordType types the record for the index-anchored join. The legs are ORDERED (the list order is semantic) and chain leg[k].target into leg[k+1].source, so a leg is a hop specification, not a data record — it names the predicates the projected SPARQL joins on, never an incidence itself."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "[ logic:legRecordType ex:Incidence ; logic:legSource ex:incidenceCoface ; logic:legTarget ex:incidenceFace ; logic:legValue ex:incidenceSign ]"@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use a JoinLeg outside a logic:joinPath list, and do not confuse it with the incidence record it describes: a leg names role/value PREDICATES, not endpoints or signs."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Give each leg exactly one logic:legSource, one logic:legTarget, and one logic:legValue predicate (and optionally one logic:legRecordType class); place the legs in composition order inside the joinPath rdf:List."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use as a member of a logic:joinPath to declare one hop of a multi-hop join whose leaf value participates in the group product."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:AggregateBalanceConstraint
    a logic:Class ;
    logic:subClassOf logic:ConstraintSugar ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Aggregate Balance Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Compact sugar for a double-entry balance constraint: a logic:onClass target whose postings (logic:balancePostingPredicate) are partitioned by a partition predicate (logic:balancePartitionPredicate) into a debit side (logic:balanceDebitValue) and a credit side (logic:balanceCreditValue), each posting's numeric amount is read via logic:balanceAmountNodePredicate then logic:balanceValuePredicate, and the group key via logic:balanceAmountNodePredicate then logic:balanceGroupPredicate; within every group the two partition-sums must be EQUAL. Generalizes the single-aggregate logic:AggregateConstraint to a partitioned two-sum equality over a value-key group. Expands to a canonical logic:Constraint carrying an honest reified FOL integrity plus a structured balance satellite, lowered to a SELECT $this … GROUP BY $this ?group HAVING(sumDebits != sumCredits) sh:SPARQLConstraint. Frontend-only authoring shortcut."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:entryBalances a logic:AggregateBalanceConstraint ; logic:onClass ex:JournalEntry ; logic:balancePostingPredicate ex:posting ; logic:balancePartitionPredicate ex:direction ; logic:balanceDebitValue ex:debit ; logic:balanceCreditValue ex:credit ; logic:balanceAmountNodePredicate ex:amount ; logic:balanceValuePredicate ex:value ; logic:balanceGroupPredicate ex:currency ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use it for a single aggregate compared to a constant (that is logic:AggregateConstraint) or when there is no debit/credit partition and per-currency grouping."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name the target class, the posting predicate, the partition predicate and its two debit/credit values, the amount-node predicate, the numeric-value predicate, and the group-key predicate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when validity requires that, per group, the sum of debit-partition amounts equals the sum of credit-partition amounts (double-entry bookkeeping)."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:balancePostingPredicate
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "balance posting predicate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The predicate of a logic:AggregateBalanceConstraint from the focus to each posting aggregated in the balance ($this <posting> ?posting)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:balancePartitionPredicate
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "balance partition predicate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The predicate of a logic:AggregateBalanceConstraint on a posting whose value selects its partition — the debit side (logic:balanceDebitValue) or the credit side (logic:balanceCreditValue)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:balanceDebitValue
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "balance debit value"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:balancePartitionPredicate value marking a DEBIT posting in a logic:AggregateBalanceConstraint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:balanceCreditValue
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "balance credit value"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The logic:balancePartitionPredicate value marking a CREDIT posting in a logic:AggregateBalanceConstraint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:balanceAmountNodePredicate
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "balance amount node predicate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The predicate of a logic:AggregateBalanceConstraint from a posting to its amount node (?posting <amountNode> ?amount); both the numeric value and the group key hang off this shared amount node, so a value and its group key are always read from the same amount."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:balanceValuePredicate
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "balance value predicate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The predicate of a logic:AggregateBalanceConstraint from the amount node to its numeric value (?amount <value> ?val), the quantity summed per partition."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:balanceGroupPredicate
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "balance group predicate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The predicate of a logic:AggregateBalanceConstraint from the amount node to the group key (?amount <group> ?group); the balance equality is asserted per group (e.g. per currency)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:balancedByGroup
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "balanced by group"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The reified relation an expanded logic:AggregateBalanceConstraint carries as its honest FOL integrity: balancedByGroup(this, posting, partition, debit, credit, amountNode, value, group). The realized FOL formula core has no aggregate node, so this predication makes the double-entry-balance meaning complete in the FOL canon while the structured satellite drives the real GROUP BY/HAVING SPARQL projection."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:termLangMatches
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "term language matches"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A language-tag introspection relation the procedural-constraint SPARQL fragment lowers to a case-insensitive FILTER ( REGEX(LANG(x), 'pattern', 'i') ) over a bound term (rather than a triple pattern). It mirrors logic:termRegex but matches against the value's LANGUAGE TAG rather than its lexical form; it binds nothing, only tests the tag against a literal pattern."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:termHasLang
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "term has language tag"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A language-tag presence relation the procedural-constraint SPARQL fragment lowers to a unary FILTER ( LANG(x) != \"\" ) over a bound term. The companion to logic:termLangMatches: it restricts a language-tag check to genuinely tagged literals, so a plain or typed literal is never swept into a tag-pattern violation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .
logic:onClass
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "on class"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The class parameter of a canonical logic: construct. On compact constraint sugar it names the focus class whose instances the expanded logic:Constraint ranges over (rdf:type(this, C) becomes the range-restricting universal guard); on a qualified logic:Restriction it names the class whose values are counted. The latter use lowers to OWL onClass."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:choicePredicate
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "choice predicate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A member predicate of a logic:ChoiceGroupConstraint's choice group. Multi-valued (at least two); the ∃-requiredness of each is combined per logic:choiceMode."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:choiceMode
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "choice mode"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The cardinality mode of a logic:ChoiceGroupConstraint: 'exactly-one', 'at-most-one', or 'exactly-one-of-N' (an alias of 'exactly-one')."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:trigger
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "trigger"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The trigger predicate of a logic:GuardedImplicationConstraint: the antecedent condition (optionally pinned to a logic:triggerValue) that, together with the target class, guards the required companion."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:triggerValue
    a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "trigger value"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "An optional fixed object value that pins a logic:GuardedImplicationConstraint's trigger to trigger(this, value) rather than the mere presence of the trigger predicate. An IRI or a literal."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:requires
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "requires"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A required companion predicate of a logic:GuardedImplicationConstraint: when the guard holds, ∃ a value on this predicate is required. Multi-valued (a conjunction of existentials)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:anyOf
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "any of"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A member predicate of a logic:DisjunctiveRequirednessConstraint: at least one of the logic:anyOf predicates must have a value on the focus (a disjunction of existentials)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:valuePath
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "value path"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The path predicate of a logic:PathValueTypeConstraint: every value the focus carries on this predicate must be an instance of logic:valueClass."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:valueClass
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "value class"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The required value class of a logic:PathValueTypeConstraint: the class every value on logic:valuePath must belong to."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:roleA
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "role A"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The first role predicate of a logic:CrossNodeConstraint (paired with logic:roleB under logic:crossMode)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:roleB
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "role B"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The second role predicate of a logic:CrossNodeConstraint (paired with logic:roleA under logic:crossMode)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:crossMode
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "cross mode"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The mode of a logic:CrossNodeConstraint: 'co-occur' (the two roles must be present together) or 'differ' (the two roles must never bind the same value)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:forbiddenPredicate
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "forbidden predicate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The forbidden predicate of a logic:ForbiddenPatternConstraint: the focus must carry no value on this predicate (or, when logic:forbiddenValue is present, not that specific value)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:forbiddenValue
    a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "forbidden value"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "An optional fixed object value that narrows a logic:ForbiddenPatternConstraint to forbid only forbiddenPredicate(this, value) rather than any value on the predicate. An IRI or a literal."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:minInclusiveBound
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "min inclusive bound"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The inclusive lower literal bound of a logic:ValueRangeConstraint: every value on the constraint's logic:valuePath must be greater than or equal to this literal (projected to sh:minInclusive on the target class shape). At least one of the two inclusive bounds must be present."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:maxInclusiveBound
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "max inclusive bound"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The inclusive upper literal bound of a logic:ValueRangeConstraint: every value on the constraint's logic:valuePath must be less than or equal to this literal (projected to sh:maxInclusive on the target class shape). At least one of the two inclusive bounds must be present."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:minExclusiveBound
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "min exclusive bound"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The exclusive lower literal bound of a logic:ValueRangeConstraint: every value on the constraint's logic:valuePath must be strictly greater than this literal (projected to sh:minExclusive on the target class shape). The open-bound peer of logic:minInclusiveBound; an inclusive bound wins over the exclusive one on the same endpoint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:maxExclusiveBound
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "max exclusive bound"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The exclusive upper literal bound of a logic:ValueRangeConstraint: every value on the constraint's logic:valuePath must be strictly less than this literal (projected to sh:maxExclusive on the target class shape). The open-bound peer of logic:maxInclusiveBound; an inclusive bound wins over the exclusive one on the same endpoint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:aggFunction
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "aggregate (constraint) function"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The aggregate function of a logic:AggregateConstraint (COUNT, SUM, MIN, MAX) applied over logic:aggPath. Distinct from logic:aggregateFunction, which carries a reduce RULE's head aggregation; this drives a constraint's GROUP BY/HAVING violation SELECT."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:aggDistinct
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "aggregate distinct"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Whether a logic:AggregateConstraint aggregates over DISTINCT values (COUNT(DISTINCT ?x)). Absent means false."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:aggPath
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "aggregate path"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The predicate a logic:AggregateConstraint aggregates over the focus: the objects of $this <aggPath> ?x are the values logic:aggFunction is applied to."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:aggComparator
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "aggregate comparator"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The relational comparator of a logic:AggregateConstraint's invariant (=, !=, <, <=, >, >=; the Unicode ≠/≤/≥ are accepted). The projected sh:SPARQLConstraint HAVING uses the negation of this operator, because a sh:select returns the VIOLATING focus nodes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:aggCompareTo
    a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "aggregate compare to"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The right-hand side a logic:AggregateConstraint's aggregate is compared to: an IRI object is a compared PROPERTY of the focus ($this <prop> ?rhs), and a literal is a fixed comparison value."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:joinPath
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "join path"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The ordered join specification of a logic:JoinAggregateConstraint: an rdf:List of at least two logic:JoinLeg records whose target/source predicates chain the endpoints (leg[k].target is the shared join variable leg[k+1].source re-binds). The list order is SEMANTIC — it is the composition order of the hops — so a joinPath is a traversal specification, not an unordered set of predicates; a single-element list is not a JOIN and is rejected."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:legRecordType
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "leg record type"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The optional class typing the reified relation record a logic:JoinLeg traverses (e.g. an incidence-record class), emitted as an index-anchoring `?record a <Type>` triple in the projected join and a well-formedness guard. Optional: a leg with no logic:legRecordType is bound solely by its role and value predicates. It types the RECORD node, not the endpoints the leg connects."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:legSource
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "leg source predicate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The predicate from a logic:JoinLeg's record to this leg's SOURCE endpoint (record → from). The first leg's source binds to the focus $this; every later leg's source binds to the preceding leg's logic:legTarget (the shared join variable). It names a role PREDICATE the projected SPARQL anchors the join on, never an endpoint node."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:legTarget
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "leg target predicate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The predicate from a logic:JoinLeg's record to this leg's TARGET endpoint (record → to). The intermediate legs' targets are aggregated away (summed over); the LAST leg's target is the far endpoint of the (focus, far-endpoint) group key. It names a role PREDICATE the join binds the next endpoint on, never an endpoint node."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:legValue
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "leg value predicate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The predicate from a logic:JoinLeg's record to the numeric leaf value multiplied into the group product (record → value). For the ∂² check it is the incidence sign; the projected SPARQL computes the PRODUCT of every leg's leaf value inside the aggregate. It names the value PREDICATE, not the value literal itself."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:aggThreshold
    a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "aggregate threshold"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The fixed literal value the group aggregate of a logic:JoinAggregateConstraint is tested against under logic:aggComparator (e.g. 0 for ∂²=0). Always a literal: the (focus, far-endpoint) group key already consumes the far endpoint, so unlike logic:aggCompareTo there is no per-focus property right-hand side. The projected sh:SPARQLConstraint HAVING negates the comparator, because a sh:select returns the VIOLATING groups."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:termEqual
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "term equal"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A comparison relation the procedural-constraint SPARQL fragment lowers to a FILTER ( ?a = ?b ) over two already-bound terms (rather than a triple pattern). Used by the cross-node inequality expansion; it binds nothing, only tests equality."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:termDistinct
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "term distinct"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A comparison relation the procedural-constraint SPARQL fragment lowers to a FILTER ( ?a != ?b ) over two already-bound terms (rather than a triple pattern). The inequality peer of logic:termEqual; it binds nothing, only tests inequality."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:termIn
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "term in value set"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The variadic relation used inside a reified logic:Formula to test whether its first term is a member of the remaining finite value-set arguments. The SHACL lowering realizes it as an sh:in list and the procedural SPARQL lowering as FILTER IN; it binds no term and is never interpreted as an RDF membership assertion."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:membershipGuard logic:termIn ex:status, ex:active, ex:pending ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Argument order is semantic: the tested term precedes the finite set of permitted values."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use inside a reified validation formula for finite set membership."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use as an RDF container-membership or mereological relation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Place the tested term first and the allowed constants in the remaining ordered argument carriers."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:termRegex
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "term regular expression"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The relation used inside a reified logic:Formula to test a term's lexical form against a regular-expression argument. It lowers to SPARQL REGEX and, with an explicit regex-dialect residue, to sh:pattern; it is not an exact-preservation claim because the regex dialects differ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:codeGuard logic:termRegex ex:code, \"^[A-Z]{2}$\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Regex syntax and flags remain target-dialect-sensitive projection residue."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a validation formula tests the lexical form of a bound term."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not claim exact preservation across SPARQL and SHACL regex dialects without a dialect proof."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Supply the tested term and explicit pattern argument, retaining dialect information in the loss ledger."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:directType
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "direct type guard"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A guard-only relation inside a reified constraint formula selecting direct instances of a class while excluding instances reached only through subclasses. It is not a data-triple relation; the SHACL lowering consumes it to build a subclass-excluding sh:SPARQLTarget SELECT."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:focusGuard logic:directType ex:focus, ex:ReviewedClaim ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Direct type means an asserted rdf:type at the focus node, excluding subclass-derived membership."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a constraint must target direct instances rather than the full subclass closure."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use when ordinary entailment-aware class targeting is intended."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Bind the focus term and class term in the guard so the SHACL projection can emit a SPARQL target."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:sparqlTarget
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "SPARQL target guard"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A guard-only relation inside a reified constraint formula whose second argument carries the SELECT text defining a focus set. It is not a data-triple relation; the SHACL lowering consumes it as a first-class sh:SPARQLTarget and removes the guard from the violation body."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:focusGuard logic:sparqlTarget ex:focus, \"SELECT ?this WHERE { ?this ex:reviewed true }\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The SELECT text defines focus selection only and is removed from the projected violation condition."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a constraint's focus set requires a target query rather than a named target class."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not embed the violation test itself in this guard."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Provide a focus variable and SELECT text whose result can be lowered to sh:SPARQLTarget."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:aggregateComparison
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "aggregate comparison"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The reified relation an expanded logic:AggregateConstraint carries as its honest FOL integrity: aggregateComparison(this, function, distinct, path, comparator, rhs). The realized FOL formula core has no aggregate node, so this predication makes the aggregate meaning complete in the FOL canon while the structured satellite drives the real GROUP BY/HAVING SPARQL projection."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:joinAggregateComparison
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "join aggregate comparison"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The reified relation an expanded logic:JoinAggregateConstraint carries as its honest FOL integrity: joinAggregateComparison(this, function, comparator, threshold). The realized FOL formula core has neither an aggregate nor a join node, so this predication makes the multi-hop-join product-aggregate meaning complete in the FOL canon while the structured join-aggregate satellite (the ordered leg chain) drives the real multi-hop-join GROUP BY/HAVING SPARQL projection. It carries only the scalar core, not the leg chain, which lives in the satellite."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:forall
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "for all"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a universal-quantifier logic:Formula to its body formula. The bound variables are carried by logic:quantifiedVariable; their block order is significant. Universal quantification ranges over individuals in the domain of discourse (the object level stays first-order). It is not iteration over RDF serialization order or a higher-order predicate binder."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:exists
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "exists"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates an existential-quantifier logic:Formula to its body formula. The bound variables are carried by logic:quantifiedVariable. Existential witnesses are Skolemized only at the evaluable-lowering layer; the AST node pins the binder and body, not the witness identity."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:and
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "and"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a conjunction logic:Formula to each of its operand sub-formulae (multi-valued, two or more). Conjunction is commutative and associative, so the canonical identity flattens nested conjunctions and is insensitive to operand order. It is not serial composition of transaction programs, which has state-changing path semantics."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:or
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "or"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a disjunction logic:Formula to each of its operand sub-formulae (multi-valued, two or more). Disjunction is commutative and associative, so the canonical identity flattens nested disjunctions and is insensitive to operand order. It is not a program-choice operator and does not select one branch for execution."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:not
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "not"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a strong-negation logic:Formula to its single negated sub-formula. This is explicit (classical) negation and is kept distinct from negation-as-failure, which is a body-literal flag on a derivation rule (logic:negatedBody): the two are never conflated."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:antecedent
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "antecedent"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a material-implication logic:Formula to its antecedent (the hypothesis). Paired with logic:consequent; the two name the implication's operands explicitly so the ordered, non-commutative reading never depends on serialization order."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:consequent
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "consequent"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a material-implication logic:Formula to its consequent (the conclusion). Paired with logic:antecedent; their presence marks the node as an implication, distinct from the commutative logic:iff."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:iff
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "if and only if"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a biconditional logic:Formula to each of its two operand sub-formulae. The biconditional is commutative, so the canonical identity is insensitive to which operand is which — unlike the ordered logic:antecedent / logic:consequent of an implication."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# === Logic glyph-plane enrichment: FOL/DL/modal constants and operators ======
# The nullary truth constants, the derivability/entailment turnstiles, the typed
# standard-translation modal operators, the DL concept-forming operators, the
# lens/correspondence composition operator, the sequent proof-obligation node,
# and the raw Belnap bilattice values that the GMN logic glyph plane renders.
# Reuses the existing FOL connectives (logic:forall / logic:exists / logic:and /
# logic:or / logic:not / logic:antecedent+logic:consequent / logic:iff), the
# subsumption/equivalence edges (logic:subClassOf / logic:equivalentClass), the
# typed accessibility relations, the lens legs (logic:getLeg / logic:putLeg) and
# the Belnap verdicts (logic:InfoSupported / …); it mints only the absent terms.

logic:TruthConstant
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Truth Constant"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed class of the two nullary logical truth constants — logic:Top (⊤, verum) and logic:Bottom (⊥, falsum) — the sentential identities of the propositional fragment. A truth constant is a nullary sentence: it carries NONE of the logic:FormulaConstructorConstraint constructor predicates (logic:and / logic:or / logic:not / logic:antecedent / logic:iff / logic:forall / logic:exists / logic:relation), so it is deliberately NOT a logic:Formula node (which selects exactly one such constructor) and is a distinct, sibling sentential kind — the constructed-formula grammar reaches a truth constant by reference, never by folding a constructor over it. It is DISTINCT from the raw logic:TruthValue algebra values (logic:BelnapTrue …), which are bilattice values rather than sentences, and from the logic:InformationState verdicts, which are result-level grades. The set is closed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:Top
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:TruthConstant ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "top"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The verum truth constant ⊤: the nullary logic:TruthConstant true under every interpretation — the identity a vacuous logic:and folds to. Paired with logic:Bottom (⊥). Because it carries no constructor predicate it is deliberately NOT a constructed logic:Formula node (see logic:TruthConstant). It is NOT a logic:InformationState verdict such as logic:InfoSupported (a result-level Belnap grade, not a sentence), NOT a raw logic:TruthValue such as logic:BelnapTrue (an algebra value, not a sentence), and NOT OWL Thing, the universal class expression rather than a truth-valued sentence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .

logic:Bottom
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:TruthConstant ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "bottom"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The falsum truth constant ⊥: the nullary logic:TruthConstant false under every interpretation — the identity a vacuous logic:or folds to. Paired with logic:Top (⊤). Because it carries no constructor predicate it is deliberately NOT a constructed logic:Formula node (see logic:TruthConstant). It is NOT a logic:InformationState verdict such as logic:InfoOpposed or logic:InfoNeither (result-level grades, not sentences), NOT a raw logic:TruthValue such as logic:BelnapFalse (an algebra value, not a sentence), and NOT OWL Nothing, the empty class expression rather than a truth-valued sentence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .

logic:derives
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "derives"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Syntactic derivability — the proof-theoretic turnstile ⊢: relates a premise context Γ to a logic:Formula obtainable from it by a finite proof in the governing calculus, established by exhibiting a derivation. It is NEVER the semantic logic:entails (⊨): derivability is provability in a fixed calculus while entailment is truth in every model, so the two are DISTINCT relations that MUST NOT be conflated or collapsed onto one edge — soundness and completeness are the separate meta-theorems relating them, per the ISO/IEC 24707 Common Logic model-theoretic semantics against which a calculus is judged. Domain a premise context (a logic:Sequent antecedent), range logic:Formula (stated in prose, matching the kernel convention)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:entails
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "entails"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Semantic entailment — the model-theoretic double turnstile ⊨: relates a premise context Γ to a logic:Formula true in every model of Γ, established by the absence of a countermodel. It is NEVER the syntactic logic:derives (⊢): entailment is truth in every model while derivability is provability in a fixed calculus, so the two are DISTINCT relations that MUST NOT be conflated or collapsed onto one edge — ISO/IEC 24707 Common Logic fixes this model-theoretic notion of entailment, which a sound and complete calculus's derivability then matches. Domain a premise context (a logic:Sequent antecedent), range logic:Formula (stated in prose, matching the kernel convention)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:necessarily
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "necessarily"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The box modality □ as a type-parameterized standard translation: relates a modal logic:Formula to the body formula that must hold in EVERY context accessible under the single typed accessibility relation the node names on logic:overAccessibility — necessity is truth in every accessible context of that declared type (its dual logic:possibly is truth in some). It is NEVER evaluated over the bare logic:accessibleFrom union of the typed accessibility relations, whose generic superproperty licenses no cross-type entailment, so a well-formed box node always pins one typed relation through logic:overAccessibility. It is the object-level standard-translation modal operator over the finite materialized contextual index (standard Kripke standard-translation semantics), DISTINCT from the standpoint-slice claim-modal-force register gmeow:modalForceNecessary / gmeow:modalForcePossible / gmeow:modalForceActual, which grades an assertion's force rather than translating a modal sentence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:possibly
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "possibly"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The diamond modality ◇ as a type-parameterized standard translation: relates a modal logic:Formula to the body formula that must hold in SOME context accessible under the single typed accessibility relation the node names on logic:overAccessibility — possibility is truth in some accessible context of that declared type, the dual of logic:necessarily (truth in every). It is NEVER evaluated over the bare logic:accessibleFrom union of the typed accessibility relations, whose generic superproperty licenses no cross-type entailment, so a well-formed diamond node always pins one typed relation through logic:overAccessibility. It is the object-level standard-translation modal operator over the finite materialized contextual index (standard Kripke standard-translation semantics), DISTINCT from the standpoint-slice claim-modal-force register gmeow:modalForceNecessary / gmeow:modalForcePossible / gmeow:modalForceActual, which grades an assertion's force rather than translating a modal sentence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:overAccessibility
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "over accessibility"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The carrier that types a modal logic:Formula (a logic:necessarily or logic:possibly node) with the ONE typed accessibility relation its standard translation is evaluated over — one of logic:epistemicallyPossible, logic:doxasticallyAccessible, logic:deonticallyIdeal, logic:temporallySucceeds, logic:counterfactuallyCloser, or gmeow:sharpens. Its range is the typed accessibility relations, NOT the bare logic:accessibleFrom union: a modal node evaluated over the blurred superproperty would manufacture the cross-type entailment the kernel forbids, so this edge pins the single type. Domain a modal logic:Formula, range a typed accessibility relation (stated in prose, matching the kernel convention)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:conceptIntersection
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "concept intersection"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The Description Logic concept-intersection constructor ⊓ (Concept × Concept → Concept): relates a conjunction concept expression to each operand concept its instances must simultaneously satisfy, so its extension is the intersection of the operands'. A concept-FORMING operator that builds a new complex concept, paired with logic:conceptUnion (⊔). It is NOT the sentence connective logic:and (which conjoins logic:Formula truth values, not concepts) and NOT logic:subClassOf or logic:equivalentClass (which ASSERT a relation between concepts rather than forming one). Domain and range concept expressions (stated in prose); the Description Logic Handbook fixes the constructor's semantics."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:conceptUnion
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "concept union"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The Description Logic concept-union constructor ⊔ (Concept × Concept → Concept): relates a disjunction concept expression to each operand concept, so its extension is the union of the operands' extensions. A concept-FORMING operator that builds a new complex concept, paired with logic:conceptIntersection (⊓). It is NOT the sentence connective logic:or (which disjoins logic:Formula truth values, not concepts) and NOT logic:subClassOf or logic:equivalentClass (which ASSERT a relation between concepts rather than forming one). Domain and range concept expressions (stated in prose); the Description Logic Handbook fixes the constructor's semantics."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:compose
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "compose"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Lens / correspondence composition ∘: relates a composite logic:Lens to the component lenses whose logic:getLeg and logic:putLeg legs it sequences — the operator behind the u ∘ d = id round-trip family, where composing a down-projection with its up-projection witnesses a section/retraction law, and where composition can only weaken the logic:morphismClass rung, never strengthen it. It is a DISTINCT concept from math:functionComposition (the mathematical composition of two functions) to which it is related only by a separately authored coherence law; the two MUST NOT be conflated, since a lens leg is a logic:TransactionProgram over path semantics, not a bare function. Domain and range logic:Lens (stated in prose, matching the kernel convention)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:Sequent
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Sequent"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A proof-obligation node Γ ⊢ φ: a reified judgment pairing an antecedent context Γ (its premise formulae, carried by logic:sequentContext) with a single succedent logic:Formula φ (its conclusion, carried by logic:sequentSuccedent). It is the unit a logic:derives claim is checked against — the syntactic obligation whose discharge is a proof — DISTINCT from a logic:Formula (a sentence, not an obligation) and from a logic:Rule (a productive Horn+NAF derivation, not a one-off proof goal). Its turnstile is the syntactic logic:derives (⊢); the matching semantic obligation reads with logic:entails (⊨)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:sequentContext
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Sequent ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "sequent context"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Sequent to a premise formula in its antecedent context Γ — multi-valued, since a context is a set (or, in a substructural calculus, an ordered multiset) of premises. The antecedent side of the Γ ⊢ φ obligation, paired with the single succedent logic:sequentSuccedent. Domain logic:Sequent, range logic:Formula."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:sequentSuccedent
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Sequent ;
    rdfs:range logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "sequent succedent"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:Sequent to its single succedent logic:Formula φ — the conclusion the obligation Γ ⊢ φ must discharge. Single-valued, the counterpart of the multi-valued logic:sequentContext. Domain logic:Sequent, range logic:Formula."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:TruthValue
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Truth Value"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed class of the four RAW truth values of the logic:BelnapBilattice algebra: logic:BelnapTrue, logic:BelnapFalse, logic:BelnapBoth (glut) and logic:BelnapNeither (gap). These are the algebra's carrier values, each bound to the logic:InformationState verdict that reports it through logic:realizedAsVerdict so there is no floating duplicate canon. They are DISTINCT from the logic:InformationState verdicts, which are result-level fields carrying two extra non-results (logic:InfoUndetermined, logic:InfoNotEvaluated) the raw algebra has no value for, and DISTINCT from the classical truth constants logic:Top / logic:Bottom (logic:TruthConstant), which are nullary sentences rather than algebra values. The set is closed."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:realizedAsVerdict
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:TruthValue ;
    rdfs:range logic:InformationState ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "realized as verdict"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a raw logic:TruthValue of the logic:BelnapBilattice to the logic:InformationState verdict that reports it at the result level — logic:BelnapTrue to logic:InfoSupported, logic:BelnapFalse to logic:InfoOpposed, logic:BelnapBoth to logic:InfoBoth, logic:BelnapNeither to logic:InfoNeither. It binds the algebra value to its verdict so the two never float as duplicate canon; it stops at the four Belnap grades and never reaches the non-results logic:InfoUndetermined or logic:InfoNotEvaluated, which the raw algebra has no value for. Domain logic:TruthValue, range logic:InformationState."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:BelnapTrue
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:TruthValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Belnap true"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The raw logic:BelnapBilattice value true — told true and not told false. The algebra value that logic:InfoSupported reports at the result level; DISTINCT from that verdict and from the truth constant logic:Top. Bound to its verdict by logic:realizedAsVerdict."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:realizedAsVerdict logic:InfoSupported ;
    gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .

logic:BelnapFalse
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:TruthValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Belnap false"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The raw logic:BelnapBilattice value false — told false and not told true. The algebra value that logic:InfoOpposed reports at the result level; DISTINCT from that verdict and from the truth constant logic:Bottom. Bound to its verdict by logic:realizedAsVerdict."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:realizedAsVerdict logic:InfoOpposed ;
    gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .

logic:BelnapBoth
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:TruthValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Belnap both"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The raw logic:BelnapBilattice value both — the over-determined glut, told true AND told false. The algebra value that the conclusive logic:InfoBoth reports at the result level; DISTINCT from that verdict and never globalized to OWL Nothing. Bound to its verdict by logic:realizedAsVerdict."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:realizedAsVerdict logic:InfoBoth ;
    gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .

logic:BelnapNeither
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:TruthValue ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Belnap neither"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The raw logic:BelnapBilattice value neither — the under-determined gap, told neither true nor false. The algebra value that the conclusive logic:InfoNeither reports at the result level; DISTINCT from that verdict and from the non-results logic:InfoUndetermined and logic:InfoNotEvaluated, which are not raw algebra values. Bound to its verdict by logic:realizedAsVerdict."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:realizedAsVerdict logic:InfoNeither ;
    gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .

# --- GMN glyph-plane in-scope coverage markers on the reused canonical terms --
# The newly minted glyph-plane terms above carry gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope inline;
# these standalone triples mark the reused pre-existing canonical terms in scope
# for the plane, and rule the two non-result InformationStates as named-key /
# not-a-gap so a coverage anti-join does not false-fire on them.
logic:forall gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .
logic:exists gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .
logic:and gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .
logic:or gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .
logic:not gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .
logic:antecedent gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .
logic:consequent gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .
logic:iff gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .
logic:subClassOf gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .
logic:equivalentClass gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .
logic:evidenceStrength gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .
logic:InfoSupported gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .
logic:InfoOpposed gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .
logic:InfoBoth gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .
logic:InfoNeither gmeow:gmnGlyphInScope true .
logic:InfoUndetermined gmeow:gmnGlyphNamedKeyRuled true .
logic:InfoNotEvaluated gmeow:gmnGlyphNamedKeyRuled true .

# === Modal-evaluation carriers: the all-IRI frame the reasoner's =============
# === evaluate_modal_formulas post-pass reads, and the verdict edges it =======
# === derives. The modal operators (□ logic:necessarily / ◇ logic:possibly) ===
# === are the standard translation over ONE pinned typed accessibility ========
# === relation (logic:overAccessibility), bounded over the finite =============
# === materialized named-graph world set — never over the bare ================
# === logic:accessibleFrom superproperty. The reasoner all-IRI contract =======
# === forbids the blank-node logic:argument body representation, so a modal ===
# === body's ground atom is pinned all-IRI via logic:atomSubject / ============
# === atomPredicate / atomObject. Normative in design/LOGIC-FOUNDATION.md. ====

logic:actualWorld
    a logic:PossibleWorld ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "actual world"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The shared base logic:PossibleWorld — the evaluation world from which a modal claim is read when no other is pinned. It is one materialized context of the finite world set the modal operators quantify over, never the unbounded space of all logically possible worlds."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:modalEvalWorld
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "modal evaluation world"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Carries the evaluation world w0 on a modal logic:Formula node: the world from which its □ / ◇ is read, i.e. the source of the pinned typed accessibility relation. Single-valued; the reasoner's modal post-pass hard-fails a modal formula that carries none or several. The typed relation itself is pinned by logic:overAccessibility."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:atomSubject
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "atom subject"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Binds the subject of the ground body atom P = (s, p, o) on a modal body logic:Formula node. All-IRI, because the reasoner's all-IRI contract forbids the blank-node logic:argument body representation: a modal body is scoped to a single ground atom, pinned by logic:atomSubject / logic:atomPredicate / logic:atomObject. Single-valued."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:atomPredicate
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "atom predicate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Binds the predicate of the ground body atom P = (s, p, o) on a modal body logic:Formula node. Companion of logic:atomSubject / logic:atomObject; the three pin a modal body's single ground atom all-IRI. Single-valued."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:atomObject
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "atom object"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Binds the object of the ground body atom P = (s, p, o) on a modal body logic:Formula node. Companion of logic:atomSubject / logic:atomPredicate; the three pin a modal body's single ground atom all-IRI. Single-valued."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:modalNecessityHolds
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "modal necessity holds"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Reasoner-derived verdict edge from a □ modal logic:Formula to its body: the necessity holds — the body atom is present in every world accessible from w0 over the pinned typed relation (or the accessible set is empty under a non-serial relation, a vacuous hold). Emitted by the native modal-evaluation post-pass, not hand-authored."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:modalNecessityFails
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "modal necessity fails"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Reasoner-derived verdict edge from a □ modal logic:Formula to its body: the necessity fails — some world accessible from w0 over the pinned typed relation lacks the body atom. The least such world is recorded by a companion logic:modalCounterexampleWorld row. Emitted by the native modal-evaluation post-pass, not hand-authored."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:modalNecessityUndetermined
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "modal necessity undetermined"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Reasoner-derived verdict edge from a □ modal logic:Formula to its body: the necessity is undetermined — the pinned typed relation is the SERIAL logic:deonticallyIdeal and its accessible set is empty, so the 'holds in every ideal world' condition is not vacuously true but undetermined. Emitted by the native modal-evaluation post-pass, not hand-authored."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:modalPossibilityHolds
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "modal possibility holds"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Reasoner-derived verdict edge from a ◇ modal logic:Formula to its body: the possibility holds — the body atom is present in at least one world accessible from w0 over the pinned typed relation. Emitted by the native modal-evaluation post-pass, not hand-authored."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:modalPossibilityFails
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "modal possibility fails"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Reasoner-derived verdict edge from a ◇ modal logic:Formula to its body: the possibility fails — no world accessible from w0 over the pinned typed relation carries the body atom. Emitted by the native modal-evaluation post-pass, not hand-authored."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:modalCounterexampleWorld
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "modal counterexample world"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Reasoner-derived companion of a logic:modalNecessityFails verdict: names the least (lexicographically smallest) world accessible from w0 over the pinned typed relation in which the body atom is absent — the witness that refutes the necessity. Emitted by the native modal-evaluation post-pass, not hand-authored."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:relation
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:range logic:Type ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "relation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates an atomic-predication logic:Formula to the relation it predicates, over its ordered logic:argument terms. The relation is reified as a logic:Type individual per the HiLog reflection (see logic:instanceOf and logic:orderedType): predicating over a reified relation individual keeps the object level first-order rather than admitting a predicate variable, so no separate relation or predicate class is minted."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:functionSymbol
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:FunctionTerm ;
    rdfs:range logic:Type ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "function symbol"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:FunctionTerm to the single function symbol it applies, over its ordered logic:argument terms. The symbol is reified as a logic:Type individual, exactly mirroring how logic:relation reifies an atomic predication's relation (the HiLog reflection — see logic:instanceOf and logic:orderedType): applying a reified function-symbol individual keeps the object level first-order rather than admitting a function variable, so no separate function-symbol class is minted. Functional: a function term applies exactly one symbol."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:argument
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:ArgumentStructure ;
    rdfs:range logic:TermCarrier ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "argument"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:ArgumentStructure — an atomic-predication logic:Formula (over logic:relation) or a logic:FunctionTerm (over logic:functionSymbol) — to one of its argument term-carriers. The SAME property carries both, so a function term's operands reuse the predication argument machinery. Each carrier fixes its position with logic:termIndex and its value with exactly one term-value property (logic:termIri, logic:termVariable, logic:termLiteral, logic:termSequenceMarker, or logic:termApplication), so a variadic argument list — including sequence markers and nested compound function terms — round-trips with its order and term kinds intact. It is not a direct link to the term value and carries no ordering by triple position."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:quantifiedVariable
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:range logic:TermCarrier ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "quantified variable"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a quantifier logic:Formula to one of its bound-variable term-carriers. Each carrier fixes its position in the binder block with logic:termIndex and its name with logic:termVariable; block order is significant, because bound-variable renaming preserves meaning but reordering a quantifier prefix does not."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:termIri
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:TermCarrier ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "term IRI"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Carries an IRI-constant term value on an argument term-carrier: the argument is an individual (or a reified relation or type) named by this IRI. One of the four mutually exclusive term-value properties; exactly one is present on each carrier. It is not a variable name, lexical literal, or sequence-marker string."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:termIndex
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:TermCarrier ;
    rdfs:range xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "term index"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The zero-based ordinal of a term-carrier within its ordered list — an argument within an atomic predication, or a bound variable within a quantifier block. Fixing position explicitly makes the variadic, order-significant lists round-trip independent of RDF statement order. It is not an RDF statement position, which has no graph semantics."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:termVariable
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:TermCarrier ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "term variable"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Carries a variable term value (the authored variable name, without a sigil) on a term-carrier: a single-term variable, bound by an enclosing quantifier or free. One of the four mutually exclusive term-value properties. It is not an IRI constant or a variadic sequence marker."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:termLiteral
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:TermCarrier ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "term literal"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Carries a data-literal term value (its lexical form) on an argument term-carrier; the term's datatype rides on the companion logic:termLiteralDatatype. One of the four mutually exclusive term-value properties. It is not an IRI constant, variable, or datatype declaration by itself."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:termLiteralDatatype
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:TermCarrier ;
    rdfs:range rdfs:Datatype ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "term literal datatype"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Carries the datatype IRI of a literal term value on an argument term-carrier that also bears logic:termLiteral. Absent for a plain literal. Held separately from the literal so the typed-literal term round-trips losslessly regardless of how an intermediate reader handles literal datatypes. It is not the lexical literal and cannot occur on a non-literal carrier."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:termSequenceMarker
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:TermCarrier ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "term sequence marker"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Carries a sequence-marker term value (the authored marker name) on an argument term-carrier: the argument is a logic:SequenceMarker binding a sequence of terms rather than a single one. One of the five mutually exclusive term-value properties."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:termApplication
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain logic:TermCarrier ;
    rdfs:range logic:FunctionTerm ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "term application"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Carries a compound function-term value on an argument term-carrier: the argument is the logic:FunctionTerm this property points at (a function symbol applied to ordered sub-terms), rather than a single atomic value. The fifth of the five mutually exclusive term-value properties; exactly one is present on each carrier. Because a logic:FunctionTerm's own logic:argument carriers may again bear logic:termApplication, a nested compound term round-trips recursively. It is not an IRI constant, variable, literal, or sequence marker — those are single-node values, while this points at a sub-tree."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# The ontology-level well-formedness surface mirrors the Rust frontend's typed checks. The
# choice constraints make constructor/value exclusivity executable in the projected validation
# graph; the paired implications make material implication indivisible; functional-property
# declarations above cap singleton values. Ordered-list uniqueness, contiguity, recursive-cycle
# rejection, and connective arity are checked by the Rust typed-IR parser because they compare a
# whole RDF subgraph rather than one focus property in isolation.

logic:FormulaConstructorConstraint
    a logic:ChoiceGroupConstraint ;
    logic:choiceMode "exactly-one" ;
    logic:choicePredicate logic:and ,
        logic:antecedent ,
        logic:exists ,
        logic:forall ,
        logic:iff ,
        logic:necessarily ,
        logic:not ,
        logic:or ,
        logic:possibly ,
        logic:relation ;
    logic:formalizes logic:Formula ;
    logic:onClass logic:Formula ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Formula Constructor Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Requires every logic:Formula to select exactly one constructor family: atomic relation, strong negation, conjunction, disjunction, material implication (keyed by logic:antecedent), biconditional, universal quantification, existential quantification, necessity, or possibility. A modal necessity/possibility node is a constructed formula (a compound sentence over its operand formula, unlike the nullary logic:TruthConstant siblings ⊤/⊥ which carry no constructor), so it selects the logic:necessarily or logic:possibly constructor. It does not choose among serialized operands; it prevents one node from having two incompatible logical meanings."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "A node with both logic:relation and logic:not violates this constraint because it cannot be both an atom and a negation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use this constraint to limit how many operands a selected constructor carries; arity is a separate typed-IR invariant."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Validate every authored formula tree before lowering it; reject a focus node unless exactly one listed constructor predicate is present."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when validating canonical RDF formula trees before parsing, canonicalization, or projection."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ImplicationConsequentConstraint
    a logic:GuardedImplicationConstraint ;
    logic:formalizes logic:Formula ;
    logic:onClass logic:Formula ;
    logic:requires logic:consequent ;
    logic:trigger logic:antecedent ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Implication Consequent Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Requires a logic:Formula carrying logic:antecedent to carry logic:consequent as the ordered second operand of the same material implication. It is not a rule that derives a consequent; it is a structural integrity condition on the implication node."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "An implication node with an antecedent atom but no consequent formula violates this constraint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not read the required edge as logical modus ponens; it only completes the serialized constructor."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "When logic:antecedent is present, require one resource-valued logic:consequent before constructing Formula::Implies."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when validating the ordered pair that encodes material implication."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ImplicationAntecedentConstraint
    a logic:GuardedImplicationConstraint ;
    logic:formalizes logic:Formula ;
    logic:onClass logic:Formula ;
    logic:requires logic:antecedent ;
    logic:trigger logic:consequent ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Implication Antecedent Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Requires a logic:Formula carrying logic:consequent to carry logic:antecedent as the ordered first operand of the same material implication. It is not an inverse entailment from conclusion to premise; it is the reverse structural completeness check for the serialized pair."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "A formula node with a consequent formula but no antecedent formula violates this constraint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use the constraint to infer that a consequent semantically entails its antecedent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "When logic:consequent is present, require one resource-valued logic:antecedent before constructing Formula::Implies."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use with logic:ImplicationConsequentConstraint to validate both halves of an implication."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ModalNecessityAccessibilityConstraint
    a logic:GuardedImplicationConstraint ;
    logic:formalizes logic:Formula ;
    logic:onClass logic:Formula ;
    logic:requires logic:overAccessibility ;
    logic:trigger logic:necessarily ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Modal Necessity Accessibility Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Requires a logic:Formula carrying logic:necessarily (a box modal node) to pin its standard translation to a logic:overAccessibility relation. Without one declared typed accessibility relation the box has no world set to quantify over, so the necessity claim is undefined; this structural integrity condition forbids the untyped box. Paired with logic:ModalPossibilityAccessibilityConstraint for the diamond, and bounded above by the at-most-one characteristic logic:overAccessibilityFunctionality so a modal node pins EXACTLY one; the value-set (one of the six typed accessibility relations, never the blurred logic:accessibleFrom union or a modal-force value) is enforced by the modal-accessibility-typed verify gate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "A logic:necessarily node carrying no logic:overAccessibility violates this constraint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not read the required edge as evaluating the modal; it only completes the typed-modal constructor."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "When logic:necessarily is present, require one resource-valued logic:overAccessibility naming a typed accessibility relation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when validating that a box modal node is typed by an accessibility relation."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ModalPossibilityAccessibilityConstraint
    a logic:GuardedImplicationConstraint ;
    logic:formalizes logic:Formula ;
    logic:onClass logic:Formula ;
    logic:requires logic:overAccessibility ;
    logic:trigger logic:possibly ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Modal Possibility Accessibility Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Requires a logic:Formula carrying logic:possibly (a diamond modal node) to pin its standard translation to a logic:overAccessibility relation. Without one declared typed accessibility relation the diamond has no world set to quantify over, so the possibility claim is undefined; this structural integrity condition forbids the untyped diamond. The dual of logic:ModalNecessityAccessibilityConstraint; the value-set of the relation is enforced by the modal-accessibility-typed verify gate."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "A logic:possibly node carrying no logic:overAccessibility violates this constraint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not read the required edge as evaluating the modal; it only completes the typed-modal constructor."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "When logic:possibly is present, require one resource-valued logic:overAccessibility naming a typed accessibility relation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when validating that a diamond modal node is typed by an accessibility relation."@x-gmeow-english .

# A modal logic:Formula pins EXACTLY one typed accessibility relation: presence is
# required of a modal node by the two guarded modal constraints above, and the
# at-most-one half is carried by logic:overAccessibilityFunctionality — a property
# characteristic the native reasoner decides, NOT an unqualified cardinality
# restriction, which is out of the decided fragment. A non-modal formula carries zero
# logic:overAccessibility, which the characteristic permits.

logic:TermCarrierValueConstraint
    a logic:ChoiceGroupConstraint ;
    logic:choiceMode "exactly-one" ;
    logic:choicePredicate logic:termApplication ,
        logic:termIri ,
        logic:termLiteral ,
        logic:termSequenceMarker ,
        logic:termVariable ;
    logic:formalizes logic:TermCarrier ;
    logic:onClass logic:TermCarrier ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Term Carrier Value Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Requires every logic:TermCarrier to carry exactly one term-value kind: IRI, variable, literal, sequence marker, or compound function-term application. It does not constrain the carrier's ordinal; logic:termIndex and list-level contiguity are checked independently."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "A carrier with both logic:termIri ex:a and logic:termVariable 'a' violates this constraint even though the spellings resemble one another; a carrier with both logic:termApplication and logic:termVariable violates it too."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not collapse IRI, variable, literal, sequence-marker, and application values into a shared field; their kinds affect formula meaning."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Reject a carrier unless exactly one of the five term-value predicates has exactly one value."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use whenever an RDF argument or quantifier-binding carrier is reconstructed as a typed IR term."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:FunctionSymbolConstraint
    a logic:GuardedImplicationConstraint ;
    logic:formalizes logic:FunctionTerm ;
    logic:onClass logic:FunctionTerm ;
    logic:requires logic:functionSymbol ;
    logic:trigger rdf:type ;
    logic:triggerValue logic:FunctionTerm ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Function Symbol Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Requires every declared logic:FunctionTerm to carry a logic:functionSymbol. Paired with the OWL FunctionalProperty declaration on logic:functionSymbol (which caps it at one), it makes a function term bear EXACTLY one reified function symbol. It is not the argument-list check: a function term's operands are separate logic:argument term-carriers."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "A logic:FunctionTerm with two logic:argument carriers but no logic:functionSymbol violates this constraint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not infer a function symbol from an argument; a symbol-less function term is malformed, not defaulted."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Require exactly one logic:Type-valued logic:functionSymbol on each function term before constructing Term::App."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when validating a compound function-term node reached through a carrier's logic:termApplication."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:FunctionTermArityConstraint
    a logic:GuardedImplicationConstraint ;
    logic:formalizes logic:FunctionTerm ;
    logic:onClass logic:FunctionTerm ;
    logic:requires logic:argument ;
    logic:trigger rdf:type ;
    logic:triggerValue logic:FunctionTerm ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Function Term Arity Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Requires every declared logic:FunctionTerm to carry at least one logic:argument term-carrier: a nullary application is forbidden, because a 0-ary function symbol is a constant and belongs on a carrier's logic:termIri, so an empty-argument function term would give one constant two spellings. It is not the ordinal check: zero-based uniqueness and contiguity of the argument carriers are verified across all siblings by the typed-IR frontend."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "A logic:FunctionTerm with a logic:functionSymbol but no logic:argument violates this constraint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use a function term to wrap a constant; carry the constant IRI directly on logic:termIri instead."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Require one or more logic:argument carriers on each function term, then validate the sibling index set as 0 through n minus 1."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when validating a compound function-term node reached through a carrier's logic:termApplication."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ReasoningProgramClauseConstraint
    a logic:GuardedImplicationConstraint ;
    logic:formalizes logic:ReasoningProgram ;
    logic:onClass logic:ReasoningProgram ;
    logic:requires logic:clause ;
    logic:trigger rdf:type ;
    logic:triggerValue logic:ReasoningProgram ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Reasoning Program Clause Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Requires every declared logic:ReasoningProgram to carry at least one logic:clause. A program with no clauses has nothing to resolve its goal against and is malformed. It is not the query check: the required goal atom is validated separately by logic:ReasoningProgramQueryConstraint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "A logic:ReasoningProgram with a logic:programQuery but no logic:clause violates this constraint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use this constraint to check clause well-formedness; each clause's own logic:Formula constructor-selection constraints apply independently."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Require one or more logic:Formula-valued logic:clause edges on each declared reasoning program before constructing its clause set."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when validating an authored logic:ReasoningProgram before compiling it to the native engine's clause set."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ReasoningProgramQueryConstraint
    a logic:GuardedImplicationConstraint ;
    logic:formalizes logic:ReasoningProgram ;
    logic:onClass logic:ReasoningProgram ;
    logic:requires logic:programQuery ;
    logic:trigger rdf:type ;
    logic:triggerValue logic:ReasoningProgram ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Reasoning Program Query Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Requires every declared logic:ReasoningProgram to carry a logic:programQuery. Paired with the OWL FunctionalProperty declaration on logic:programQuery (which caps it at one), it makes a program bear EXACTLY one goal atom. It is not the clause-set check: a program's facts and rules are validated separately by logic:ReasoningProgramClauseConstraint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "A logic:ReasoningProgram with logic:clause facts but no logic:programQuery violates this constraint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not infer a default goal from the clause set; a query-less program is malformed, not defaulted to some implicit answer atom."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Require exactly one logic:Formula-valued logic:programQuery on each declared reasoning program before constructing its goal."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when validating an authored logic:ReasoningProgram before compiling it to the native engine's goal resolution."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:TermCarrierIndexConstraint
    a logic:GuardedImplicationConstraint ;
    logic:formalizes logic:TermCarrier ;
    logic:onClass logic:TermCarrier ;
    logic:requires logic:termIndex ;
    logic:trigger rdf:type ;
    logic:triggerValue logic:TermCarrier ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Term Carrier Index Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Requires every declared logic:TermCarrier to carry a logic:termIndex. It is not the list-level ordering check: uniqueness and zero-based contiguity are verified across all sibling carriers by the typed-IR frontend."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "A logic:TermCarrier with logic:termVariable 'x' but no logic:termIndex violates this constraint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not infer an omitted index from RDF statement order, which has no semantic ordering."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Require one non-negative integer index on each carrier, then validate the sibling index set as 0 through n minus 1."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when validating ordered argument lists and quantifier binding blocks."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:LiteralDatatypeConstraint
    a logic:GuardedImplicationConstraint ;
    logic:formalizes logic:TermCarrier ;
    logic:onClass logic:TermCarrier ;
    logic:requires logic:termLiteral ;
    logic:trigger logic:termLiteralDatatype ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Literal Datatype Constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Requires a carrier with logic:termLiteralDatatype to carry logic:termLiteral, so a datatype IRI can never float without the lexical literal it types. It does not require a datatype for every literal; absence denotes a plain literal."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "A carrier with logic:termLiteralDatatype xsd:integer but no logic:termLiteral violates this constraint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not attach logic:termLiteralDatatype to an IRI, variable, or sequence-marker carrier."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxCBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Accept zero or one datatype IRI only on a carrier whose sole value kind is logic:termLiteral."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when reconstructing typed literal terms from their lexical and datatype components."@x-gmeow-english .

# ── Typed-IR exemplar annotation coat ────────────────────────────────────────
# These annotations teach the IR through decisions and executable examples. They are deliberately
# term-specific: no inventory prose and no generic "this term means X" placeholders.

logic:DischargeCondition
    skos:example "Use logic:DischargeFiniteClosure when a completed finite chase makes absence of the forbidden predicate decidable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not claim a discharge condition merely because a search timed out or found no witness."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Select the narrowest condition the checker actually implements, and retain logic:ObligationUnknown outside that boundary."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to state why a non-entailment or law check can return a conclusive verdict."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:DischargeVerdict
    skos:example "A bounded check that exhausts its declared corpus without deriving the forbidden predicate returns logic:ObligationDischarged."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use logic:ObligationDischarged as a synonym for 'not observed yet'."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Pair the verdict with the condition and evidence that delimit its conclusiveness."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the three-way outcome of an executable obligation or lens-law discharge."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:Formula
    skos:example "The tree for every person knows someone is a logic:forall node whose body is a logic:exists node containing a logic:relation atom."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not encode a trivially Horn binary fact as a top-level formula; place it in the axiom or rule collection."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Choose one constructor family, link its sub-formulae, and place every argument or binding in an indexed logic:TermCarrier."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for first-order quantifier or connective structure that cannot live uniquely as a Horn fact or rule."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:FormulaShape
    skos:example "A formula containing logic:or receives logic:Disjunctive in a Horn projection's residue ledger."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not treat a formula-shape tag as an evaluation profile or as the formula's truth value."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach every applicable closed-set tag to the projection loss row for the unsupported formula."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to disclose which first-order construct exceeded a target fragment."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:Lens
    skos:example "ex:alignment logic:hasLens ex:roundTripLens . ex:roundTripLens logic:getLeg ex:downProjection ; logic:putLeg ex:upProjection ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not call an unpaired one-way transform a lens or infer put by naively reversing get."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Provide typed get and put transaction programs, then discharge only the laws their evidence supports."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use as the executable bidirectional core of a logic:Correspondence."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:Module
    skos:example "A Common Logic theory named ex:geometry can import ex:orders while remaining in the same epistemic world."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use a module IRI as a world, standpoint, provenance agent, or source document."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Assign statement reifiers with logic:inModule and connect theory dependencies with logic:imports."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to preserve named-theory membership and import structure across Common Logic dialects."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ModuleContextAxis
    skos:example "One result can be indexed simultaneously by ex:geometry as its module and ex:analystView as its standpoint."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not replace the world or standpoint axis with the module axis; the dimensions coexist."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Include this facet when a reasoning contract must preserve theory membership independently of epistemic context."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to declare module membership as one coordinate of the multi-axis context model."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:ProjectionTarget
    skos:example "A contract may request both logic:CanonicalProjection and logic:CommonLogicProjection for one canonical answer."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use a projection target to select semantics or suppress unsupported canonical content."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Name each requested rendering surface and preserve a loss judgment for constructs it cannot express."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to request one or more output renderings from the same reasoning result."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:RelationalCore
    skos:example "A fixed-arity atom lowers to logic:instanceOf plus logic:naryArg0 through logic:naryArg2 before the native chase evaluates it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not force disjunctive or genuinely variadic formula structure into the relational core."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Legalize the Horn-expressible fragment into typed rules and carry every illegal remainder with its preservation judgment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use as the stable logical-to-physical waist for chase, incremental, and semiring execution."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:Rule
    skos:example "A rule can derive ex:ancestor(x,z) from ex:parent(x,y) and ex:ancestor(y,z), with its variables bound positively in the body."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not encode disjunction, strong negation, or an unstratified negative cycle as a Horn rule."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Provide one head, the positive and optional stratified-negative body atoms, and any explicit guards or aggregate specification."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for productive Horn, existential-rule, or stratified-negation derivations."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:SequenceMarker
    skos:example "ex:restCarrier logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termSequenceMarker \"rest\" ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not substitute a sequence marker for an ordinary variable that binds exactly one term."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Carry the marker name through logic:termSequenceMarker on a single indexed term carrier."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a genuinely variadic argument sequence that fixed positional predicates cannot represent."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:TermCarrier
    skos:example "An atom's second argument can be represented by a carrier with logic:termIndex 1 and logic:termVariable 'object'."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not place two value kinds on one carrier or infer its position from Turtle serialization order."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Assign one contiguous zero-based index and one of the four exclusive term-value predicates."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for each ordered atom argument and each ordered quantifier binding."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:Type
    skos:example "logic:Kind is reified as a logic:Type so it can classify entity types through logic:instanceOf."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use logic:Type as OWL punning or assume every OWL class participates in the deep-instantiation tower."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Reify a universal, state its order where needed, and connect its instances through logic:instanceOf."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a universal must itself be quantified over, classified, or placed at a higher type order."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:and
    skos:example "A conjunction node can link the atoms ex:red(x) and ex:round(x) through two logic:and edges."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use logic:and for serial program composition or with fewer than two operands."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Link at least two formula operands; canonicalization flattens nested conjunctions and sorts their keys."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when every operand formula must hold at the same logical point."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:antecedent
    skos:example "In person(x) implies mortal(x), the person atom is the sole logic:antecedent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use logic:antecedent without a paired logic:consequent or as an unordered biconditional operand."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Place exactly one hypothesis formula on the implication node and pair it with exactly one consequent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the ordered hypothesis side of material implication."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:argument
    skos:example "The atom ex:knows(alice,bob) uses carriers at indices 0 and 1 linked through logic:argument."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not link a raw term value directly or rely on RDF edge order to recover argument order."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Link each atomic formula to a logic:TermCarrier whose index set is unique and contiguous from zero."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to supply the ordered terms of a reified atomic predication."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:consequent
    skos:example "In person(x) implies mortal(x), the mortal atom is the sole logic:consequent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use logic:consequent without a paired logic:antecedent or treat it as a derived rule head."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Place exactly one conclusion formula on the implication node after its exactly one antecedent."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the ordered conclusion side of material implication."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:exists
    skos:example "An existential node can bind y and point logic:exists to the atom ex:knows(x,y)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not put a Skolem witness on the formula node or omit the indexed bound-variable block."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Link exactly one body formula and one or more contiguous logic:quantifiedVariable carriers."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when at least one assignment to the bound first-order variables must satisfy the body."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:forall
    skos:example "A universal node can bind x and point logic:forall to person(x) implies mortal(x)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use an empty binder or read universal quantification as iteration over serialized RDF nodes."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Link exactly one body and declare every bound variable in a unique contiguous carrier block."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the body must hold for every assignment to its first-order bound variables."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:iff
    skos:example "A definition can connect triangle(x) and polygonWithThreeSides(x) as the two logic:iff operands."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use logic:iff with other than two operands or when only one implication direction is justified."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Link exactly two formula operands; canonicalization sorts them because the biconditional is commutative."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when each of two formulae materially implies the other."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:imports
    skos:example "ex:geometry logic:imports ex:orders makes the imported module's sentences visible in ex:geometry."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use logic:imports for file inclusion, provenance, or cross-world accessibility."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Link the importing logic:Module to the imported module and let the module engine close the import graph."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to preserve Common Logic theory importation."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:inModule
    skos:example "A reified axiom can carry logic:inModule ex:geometry without changing the standpoint under which it holds."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not attach logic:inModule as a substitute for source provenance or epistemic perspective."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Attach the edge to the statement reifier; omit it only for the explicit top-level unnamed module."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to index a sentence by the named theory that owns it."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:instanceOf
    skos:example "ex:Fido logic:instanceOf ex:Dog and ex:Dog logic:instanceOf logic:Kind form two levels of one deep-instantiation chain."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not replace ordinary rdf:type indiscriminately or assume distinct names denote distinct instances."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Relate an individual or reified type to its next-order type and check logic:orderedType coherence where orders are declared."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for first-order HiLog-style instantiation across one or more meta-levels."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:loadBearing
    skos:example "A witness needed to reconstruct a richer source is logic:loadBearing true, while a display hint is false."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not mark presentation prose as load-bearing or silently drop an annotation marked true."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Set true only when inverse reconstruction or correctness depends on the annotation; otherwise use false or omit it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to distinguish preservation-critical annotations from droppable display metadata."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:naryArg0
    skos:example "The lowering of ex:between(a,b,c) links its tuple reifier to a through logic:naryArg0."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use logic:naryArg0 as an AST carrier or for any position other than zero."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Emit it with logic:instanceOf on the same content-addressed tuple reifier."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the first flat-binary argument of a fixed-arity reified atom."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:naryArg1
    skos:example "The lowering of ex:between(a,b,c) links its tuple reifier to b through logic:naryArg1."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use logic:naryArg1 for the first or third tuple position."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Emit exactly the second positional edge on the same reifier used by logic:naryArg0."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the second flat-binary argument of a fixed-arity reified atom."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:naryArg2
    skos:example "The lowering of ex:between(a,b,c) links its tuple reifier to c through logic:naryArg2."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not emit logic:naryArg2 for a binary atom or reuse it for later positions."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Emit it only when a third fixed argument exists; extend the closed positional vocabulary for higher fixed arity."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the third flat-binary argument of a fixed-arity reified atom."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:not
    skos:example "A formula node with logic:not ex:licensed represents the strong negation of the ex:licensed formula."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use logic:not for closed-world negation-as-failure or with more than one operand."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Link exactly one formula operand and retain strong-negation residue when a target cannot express it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for explicit classical negation inside the first-order formula tree."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:or
    skos:example "A covering formula can link ex:Cat(x), ex:Dog(x), and ex:Bird(x) through three logic:or edges."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use logic:or for program choice or with fewer than two operands."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Link at least two formula operands; canonicalization flattens nested disjunctions and sorts their keys."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when at least one operand formula must hold."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:quantifiedVariable
    skos:example "A two-variable quantifier links carriers at indices 0 and 1 with logic:termVariable values 'x' and 'y'."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not bind an IRI, literal, sequence marker, duplicate index, or gapped index through this property."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Link one or more logic:TermCarrier nodes whose sole values are variable names and whose indices are contiguous."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to declare the ordered binding block of logic:forall or logic:exists."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:relation
    skos:example "An atomic formula for ex:knows(alice,bob) carries ex:knows as its sole logic:relation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not put a variable or literal in the relation slot or combine it with a connective constructor on the same formula."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Provide exactly one reified relation IRI and an ordered, non-empty logic:argument carrier list."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to mark a logic:Formula as an atomic predication."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:termIndex
    skos:example "The object argument of a binary atom uses logic:termIndex 1 after its subject argument at index 0."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not repeat an index, leave a gap, use a negative value, or infer position from RDF serialization."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Assign exactly one non-negative integer per carrier and validate siblings as the contiguous set 0 through n minus 1."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use to preserve argument and binder order independently of graph statement order."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:termIri
    skos:example "A constant argument naming ex:Alice is carried as logic:termIri ex:Alice."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not store a variable name, lexical literal, or sequence marker in logic:termIri."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Place exactly one IRI value on a carrier that has no other term-value property."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for an individual, reified type, or reified relation constant in a term position."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:termLiteral
    skos:example "The integer lexical value 42 is carried with logic:termLiteral '42' and logic:termLiteralDatatype xsd:integer."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not place an IRI or variable in the literal slot or attach two datatype IRIs."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Carry exactly one lexical literal and optionally one companion datatype IRI."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a data literal appearing as an atomic formula argument."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:termLiteralDatatype
    skos:example "A carrier for the lexical value '2026-07-12' can use logic:termLiteralDatatype xsd:date."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not attach a datatype without logic:termLiteral or use a literal string in place of the datatype IRI."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Add at most one datatype IRI to a literal-valued carrier; omit it for a plain literal."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use when the literal term's RDF datatype must round-trip explicitly."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:termSequenceMarker
    skos:example "A variadic carrier can use logic:termSequenceMarker 'rest' to bind the remaining argument sequence."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not use a sequence marker for one ordinary term or combine it with logic:termVariable."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Place exactly one non-empty marker name on an indexed carrier and disclose variadic residue to fixed-arity targets."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for the Common Logic sequence-variable form in a genuinely variadic atom."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:termVariable
    skos:example "A carrier with logic:termVariable 'person' represents the authored variable name without a question-mark sigil."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not include a surface sigil, use an empty name, or combine the variable with another term-value kind."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Store one non-empty authored name; the canonicalizer resolves bound names through the quantifier environment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a bound or free single-term variable in an argument or binder carrier."@x-gmeow-english .

# ── The evaluable n-ary reification vocabulary (flat-binary lowering) ─────────
# A fixed-arity n-ary atom Rel(a₀,…,aₙ) is realized in the binary relational core
# as a conjunction of ordinary binary atoms over a single content-addressed
# reifier node R — the standard n-ary-relations encoding under the HiLog
# reflection: logic:instanceOf(R, Rel) types the tuple by its reified relation
# individual, and each argument sits in a flat positional predicate
# logic:naryArg0 … logic:naryArgN. Unlike the two-level logic:argument /
# logic:termIndex Formula-AST carriers, these are flat binary predicates so every
# reified atom is directly evaluable by the binary chase. The reifier node R is a
# content-addressed IRI (a lowering-boundary artifact, not an authored term); a
# ground n-ary fact and every derivation of it share one R, giving identity and
# provenance parity with a binary derivation.

logic:naryArg0
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "n-ary argument 0"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a reifier node to the term in argument position 0 of the n-ary tuple it reifies. The flat-binary positional predicate the evaluable lowering emits for the first argument of a fixed-arity n-ary atom Rel(a₀,…): logic:naryArg0(R, a₀) beside logic:instanceOf(R, Rel). Directly evaluable in the binary relational core; the zero-based position is fixed in the predicate itself so no separate index carrier is needed. It is not a logic:argument AST-carrier edge."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:naryArg1
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "n-ary argument 1"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a reifier node to the term in argument position 1 of the n-ary tuple it reifies. The flat-binary positional predicate the evaluable lowering emits for the second argument of a fixed-arity n-ary atom; companion to logic:naryArg0. Directly evaluable in the binary relational core. It is not the first position and is not a logic:argument AST-carrier edge."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:naryArg2
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "n-ary argument 2"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates a reifier node to the term in argument position 2 of the n-ary tuple it reifies. The flat-binary positional predicate the evaluable lowering emits for the third argument of a fixed-arity n-ary atom; companion to logic:naryArg0 and logic:naryArg1. Directly evaluable in the binary relational core. The enumerated positional predicates run 0 … N for the maximum fixed arity in play (ternary here); a higher arity extends the closed set with the next positional predicate. It is not emitted for a binary atom and is not a logic:argument AST-carrier edge."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# ── The full-FOL residue shape vocabulary (loss-ledger disclosure) ────────────
# The closed value set a Horn-fragment projection target discloses when it cannot
# carry a logic:Formula: each tag names which first-order construct exceeds the
# Horn+NAF fragment, so the loss ledger states the kind of residue carried-and-
# flagged in the canonical logic: layer rather than emitting an opaque note.

logic:FormulaShape
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Formula Shape"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed class of first-order shape tags a Horn-fragment projection target discloses when it cannot represent a logic:Formula. Each individual names one construct that pushes a formula beyond the binary Horn+NAF fragment, so the loss ledger records which constructs were carried-and-flagged in the canonical logic: layer instead of one opaque note. The set is closed — its individuals are logic:Disjunctive, logic:Nested, logic:Quantified, logic:StrongNegation, and logic:Variadic — so a misspelt tag names no declared value."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:Disjunctive
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormulaShape ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Disjunctive"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The residue shape tag for a formula in which a disjunction (logic:or) appears — a choice the single determinate body of a Horn rule cannot express. Disclosed when a Horn-fragment target carries such a formula as flagged unsupported residue."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:Nested
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormulaShape ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Nested"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The residue shape tag for a formula that is a connective or quantifier tree rather than a flat predication — it carries logical structure (logic:and, logic:or, logic:not, an implication, logic:iff, or a quantifier) that the flat body of a Horn rule cannot hold. Disclosed when a Horn-fragment target carries such a formula as flagged unsupported residue."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:Quantified
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormulaShape ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Quantified"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The residue shape tag for a formula in which an explicit quantifier (logic:forall or logic:exists) appears — quantification nested or alternating beyond the implicit universal closure of a Horn rule. Disclosed when a Horn-fragment target carries such a formula as flagged unsupported residue."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:StrongNegation
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormulaShape ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Strong Negation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The residue shape tag for a formula in which strong (explicit, classical) negation (logic:not) appears — distinct from negation-as-failure, which a Horn rule body already carries. Disclosed when a Horn-fragment target carries such a formula as flagged unsupported residue."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:Variadic
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormulaShape ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Variadic"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The residue shape tag for an atomic predication whose argument arity is not the binary Horn shape: it carries a logic:SequenceMarker or a non-binary (unary or n-ary) argument list — the generalization beyond the fixed arity-three triple. Disclosed when a Horn-fragment target carries such a formula as flagged unsupported residue."@x-gmeow-english .

# ── The expressiveness-boundary axis (loss-ledger disclosure) ────────────────
# The closed value set naming which logical fragment a defining statement
# inhabits — the machine-readable first-order-vs-second-order axis of the loss
# ledger. Orthogonal to logic:PreservationKind (answer polarity) and to
# logic:FormulaShape (which first-order constructs a Horn target cannot carry):
# some statements (e.g. compactness's "every open cover has a finite subcover")
# genuinely exceed first-order logic altogether, and that boundary is recorded
# here as an honest ledger entry rather than faked as a first-order formula.

logic:ExpressivenessBoundary
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Expressiveness Boundary"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed class naming which logical fragment a defining statement inhabits — the machine-readable first-order-vs-second-order axis of the loss ledger, distinct from logic:PreservationKind (answer polarity) and logic:FormulaShape (first-order constructs a Horn target cannot carry). Its individuals are logic:FirstOrder and logic:SecondOrder; the set is closed, so a misspelt value names no declared boundary."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:FirstOrder
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ExpressivenessBoundary ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "First-Order"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The expressiveness boundary of a defining statement authorable as a first-order logic:Formula (∀/∃ over individuals of the reified signature). First-order mathematical laws — continuity, connectedness, the T0–T4 separation axioms — sit here; the grounding peers author them as logic:Formula objects at this boundary."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:SecondOrder
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:ExpressivenessBoundary ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Second-Order"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The expressiveness boundary of a defining statement that genuinely exceeds first-order logic — quantification over families/subsets or an appeal to finiteness (e.g. compactness's 'every open cover has a finite subcover'). Not authorable as a logic:Formula; carried in the loss ledger as an honest boundary with logic:preservationKind logic:Unsupported, never as a fake first-order formula."@x-gmeow-english .

logic:expressivenessBoundary
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "expressiveness boundary"@x-gmeow-english ;
    rdfs:range logic:ExpressivenessBoundary ;
    skos:definition "Relates a defining law or a loss-ledger boundary record to the logic:ExpressivenessBoundary its statement inhabits."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The fragment-certified refutation kernel's decidability surface, shipped as
# first-class ontology content. The kernel (crates/logic/src/reason/refute.rs) is
# the SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH: its decided_fragments() and retained_boundaries()
# registry is projected here as logic:DecidedFragment / logic:RefutationPattern
# individuals and logic:expressivenessBoundary records, and the Rust agreement test
# module_ttl_projects_the_kernel_registry proves this manifest is EXACTLY that
# projection (drift either way fails). Each characterization is TECHNICAL — the
# refutation pattern and the completeness / boundary bound — with no process refs.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

logic:RefutationPattern
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Refutation Pattern"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The closed class of decision-procedure schemata a certified-complete refutation family closes under. Several decided families may share one pattern (a cardinality count and an OWL hasSelf self-edge are both the counting pigeonhole). Its individuals are the state-of-the-art patterns the native kernel decides; the set is the projection of the Rust RefutationPattern enum."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:DecidedFragment
    a logic:Class ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Decided Fragment"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A certified-complete construct family the native refutation kernel decides: it carries the logic:RefutationPattern it closes under (logic:decidesUnderPattern) and a technical completeness bound (logic:fragmentCompletenessBound). One individual per decided family; the set is the projection of the Rust decided_fragments() registry, outside which the kernel honestly withholds."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .

logic:decidesUnderPattern
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "decides under pattern"@x-gmeow-english ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DecidedFragment ;
    rdfs:range logic:RefutationPattern ;
    skos:definition "Relates a logic:DecidedFragment to the logic:RefutationPattern its family closes under."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:fragmentCompletenessBound
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "fragment completeness bound"@x-gmeow-english ;
    rdfs:domain logic:DecidedFragment ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    skos:definition "The technical completeness bound of a logic:DecidedFragment: the precise fragment characterization under which the kernel's decision is complete, free of any process reference."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:fragmentBoundaryReason
    a logic:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "fragment boundary reason"@x-gmeow-english ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    skos:definition "The technical reason a retained-withhold record lies outside the certified-complete refutation fragment: the construct the kernel deliberately does not decide and why, free of any process reference."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

# ── logic:RefutationPattern individuals (the decision-procedure schemata) ─────────

logic:counting-pigeonhole
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:RefutationPattern ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Counting Pigeonhole"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A finite pigeonhole count of distinct fillers or edges against a numeric bound: the count exceeds (or collapses) the bound, so no model can satisfy it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:value-space-cardinality
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:RefutationPattern ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Value-Space Cardinality"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A finite datatype value-space cardinality bounding the distinct-literal count a cardinality restriction can force onto a datatype property."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:case-split-exhaustion
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:RefutationPattern ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Case-Split Exhaustion"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "An exhaustive, terminating case-split over a bounded disjunction: every branch is refuted, so the disjunction as a whole is refuted."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:complement-clash
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:RefutationPattern ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Complement Clash"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A propositional complement clash: a class expression forced to inhabit both a class and its complement in a definition position, decided without model search."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:arithmetic-equality-collapse
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:RefutationPattern ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Arithmetic Equality Collapse"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "An equality / inequality arithmetic collapse over a finite set of named individuals: an (inverse-)functional identity forced against an asserted distinctness."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:malformed-metamodel
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:RefutationPattern ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Malformed Metamodel"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A decidable metamodel malformation of the finite triple set, such as a structurally broken RDF list, adjudicated independently of object-level entailment."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:nominal-clash
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:RefutationPattern ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Nominal Clash"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A finite closed-set intersection emptiness over nominal enumerations: an individual forced into two or more disjoint OWL oneOf sets."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

# ── logic:DecidedFragment individuals (one per certified-complete family) ─────────

logic:complement-refutation
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DecidedFragment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Complement Refutation"@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:decidesUnderPattern logic:complement-clash ;
    logic:fragmentCompletenessBound "A class expression forced into both C and its complement not-C in a class-definition position; complete because complementhood is decided propositionally, every branch that types an individual into not-C closing against a derivable C-membership with no model search." ;
    skos:definition "The certified-complete complement-refutation family: a class constrained by a negated class expression forced unsatisfiable-yet-nonempty, decided under the complement clash pattern."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:number-cardinality-counting
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DecidedFragment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Number / Cardinality Counting"@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:decidesUnderPattern logic:counting-pigeonhole ;
    logic:fragmentCompletenessBound "A min, max, or exact (qualified) cardinality bound on a populated class; complete because the distinct fillers under the identity stance are finitely counted and a collapsed bound (min N greater than max M, or N distinct forced fillers exceeding max M) is a pigeonhole violation with no unbounded search." ;
    skos:definition "The certified-complete number/cardinality-counting family: min/max/exact (qualified) cardinality bounds decided by counting distinct fillers under the identity stance, under the counting pigeonhole pattern."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:union-disjoint-case-split
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DecidedFragment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Union / Disjoint Case-Split"@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:decidesUnderPattern logic:case-split-exhaustion ;
    logic:fragmentCompletenessBound "A bounded union C subClassOf (D1 or ... or Dn) whose members are pairwise disjoint; complete because the finite disjunction is exhaustively case-split and every branch closes under refutation in a terminating propositional decision." ;
    skos:definition "The certified-complete union-plus-disjoint refutation family: a bounded disjunctive membership every branch of which closes, under the case-split exhaustion pattern."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:nominal-enumeration-counting
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DecidedFragment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Nominal Enumeration Counting"@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:decidesUnderPattern logic:nominal-clash ;
    logic:fragmentCompletenessBound "An individual typed into two or more pairwise-disjoint OWL oneOf enumerations; complete because nominal membership is a finite closed-set intersection whose emptiness is decided by counting, with no anonymous-individual generation." ;
    skos:definition "The certified-complete nominal-enumeration-counting family: a nominal pigeonhole across disjoint OWL oneOf enumerations, under the nominal clash pattern."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:datatype-value-space
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DecidedFragment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Datatype Value-Space"@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:decidesUnderPattern logic:value-space-cardinality ;
    logic:fragmentCompletenessBound "A facet-restricted datatype whose finite value-space cardinality is provably smaller than the distinct literals a cardinality bound forces onto it; complete because the value-space count is derived from the math-grounded finite-cardinality table, bounding the pigeonhole exactly." ;
    skos:definition "The certified-complete datatype value-space family: a facet-restricted datatype whose finite value space is too small for the distinct values forced onto it, under the value-space cardinality pattern."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:inverse-functional-identity-collapse
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DecidedFragment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Inverse-Functional Identity Collapse"@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:decidesUnderPattern logic:arithmetic-equality-collapse ;
    logic:fragmentCompletenessBound "An inverse-functional or functional property forcing two OWL differentFrom (or distinct-nominal) individuals to be identified; complete because the identity collapse is a decidable equality / inequality arithmetic over a finite set of named individuals." ;
    skos:definition "The certified-complete inverse-functional / functional identity-collapse family: an (inverse-)functional property collapsing two distinct individuals, under the arithmetic equality collapse pattern."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:malformed-rdf-list
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DecidedFragment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Malformed rdf:List"@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:decidesUnderPattern logic:malformed-metamodel ;
    logic:fragmentCompletenessBound "An rdf:nil node bearing rdf:first or rdf:rest (a structurally broken RDF list); complete because list well-formedness is a decidable metamodel property of the finite triple set, independent of object-level entailment." ;
    skos:definition "The certified-complete malformed-rdf:List family: an rdf:nil node bearing a list edge, decided under the malformed metamodel pattern."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:has-self-membership
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:DecidedFragment ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "hasSelf Membership"@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:decidesUnderPattern logic:counting-pigeonhole ;
    logic:fragmentCompletenessBound "An OWL hasSelf (exists p.Self) restriction in a refutation position where a self-edge x p x forces membership disjoint with a held class; complete because self-membership is a single reflexive-edge count with no unbounded quantifier alternation." ;
    skos:definition "The certified-complete OWL hasSelf membership-refutation family: a self-restriction in a disjointness position clashed by a self-edge, decided under the counting pigeonhole pattern."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

# ══ The RDF 1.2 statement-metadata lowering ═══════════════════════════════════════════
#
# RDF 1.2 attributes a claim to a vantage by REIFYING a statement: a reifier node carries
# rdf:reifies <<( s p o )>> plus ordinary annotations (who says it, with what polarity, with
# what support). The annotations are plain triples and always reached the reasoner. The one
# thing it could not see was the OBJECT of rdf:reifies — WHICH statement an attribution is
# about — because that object is a triple TERM, and the engine's EDB carries IRIs, blank
# nodes and literals.
#
# The consequence was not a nuance. Two co-equal vantages taking opposite stances on one
# statement is the canonical governed situation the standpoint layer exists for, and to the
# reasoner it read as two unrelated nodes with unrelated opinions: nothing joined them,
# because the only edge that says they are about the same claim was the invisible one.
#
# The three predicates below are that edge, DECOMPOSED. They are a GENERATED LOWERING in the
# Principle-17 sense, exactly as SSSOM, EDOAL and FnO are: canonical RDF 1.2 stays the
# authority, the authored data is never rewritten, and the lowering is derived from it on
# the way into the reasoning world (gmeow_logic::statement_lowering). What the lowering
# does not preserve is recorded, narrowly, as logic:rdf12-nested-triple-term below — not as
# a blanket claim that RDF 1.2 is outside the reasoner, which is what the CLI used to say
# and is no longer true.

logic:reifiedStatementSubject
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "reified statement subject"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates an RDF 1.2 reifier to the SUBJECT of the statement it reifies — one third of the lowering that makes rdf:reifies joinable. Derived, never asserted: it is read off the authored rdf:reifies triple term on the way into the reasoning world, so the canonical RDF 1.2 form stays the single source and the authored data is not rewritten. Multi-valued only in the sense that one reifier reifies one statement; two reifiers over the SAME statement each carry their own copy, which is precisely what lets a rule join two attributions that disagree."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:cmdrOnRollbackOverHotfix logic:reifiedStatementSubject ex:rollback ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Read it in a rule body that must join a statement attribution to the claim it is about; never author it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid asserting it by hand: an authored copy that disagrees with the rdf:reifies term is a second source of truth for the same statement, and the reasoner would join on the wrong one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Author the canonical rdf:reifies <<( s p o )>> form; the lowering supplies this edge, and a rule joins two reifiers by matching all three."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "One third of the reifier-to-statement lowering. Decomposed rather than carried as a term because the engine's fact surface is (subject, predicate, object) over IRIs, blank nodes and literals — a triple term has no place in it, and forcing one in returns a malformed IRI."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:reifiedStatementPredicate
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "reified statement predicate"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates an RDF 1.2 reifier to the PREDICATE of the statement it reifies, as an IRI in object position. Derived, never asserted. It is the third of the lowering that stops two attributions about DIFFERENT relations between the same two things from being joined as though they were about one: 'rollback is strictly preferred to hotfix' and 'rollback supersedes hotfix' share both endpoints and are not the same claim."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:cmdrOnRollbackOverHotfix logic:reifiedStatementPredicate gmeow:strictlyOver ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Read it in a rule body that must join attributions about the SAME relation, not merely the same endpoints."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid omitting it from a join over the other two: subject and object alone identify a pair of things, not a claim."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Match it alongside logic:reifiedStatementSubject and logic:reifiedStatementObject; all three together identify the statement."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Carried in OBJECT position as an ordinary IRI, because the engine's fact surface has no predicate-variable slot. That is what makes the reified predicate joinable at all."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:reifiedStatementObject
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "reified statement object"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates an RDF 1.2 reifier to the OBJECT of the statement it reifies. Derived, never asserted. Completes the triple that identifies which claim an attribution is about, so two vantages can be shown to be speaking about one statement rather than two."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:cmdrOnRollbackOverHotfix logic:reifiedStatementObject ex:hotfix ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Read it in a rule body joining a statement attribution to the claim it is about."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid asserting it by hand, for the same reason as its two siblings: the rdf:reifies term is the authority."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Match it as the third of the three; the lowering emits all three or none."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "The third of the reifier-to-statement lowering. A statement whose object is itself a triple term is NOT lowered — that residue is recorded at logic:rdf12-nested-triple-term rather than silently flattened."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:contestedByAttribution
    a logic:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "contested by attribution"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Relates one statement attribution to a DIFFERENT attribution of the SAME statement whose support status is the opposite of its own — the derived record that a claim is contested rather than settled. Symmetric in effect (each of the two disagreeing attributions derives the edge to the other), because contestation is a relation between vantages and neither is the privileged one: adjudicating requires knowing both parties, and an edge in one direction would name a challenger without naming what it challenges. It is what the statement-metadata lowering is FOR: before it, two co-equal vantages opposing each other over one statement were two unrelated nodes with unrelated opinions, because the only thing that said they were about the same claim was the rdf:reifies triple term the reasoner could not read."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:cmdrOnRollbackOverHotfix logic:contestedByAttribution ex:onCallOnRollbackOverHotfix ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Read it to find claims that two vantages disagree about, so a recommendation resting on one of them can be presented as contested rather than as settled."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid asserting it: a hand-written contestation is an opinion about a disagreement rather than the disagreement itself, and it would survive the withdrawal of the objection that caused it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Author the two attributions in canonical RDF 1.2 form with their support statuses; logic:ruleAttributionContestedByOpposingVantage derives the edge."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Derived, never asserted. The two attributions must reify the SAME statement — same subject, same predicate, same object — because two vantages disagreeing about different claims are not in conflict at all."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxRBox .

logic:ruleAttributionContestedByOpposingVantage
    a logic:Rule ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "contested: two vantages, one statement, opposite support"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "Derives logic:contestedByAttribution between two DISTINCT reifiers of the SAME statement whose gmeow:standpointSupportStatus values are gmeow:supportSupported and gmeow:supportOpposed. Positive Horn, joined over the three lowered statement components. It is the proof that the statement-metadata lowering is not decorative: the join is over logic:reifiedStatementSubject / Predicate / Object, none of which existed before it, and without them the rule's body cannot be written at all — the two attributions have no shared variable, and a rule that dropped the join would declare every affirmation in the graph contested by every opposition anywhere in it."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleAttributionContestedByOpposingVantage ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?affirming" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:contestedByAttribution ;
        rdf:object    "?opposing"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?affirming" ;
        rdf:predicate gmeow:standpointSupportStatus ;
        rdf:object    gmeow:supportSupported
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?opposing" ;
        rdf:predicate gmeow:standpointSupportStatus ;
        rdf:object    gmeow:supportOpposed
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?affirming" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:reifiedStatementSubject ;
        rdf:object    "?s"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?opposing" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:reifiedStatementSubject ;
        rdf:object    "?s"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?affirming" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:reifiedStatementPredicate ;
        rdf:object    "?p"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?opposing" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:reifiedStatementPredicate ;
        rdf:object    "?p"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?affirming" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:reifiedStatementObject ;
        rdf:object    "?o"
    ] , [
        rdf:subject   "?opposing" ;
        rdf:predicate logic:reifiedStatementObject ;
        rdf:object    "?o"
    ] ;
    logic:distinctBody [
        rdf:subject "?affirming" ;
        rdf:object  "?opposing"
    ] .

# ── Retained-withhold records (constructs the kernel deliberately does NOT decide) ─
# Reuse logic:expressivenessBoundary: each record inhabits logic:FirstOrder (these
# constructs are first-order-expressible, but the kernel declines to certify them)
# and carries a technical logic:fragmentBoundaryReason.

logic:xsd-pattern-facet
    a logic:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "xsd:pattern Facet Boundary"@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:expressivenessBoundary logic:FirstOrder ;
    logic:fragmentBoundaryReason "An xsd:pattern facet requires the XML Schema regular-expression dialect, with its Unicode block and category escapes and XSD-specific quantifier semantics, which is not the host platform regular-expression language; the value-space emptiness it induces cannot be decided without an XSD regex evaluator, so it lies outside the certified fragment." ;
    skos:definition "The retained-withhold record for xsd:pattern facets: a datatype value-space emptiness the kernel does not decide because it needs the XML-Schema regular-expression dialect rather than a host regex engine."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:non-binary-property-chain
    a logic:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Non-Binary Property Chain Boundary"@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:expressivenessBoundary logic:FirstOrder ;
    logic:fragmentBoundaryReason "A property chain of length other than two (an n-ary role composition) does not reduce to the binary role composition the counting and identity deciders certify; its closure couples an unbounded number of role edges, so it lies outside the certified fragment." ;
    skos:definition "The retained-withhold record for non-binary property chains: an n-ary role composition the kernel does not certify because it does not reduce to the binary composition its deciders handle."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:entangled-existential-cardinality
    a logic:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Entangled Existential + Cardinality Boundary"@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:expressivenessBoundary logic:FirstOrder ;
    logic:fragmentBoundaryReason "A configuration entangling an existential OWL someValuesFrom filler with a number or qualified-cardinality bound on the same property couples witness generation with counting; the family sub-deciders certify each in isolation only, so the entangled full-DL case lies outside the certified fragment." ;
    skos:definition "The retained-withhold record for entangled full-DL existential-plus-cardinality configurations: witness generation coupled with counting on one property, which the kernel certifies only in isolation."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:rdf12-nested-triple-term
    a logic:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "Nested Triple Term Boundary"@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:expressivenessBoundary logic:FirstOrder ;
    logic:fragmentBoundaryReason "The statement-metadata lowering decomposes an RDF 1.2 reifier's rdf:reifies term into logic:reifiedStatementSubject / logic:reifiedStatementPredicate / logic:reifiedStatementObject, which is exact for a statement whose subject and object are IRIs, blank nodes or literals and preserves exactly two things it cannot: a NESTED triple term (a reified statement whose own subject or object is itself a triple term has no non-term component to decompose into, so it is not lowered and no component edge is emitted for it), and the reified statement's identity AS A TERM (the three components are joinable, but nothing in the fact surface denotes the statement itself, so a rule may quantify over the components and may not quantify over the statement)." ;
    skos:definition "The retained-withhold record for the RDF 1.2 statement-metadata lowering: exactly what decomposing an rdf:reifies triple term into three joinable component edges does not preserve. NARROW on purpose — a flat reified statement IS reasoned over, and the blanket claim that RDF 1.2 statement metadata lies outside the reasoner is no longer true. Two things are withheld: a nested triple term, which has no non-term component to decompose into and is therefore not lowered at all; and the statement's identity as a TERM, since the lowering yields three edges about the reifier rather than a name for the statement, so a rule can join on the components and cannot quantify over the statement itself."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:example "ex:nestedAttribution rdf:reifies <<( ex:a ex:p <<( ex:c ex:q ex:d )>> )>> ; logic:expressivenessBoundary logic:FirstOrder ."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:useWhen "Cite it when reporting that a particular RDF 1.2 attribution was NOT reasoned over, so the withhold names its exact cause instead of arriving as a blanket disclaimer about statement metadata."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:avoidWhen "Avoid citing it for a FLAT reified statement: those are lowered and reasoned over, and quoting this record there would report a withhold that did not happen — the failure mode the record was written to end."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:howToUse "Read it alongside logic:reifiedStatementSubject / logic:reifiedStatementPredicate / logic:reifiedStatementObject: those three are what the lowering DOES yield, and this is the residue beside them."@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:scopeNote "Two withholds, not one, and they fail for different reasons: a nested triple term has no non-term component to decompose into, while the statement's term identity is absent from every lowering however flat the statement is. Reporting either as the other would misdescribe what a rule may quantify over."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxABox .

logic:formalizes
    a logic:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "formalizes"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "A bridge annotation from a logic: foundational term to the gmeow: domain concept it formalizes: the central reasoning vocabulary (argumentation semantics, Belnap information states) names the domain construct that mirrors it, so the domain term's documentation can surface the formalization without the domain slice ever asserting a logic: triple. Subject is a logic: term, object a gmeow: term, both stated in prose; an annotation property, never a reasoned axiom, so it carries no DL or EL profile weight."@x-gmeow-english .
vann:preferredNamespacePrefix
    a logic:AnnotationProperty .

vann:preferredNamespaceUri
    a logic:AnnotationProperty .

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Foundational class-coverings. Each is a full-FOL logic:Formula disjunction
# ∀x. Whole(x) → (S₁(x) ∨ … ∨ Sₙ(x)); the canonical RDF 1.2 projection carries it
# exact, and the OWL projection lowers it to OWL unionOf (an exhaustive cover). The
# member disjointness is the separate OWL disjointWith axioms, never folded into the
# formula. Governance for each is the logic:FormalizationCandidate block below.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Covering: every gmeow:Entity is one of the four top sortals (Agent, InformationObject,
# PhysicalObject, SocialObject). A non-disjoint cover — the SocialObject ∩ InformationObject
# overlap is preserved, so this projects to OWL unionOf (not OWL disjointUnionOf).
logic:coverEntitySortals
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:forall logic:coverEntitySortalsBody ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable logic:coverEntitySortalsVar .
logic:coverEntitySortalsVar
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "x" .
logic:coverEntitySortalsBody
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:antecedent logic:coverEntitySortalsAnte ;
    logic:consequent logic:coverEntitySortalsCons .
logic:coverEntitySortalsAnte
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:Entity ;
    logic:argument logic:coverEntitySortalsAnteArg .
logic:coverEntitySortalsAnteArg
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "x" .
logic:coverEntitySortalsCons
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:or logic:coverEntitySortalsAgent ,
        logic:coverEntitySortalsInfo ,
        logic:coverEntitySortalsPhysical ,
        logic:coverEntitySortalsSocial .
logic:coverEntitySortalsAgent
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:Agent ;
    logic:argument logic:coverEntitySortalsAgentArg .
logic:coverEntitySortalsAgentArg
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "x" .
logic:coverEntitySortalsInfo
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:InformationObject ;
    logic:argument logic:coverEntitySortalsInfoArg .
logic:coverEntitySortalsInfoArg
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "x" .
logic:coverEntitySortalsPhysical
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:PhysicalObject ;
    logic:argument logic:coverEntitySortalsPhysicalArg .
logic:coverEntitySortalsPhysicalArg
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "x" .
logic:coverEntitySortalsSocial
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:SocialObject ;
    logic:argument logic:coverEntitySortalsSocialArg .
logic:coverEntitySortalsSocialArg
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "x" .

# Covering: every logic:Object is individuated as exactly one of the three individuation
# kinds (Collection, Quantity, FunctionalComplex). The pairwise disjointness of the three
# is already asserted (their OWL disjointWith axioms); this covering supplies the
# exhaustiveness, completing the individuation partition of Objects. The stereotype
# meta-class partition (Kind/SubKind/Role/Phase/Category/Mixin/Relator) is a DISTINCT,
# meta-level partition owned by the logic:StereotypeCardinality discipline (foundation
# rules); this covering deliberately does not restate it, to avoid double-encoding.
logic:coverObjectIndividuation
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:forall logic:coverObjectIndividuationBody ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable logic:coverObjectIndividuationVar .
logic:coverObjectIndividuationVar
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "x" .
logic:coverObjectIndividuationBody
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:antecedent logic:coverObjectIndividuationAnte ;
    logic:consequent logic:coverObjectIndividuationCons .
logic:coverObjectIndividuationAnte
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation logic:Object ;
    logic:argument logic:coverObjectIndividuationAnteArg .
logic:coverObjectIndividuationAnteArg
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "x" .
logic:coverObjectIndividuationCons
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:or logic:coverObjectIndividuationCollection ,
        logic:coverObjectIndividuationQuantity ,
        logic:coverObjectIndividuationFunctionalComplex .
logic:coverObjectIndividuationCollection
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation logic:Collection ;
    logic:argument logic:coverObjectIndividuationCollectionArg .
logic:coverObjectIndividuationCollectionArg
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "x" .
logic:coverObjectIndividuationQuantity
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation logic:Quantity ;
    logic:argument logic:coverObjectIndividuationQuantityArg .
logic:coverObjectIndividuationQuantityArg
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "x" .
logic:coverObjectIndividuationFunctionalComplex
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation logic:FunctionalComplex ;
    logic:argument logic:coverObjectIndividuationFunctionalComplexArg .
logic:coverObjectIndividuationFunctionalComplexArg
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "x" .

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Definitional axioms for the deception / standpoint / falsehood family.
# Four object-level full-FOL logic:Formula definitions, each logic:formalizes-linked
# to the gmeow: prose it encodes and paired with a reviewed logic:FormalizationCandidate
# (harvest back-link + drift-checked source hash). Each formula exceeds the monotone
# Horn+NAF fragment (nested existential, biconditional, strong negation), so it is
# realized ONLY in the OWL / full-FOL projection and disclosed in the loss ledger — the
# native StratifiedNAF chase does NOT enforce it (RiskProjectionOnly, mirroring the
# foundational class coverings). The intent-never-entailed guardrail is unaffected: none of these
# formulas is a productive rule and none carries gmeow:deceptiveIntentClaim, so
# logic:IntentNotFromDeceptionObligation's Arm A (no rule head) stays discharged.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Axiom 1: deception ⇒ a divergent held/projected doxastic pair (the gap IS the act) ──
# ∀e. eventType(e, eventTypeDeception) →
#     ∃h,p. heldStandpoint(e,h) ∧ projectedStandpoint(e,p)
#           ∧ DoxasticStandpointClaim(h) ∧ DoxasticStandpointClaim(p)
#           ∧ ¬∃m. (claimModality(h,m) ∧ claimModality(p,m))
# A NECESSARY condition, never a biconditional: a licensed-falsehood projection also has a
# held/projected gap yet is not deception (Axiom 3 carves it out), so the gap is necessary
# but not sufficient for deception.
logic:deceptionHeldProjectedDivergence
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:formalizes gmeow:eventTypeDeception ;
    logic:forall logic:dhpdBody ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable logic:dhpdVarE .
logic:dhpdVarE
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "e" .
logic:dhpdBody
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:antecedent logic:dhpdAnte ;
    logic:consequent logic:dhpdCons .
logic:dhpdAnte
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:eventType ;
    logic:argument logic:dhpdAnteArgE , logic:dhpdAnteArgKind .
logic:dhpdAnteArgE
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "e" .
logic:dhpdAnteArgKind
    logic:termIndex 1 ;
    logic:termIri gmeow:eventTypeDeception .
logic:dhpdCons
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:exists logic:dhpdConsBody ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable logic:dhpdVarH , logic:dhpdVarP .
logic:dhpdVarH
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "h" .
logic:dhpdVarP
    logic:termIndex 1 ;
    logic:termVariable "p" .
logic:dhpdConsBody
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:and logic:dhpdHeld ,
        logic:dhpdProjected ,
        logic:dhpdHeldDox ,
        logic:dhpdProjectedDox ,
        logic:dhpdDivergent .
logic:dhpdHeld
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:heldStandpoint ;
    logic:argument logic:dhpdHeldArgE , logic:dhpdHeldArgH .
logic:dhpdHeldArgE
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "e" .
logic:dhpdHeldArgH
    logic:termIndex 1 ;
    logic:termVariable "h" .
logic:dhpdProjected
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:projectedStandpoint ;
    logic:argument logic:dhpdProjectedArgE , logic:dhpdProjectedArgP .
logic:dhpdProjectedArgE
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "e" .
logic:dhpdProjectedArgP
    logic:termIndex 1 ;
    logic:termVariable "p" .
logic:dhpdHeldDox
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:DoxasticStandpointClaim ;
    logic:argument logic:dhpdHeldDoxArg .
logic:dhpdHeldDoxArg
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "h" .
logic:dhpdProjectedDox
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:DoxasticStandpointClaim ;
    logic:argument logic:dhpdProjectedDoxArg .
logic:dhpdProjectedDoxArg
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "p" .
logic:dhpdDivergent
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:not logic:dhpdShared .
logic:dhpdShared
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:exists logic:dhpdSharedBody ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable logic:dhpdVarM .
logic:dhpdVarM
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "m" .
logic:dhpdSharedBody
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:and logic:dhpdHeldModality , logic:dhpdProjectedModality .
logic:dhpdHeldModality
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:claimModality ;
    logic:argument logic:dhpdHeldModalityArgH , logic:dhpdHeldModalityArgM .
logic:dhpdHeldModalityArgH
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "h" .
logic:dhpdHeldModalityArgM
    logic:termIndex 1 ;
    logic:termVariable "m" .
logic:dhpdProjectedModality
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:claimModality ;
    logic:argument logic:dhpdProjectedModalityArgP , logic:dhpdProjectedModalityArgM .
logic:dhpdProjectedModalityArgP
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "p" .
logic:dhpdProjectedModalityArgM
    logic:termIndex 1 ;
    logic:termVariable "m" .

# ── Axiom 2: falsehood ≡ a necessarily-denying standpoint claim (refuted, no isFalse flag) ──
# ∀c. claimModality(c, gmeow:refuted) ↔
#     (StandpointClaim(c) ∧ claimPolarity(c, gmeow:polarityDeny)
#      ∧ claimModalForce(c, gmeow:modalForceNecessary))
# An EQUIVALENCE definition: "falsehood = refuted StandpointClaim" made formal as the identity
# of the refuted modality with its canonical axis signature □¬ (deny + necessary). Frame-relative
# settled-false, never a global truth verdict — there is no isFalse flag; to be false-in-frame IS
# to hold a claim of this necessarily-denying modality.
logic:falsehoodIsRefutedClaim
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:formalizes gmeow:refuted ;
    logic:forall logic:firBody ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable logic:firVarC .
logic:firVarC
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "c" .
logic:firBody
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:iff logic:firRefuted , logic:firSignature .
logic:firRefuted
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:claimModality ;
    logic:argument logic:firRefutedArgC , logic:firRefutedArgV .
logic:firRefutedArgC
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "c" .
logic:firRefutedArgV
    logic:termIndex 1 ;
    logic:termIri gmeow:refuted .
logic:firSignature
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:and logic:firClaim , logic:firPolarity , logic:firForce .
logic:firClaim
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:StandpointClaim ;
    logic:argument logic:firClaimArg .
logic:firClaimArg
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "c" .
logic:firPolarity
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:claimPolarity ;
    logic:argument logic:firPolarityArgC , logic:firPolarityArgV .
logic:firPolarityArgC
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "c" .
logic:firPolarityArgV
    logic:termIndex 1 ;
    logic:termIri gmeow:polarityDeny .
logic:firForce
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:claimModalForce ;
    logic:argument logic:firForceArgC , logic:firForceArgV .
logic:firForceArgC
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "c" .
logic:firForceArgV
    logic:termIndex 1 ;
    logic:termIri gmeow:modalForceNecessary .

# ── Axiom 3: licensed falsehood ⊢ ¬deception (the fiction/satire safety partition) ──
# ∀e,c. (projectedStandpoint(e,c) ∧ claimVeridicality(c, gmeow:veridicalityLicensedFalsehood))
#       → ¬ eventType(e, gmeow:eventTypeDeception)
# The safety property: a socially-licensed non-truth-asserting projection (fiction, satire,
# sarcasm) is NOT deception, even though it too carries a held/projected gap. Composes with
# Axiom 1 to partition the gap into deception vs. licensed non-deception.
logic:licensedFalsehoodNotDeception
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:formalizes gmeow:veridicalityLicensedFalsehood ;
    logic:forall logic:lfndBody ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable logic:lfndVarE , logic:lfndVarC .
logic:lfndVarE
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "e" .
logic:lfndVarC
    logic:termIndex 1 ;
    logic:termVariable "c" .
logic:lfndBody
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:antecedent logic:lfndAnte ;
    logic:consequent logic:lfndCons .
logic:lfndAnte
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:and logic:lfndProjected , logic:lfndVeridicality .
logic:lfndProjected
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:projectedStandpoint ;
    logic:argument logic:lfndProjectedArgE , logic:lfndProjectedArgC .
logic:lfndProjectedArgE
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "e" .
logic:lfndProjectedArgC
    logic:termIndex 1 ;
    logic:termVariable "c" .
logic:lfndVeridicality
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:claimVeridicality ;
    logic:argument logic:lfndVeridicalityArgC , logic:lfndVeridicalityArgV .
logic:lfndVeridicalityArgC
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "c" .
logic:lfndVeridicalityArgV
    logic:termIndex 1 ;
    logic:termIri gmeow:veridicalityLicensedFalsehood .
logic:lfndCons
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:not logic:lfndDeception .
logic:lfndDeception
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:eventType ;
    logic:argument logic:lfndDeceptionArgE , logic:lfndDeceptionArgKind .
logic:lfndDeceptionArgE
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "e" .
logic:lfndDeceptionArgKind
    logic:termIndex 1 ;
    logic:termIri gmeow:eventTypeDeception .

# ── Axiom 4: the bullshit modality ≡ the truth-indifferent configuration (Frankfurt) ──
# ∀c. claimModality(c, gmeow:bullshit) ↔
#     (StandpointClaim(c) ∧ claimTruthDirectedness(c, gmeow:truthIndifferent))
# An EQUIVALENCE definition: bullshit is the stance held with indifference to truth — neither
# aimed at truth (as a sincere claim) nor at its negation (as a lie). truthIndifferent uniquely
# picks the bullshit modality among the five, so it is definitional, not merely necessary.
logic:bullshitTruthIndifference
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:formalizes gmeow:bullshit ;
    logic:forall logic:btiBody ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable logic:btiVarC .
logic:btiVarC
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "c" .
logic:btiBody
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:iff logic:btiBullshit , logic:btiSignature .
logic:btiBullshit
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:claimModality ;
    logic:argument logic:btiBullshitArgC , logic:btiBullshitArgV .
logic:btiBullshitArgC
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "c" .
logic:btiBullshitArgV
    logic:termIndex 1 ;
    logic:termIri gmeow:bullshit .
logic:btiSignature
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:and logic:btiClaim , logic:btiTruth .
logic:btiClaim
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:StandpointClaim ;
    logic:argument logic:btiClaimArg .
logic:btiClaimArg
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "c" .
logic:btiTruth
    a logic:Formula ;
    logic:relation gmeow:claimTruthDirectedness ;
    logic:argument logic:btiTruthArgC , logic:btiTruthArgV .
logic:btiTruthArgC
    logic:termIndex 0 ;
    logic:termVariable "c" .
logic:btiTruthArgV
    logic:termIndex 1 ;
    logic:termIri gmeow:truthIndifferent .

# ── Governance: reviewed FormalizationCandidates for the four axioms above ────────────
# Each harvests its axiom from exactly one prose field (logic:candidateSourceField) of the
# gmeow: term it formalizes; logic:candidateSourceHash is the sha256 of that field's
# @x-gmeow-english lexical text, so a later prose edit surfaces as drift under make verify.
# All four are realized in the OWL / full-FOL projection only (RiskProjectionOnly,
# SoundUnderApproximation) and disclosed in the loss ledger — the native monotone chase does
# not enforce a nested-existential, biconditional, or strong-negation head.

logic:cqDeceptionDivergenceHolds
    a logic:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "deception divergence holds"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The positive competency case the deception-divergence necessary-condition must satisfy: a gmeow:Event of gmeow:eventTypeDeception that carries a gmeow:heldStandpoint and a gmeow:projectedStandpoint — both gmeow:DoxasticStandpointClaims — whose claim modalities differ satisfies the axiom. The held/projected gap is the deceptive act; the well-formed case the formula admits."@x-gmeow-english .
logic:cqDeceptionDivergenceGapRejected
    a logic:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "deception without a divergent gap is rejected"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The negative countermodel the deception-divergence axiom must respect: a gmeow:Event typed gmeow:eventTypeDeception whose held and projected standpoints coincide (no divergent claim-state) violates the necessary condition in the OWL / full-FOL projection. HONEST SCOPE: the native monotone StratifiedNAF chase does NOT enforce this existential, negation-bearing condition, so the rejection is realized only in the projection and is disclosed in the loss ledger."@x-gmeow-english .
logic:candidateDeceptionHeldProjectedDivergence
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "deception is a divergent held/projected gap"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The necessary-condition formalization candidate governing logic:deceptionHeldProjectedDivergence: every gmeow:eventTypeDeception event carries a gmeow:heldStandpoint and a gmeow:projectedStandpoint doxastic claim whose modalities diverge — the gap is the act, not a truth verdict. A necessary, not sufficient, condition (a licensed-falsehood projection also has a gap yet is not deception; see logic:candidateLicensedFalsehoodNotDeception). Realized in the OWL / full-FOL projection (nested existential with strong negation), non-monotone, disclosed in the loss ledger — NOT natively enforced. Harvested from gmeow:eventTypeDeception's skos:definition, so an edit to that prose surfaces as drift. Accepted through review with a positive case (logic:cqDeceptionDivergenceHolds) and a negative countermodel (logic:cqDeceptionDivergenceGapRejected)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:candidateSourceHash "sha256:b8643c1fb0583812f11a418b01244c78af2e5f97edc306479a6ae717dfcec391"^^xsd:string ;
    logic:candidateExtractionProvenance "hand-harvested from gmeow:eventTypeDeception's skos:definition, which states that a deception projects a standpoint diverging from the one actually held (design/LOGIC-FOUNDATION.md, §Typed formalization governance)"^^xsd:string ;
    logic:candidateScope "necessary condition: gmeow:eventTypeDeception ⇒ a divergent held/projected DoxasticStandpointClaim pair"^^xsd:string ;
    logic:candidateContract logic:StratifiedNAFProfile ;
    logic:candidateCategory logic:CategoryNecessaryCondition ;
    logic:candidateLifecycle logic:CandidateAccepted ;
    logic:reviewedBy logic:reviewerLogicFoundation ;
    logic:candidatePositiveCase logic:cqDeceptionDivergenceHolds ;
    logic:candidateNegativeCase logic:cqDeceptionDivergenceGapRejected ;
    logic:candidateProjectionBehavior logic:SoundUnderApproximation ;
    logic:candidateSemanticRisk logic:RiskProjectionOnly ;
    logic:candidateFormalizes gmeow:eventTypeDeception ;
    logic:candidateSourceField logic:ProseFieldDefinition .

logic:cqFalsehoodRefutedHolds
    a logic:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "falsehood as refuted claim holds"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The positive competency case the falsehood-as-refuted-claim equivalence must satisfy: a gmeow:StandpointClaim whose gmeow:claimModality is gmeow:refuted is exactly one whose gmeow:claimPolarity is gmeow:polarityDeny and gmeow:claimModalForce is gmeow:modalForceNecessary (□¬ — settled-false-in-frame). The well-formed case the biconditional admits; there is no separate isFalse flag."@x-gmeow-english .
logic:cqFalsehoodRefutedGlobalTruthRejected
    a logic:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "falsehood as global truth verdict is rejected"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The negative countermodel the falsehood-as-refuted-claim equivalence must respect: reading gmeow:refuted as a frame-independent isFalse flag on the proposition — rather than as the necessarily-denying stance of one standpoint — is rejected (Principle 11: no global truth verdict). HONEST SCOPE: the equivalence is realized in the OWL / full-FOL projection; the native monotone chase does not enforce a biconditional, so this is disclosed in the loss ledger."@x-gmeow-english .
logic:candidateFalsehoodIsRefutedClaim
    a logic:NamedIndividual ,
        logic:FormalizationCandidate ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/logic> ;
    rdfs:label "falsehood is a refuted standpoint claim"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The equivalence-definition formalization candidate governing logic:falsehoodIsRefutedClaim: to carry gmeow:claimModality gmeow:refuted is exactly to be a gmeow:StandpointClaim that necessarily denies its proposition (gmeow:polarityDeny ∧ gmeow:modalForceNecessary = □¬). This pins 'falsehood = refuted StandpointClaim' as a frame-relative configuration, never a global truth flag, and ties the legacy five-value modality twin to its canonical axes. Realized in the OWL / full-FOL projection (biconditional), non-monotone, disclosed in the loss ledger — NOT natively enforced. Harvested from gmeow:refuted's skos:definition. Accepted through review with a positive case (logic:cqFalsehoodRefutedHolds) and a negative countermodel (logic:cqFalsehoodRefutedGlobalTruthRejected)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:candidateSourceHash "sha256:5a0209094cea0065b721afa45f2bda0dacaf1b8c659bf77f5c52c7632f6b0b0e"^^xsd:string ;
    logic:candidateExtractionProvenance "hand-harvested from gmeow:refuted's skos:definition, which states the standpoint DENIES the proposition (□¬, settled-false-in-frame) (design/LOGIC-FOUNDATION.md, §Typed formalization governance)"^^xsd:string ;
    logic:candidateScope "equivalence: gmeow:claimModality gmeow:refuted ⟺ StandpointClaim with polarityDeny ∧ modalForceNecessary"^^xsd:string ;
    logic:candidateContract logic:StratifiedNAFProfile ;
    logic:candidateCategory logic:CategoryEquivalenceDefinition ;
    logic:candidateLifecycle logic:CandidateAccepted ;
    logic:reviewedBy logic:reviewerLogicFoundation ;
    logic:candidatePositiveCase logic:cqFalsehoodRefutedHolds ;
    logic:candidateNegativeCase logic:cqFalsehoodRefutedGlobalTruthRejected ;
    logic:candidateProjectionBehavior logic:SoundUnderApproximation ;
    logic:candidateSemanticRisk logic:RiskProjectionOnly ;
    logic:candidateFormalizes gmeow:refuted ;
